      {
        "id": "D1",
        "name": "Patches never built by the author or by CI, and the reviewer burden of \"did you compile test this, under which config\"",
        "dive": {
          "id": "D1",
          "name": "Patches whose touched objects are never built by the author or by CI, and the reviewer burden of \"did you compile test this, under which config\"",
          "refined_description": "Problem statement. A Linux patch touches files whose build depends on a Boolean condition over CONFIG_* options, and over the architecture. The kernel's documented answer to \"does it build\" is a set of canned configurations (allnoconfig, allmodconfig, allyesconfig, sometimes defconfig) plus the submitter's judgement about \"applicable\" options and architectures. Nothing tells a submitter, reviewer or bot, per touched object, whether those configurations actually compile it, or what the smallest change to a base config is that would. As a result: (a) authors often compile-test one config (e.g. \"arm64 defconfig, W=1\"), or not at all; (b) reviewers cannot judge how much a \"compile tested\" claim covers; (c) bots (0-day, linux-next, netdev NIPA, Media CI) sample a fixed config set and give no per-object coverage statement; (d) drivers and files that no standard config builds stay unbuilt until a maintainer hand-edits Kconfig (COMPILE_TEST) or the file is found to have no Kconfig/Makefile entry at all.\n\nWhat this dossier now supports, with caveats. Strong: the documented burden (checklist, netdev, KVM, Media, tip), the sampled nature of CI configs, and a steady 2022-2026 stream of maintainer-written COMPILE_TEST patches whose stated purpose is build coverage. Moderate: the krepair numbers (a single research group, line-level, includes #ifdef arms). Weak or absent: any direct statement from a reviewer or maintainer that they want a per-object \"which config builds this\" tool; any measurement of how often a merged patch's objects were never built by any CI config; any survey of kernel developers on compile testing. In the threads re-opened, builds are chosen by named configs (allmodconfig, defconfig, i386 allyesconfig) and failures are found after the fact by bots; nobody in them asks for a tool. Maintainers also push back in the other direction: compile testing is \"not sufficient\" (staging), and a Kconfig fix is often preferred to config tooling.\n\nSearch limits. The shared WebSearch budget was exhausted. The three new searches I attempted returned no results, so this refinement has zero new searches. Everything below comes from direct WebFetch of about 30 specific URLs, mostly re-opening the load-bearing sources. The 15-new-searches requirement is not met. Items marked reopened_and_verified=false are carried over from the earlier dossiers or gap-scout claims.\n\nCorrections to earlier claims after re-opening:\n- G19: the quoted sentence is Yixun Lan's patch text of 2025-02-18, not Geert Uytterhoeven's reply. Geert's reply of 2025-03-17 was \"Perhaps this is an issue with the uart driver?\"\n- G62/[1050027]: the \"allmodconfig ... hide problems\" sentence is LWN's own wording (Corbet, 2025-12-12), not a verbatim Torvalds quote. Ojeda's position is a paraphrase.\n- G1/G0: the \"choice option\" sentence is Guenter Roeck's. The \"allmodconfig ... assume ... set?\" sentence is Damien Le Moal's.\n- [31]/LWN 608614: the \"never been compiled\" sentence is Corbet's summary of Stephen Rothwell (Kernel Summit 2014), not a Rothwell quote. The URL that holds it is https://lwn.net/Articles/608617/ (dated 2014-08-20).\n- G10: the series author is Rosen Penev, and the cover letter is dated 2025-08-26.\n- G14: the author is Atul Akella (remoteproc mtk_scp), posted 2026-10-15.",
          "who_has_it": "Patch authors, especially drive-by and janitor contributors without cross-compilers or the target hardware. Reviewers and subsystem maintainers who must decide whether a \"compile tested\" claim means anything. Linux-next integrators, 0-day, KernelCI, netdev NIPA and Media CI operators, who pick configs by hand or by sampling. Maintainers of drivers that are tied to one architecture or SoC and so are built by no standard config. Kbuild-based projects with board or defconfig granularity (U-Boot, barebox, coreboot, BusyBox), which have the same unbuilt-code problem (gap-scout claims G26-G47, not re-opened here).",
          "evidence": [
