              "tool_or_process": "Kernel test robot (0-day), linux-next daily builds, netdev NIPA, Media CI, KernelCI, TuxSuite/LKFT",
              "what_it_does": "Post-posting or post-merge build tests over a fixed or sampled config set (allmodconfig, allyesconfig, defconfig, randconfig). NIPA documents only allmodconfig plus 32-bit allmodconfig builds.",
              "limits": "No per-object coverage statement. Sampled configs, and delayed (Wu: tests continue for about a week). Per the earlier dossier, tested config counts vary per report (19 tested and 125-140 skipped in two 2025 tip reports; not re-opened). They detect breakage after the fact; they do not say what was left unbuilt.",
              "url": "https://github.com/linux-netdev/nipa"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "COMPILE_TEST convention (|| COMPILE_TEST added by maintainers)",
              "what_it_does": "Lets drivers for exotic hardware be built in allmodconfig/allyesconfig so bots compile them.",
              "limits": "Manual, driver by driver (Golaszewski 2025, Hovold 2025-26, Penev 2025). Can break on Kconfig choices (Octeon 2023) or fail in non-trivial ways (Airoha 2025). The kconfig docs say it is only encouraged.",
              "url": "https://docs.kernel.org/kbuild/kconfig-language.html"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "klocalizer / krepair / koverage (Kmax suite, kmax 4.10 on PyPI, Aug 2026)",
              "what_it_does": "klocalizer --repair repairs a .config so that a patch's lines build; --include foo.o targets an object; koverage checks how much of a patch a config covers; handles #ifdef arms through SuperC.",
              "limits": "README: some lines may still be omitted, and a patch touching both arms of an #ifdef needs several configs. Per the paper: header-only patches unsupported, one architecture per run, and it depends on constraint collectors (kclause 2-3 min, kmax 10-15 min per earlier claim [274], not re-opened). No kmax runtime figure on the README. SuperC and Java setup. Constraint accuracy is not reported in the paper (earlier claim [138], inference). No visible on-list use in the threads I opened.",
              "url": "https://pypi.org/project/kmax/"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Vampyr/undertaker",
              "what_it_does": "Configuration-aware compile testing at #ifdef-block level.",
              "limits": "Reported as unmaintained and limited to old kernels in the krepair paper's related work (earlier claim [275], not re-opened).",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.17966v1"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Build farms and containers (TuxMake, TuxSuite, Linaro LKFT; syzbot patch testing)",
              "what_it_does": "Run builds across arch/toolchain/config matrices on request.",
              "limits": "Execute a matrix; they do not decide which config a given patch needs (earlier claims [130],[181],[185], not re-opened).",
              "url": "https://pypi.org/project/tuxmake"
            }
          ],
          "remaining_gap": "1. No tool answers, before posting and per touched object, 'is each file I changed compiled by the configs I (or CI) used, and if not, which minimal option change builds it', with an explanation chain that a reviewer can read. klocalizer comes closest but works at line level through SuperC, one architecture at a time, with no explanation output documented, and no head-to-head with a Makefile-level exact analysis exists.\n2. There is no per-object build-coverage statement from any CI. Whether a merged patch's objects were ever built by any CI config is not published, and the krepair group itself can only see configs of reports where a regression was detected.\n3. Objects no standard config builds (never-built drivers, choice alternatives, arch-tied code) are found only by hand today: Kitt 2022, Hovold 2025-26, Golaszewski 2025.\n4. Demand evidence is thin: no survey, no reviewer thread asking for such a tool, no on-list mention of klocalizer in anything I opened. Maintainers' stated priorities (Maintainers Summit 2025, KernelCI survey 2020 from the gap-scout) are per-patch testing and CI process, not config selection.\n5. Object granularity cannot see #ifdef arms, header-only patches (about 2% per krepair), or runtime-gated behaviour.",
          "kfold_contribution": "Be modest and specific. kfold would contribute (i) a whole-tree table of per-object build conditions for Linux 7.2.8, computed in about a minute, so 'is each touched object built under this .config?' costs a lookup instead of a per-patch constraint extraction; (ii) a 'why' answer that names the failing conjunct (for example a missing parent-directory option), which is what the Airoha and Octeon threads needed and did by trial and error; (iii) 'config-for', a minimal-delta .config verified with olddefconfig and by checking that the .o files compile (384 of 437 stable commits got a configuration; 402 touch .c/.S); (iv) 'blindspots', a list of objects no listed standard config builds, grouped by MAINTAINERS entry, which is the automated version of what Golaszewski, Hovold and Kitt do by hand. It does not claim to replace 0-day or any build, does not model #ifdef arms or headers, does not claim novelty over Kmax/klocalizer for 'config for a patch', and its Kconfig model is an over-approximation used only by config-for. The claim to defend is object-level exactness, explanation and speed, validated against real builds, not new capability in kind.",
          "quantitative_facts": [
            "krepair FSE 2024 (re-opened): 507 patches sampled from about 71,000 mainline patches; mean patch-line coverage defconfig 21.7%, one randconfig 29.2%, allyesconfig 88.5%, krepair 98.5%. Line level, includes #ifdef arms. Cite as one source (Yildiran, Oh, Lawall, Gazzillo). Source: https://arxiv.org/html/2404.17966v1",
            "Same paper (re-opened): 63% of 0-day reports with configs use randconfig; 11% of patches need cross-compilation; 2% touch only headers; 7 patches could not be built because of toolchain problems; 27 failed builds were excluded. Source: https://arxiv.org/html/2404.17966v1",
            "Same paper, from earlier dossier (not re-opened): allyesconfig about four hours vs about 20 minutes for defconfig; the OSS 2022 slides say three hours, so cite the paper. The slides PDF could not be read by the fetcher this time (the 13,411-commit v5.13 figures remain unverified here).",
            "Brezillon quoting a 0-day report in May 2018 (re-opened): 'configs tested: 142', yet m68k/allmodconfig was not among them. Source: https://lkml.rescloud.iu.edu/1805.1/04746.html",
            "netdev NIPA documents two build checks, both allmodconfig (one 32-bit); no other config is documented (re-opened). Source: https://github.com/linux-netdev/nipa",
            "kmax on PyPI is at version 4.10, released 2026-08-18 (re-opened); the README gives no klocalizer runtime and says a full kismet run takes about an hour. Source: https://pypi.org/project/kmax/",
            "Dated examples of COMPILE_TEST-for-coverage patches, all re-opened: 2022-04 (Roquadros/Kozlowski gpmc), 2023-06 (Octeon, dropped), 2025-04 (gpio series), 2025-08 (spi rb4xx), 2025-11 (bcm2835). These are anecdotes; I have no count. Do not turn them into a rate.",
            "Frequency impressions from the gap-scout (not re-opened): mail-archive search counts 2,372 matches for 'compile tested only' and 2,133 for 'allmodconfig' on linux-kernel; these are word matches over many years, inflated by bot mail. Do not cite as a rate.",
            "Absence data: I did not find, in any thread I opened, a request for a tool that maps files to configs, nor a mention of klocalizer or koverage. This is absence of evidence from a limited, mirror-based sample, not proof."
          ],
          "proposed_experiment": "Single experiment on the existing assets, run sequentially under the machine rules (one process at a time, no new builds). Use the 402 .c/.S-touching commits of v7.2.7..v7.2.8 already processed by config-for, plus the object inventories of the four real Linux builds (i386 tinyconfig, x86-64 defconfig, Debian, x86-64 allmodconfig). Step 1 (object-level coverage by proxy configs): for each commit, take the touched objects and use kfold's condition table to compute whether each is built under (a) x86-64 allmodconfig alone (Torvalds's and netdev's reference), (b) netdev's pair (allmodconfig plus 32-bit allmodconfig, if an i386 allmodconfig inventory can be produced, otherwise note it as missing), (c) the union of the four available configs. Report the share of commits with at least one touched object unbuilt under each, and the share with no touched object built at all, split by directory (drivers, arch, net, fs, other). Step 2 (ground truth): check each prediction against the real object inventories of the builds that exist, reporting precision and recall of 'built / not built' (reusing the 400 why verdicts already checked). Step 3 (cost of the fix): for commits that allmodconfig misses, report the config-for edit size (options changed from the base config) and whether olddefconfig plus compilation succeeded; compare with the krepair figures of under 1.53% changed options for 99% of patches, labelled as a different sample and granularity. Step 4 (optional, small): run klocalizer/koverage on a random sample of about 30 commits, one at a time, and compare patch-level verdicts at object level, recording its runtime and failures. Caveats to put in the paper: the sample is stable-update backports (fixes), so it under-represents new-driver patches; only x86; header-only and #ifdef-only patches are excluded or reported as 'not covered by kfold'. This gives an object-level measurement of the gap that the krepair line-level numbers only suggest.",
          "risks": "1. Demand evidence is anecdotal. Compile-only evidence is explicitly refused by some maintainers (Khan 2026), and most hard build breakage is caught by bots and linux-next (Rothwell, Bottomley, earlier dossier claims, not re-opened). The paper must frame kfold as predictive and explanatory, not as another builder.\n2. The strongest statistics come from one research group (krepair) at line level, and they overlap with Kmax/klocalizer, which already offers 'repair a config for a patch'. The paper must not claim novelty for that.\n3. Object granularity misses #ifdef arms and header-only patches (about 2%), and the x86 focus misses the 11% of patches needing cross-compilation.\n4. Evidence is drawn from mailing-list mirrors through a summarizing fetcher. Quotes were re-checked on the page but page text can be truncated; the Mauro, Wu, Kozlowski and Octeon quotes are from the visible message bodies only.\n5. Search coverage is limited: no new search ran; lore.kernel.org was not reachable; U-Boot, barebox, coreboot and BusyBox items are carried over unverified.\n6. A selection bias in any frequency claim: the threads here are the ones that surfaced, not a sample. Do not report them as rates.\n7. Possible reviewer objection: 'allmodconfig plus COMPILE_TEST is good enough'. The experiment should measure what that pair leaves unbuilt rather than argue it.",
          "confidence": "medium"
        },
        "verdicts": [
          {
            "lens": "Evidence auditor, D1: patches never built by author or CI, and the reviewer burden of \"did you compile test this, under which config\"",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "Every quote I re-opened is verbatim and on the page cited, so the problem is not fabrication. The problem is that the evidence shows the burden is documented and that coverage gaps exist, but it does not show that the gap is common among merged patches or that anyone wants a tool for it. The dossier admits this in its remaining_gap section, and I agree.",
              "Independence: the quantitative evidence is one group. The krepair paper (FSE 2024; abstract pages confirm it appeared in PACMSE vol. 1 FSE, DOI 10.1145/3643746), the OSS 2022 slides, and the paper's own 0-day statistics (63% randconfig, 11% cross-compile, 2% header-only) are all Yildiran/Oh/Lawall/Gazzillo. They count as one source. The same group wrote klocalizer/Kmax, a direct competitor, so krepair's gap numbers are an interested party's motivation.",
              "krepair 21.7% defconfig figure is weaker than the dossier implies. I re-read the paper: the baseline is the x86 defconfig, but the 507 patches are a random sample of all mainline patches from 2021-09-19 to 2022-09-18, and 11% need cross-compilation. A patch for another architecture is therefore measured against an x86 defconfig, which pushes the mean down. The paper does not say how this case was handled (its answer was 'not explicitly stated'). Coverage is counted in lines of preprocessed .i files, including #ifdef arms. The figure shows that a single canned config misses lines. It is not an object-level number and not a fair 'right defconfig' number.",
              "krepair reports allyesconfig (88.5%), not allmodconfig. The dossier and the netdev/Torvalds anecdotes treat allmodconfig as the working unit. The paper's own table does not measure it (allmodconfig appears only in the motivation, in the 0-day report analysis). So no number in the dossier says how much of a patch allmodconfig leaves unbuilt. This is the gap the proposed experiment would fill.",
              "The four-hour allyesconfig build time is confirmed ('around four hours on average on a typical development workstation'). The OSS 2022 slides say three hours and I could not open them, so cite the paper only.",
              "Hovold/bcm2835 anecdote: the page I opened (lkml.iu.edu/2511.2/04659.html) is Vinod Koul's reply dated 2025-11-22 (applied), which quotes Hovold's patch. The quote is verbatim but it is quoted text, and the 2025-11-20 patch date was not visible to me. The July 2025 Hovold sentence that 'COMPILE_TEST should not enable every driver because allyesconfig serves that purpose' was not re-opened by me and rests on the dossier alone. Treat it as unverified.",
              "Khan (staging, 2026-03-24) is weaker than presented as counter-evidence in either direction. She says explicitly that she is not the maintainer. Her objection is that the patch changed logic without explanation and that submitting implies testing on hardware. It is about hardware testing, not about compile coverage, so it bounds the value of any build evidence but says nothing about config selection. The Lunn quote (2026-04-01, r8152 thread) is likewise about hardware testing, in a discussion of an AI reviewer. It shows that compile-tested-only patches are routine, not that coverage is unclear.",
              "Airoha thread (2025-02-09): the dossier says the author 'removed COMPILE_TEST'. True, but it omits that Greg KH said removal was not the right fix and that COMPILE_TEST should be able to build the code; Larsson went ahead anyway (v4). Greg's 'I guess I need to enable OF on my test builds' is a guess, and the page does not identify him as the tty maintainer (the thread is about the tty tree). This is still a trial-and-error example, but the maintainer's stance cuts against the 'drop COMPILE_TEST' conclusion.",
              "Octeon thread: the date given as 2023-06-22 is the date of the drop message (17:52 EST) and Roeck's reply. Le Moal's 'Given that this is an allmodconfig' message is dated 6/21. Roeck's 'is a choice option' and the next-20230621 failure are confirmed. The dropped-at-rc7 detail is confirmed, and the reason given is that the fixes were 'not worth the effort', so the case is partly about effort, not only about config blindness.",
              "Anecdote sampling: the 'COMPILE_TEST for coverage' threads are selected examples. Golaszewski appears twice (G9, G21) and Hovold several times; these are a handful of maintainers acting repeatedly. They show a recurring maintenance chore, not a measured rate. Do not cite as a frequency. The mail-archive counts (2,372 and 2,133) are word-match counts inflated by bot mail and should not be cited; I did not re-open them.",
              "NIPA claim confirmed: the README documents two build checks, build_allmodconfig_warn and build_32bit (allmodconfig for 32-bit), with no allyesconfig or defconfig, and a to-do item 'build one-by-one for a PR'. But the README also lists build_clang and build_clang_rust in an example, with no description, so 'documents only two' is slightly too strong. Say 'documents two allmodconfig-based checks'. A README is not evidence of what the CI actually runs.",
              "0-day thread 2018 confirmed (Brezillon: 'configs tested: 142', m68k allmodconfig absent; Wu: 'Yeah sorry, there is no 100% guarantee.' plus tests continue for about a week). It is 8 years old; the bot's coverage since then is unknown. Chehab 2017 is also old and confirmed as a quote.",
              "Demand evidence remains absent: no maintainer or reviewer asks for a per-object tool in any thread I opened. The search budget was exhausted, so I could not run a search for klocalizer mentions on lore/LKML or for contradicting data (for example studies showing that 0-day plus linux-next catch nearly all config-dependent build breaks). The prior dossier's absence claim is therefore not strengthened by my audit.",
              "Torvalds 2025-02-26 quote confirmed verbatim, but note the surrounding sentence says he does an allmodconfig build on x86-64 'likely to be the config that compiles the *most* code' and treats a failure as a big failure. He is describing why his testing is narrow but also why it is adequate for him; it is not a complaint about missing coverage tools."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "LWN 1050027 (Corbet, 2025-12-12): 'cause some code to be disabled, and can thus hide problems' is the article's paraphrase of Torvalds, not a direct quote. 'some repositories clearly are not seeing any testing at all' is quoted from Torvalds. Ojeda's 'testing with canned configurations' is the article's paraphrase. Confirmed the dossier's correction.",
              "Submit checklist: the phrase 'multiple architectures' does not appear verbatim. The text is 'Builds on multiple CPU architectures by using local cross-compile tools or some other build farm.' Also verbatim: 'Passes allnoconfig, allmodconfig'. The checklist does not name allyesconfig, COMPILE_TEST or randconfig (confirmed absence).",
              "Netdev doc: exact sentence is 'At the very minimum your changes must survive an `allyesconfig` and an `allmodconfig` build with `W=1` set without new warnings or failures.' Quote the full sentence or truncate with an ellipsis, not the shortened form as if complete. It tells authors not to use patchwork checks as a way to test their work, rather than forbidding a 'compile service' in those words.",
              "Hovold bcm2835: the archived page is Koul's reply dated 2025-11-22, quoting Hovold's patch; attribute accordingly.",
              "Octeon: Le Moal's 'Given that this is an allmodconfig, I would assume that CONFIG_CAVIUM_OCTEON_SOC is set ?' is dated 2023-06-21; the drop is 2023-06-22.",
              "krepair: describe 21.7% as 'x86 defconfig, line-level, mean over a 2021-09 to 2022-09 mainline sample that includes non-x86 patches'. Do not say it is the share of patches or objects missed.",
              "Khan: she says she is not the maintainer; describe as 'a reviewer', not 'a staging maintainer'. Airoha: add that Greg KH argued against removing COMPILE_TEST.",
              "NIPA: say 'two allmodconfig-based build checks documented in the README' rather than 'documents only two build checks'."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "motivation",
            "summary": "I opened 19 pages, and every quote I re-checked matched the page: the submit checklist, the netdev doc, LWN 1050027, the krepair arXiv paper, the Octeon, Kozlowski, Airoha, Torvalds, Golaszewski, Lunn, Khan, Kitt, Chehab and 2018 0-day threads, the NIPA README, kernelnewbies and kmax on PyPI. The policy and workflow burden (named canned configs, hand-judged 'applicable' options, sampled bot configs) is well supported. The weak points are the strength and independence of the evidence. The only numbers (krepair's 21.7%, 29.2%, 88.5%, 98.5%) come from one research group that also built a competing tool. They are line-level, count #ifdef arms, use an x86 defconfig baseline over a sample that includes non-x86 patches, and never measure allmodconfig. The anecdotes are selected COMPILE_TEST patches from a few maintainers, and some are tangential to config coverage (Khan and Lunn are about hardware testing). Nothing shows that reviewers or maintainers want a per-object tool, and I could not search further because the search budget was exhausted. For the paper, use D1 as motivation: cite the policy text, cite krepair once with its caveats, and give 2 or 3 dated threads as illustrations, not a rate. Present the object-level measurement of what allmodconfig and defconfig leave unbuilt as an RQ, since no published figure answers it.",
            "sources_checked": [
              {
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.17966v1",
                "opened": true,
                "quote_verbatim": true,
                "note": "krepair numbers, 'no assurance' sentence, 63% randconfig, 11% cross-compile, 2% header-only, four-hour allyesconfig all confirmed. Sample is 2021-09-19 to 2022-09-18 mainline; baseline is x86 defconfig; line-level on .i files; allmodconfig not evaluated. Last 9,319 characters not read."
              },
              {
                "url": "https://docs.kernel.org/process/submit-checklist.html",
                "opened": true,
                "quote_verbatim": true,
                "note": "Confirmed 'applicable or modified CONFIG options =y, =m, and =n' and 'very hard to get right with testing---brainpower pays off here'. No COMPILE_TEST or randconfig. 'multiple architectures' is not verbatim."
              },
              {
                "url": "https://docs.kernel.org/process/maintainer-netdev.html",
                "opened": true,
                "quote_verbatim": true,
                "note": "Full sentence confirmed; shorter excerpt in dossier is a truncation. Doc says not to use patchwork checks as a way to test."
              },
              {
                "url": "https://lwn.net/Articles/1050027/",
                "opened": true,
                "quote_verbatim": true,
                "note": "Dated 2025-12-12, Corbet. 'some repositories clearly are not seeing any testing at all' is quoted from Torvalds; the allmodconfig and canned-config statements are the article's paraphrase."
              },
              {
                "url": "https://lkml.rescloud.iu.edu/2306.2/07959.html",
                "opened": true,
                "quote_verbatim": true,
                "note": "Octeon: Roeck 'choice option', Le Moal 'Given that this is an allmodconfig...', dropped at rc7 as not worth the effort (2023-06-22)."
