          },
          {
            "lens": "Hostile competitor analyst, D1",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "use_in_paper": "rq3_task",
            "summary": "The slice of D1 that is \"given a patch, find a config that builds its changed code, and measure patch coverage\" is already addressed by a published, open-source, partly adopted tool family (klocalizer/krepair/koverage, FSE 2024: 98.5% mean line coverage vs 88.5% allyesconfig on 507 patches). It handles #ifdef arms, which kfold cannot. A hostile reviewer will also say that the \"kmax is slow\" argument is mostly gone, because krepair now collects Makefile constraints per file on demand, with caching: a few minutes per patch, under a minute for 93.8% of cached runs. What remains open is narrower. (1) Object-level exactness validated against real build inventories: klocalizer's own docs concede its formulas may be inexact and \"were wrong in some cases\", and the FSE paper reports no constraint-accuracy measurement. (2) Explanation chains: no competitor documents a \"why is OBJ not built under this .config\" output naming the failing conjunct. (3) Whole-tree \"blindspots\" by MAINTAINERS entry, which today are found by hand through COMPILE_TEST patches. (4) A single object-level table reusable by CI, CVE triage and other Kbuild projects (Barebox, U-Boot, coreboot, BusyBox). Demand evidence is thin: I found no reviewer or maintainer asking for such a tool, and no on-list mention of klocalizer. Several maintainers say compile-testing is insufficient anyway. Best use: motivate with the krepair numbers (cited as someone else's line-level result) and make the kfold-versus-klocalizer/koverage head-to-head on the v7.2.7..v7.2.8 commits an RQ3 task, framed as exactness and explanation, never as \"first\" and not as speed against krepair.",
            "issues": [
              "PRIOR ART MISSING FROM THE DOSSIER: JMake (Lawall and Muller, Inria HAL hal-01555711, ca. 2017, 'Dependable compilation for kernel janitors') is a tool that checks that the lines a patch changes are really compiled. I could not open it: every HAL, DBLP and Semantic Scholar fetch returned an Anubis access-denied page or 403, and the search budget was exhausted. What I did see: (a) a search-engine summary saying its finding was that allyesconfig with the right architecture is sufficient for most file-modifying commits and that the check takes about 30 seconds or less (unverified secondary text), and (b) the krepair paper's related-work sentence, via the fetch summarizer: 'is a previous attempt to find a configuration that covers a patch. However, it tries only a fixed set of standard configurations. JMake also introduces a mutation-based approach to determining if a line of code is covered.' This is both direct prior art for the janitor/pre-posting workflow and a possible counter-claim that a fixed config set is enough. It must be read and cited before submission, and the paper must not call pre-posting coverage checking new.",
              "SPEED IS NOT A DEFENSIBLE DIFFERENTIATOR AGAINST KREPAIR. The krepair paper (arxiv 2404.17966, Section 4.3, via fetch summarizer) says Kconfig constraints are cached per kernel version and architecture, and Kbuild Makefile constraints 'only need to be collected once for a given file'. It says the authors 'modified the kmax interface to support collecting per-file constraints on-demand'. Reported runtime: 'the large majority of krepair runtimes take only a few minutes, even without caching', with caching the maximum is about 19 minutes and under one minute for 93.8% of runs. The earlier dossier claim [274] that 'kmax 10-15 minutes is the bottleneck kfold replaces' is therefore not supported. The 10-15 minute figure (with kclause 2-3 min and SuperC about a minute) is the paper's per-line cost listing, not a current whole-tree cost. The dossier's 84 s for the 2017 Kmax x86 run (a slides summary, not re-opened) would also make kfold's ~1 minute whole-tree run comparable, not faster. Claim speed only for 'whole-tree table, all objects, one pass', never against klocalizer per-patch.",
              "klocalizer is NOT strictly one-architecture-per-run as the dossier implies. docs/advanced.md says it tries architectures in turn and stops at the first one that satisfies the constraints, while 'Passing multiple architectures is not supported' for generating an arbitrary config. The accurate statement is: no multi-architecture patch coverage in one invocation, and no union across architectures.",
              "NIPA: the README names build_allmodconfig_warn (W=1 C=1) and build_32bit (allmodconfig for 32-bit) as the two described build checks, but also names build_clang and build_clang_rust in a --disable-test example, with no config described. 'Documents only two build checks' should read 'describes two build checks, both allmodconfig'.",
              "Adoption evidence for the competitor is mixed. Positive: the krepair paper says one repaired config exposed errors, 'This patch has been accepted for inclusion in mainline Linux' (via fetch summarizer), and the kmax README credits the Intel 0-day team for working to include kismet (not klocalizer or krepair) in the kernel test robot. A search result confirmed only kismet, not krepair or klocalizer, in the robot. Negative: kmax PyPI downloads were 30 per day, 189 per week and 330 per month on pypistats.org (includes mirrors and bots), GitHub 72 stars, 29 forks (earlier fetch), and there is no on-list use in any thread seen. Setup needs SuperC, Java, CLASSPATH and COMPILER_INSTALL_PATH. So 'nobody uses it' is not defensible. 'Research-grade, small user base, installation-heavy' is.",
              "A production competitor for the object-level built-or-not question exists outside patch review. CIP kernel-cve-triage (Siemens/CIP) has checks check_introduced_by_file_compiled and check_fixed_by_file_compiled and needs the kmax toolsuite. README (seen): formula generation on a new kernel revision is 'computationally expensive and can take a noticeable amount of time', cached afterwards, and the tool is 'currently tailored to our use case'. It is the strongest 'is this file built under this config' adoption evidence, but it is Kmax-based, per revision, and for CVE applicability (D9), not for patch submission. PatchLens (FSE 2026, arXiv 2606.25863, abstract seen) recovers Boolean 'Vulnerability Impact Conditions' per patch through AST-level hunk alignment and 'lightweight build system analysis', on 1,192 Linux patches (average 1.84 variables). It handles source-level presence conditions, which kfold does not. Both bound any claim that nobody computes patch-level or object-level conditions.",
              "Existing in-kernel and CI mechanisms cover much of the practical need for hard build breaks: allmodconfig/allyesconfig norms, COMPILE_TEST (the Kconfig docs say 'it is encouraged to increase build coverage by adding an \"|| COMPILE_TEST\" clause'), linux-next daily builds and 0-day (kernel test robot) sampled configs. The earlier dossier shows this is the working practice and that maintainers see no pain big enough to ask for a tool. D1's demand claim therefore rests on anecdotes plus krepair line-level numbers from one research group.",
              "Not verified in this pass: tuxmake PyPI page (fetch failed with a page-load error), kernelconfig.io (fetched, but it only says 'search config information for linux kernel modules' and shows no file-to-config mapping), cvehound (not re-fetched), Kconfirm, ConfigFix (Kconfig-side, not build-side). The cip-project/kernel-cve-triage GitHub URL returned 404, so the GitLab README was used. WebSearch budget was exhausted, so no new discovery searches were possible. Any 2025-2026 competitor not in the earlier sweep could still be missing."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Replace 'kmax 10-15 min is the bottleneck kfold replaces' with: krepair collects Makefile constraints per file on demand with caching; per-patch runs take a few minutes, under a minute for 93.8% of cached runs. kfold's speed argument is limited to 'one whole-tree table in about a minute, reusable for every object, patch and CI query'.",
              "Replace 'klocalizer works on one architecture per run' with: it tries architectures in turn and stops at the first satisfying one; it does not support passing several architectures, and does not give a union.",
              "Replace 'NIPA documents only two build checks' with: NIPA's README describes two build checks, both allmodconfig (one 32-bit), and names clang and clang-rust build tests without describing their configs.",
              "Add JMake (Lawall and Muller, 2017) to related work and the novelty caveats once the paper is read. Do not write that no pre-posting 'was my changed code compiled' checker exists.",
              "Do not call krepair's 98.5% an object-level number. It is a mean over patch lines on 507 patches, includes #ifdef arms, and was measured against a different sample than the stable-update backports. Compare only after rerunning koverage on the same commits.",
              "Quote precision: the verbatim 'extracted formulas' sentences in kmax docs/advanced.md are 'The extracted formulas may not be exact.' and 'Klocalizer's formulas were wrong in some cases.' Use them as documented limits, not as a measured error rate.",
              "Soften kfold_contribution item (iv) and 'explanation chains': say 'no explanation output is documented in the README or paper I read', not 'klocalizer has none'. koverage emits JSON and I did not inspect it. The 'why' differentiator must be shown by a worked example on the same unbuilt object."
            ],
