          {
            "lens": "Fit and experiment designer, D1: patches whose touched objects no author or CI config builds; reviewer burden of 'did you compile test this, under which config'",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "FIT. Only one slice of D1 is an object-selection problem: 'is the .c/.S file I touched compiled under config C, and if not, what is the smallest edit that compiles it'. Kbuild Makefile conditions plus a Kconfig model answer exactly that. The rest of D1 is not kfold's: whether the author ran a build or a test (a process question; Khan 2026 refuses compile-only evidence), whether CI covers the patch (CI scheduling), and cross-compilation (toolchain; krepair reports 11% of its sampled patches need it).",
              "NECESSARY, NOT SUFFICIENT. 'Object built' does not mean 'patched lines compiled'. Patches that touch #ifdef arms are invisible at object granularity. krepair's own motivating example needs at least two configs for one patch (opened). Header-only patches are out of scope (krepair says about 2%; the fetched text suggests the figure may be pre-filtering, so treat it as uncertain). If the paper says 'compile-test coverage' without this qualifier, a PLDI reviewer will object at once.",
              "CIRCULARITY IN THE DOSSIER'S STEP 1. For the four configs with real builds, 'is touched object O built under C' has ground truth in the object inventories themselves. Comparing kfold's table with an inventory validates kfold; it says nothing new about the gap. The gap statistic (share of commits that allmodconfig leaves unbuilt) can be computed from the real allmodconfig inventory without kfold. kfold adds value only where no inventory exists: allyesconfig (a multi-hour build, not in the assets), 32-bit allmodconfig, and any submitter's own .config. The experiment must therefore keep three things apart: the gap measured on ground truth, kfold's prediction accuracy, and the repair step.",
              "SCOPE CONFOUND. kfold analyzes an x86 tree. Many commits in a stable update touch arm/arm64/riscv or SoC-only code whose objects cannot be reached from x86 even with COMPILE_TEST. Counting them as 'unbuilt by allmodconfig' would inflate the gap. Touched objects need three classes: built by x86-64 allmodconfig; buildable on x86 by some other config (the claim); not buildable on x86 at all (out of scope, report separately). Commits where config-for failed (402 touch .c/.S, 384 got a config) stay in the denominator.",
              "BASELINE STRENGTH. The reviewer objection 'allmodconfig plus COMPILE_TEST is good enough' is only the weakest form. The strong baseline is 'make allyesconfig; make path/file.o', which krepair says covers 88.5% of patch lines. Objects that allyesconfig misses are those needing an option =n or a choice alternative (the Octeon case) or an exclusive pair of options. That class is where kfold must show value. Otherwise the honest result is that allyesconfig plus a single-file make already works and kfold only saves time and explains.",
              "DEMAND EVIDENCE IS THIN. The documented burden is strong (submit checklist, netdev rules, which I re-opened). Direct requests for a per-object tool, surveys, and rates of never-built merged patches are absent. Mailing-list anecdotes (Octeon, Airoha, Kitt, Golaszewski, Hovold) are selected examples and must not become rates. The experiment measures the gap of canned configs. It cannot measure how often authors skipped compile testing, because that ground truth does not exist in the assets.",
              "UNVERIFIED FIGURES IN THE DOSSIER. The 'under 1.53% changed options for 99% of patches' krepair figure and the 'four hours vs 20 minutes' build times were not in the part of the paper I read (I read the first 100,000 of about 109,000 characters). Cite them only after opening them directly.",
              "NIPA vs POLICY. The netdev doc (opened) requires allyesconfig and allmodconfig with W=1 before posting, while the dossier says NIPA documents only allmodconfig checks. That mismatch (policy asks for two configs, CI documents one) is a fair illustration, but I did not re-open the NIPA README, so mark it as carried over."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Dossier step 2 proposes reusing the 400 why verdicts as ground truth. Those cover other objects. Compute precision and recall directly on the set of touched objects of the 402 commits, against the four inventories (i386 tinyconfig, x86-64 defconfig, Debian, x86-64 allmodconfig). That gives far more relevant ground truth.",
              "Dossier step 1(b) needs an i386 allmodconfig inventory, which is not among the four builds. Report it as a kfold prediction only (labelled unvalidated) and do not build it (multi-hour, and CLAUDE.md forbids rerunning heavy builds here without asking the user).",
              "krepair numbers (21.7% defconfig, 29.2% one randconfig, 88.5% allyesconfig, 98.5% krepair) are confirmed as stated, but they are mean patch-line coverage over 507 patches sampled from about 71,000 (September 2021 to September 2022), with removed lines judged by their enclosing #ifdef block. They are not object-level and cannot be compared with kfold's numbers. Cite them only as motivation, never as a comparison target.",
              "Step 3 compares edit sizes with krepair across different samples and granularity. Keep the comparison descriptive, or drop it. A real comparison needs the klocalizer sample (step 4) on the same commits.",
              "Add a cheap line-level bound that the dossier lacks. For commits whose touched objects are all built by allmodconfig, estimate how many touched hunks lie inside preprocessor conditionals. Use DWARF line tables if the allmodconfig build kept debug info, or koverage on the 30-commit sample. This turns the #ifdef limitation into a number."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "rq3_task",
            "summary": "DECISION. Use D1 as one developer task in RQ3, called 'pre-posting object coverage and repair'. Back it with one or two sentences of introduction motivation (the submit checklist, the netdev rule, and krepair's finding that canned configs give no assurance that patched code is compiled). Add a limitation paragraph on #ifdef arms, headers, non-x86 code and compile-only evidence. Related work covers klocalizer, krepair, koverage, Vampyr and undertaker. Do not make it a headline claim, and do not drop it. Reasons tied to PLDI reviewers. (1) 'Demand' is anecdotal; an RQ3 task that measures a gap on real data avoids having to argue demand. (2) 'Novelty over klocalizer' is best handled by a head-to-head on the same commits with equal terms. (3) 'Object vs line level' is the main soundness objection and must be stated up front. (4) A PLDI audience wants the exactness and speed of the analysis shown against builds, not a survey of kernel culture.\\n\\nEXPERIMENT (single strongest). Title: object-level coverage gap of canned configs, and its repair, on v7.2.7..v7.2.8.\\nInputs: the 402 .c/.S-touching commits; the kfold build-condition table for Linux 7.2.8; the four real object inventories; the existing config-for outputs (384 configs, olddefconfig and .o verification results). Optional: 30 sampled commits for klocalizer.\\nStep A, gap on ground truth (no kfold needed). For every touched object classify it as: in x86-64 allmodconfig inventory; in defconfig, Debian or tinyconfig inventories only; or in none. Then use the kfold condition table with the Kconfig model K, plus a ground-truth-free check, to split 'none' into 'buildable on x86 by some config' and 'not buildable on x86'. Metrics: share of commits with at least one touched object unbuilt by allmodconfig; share with no touched object built; the same for the union of the four inventories; split by directory (drivers, arch, net, fs, other). Baseline: allmodconfig alone, then the union. Headline form: 'In x% of 402 commits (y of N in-scope), at least one touched object is not built by x86-64 allmodconfig; z% have none built; after removing non-x86 objects the numbers are x'/z'.'\\nStep B, kfold prediction accuracy on the same objects. For the four configs with inventories, report precision, recall and F1 of kfold's built/not-built predictions per touched object, plus the list of every disagreement categorized by cause (Kconfig model over-approximation, Makefile construct unsupported, generated or conditional-rule objects). Report 'all disagreements explained' as the claim, not just a percentage. Extension where kfold, not an inventory, is the only source: predicted allyesconfig (generate with make allyesconfig, which runs Kconfig only and builds nothing) and predicted 32-bit allmodconfig. Label these 'predicted, unvalidated', and report which touched objects they add over allmodconfig.\\nStep C, repair. For commits missed by allmodconfig and by allyesconfig (predicted), report the config-for success rate (olddefconfig keeps all required options, .o compiles), the number of options changed from the base config (base = defconfig and = allmodconfig), and for failures the cause. Include all 402 in the denominator. The baseline is 'allyesconfig plus make file.o' (success iff the object is in the predicted allyesconfig set) with its edit size from defconfig, which is typically hundreds of options.\\nStep D, why-explanations. For each unbuilt object, record the chain length and the kind of the failing conjunct (parent-directory option, own option, Kconfig dependency, architecture). This quantifies the 'which config' explanation the reviewer would see, and can be shown with two or three case studies reconstructed from the Airoha and Octeon cases (as illustrations, not evidence).\\nStep E (optional, 3 to 4 extra person-days). Run klocalizer or koverage on 30 randomly chosen commits, one at a time, on the same inputs. Compare: per-commit object-level verdict, config size, runtime, failures. This is the only head-to-head, and also the only line-level data point (koverage reports patch-line coverage), which gives a bound on the #ifdef gap.\\nEffort: about 6 to 8 person-days for A to D (scripting over existing artifacts, no new builds), plus 3 to 4 for E. On this machine every step runs sequentially in a single process. A to D need only table lookups and the already-computed config-for logs; make allyesconfig is a one-time Kconfig run; the klocalizer step must run strictly one commit at a time, and its Java and SuperC setup is the risk. Nothing here needs the multi-hour builds.\\nFalsification (state thresholds before running). The claim fails if any of: (i) after dropping non-x86 objects, allmodconfig or the union of the four builds leaves unbuilt objects in fewer than about 3% of commits (the gap is negligible on this sample); (ii) predicted allyesconfig plus single-file make covers essentially all of the objects that config-for covers and edit size is irrelevant (kfold then adds only speed and explanation, which must be stated as the contribution); (iii) kfold's per-object accuracy on the four inventories is below about 95%, or disagreements are unexplained; (iv) on the 30-commit sample klocalizer matches kfold's verdicts and succeeds as often, so there is no object-level advantage beyond runtime. Each of these outcomes must be reported, not hidden.\\nGround truth check. Available: four inventories (built or not for 4 configs), .o compile results from config-for, olddefconfig outputs. Not available: what authors or CI actually ran, whether any commit was ever compile-tested, i386 or arm allmodconfig inventories, a allyesconfig inventory. So the experiment claims only 'canned configs leave this much unbuilt, and this much is repairable', never 'x% of kernel patches are untested'.\\nThreats. Construct: 'built' is not 'tested' or 'patched lines compiled'. Internal: K is an over-approximation, and the verification in config-for uses the same tooling that generated the repair; the sample is selected after the fact, and config-for success counted per commit, not per object. External: stable backports are mostly fixes to existing code and under-represent new drivers; a single release; x86 only; 11% cross-compilation and about 2% header-only patches fall outside. Comparison: line-level krepair statistics are not comparable with object-level numbers. Multiple testing and choice of cutoffs should be pre-registered. Researcher degrees of freedom in 'in scope' classification must be fixed before looking at results.\\nExpected size of the effect, with uncertainty: the dossier gives no object-level estimate. krepair's line-level figures (defconfig 21.7%, allyesconfig 88.5%) suggest allmodconfig misses a non-trivial minority but a number for it is unknown to me; do not state a prediction in the paper.\\nRisk to the paper: if Step A shows a small gap, report the result, shrink the claim to explanation and speed, and move the task to a single paragraph.\"",
