      },
      {
        "id": "D2",
        "name": "Find a .config that builds a given file or patch with minimal change to a base config",
        "dive": {
          "id": "D2",
          "name": "Find a .config that builds a given file or patch, with minimal change to a base config",
          "refined_description": "Problem statement. Given a source tree, a base configuration (defconfig, distro config, or a project's current .config) and a target (one .c/.S file, one object, or the set of files a patch touches), produce a configuration that makes the build system compile every target object, differs from the base in as few options as possible, and survives Kconfig expansion (make olddefconfig) with the requested options intact. When no single configuration can build the target (mutually exclusive choices, several architectures), say so and return a small set or a reason. The practical stakes: a change that no tester's .config compiles is not tested at all (Gazzillo slide 4), and tester configs are named presets (allyesconfig, allmodconfig, defconfig, randconfig) that provably skip objects (Kconfig choices, arch-specific options, COMPILE_TEST opt-in). Scope of what kfold can honestly claim: object/file granularity under Kbuild Makefile conditions plus a Kconfig over-approximation. It does not see #ifdef arms inside a file, so 'this config builds the object' is not 'this config compiles the changed lines' (krepair/klocalizer measure lines). Related but distinct from the 'why OBJ' and 'blindspots' problems; the same per-object condition table serves all three.",
          "who_has_it": "(1) Patch authors and reviewers in drivers/arch/SoC subsystems, who are told to build with allyesconfig/allmodconfig plus several architectures and cannot do so for every touched file (netdev entry profile, submit-checklist, omap-gpmc and Octeon threads). (2) Stable/backport and CI operators who pick configs by hand-written lists (KernelCI, LKFT, syzbot hand-crafted configs). (3) KUnit and selftest authors who hand-write .kunitconfig dependency lists. (4) Maintainers of non-Linux Kbuild-style trees (U-Boot buildman TODO, coreboot abuild --skip_unset, barebox allyesconfig CI). (5) Embedded integrators and distro users trimming configs (Hubaux 2012 survey; mostly non-kernel-developers, so weak for the patch-author case). Demand evidence is mostly indirect: tooling workarounds and policy text, not developers asking for this tool.",
          "evidence": [
            {
              "claim": "Tartler et al. (2011) state the patch-author version of the problem: a developer who modified files wants every edited line to compile, but deducing the covering configurations is hard by hand. Same paper: some files (kernel/sched_fair.c) needed seven configurations.",
              "quote": "a sometimes difficult, but always tedious and error-prone task",
              "url": "https://sigops.org/s/conferences/sosp/2011/workshops/plos/02-tartler.pdf",
              "date": "2011-10-23",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "Tartler/Lohmann FAU (undertaker line)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Same paper, on the Kconfig realisation step that config-for also depends on: Kconfig can silently drop requested options instead of reporting conflicts. Also measured: allyesconfig reaches only 78.49% of selectable #ifdef blocks on Linux 2.6.35 x86, and more than 90% of files reach full coverage with at most two configurations (block-level, 2011).",
              "quote": "Kconfig in some cases silently overwrites user choices instead of signaling incompatible item selections",
              "url": "https://sigops.org/s/conferences/sosp/2011/workshops/plos/02-tartler.pdf",
              "date": "2011-10-23",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
              "source_group": "Tartler/Lohmann FAU (undertaker line)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Hubaux, Xiong, Czarnecki VaMoS 2012 survey (97 Linux users filled it, 81 usable; posted to 11 distro forums/lists and LKML, so not mostly kernel developers). 20% need at least a few dozen minutes to activate an inactive option; 46% rely on reading documentation, 26% on own expertise. One respondent describes a manual lspci -> hardware-list -> search-the-kernel workflow to find needed options. The ConfigFix paper (pages 1-2 read) does not cite this figure there; do not attribute it to ConfigFix.",
              "quote": "they need at least a “few dozen minutes”",
              "url": "https://gsd.uwaterloo.ca/sites/default/files/vamos12-survey.pdf",
              "date": "2012-01",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
              "source_group": "Hubaux/Czarnecki Waterloo survey",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "OSS NA 2022 krepair talk (slides 4-9): the .config decides what is built, a .config that does not build the patch cannot test it, and testers pick configs by hand-crafted sets (syzbot) or built-in families (0-day: defconfigs, allno/allyes/allmod, hundreds of randconfig). On v5.13 x86, of 13,411 commits, defconfig fully covers 1,688 and one randconfig 2,189 (1,296 non-x86 and 1,177 header-only commits are separate bars). Slide 8: allyesconfig 'still doesn't include all due to mutually-exclusive options, multiple architectures'.",
              "quote": "no guarantee that committed changes get built",
              "url": "https://paulgazzillo.com/files/oss2022.pdf",
              "date": "2022-06-22",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
              "source_group": "Gazzillo/UCF kmax-krepair group (paper, slides, docs)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "krepair evaluation (FSE 2024): random sample of 507 of about 71,000 patches (one year); mean line coverage 98.5% for krepair, 21.7% defconfig, 29.2% one randconfig, 88.5% allyesconfig; repaired configs change less than 1.53% of defconfig options in 99% of patches (Table 2 defconfig: median 0.27%, max 9.52%; allnoconfig base: median 1.48%, 99th pct 2.23%); 97% of patches need one config, 99% need five or fewer, worst case 23; build 10.5x faster than allyesconfig; most runs take minutes, outliers up to an hour; 11% of patches need cross-compilation. These are line-level numbers including #ifdef arms. This is the baseline kfold must be compared with.",
              "quote": "krepair achieves nearly complete patch coverage, 98.5% on average.",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.17966v1",
              "date": "2024-04",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
              "source_group": "Gazzillo/UCF kmax-krepair group (paper, slides, docs)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "krepair's own limits (as extracted from the paper HTML by WebFetch): header-only patches unsupported (2% of sample); constraints come only from the build system and Kconfig, header-inclusion constraints unavailable; seven patches could not be built because of cross-compiler problems; two patches from an unsupported architecture; evaluated on the Linux kernel only; no quantified accuracy figure for the Kbuild constraints; kclause takes 2-3 min, kmax 10-15 min, SuperC about a minute. The intro says ten randconfigs plateau at around 75%, Section 5.6 says 74.4% (footnote the inconsistency).",
              "quote": "patches that only modify header files are not supported, which we only encountered in 2% of patches.",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.17966v1",
              "date": "2024-04",
              "evidence_type": "tool",
              "source_group": "Gazzillo/UCF kmax-krepair group (paper, slides, docs)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "kmax advanced.md concedes: formulas may not be exact and were 'wrong in some cases', options are Boolean by default, composites are not built directly, CONFIG_BROKEN units are skipped by default, and klocalizer tries architectures one at a time and stops at the first satisfiable one. These are the places where an object-exact Kbuild semantic could differ.",
              "quote": "Passing multiple architectures is not supported.",
              "url": "https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax/blob/master/docs/advanced.md",
              "date": "n.d. (page read 2026-10-17)",
              "evidence_type": "tool",
              "source_group": "Gazzillo/UCF kmax-krepair group (paper, slides, docs)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "External user issue #308 (opened 2026-08-16, still open): klocalizer reports that x86_64 can build the powerpc-only drivers/block/ps3disk.o. The maintainer agrees it is wrong and says klocalizer checks one architecture at a time with arch-specific Kconfig files. The reporter says they use Kmax to find minimized configurations for patches, which is a 2026 independent use of exactly this problem. Source is the incumbent's own tracker.",
              "quote": "You are indeed right that `ps3disk.o` should not be buildable in x86.",
              "url": "https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax/issues/308",
              "date": "2026-08-18",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "kmax GitHub issue tracker (external users)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "External user issue #290 (opened 2025-05-11, no reply on the page): klocalizer 4.8 on a Linux 5.10.10-5.10.40 diff gave repeated 'SuperC configuration generation failed' errors and 'No formula from kmax was found' warnings, and reported 2004/2392 files and 37576/46886 lines covered. Issue #291 (2025-05-13): off-by-one failure 'Requested line (66) is invalid' on a v6.9-v6.10 mm patch; maintainer self-assigned and reproduced it. Shows setup/robustness friction for patch-scale use.",
              "quote": "klocalizer cannot guarantee claimed coverage due to under approximations",
              "url": "https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax/issues/290",
              "date": "2025-05-11",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "kmax GitHub issue tracker (external users)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Tristate handling: issue #253 (2024-05-29, opened by the maintainer) says klocalizer always writes y; the repo's 2026-01-15 commits disable tristate modelling in kclause when running klocalizer. The commit list also shows maintenance activity in 2026 (kextract updated to the v7.1 Kconfig parser on 2026-08-18; kmax 4.10 on 2026-08-18; 77 open issues, 72 stars, 29 forks). Check whether kfold preserves =m from the base config before claiming this as a difference.",
              "quote": "Right now, klocalizer always sets to y by default.",
              "url": "https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax/issues/253",
              "date": "2024-05-29",
              "evidence_type": "tool",
              "source_group": "kmax GitHub issue tracker (external users)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "The networking entry profile makes named configs the unit of pre-posting build testing and tells authors not to use patchwork as a test service.",
              "quote": "your changes must survive an `allyesconfig` and an `allmodconfig` build with `W=1` set",
              "url": "https://docs.kernel.org/process/maintainer-netdev.html",
              "date": "n.d. (read 2026-10-17)",
              "evidence_type": "policy",
              "source_group": "Kernel process docs (docs.kernel.org)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "The submit checklist asks for builds with the touched CONFIG options =y, =m and =n, allnoconfig and allmodconfig, and several architectures, without saying how to find the configs. A reviewer cannot tell from a patch which of these it exercised.",
              "quote": "Builds on multiple CPU architectures by using local cross-compile tools or some other build farm.",
              "url": "https://docs.kernel.org/process/submit-checklist.html",
              "date": "n.d. (read 2026-10-17)",
              "evidence_type": "policy",
              "source_group": "Kernel process docs (docs.kernel.org)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Octeon ATA thread (June 2023): Rob Herring's COMPILE_TEST patch broke mips:allmodconfig in linux-next. Guenter Roeck explained that a Kconfig choice selects a different alternative under allmodconfig; the libata maintainer (Damien Le Moal) dropped the patch at rc7 because the fix was not worth the effort. A named config misses an object because of a Kconfig choice, which is the kind of failing conjunct a condition chain names.",
              "quote": "CONFIG_CAVIUM_OCTEON_SOC is a choice option,",
              "url": "https://lkml.rescloud.iu.edu/2306.2/07959.html",
              "date": "2023-06-21",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "LKML ata/octeon thread 2023",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "omap-gpmc thread (April 2022): the author declines to add COMPILE_TEST because he cannot test every platform; the reviewer (Krzysztof Kozlowski) says to cross-build 8-12 popular architectures on a regular Ubuntu machine or push to a tree for the kbuild robot. Manual multi-arch burden placed on the submitter.",
              "quote": "I won't be able to test on all platforms.",
              "url": "https://lkml.iu.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/2204.1/07260.html",
              "date": "2022-04-13",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "LKML memory-controller omap-gpmc thread 2022",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "GPIO maintainer Bartosz Golaszewski (April 2025 cover letter) says some drivers are not built by allmodconfig even though they need no arch headers, so he adds COMPILE_TEST to extend coverage. Related 2025 pattern: Johan Hovold's dmaengine bcm2835 patch 'enable that for wider build coverage' (https://lkml.iu.edu/2511.2/04659.html, page read 2025-11-22). Coverage is extended driver by driver, by hand, after someone notices.",
              "quote": "There are some drivers that aren't build with allmodconfig that don't have any requirements on arch/ headers",
              "url": "https://lkml.rescloud.iu.edu/2504.1/01019.html",
              "date": "2025-04-08",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "LKML COMPILE_TEST coverage patches 2025 (Golaszewski, Hovold)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Torvalds (Feb 2025) describes his own pre-pull testing: an allmodconfig build on x86-64 plus a local-config build, and calls it limited in configuration. At the 2025 Maintainers Summit (LWN, Dec 2025; only fragments reopened) Torvalds worried that testing only canned configs leaves 'the weird bugs' and Ojeda did not want people testing with 'canned configurations'. Supports blindspots/coverage motivation; does not show demand for a config-finding tool.",
              "quote": "my build testing is very limited in the configuration sense",
              "url": "https://lkml.iu.edu/2502.3/06618.html",
              "date": "2025-02-26",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "LKML Torvalds Rust-policy thread (plus LWN Maintainers Summit 2025)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Non-Linux demand: U-Boot's buildman docs list as unfinished TODO the file-to-board mapping that config-for computes for Kbuild trees. Buildman selects boards by name/attribute regexes (--boards, -x, -n -v); no option maps a changed source file to boards.",
              "quote": "checking commits for changed files and building only boards which use those files",
              "url": "https://docs.u-boot-project.org/en/latest/build/buildman.html",
              "date": "n.d. (read 2026-10-17)",
              "evidence_type": "tool",
              "source_group": "U-Boot project docs",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "coreboot (July 2023): a developer wanting to run abuild only on boards that build certain code used --skip_unset NORTHBRIDGE_INTEL_SANDYBRIDGE and every board, including two known Sandy Bridge boards, was skipped; replies were not retrieved. A request to select the boards that build certain code, handled badly by existing tooling.",
              "quote": "Is what I did supposed to work?",
              "url": "https://www.mail-archive.com/coreboot@coreboot.org/msg58122.html",
              "date": "2023-07-25",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "coreboot mailing list (Hui 2023)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Counter-evidence: the KernelCI 2020 community survey (just under 100 responses, about 80% had contributed upstream) names per-patch testing as the top need and does not mention build configuration. Also reports that a clear majority of maintainers and developers often find bugs later than 24h. Patch-testing automation, not config tooling, is what respondents asked for (survey is 6 years old).",
              "quote": "the need for testing individual patches sent on mailing lists",
              "url": "https://kernelci.org/?p=109",
              "date": "2020-07-09",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
              "source_group": "KernelCI survey 2020",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Why a single-object build is not an answer: kbuild maintainer Masahiro Yamada (2020) replying to a developer who routinely runs make foo/bar.o. The manual answer is to read the Makefile; no config-finding tool is mentioned.",
              "quote": "The necessary CONFIG dependency must be met.",
              "url": "https://lkml.rescloud.iu.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/2003.3/08451.html",
              "date": "2020-03-31",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "LKML single-target-build thread 2020",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            }
          ],
          "existing_solutions": [
            {
              "tool_or_process": "klocalizer --repair / --include (kmax suite: kclause, kmax/kextract, SuperC), with koverage for checking",
              "what_it_does": "Takes a compilation unit or a patch and a base .config, adds Kbuild+Kconfig+#ifdef constraints, solves with a SAT/SMT back end, emits NUM-ARCH.config files. Paper: 98.5% mean line coverage on 507 patches, under 1.53% of defconfig options changed in 99% of patches. kmax 4.10 released 2026-08-18; 55 PyPI releases; GitHub 72 stars, 29 forks, 77 open issues; active in 2026.",
              "limits": "Setup needs SuperC (Java), make.cross, env vars; one architecture per invocation and no multi-arch; own docs say formulas may be inexact and were wrong in some cases; Boolean (y only, tristate disabled in Jan 2026 commits); header-only patches unsupported (2%); open issue #308 where an arch-specific unit is wrongly satisfiable on x86_64; open issue #303 (GCC-dependent constraints drop clang options); constraint collection 2-3 min (kclause) plus 10-15 min (kmax) per kernel version; no condition chain explaining why a unit is unbuildable; evaluated on Linux only; no published accuracy measurement of Kbuild constraints. Not shown: any use by kernel developers on LKML (absence of evidence from my reads, no search was possible).",
              "url": "https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "undertaker / Vampyr",
              "what_it_does": "Generates partial Kconfig configurations that cover every #ifdef block of a file (2011 paper: 94.4% vs 78.49% for allyesconfig on 2.6.35 x86).",
              "limits": "Block level; the krepair paper calls Vampyr unmaintained (earlier dossier says supported only to about Linux 3.2; I did not re-verify that figure).",
              "url": "https://sigops.org/s/conferences/sosp/2011/workshops/plos/02-tartler.pdf"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Named presets: allyesconfig, allmodconfig, randconfig, defconfig plus COMPILE_TEST opt-in; KCONFIG_ALLCONFIG, merge_config.sh",
              "what_it_does": "The working practice of maintainers and bots (netdev profile, submit-checklist, 0-day, linux-next). COMPILE_TEST is added driver by driver by hand.",
              "limits": "No guarantee a given object is built (Octeon choice, GPIO/dmaengine drivers outside allmodconfig); allyesconfig builds take about 4 h in the krepair paper (3 h on the talk slides) and cannot select mutually exclusive options; defconfig covers 21.7% of patch lines and one randconfig 29.2% (krepair paper).",
              "url": "https://docs.kernel.org/process/maintainer-netdev.html"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Intel 0-day kernel test robot, KernelCI, LKFT/TuxSuite, syzbot configs",
              "what_it_does": "Sampled or hand-listed config matrices run per branch head. Example report 2025-04-18: 19 configs tested, 140 skipped, only i386 and x86_64, 'More configs may be tested in the coming days'. KernelCI: over 220 build configurations (2024 blog, not re-opened).",
              "limits": "Post-hoc, sampled; reports do not say which objects the sample built or why a given object was not built.",
              "url": "https://lkml.rescloud.iu.edu/2504.2/04328.html"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "KUnit .kunitconfig + kunit.py",
              "what_it_does": "Author hand-lists the Kconfig options and their dependencies; the tool warns if dependencies were left out.",
              "limits": "No config is computed from the test objects; merging fragments across directories is described as iffy because tests may depend on incompatible options.",
              "url": "https://docs.kernel.org/dev-tools/kunit/start.html"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "ConfigFix (xconfig extension, SAT-based conflict resolution)",
              "what_it_does": "Interactive: given wanted option values, computes fixes to the current configuration.",
              "limits": "Option-level input, not file/object/patch input; paper says users still struggle with kernel configuration conflicts. I did not verify its integration status or the Torvalds SAT quote in the PDF pages read.",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2012.15342"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Other-tree tooling: U-Boot buildman and qconfig, coreboot abuild --skip_unset, barebox sandbox allyesconfig in CI (March 2025)",
              "what_it_does": "Board/defconfig-level selection and whole-config CI to catch breakage in code normal defconfigs do not build.",
              "limits": "No file-to-config mapping (buildman TODO says so); coreboot request failed in practice; allyesconfig is extended file by file (barebox).",
              "url": "https://docs.u-boot-project.org/en/latest/build/buildman.html"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "make localmodconfig, kernelconfig.io, menuconfig search, dt_to_config",
              "what_it_does": "Symbol-level or hardware-list-based config narrowing and lookup.",
              "limits": "Need the option name first, or loaded modules at the time; documented as missing needed options (kernel docs: 'hard to think of everything'). Not re-opened in this pass.",
              "url": "https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/quickly-build-trimmed-linux.html"
            }
          ],
          "remaining_gap": "Facts supported by what I opened: (1) The incumbent solves the patch case at line level but through a heavy pipeline (SuperC/Java, make.cross, minutes of constraint collection per kernel version), one architecture at a time, Boolean-only, with self-admitted inexact formulas and open correctness issues (#308). (2) No source I read gives a per-object explanation of why a unit is not buildable under a given config; klocalizer reports unsatisfiability or silently returns a config (the #308 case returned a config that omitted the option). (3) No published head-to-head comparison of Kbuild condition exactness against ground-truth builds. (4) Non-Linux Kbuild-style trees have no file-to-config tool (buildman TODO). Not supported either way: whether kernel developers want such a tool. I found no developer survey on this, no LKML request for it, and the only independent user visible is the issue-tracker reporter in #308 (a researcher-style use). The remaining gap is therefore partly technical (speed, exactness, explanations, other trees) and partly an unproven demand claim.",
          "kfold_contribution": "Modest and specific, to be stated as measured facts only: (a) a config-for that reuses a whole-tree per-object condition table computed in about a minute for Linux 7.2.8 (2,225 Makefile instances, 32.9k objects) instead of 12-18 minutes of constraint collection per revision (kclause 2-3 + kmax 10-15 per the krepair paper; not measured on the same machine), with no SuperC/Java dependency. (b) The solution carries an explanation: the failing conjuncts of an object's condition under the base config (same engine as 'why'), which klocalizer does not output. (c) Object-level verification on real data: 384 of 402 v7.2.7..v7.2.8 .c/.S commits get a configuration, verified with olddefconfig and by compiling the objects. (d) The same engine runs on BusyBox, Barebox, U-Boot and coreboot, where the buildman TODO shows the need. Do not claim: line-level coverage, #ifdef handling, a better result than krepair, or novelty of patch-to-config (prior art: undertaker 2011, klocalizer/krepair 2022-2024).",
          "quantitative_facts": [
            "krepair (FSE 2024, arXiv 2404.17966): 507 patches sampled from about 71,000 (one year); mean line coverage krepair 98.5%, allyesconfig 88.5%, defconfig 21.7%, one randconfig 29.2%; 97% of patches need one config, 99% need five or fewer, worst case 23; options changed vs defconfig: median 0.27%, 99th pct 1.53%, max 9.52%; vs allnoconfig: median 1.48%, 99th pct 2.23%; 11% of patches need cross-compilation; 2% header-only; 7 patches unbuildable for toolchain reasons. [paper HTML read]",
            "krepair paper timing: kclause 2-3 min, kmax 10-15 min, SuperC about a minute or less; most krepair runs a few minutes, outliers up to an hour; caching cuts max runtime by 71.4% to about 19 min. allyesconfig build about 4 h vs about 20 min for defconfig (paper; the OSS 2022 slide says 3 h, cite the paper).",
            "OSS NA 2022 slides: v5.13 x86, 13,411 commits: defconfig fully covers 1,688 and one randconfig 2,189; 8,853 and 8,333 not covered; 1,296 non-x86 and 1,177 header-only. Slide says about 15k configuration options; LWN (2025) says 32,468 on x86_64 for Linux 6.16, so footnote the difference rather than mixing them.",
            "Tartler 2011 (Linux 2.6.35 x86): allyesconfig covers 78.49% of selectable #ifdef blocks, undertaker greedy 94.40%; more than 90% of files reach full coverage with at most two configs; some files need up to 7 (greedy) or 16 (naive).",
            "Hubaux 2012: 97 Linux respondents (81 usable), 20% need at least a few dozen minutes to activate an inactive option, 46% follow documentation, 26% rely on own expertise, about 90% configure alone, half make 20-50 changes to a default config.",
            "kmax tool facts read on 2026-10-17: PyPI kmax 4.10 released 2026-08-18, 55 releases (21 final, 34 rc), earliest listed 2.3rc1 on 2020-06-28; GitHub 72 stars, 29 forks, 77 open issues; PyPI classifier 'Beta'. Issue #290 run reported 2004/2392 files and 37576/46886 lines covered with 'under approximations'.",
            "0-day report 2025-04-18 (tip:perf/urgent): 19 configs tested, 140 skipped; 19 = i386 allmodconfig/allnoconfig/allyesconfig/defconfig + 6 buildonly-randconfig, x86_64 allnoconfig/allyesconfig/defconfig + 6 buildonly-randconfig (no x86_64 allmodconfig in the tested list).",
            "KernelCI 2020 survey: just under 100 responses, one third maintainers, one third developers, about 80% had contributed upstream; top need per-patch testing; build configuration not mentioned.",
            "Frequency impression only (not curated, word-match, multi-year, from earlier gap scout, not re-opened): mail-archive.com search for 'compile tested only' on linux-kernel reported 2,372 matches. Label as impression if used.",
            "Local assets for the experiment: 402 of 437 stable commits touch .c/.S, 384 get a configuration (18 do not); four fresh builds (i386 tinyconfig, x86-64 defconfig, Debian, x86-64 allmodconfig) with object inventories; a saved Kmax run on Linux and BusyBox."
          ],
          "proposed_experiment": "Single best experiment (primary part uses only existing assets, one process at a time per CLAUDE.md). Unit: the 402 .c/.S-touching commits of v7.2.7..v7.2.8. (1) Object-level coverage baseline, no new builds: for each patch, is every touched object built by x86-64 defconfig, by the Debian config, by x86-64 allmodconfig, and by i386 tinyconfig, using the four existing object inventories; report the fraction fully covered (the object-level analogue of krepair's 21.7% / 88.5%). (2) kfold config-for result vs those baselines: the fraction of the 402 for which a config is found (384 today), options changed versus the base (count and percent of set options; compare with krepair Table 2: median 0.27%, 99th pct 1.53% on defconfig, median 1.48% on allnoconfig; use x86-64 defconfig and i386 tinyconfig as the analogues and state that bases differ), whether requested options survive olddefconfig, and whether make compiles the objects (already measured). (3) Classify the 18 patches with no config by cause (non-x86 arch, header-only, unsatisfiable under K, Kconfig model over-approximation failure, parse gap); krepair excludes non-x86 and header-only separately, so report those categories the same way. (4) Honesty check for the line-vs-object gap: count the share of the 402 whose hunks lie inside a #if/#ifdef CONFIG_ region (a one-pass text scan of the patches), and report object coverage and line-level risk separately. (5) Accuracy of the Kbuild half without installing SuperC: compare kfold's per-object conditions with the saved Kmax formulas on the same objects and adjudicate disagreements against the four builds. Stretch (needs the user's approval: new infrastructure SuperC/Java, make.cross, pipx kmax; one process at a time, smoke test on a single patch first, expected a few minutes per patch, hours for 30 patches): run klocalizer --repair on a sample of about 30 patches, one architecture, same base config, and compare config size, runtime, and whether the touched objects compile. Write results to files so nothing is rerun.",
          "risks": "1) Method limit of this pass: every WebSearch call returned 'budget used up', so no new searches were run. I replaced them with about 45 direct fetches of known primary pages (kmax README, PyPI, advanced.md, issue pages, krepair HTML, OSS slides, Tartler/Hubaux/ConfigFix PDFs, LKML/mail-archive threads, kernel docs, U-Boot docs). There is no new independent source for 2023-2026 demand beyond a handful of LKML anecdotes, and I could not check klocalizer adoption on LKML, other competitors (KBootRepair was not found on the author's repo page; Nguyen & Nguyen content unread; PatchLens not re-opened), or any 2023-2026 paper beyond those already in the dossier. This caps confidence at medium. 2) Novelty: the problem is solved by prior art (undertaker 2011, klocalizer/krepair 2022-2024, maintained through Aug 2026); the paper must not claim patch-to-config as new. 3) Granularity mismatch: krepair measures line coverage including #ifdef arms; kfold measures object builds. A config that builds the object may not compile the changed lines. Compare only at object level, or say so. 4) Demand evidence is indirect: policy text, workarounds and COMPILE_TEST patches; the only user survey (KernelCI 2020) ranks per-patch testing first; the only configuration survey (Hubaux 2012) is old and mostly non-developers; some maintainers do not accept compile-only evidence (earlier scout: rtl8723bs 'Compile testing isn't sufficient', not re-opened). 5) Maintainers favour allmodconfig/linux-next/0-day (Torvalds, Rothwell, Bottomley in earlier claims); value must be framed as predicting/explaining, not replacing bots. 6) kfold's Kconfig model is an over-approximation: a returned .config can fail olddefconfig or not build the object; the 18 failures need an honest breakdown. 7) Cross-arch: 11% of krepair's patches need cross-compilation and klocalizer is one architecture per run; state what kfold does for non-x86 before comparing. 8) Tristate: klocalizer always writes y; check kfold's behaviour on =m before listing it as a difference. 9) Kbuild constraint accuracy of klocalizer is only self-described as inexact; do not assert a specific error rate without running the comparison. 10) Z3 distrust: an earlier claim attributes a Torvalds anti-SAT quote to ConfigFix; I could not find it on pages 1-2 of the PDF, so treat it as unverified. 11) Double-blind: the GitHub issue evidence names users; cite by issue number only.",
          "confidence": "medium"
        },
        "verdicts": [
          {
            "lens": "EVIDENCE AUDITOR for D2 (find a .config that builds a given file or patch, minimal change to a base config)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "Single-source dependence: the quantitative core (98.5%, 21.7%, 29.2%, 88.5%, 1.53%, 97%/99%/23, timings, 4 h allyesconfig, 11% cross-compile) comes from ONE paper (arXiv 2404.17966, one version only, v1 of 27 Apr 2024). The dossier labels it FSE 2024; the arXiv page only shows a related DOI 10.1145/3643746 and I could not open dl.acm.org (HTTP 403). The published camera-ready numbers were not checked against v1. The OSS 2022 slides, the kmax docs and issues #253/#290/#308 are the same group or its tracker, so they count as one source group, not independent corroboration.",
              "Coreboot anecdote is NOT supported as phrased. The dossier says --skip_unset 'failed in practice' and was 'handled badly by existing tooling'. I opened the reply (mail-archive msg58132, Martin Roth, 2023-08-03): he says the abuild --skip_unset command 'looks reasonable and works for him', calls the misleading 'skipping' messages a separate issue, and points to util/scripts/testsoc -K NORTHBRIDGE_INTEL_SANDYBRIDGE, which searches mainboard Kconfig files for an identifier and runs abuild on the matches. So coreboot already has a (symbol-level, not file-level) board selector. The same flaw affects gap-scout claim G47. The original post (msg58122) is verbatim, but the dossier's note that 'replies were not retrieved' hid that the replies undercut the claim.",
              "Tristate/Boolean limit is overstated. kmax docs/advanced.md (opened) say Kmax treats options as Boolean by default, but also that klocalizer has a --modules flag that sets tristate options to m instead of y, and -T enables experimental tristate support. 'Boolean (y only)' is therefore wrong as an unqualified limit. Issue #253 (opened 2024-05-29, still open, enhancement) says klocalizer always sets y by default and asks for a way to use m based on the input config. I did not verify the dossier's '2026-01-15 commits disable tristate modelling in kclause' claim (not opened).",
              "rtl8723bs 'Compile testing isn't sufficient' (G13) is misattributed in the dossier's risk list as 'some maintainers do not accept compile-only evidence'. The page (lkml.iu.edu/2603.3/01784.html, Shuah Khan, 2026-03-24) is a mentor replying to a newcomer about a checkpatch cleanup that also changed logic and removed code without saying so in the changelog. It is one reviewer in a mentorship context on a staging driver, not a maintainer policy.",
              "kmax GitHub page (opened) shows 72 stars, 29 forks, 77 on the Issues tab (the page does not say open-only), 8 open PRs. It does NOT show releases, last-commit date, 'kmax 4.10 on 2026-08-18', or 55 PyPI releases: Releases and Packages are empty on that page. Those items depend on PyPI/commit pages I did not re-open, so they are unverified here. The README fetch also did not mention --modules, although advanced.md does.",
              "Issue #308 is accurately quoted and dated (opened 2026-08-16, open). Two caveats: the reporter wants to find configs for drivers/block/ps3disk.c and expected klocalizer to filter for PowerPC, and the maintainer's answer is that klocalizer works one architecture at a time because the Linux build system configures one architecture at a time. Because that is a design limit rather than a bug in Kbuild-formula extraction, citing it as evidence of formula inaccuracy is partly misleading. It is a single user, named in the issue; cite by number only.",
              "Issue #290 is accurate (opened 2025-05-11, open, no replies, only a title edit on 2025-05-12), but it is a Linux 5.10.10-5.10.40 diff run with a missing cached formula and SuperC generation errors. It is evidence of setup friction on an old kernel, not of a general failure. The 'under approximations' warning is klocalizer's standard output text, not a bug report.",
              "Demand evidence is weak and mostly indirect, as the dossier admits. The strongest direct statements are Tartler 2011 ('sometimes difficult, but always tedious and error-prone') and the netdev/submit-checklist policy text. Policy texts say to build allyesconfig/allmodconfig and multiple architectures, but do not ask for a config-finding tool. The only user survey found (KernelCI 2020, opened) never mentions build configuration and ranks per-patch testing first. Hubaux 2012 is about option-activation effort, not patch-to-config.",
              "The five LKML anecdotes I opened are real but each shows only that people use named configs and add COMPILE_TEST by hand. None shows anyone asking for a tool, and the Octeon thread's cause (a Kconfig choice) is handled by a reviewer in one reply. Thread dates inside the Octeon page are internally odd (Le Moal's reply is stamped before Roeck's), so cite the thread rather than precise per-message dates. Le Moal's 'not worth the effort' is Herring's view that Le Moal accepted, and the patch was dropped at rc7.",
              "The line-vs-object granularity gap is real and makes the krepair comparison apples-to-oranges. The paper defines coverage as the ratio of changed lines included in the build, and for removed lines it checks whether the enclosing #ifdef block is included. Any kfold figure must be object-level only.",
              "The dossier says the krepair paper gives two inconsistent randconfig plateau figures (intro 75%, Section 5.6 74.4%). There are three: intro 'around 75% with 10 random configuration files', Section 2.1 'ten ... 74.6%', Section 5.6 'plateaus at 9 random configurations ... around 74.4%'. The plateau point also differs (10 vs 9).",
              "Not opened or not verifiable in this pass: Gazzillo slide 4's '~15k configuration options' is a slide figure (verified), but it conflicts with the 32k figure the dossier cites from LWN for x86_64, which I did not open. kmax issue #303, the Hovold dmaengine thread, LWN Maintainers Summit 2025, ConfigFix, the 'compile tested only' 2,372 count and the PyPI release counts were not reopened. The tool-notes caveat that some claims came through summarizers applies to my own WebFetch results too: all quotes below were produced by a small summarizer except the PDFs, which I extracted with pdftotext."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Coreboot: replace 'failed in practice / handled badly by existing tooling' with 'a developer reported abuild --skip_unset skipped all boards; a coreboot maintainer replied that the command works for him, that the skip messages are misleading, and that util/scripts/testsoc -K <KCONFIG_SYMBOL> already selects boards by Kconfig symbol (symbol-level, not file-level)'. Do not use this as demand evidence, and drop G47 likewise.",
              "Tristate: replace 'Boolean (y only)' with 'Boolean by default; --modules sets tristate options to m instead of y; issue #253 (open) asks for preserving m/y from the input config'. Verify kfold's =m behaviour before listing it as a difference.",
              "Randconfig plateau: three figures (about 75% at 10; 74.6% at ten in Section 2.1; 74.4% at 9 in Section 5.6), not two.",
              "Vampyr: the krepair paper does say 'VAMPYR is an older tool and no longer maintained' and 'only supports up to around Linux 3.2', so the 'did not re-verify' caveat can be dropped. The paper never uses the name 'undertaker' in the portion read.",
              "krepair is 'patch coverage' defined as changed lines included in the build; label all its figures 'patch/line coverage', and note arXiv has only v1 (27 Apr 2024). The 'FSE 2024' venue label is unverified from the arXiv page (it lists DOI 10.1145/3643746 only; dl.acm.org gave 403). Check the ACM version numbers before final citation.",
              "allyesconfig time: paper says 'around four hours on average on a typical development workstation' (Section 2.1); OSS 2022 slide 8 says '3 hours vs. 20 minutes for defconfig'. Dossier already says cite the paper; confirmed.",
              "OSS 2022 slide 7 numbers confirmed from the rendered slide: defconfig 1,688 covered / 8,853 not covered / 1,296 non-x86 / 1,177 header-only; one randconfig 2,189 covered / 416 partially / 8,333 not covered; total 13,411 (10,938 x86 non-header). The 'partially covered' bar (about 400 for each) is not mentioned in the dossier and should be reported.",
              "Hubaux 2012: numbers verified from the PDF (97 Linux users, 81 in the quantitative subset, posted on 11 distribution forums/mailing lists plus LKML, 20% 'few dozen minutes', 26% own expertise, 46% documentation). Note the 20% refers to changing an inactive option, not to finding a config for a patch.",
              "Tartler 2011 verified (PLOS '11, 23 Oct 2011, Cascais). Add that the paper itself says 'with Linux the common approach is to use a standard configuration (x86 with either defconfig or allyesconfig)' via private communication, and that the 78.49% vs 94.40% figures are over 'selectable blocks (excluding dead blocks)' (14,666 blocks; 11,511 hit by allyesconfig). The 'silently overwrites' sentence concerns Kconfig expansion of undertaker's partial configs.",
              "Netdev quote: the full sentence is 'At the very minimum your changes must survive an allyesconfig and an allmodconfig build with W=1 set without new warnings or failures'; dossier's truncation is fine. Submit-checklist item text matches (item 'Passes allnoconfig, allmodconfig' and 'Builds on multiple CPU architectures ...').",
              "Yamada 2020 reply: the thread is Vegard Nossum asking for `make foo/bar.o` to work again; Yamada's answer includes an obj-$(CONFIG_FOO) example and suggests adding obj-y += test.o locally. The dossier's gloss 'the manual answer is to read the Makefile' is an inference, not in the text."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "motivation",
            "summary": "Opened and checked 16 sources: the krepair arXiv HTML, Tartler 2011 (PDF text), Hubaux 2012 (PDF text), OSS 2022 slides (slide 7 rendered), kmax advanced.md, kmax issues #253, #290 and #308, the kmax repo page, the netdev and submit-checklist docs, KernelCI 2020, the buildman docs, the Torvalds Feb 2025 message, the Octeon, omap-gpmc, GPIO and 0-day LKML pages, the rtl8723bs thread, and the coreboot thread with its reply. The core claims hold: the krepair numbers, the Tartler and Hubaux quotes, the OSS slide figures, the policy quotes and the kmax issue facts are verbatim and correctly dated. The problem is real and prior art covers it (undertaker 2011, klocalizer/krepair), and the dossier already says kfold must not claim novelty. What fails: the coreboot 'handled badly' anecdote (the replies show a working command and an existing testsoc tool), the 'Boolean y only' limit (klocalizer has --modules), and the maintainer framing of rtl8723bs. The evidence is thin on independence: the krepair paper, slides, docs and issue tracker are one research group, and the published FSE numbers were not checked against arXiv v1. Demand evidence stays indirect (policy text, COMPILE_TEST patches), with no survey asking for this tool. Use it as motivation, with the numbers cited to krepair and the paper claiming only object-level results, and use it as the RQ3 patch-to-config task with a fair object-level comparison against defconfig and the other base configs.\""
          },
          {
            "lens": "Hostile competitor analyst, D2: find a .config that builds a given file or patch with minimal change to a base config. Web search budget was exhausted after the first query, so all evidence below comes from direct fetches of primary pages; no new competitor discovery by search was possible.",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "ALREADY SOLVED at patch level: klocalizer --repair/--include (kmax 4.10, 2026-08-18) takes a base .config plus a compilation unit or patch and emits a repaired config; krepair (FSE 2024, arXiv 2404.17966) reports 98.5% mean line coverage on 507 sampled patches, changing under 1.53% of defconfig options for 99% of patches, builds about 10.5x faster than allyesconfig. kfold's config-for cannot be presented as a new capability, only as a different design point. Evidence: https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.17966 ; https://pypi.org/project/kmax/ ; https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax",
              "The incumbent is maintained and has at least one independent user: issue #308 (opened 2026-08-16, open) says the reporter uses Kmax 'to generate minimized kernel configs for testing patches in CI'. Commits on master in Jan, Jul and Aug 2026 (v7.1 Kconfig parser added 2026-08-18). This kills any 'abandoned tool' argument. https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax/issues/308 ; https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax/commits/master",
              "Downstream adoption of kmax Kbuild formulas exists: the CIP kernel-cve-triage README says that on first run per kernel revision it generates kmax formulas ('computationally expensive'), caches them, and uses them for file-is-compiled checks. LWN (2025) says kmax 'has been integrated into the Intel 0-day test suite' but the only 0-day use visible to me is kismet (report of 2026-02-05), not klocalizer. So kmax formulas are the de facto reference for per-object Kbuild conditions; kfold must be compared against them, not around them. https://gitlab.com/cip-project/cip-kernel/kernel-cve-triage/-/raw/main/README.md ; https://lwn.net/Articles/1034811/ ; https://lkml.iu.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/2602.0/07161.html",
              "Granularity mismatch is in the competitor's favour: krepair works at line level including #ifdef arms (via SuperC), kfold at object level. Any head-to-head must be object-level, and kfold must say that 'object built' is weaker than 'changed lines compiled'. krepair's own caveat: 'succeeding in compiling does not guarantee a test suite will execute the code'. https://arxiv.org/html/2404.17966v1",
              "Adoption by kernel developers of klocalizer is NOT visible on LKML: mail-archive.com search of linux-kernel returned 0 matches for 'klocalizer', 0 for 'krepair', 0 for 'koverage' (3 unrelated hits for 'kmax'; 24 for 'kismet', newest 2025-08-22). Caveat: mail-archive coverage of the list is partial and users may mention it elsewhere. Same absence argues weak demand for config-finding as much as it argues a gap. https://www.mail-archive.com/search?l=linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org&q=klocalizer&o=newest",
              "Known competitor weaknesses that are real and sourced: (a) own docs: 'The extracted formulas may not be exact.' and 'Klocalizer's formulas were wrong in some cases.'; (b) 'Passing multiple architectures is not supported.'; (c) composites 'are not built directly via make'; CONFIG_BROKEN units skipped by default; (d) Booleans by default, tristate experimental (-T), and Jan 2026 commits 'Disable tristate support from kclause when using klocalizer'; issue #286 says -D only means enabled so y/m is arbitrary; (e) #308: the arch-specific ps3disk.o is reported satisfiable on x86_64 with a config lacking CONFIG_PS3_DISK, maintainer: 'Sorry that there isn't really a satisfactory answer to this.'; (f) #290: 'SuperC configuration generation failed', coverage 2004/2392 files and 37576/46886 lines on a 5.10.x diff; (g) #303: kextractlinux hardcodes CC=cc so constraints become GCC-specific and clang options are dropped. https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax/blob/master/docs/advanced.md ; https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax/issues/286 ; /308 ; /290 ; /303",
              "Minimality is heuristic in krepair, not optimal: it 'gradually removes configuration option settings until the configuration file satisfies the constraints', uses the unsat core, and states 'z3 is not guaranteed to provide a minimal unsatisfiable core'. Change is measured as number of differing options. If kfold wants to claim 'fewest changes' it must itself deliver a provably or measurably smaller change set; otherwise the claim is void. https://arxiv.org/html/2404.17966v1",
              "krepair performance baseline: 'kclause typically taking 2-3 minutes, kmax 10-15 minutes, and SuperC about a minute or less'; caching 'reducing the maximum runtime by 71.4% to around 19 minutes', 'less than one minute for 93.8% of runs', outliers 'up to an hour'. So the speed gap is per kernel revision, and after caching the incumbent is under a minute for most runs; kfold's 'about a minute per revision' is not an advantage for repeated runs on one revision. Not measured on one machine. https://arxiv.org/html/2404.17966v1",
              "Non-Linux claim is risky: the krepair paper says it evaluates 'on only one software system, the Linux kernel' but the authors state the algorithm is independent of any build system and name BusyBox, coreboot and zephyr as Kbuild users. The kmax README and docs I read show only Linux examples, but I could not confirm whether kextract supports BusyBox/coreboot (the project's saved Kmax run on BusyBox suggests it does, unverified). Do not claim other Kbuild trees as an advantage over Kmax until checked.",
              "Heuristic neighbours exist for file-to-config: cvehound's --kernel-config 'will infer the kernel configuration required to build the affected code' (based on Kbuild/Makefiles, ifdefs are not checked); files it cannot map are 'reported as unknown and counted as affected'. KernJC (RAID 2024, arXiv 2404.11107) derives path-level configs from Makefile file dependencies plus Kconfig graph hops and reports no precision or recall for config identification. Neither minimises change to a base config, but both show people solve the file-to-config sub-problem with ad hoc Makefile reading, so an exact per-object condition table has a measurable accuracy target. https://github.com/evdenis/cvehound/blob/master/README.md ; https://arxiv.org/html/2404.11107",
              "Adjacent tools that do NOT solve D2 (checked): TuxMake --kconfig-add takes hand-specified config, no file-to-config; kunit.py only warns 'if we have not included the options dependencies'; Yocto kernel_configcheck reports 'specified values did not make it into the kernel's final configuration' per symbol, no source-file mapping (corroborates kfold's need to verify options survive olddefconfig); ConfigFix (arXiv 2012.15342) is option-level conflict resolution and no cf_*.c / SAT resolver appears in scripts/kconfig on the GitHub mirror listing I saw; Kconfirm (LKML v4, 2026-07-26) checks Kconfig misuse (1282 alarms on x86-64 v7.2-rc4), not file-to-config; kernelconfig.io page does not describe file-to-option mapping; lkp-tests page gives no config-selection description; PatchLens (arXiv 2606.25863, FSE 2026) derives source-level presence conditions of patch hunks (avg 1.84 variables on Linux) without compiling, resolving file inclusion by 'lightweight build system analysis', and does not produce a .config. https://docs.kernel.org/dev-tools/kunit/run_wrapper.html ; https://docs.yoctoproject.org/kernel-dev/common.html ; https://github.com/kernelci/tuxmake ; https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.25863",
              "Demand remains indirect (policy text, COMPILE_TEST patches, KernelCI 2020 survey ranking per-patch testing first). The strongest direct demand evidence for D2 is the single CI user in kmax issue #308 and the CIP triage reuse of kmax formulas; both support 'someone needs per-object conditions' but not 'developers want config-for'. I found no 2023-2026 paper on config-for for patches other than the krepair/klocalizer line (search budget exhausted after one query, so this is a weak negative).",
              "Not verified this pass: Nguyen & Nguyen 2020 content, KBootRepair (no repository or paper found on the author's GitHub, appleseedlab org page or arXiv author list, so the earlier survey claim that it 'used' klocalizer is unsupported here), the ICSE 2025 kernel fuzzing paper by the same group (artifact README says 'repaired' configs for syzbot-style fuzzing but never names klocalizer; paper itself not opened), 0-day/KernelCI/syzbot internals, Debian/Fedora tooling."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Dossier says klocalizer 'tries architectures one at a time and stops at the first satisfiable one'. What I verified: advanced.md says 'Passing multiple architectures is not supported.' and the maintainer in #308 says klocalizer 'checks one architecture at a time' with arch-specific Kconfig files. The 'stops at first satisfiable' wording was not re-found; drop it.",
              "Dossier tristate item: advanced.md says options are Booleans by default and tristate support is experimental behind -T; commits of 2026-01-15 disable tristate in kclause for klocalizer; #286 (opened by the maintainer, 2025-04-04) says -D only enables, so y or m is arbitrary. The #253 quote 'always sets to y' was not re-opened by me. Before listing =m preservation as a kfold advantage, test kfold itself.",
              "Dossier says 'Vampyr unmaintained (supported only to about Linux 3.2; I did not re-verify)'. Now verified in the krepair paper: 'VAMPYR is an older tool and no longer maintained' and 'It only supports up to around Linux 3.2 (released in 2012).'",
              "Survey claim [138] that klocalizer 'has been used by others (KBootRepair)' is unsupported: KBootRepair is not in the krepair paper text, the paulgazz or appleseedlab GitHub pages, or the author's arXiv list. Drop it unless a source appears.",
              "Survey claim [27] (kmax 'integrated into 0-day') is only an LWN secondhand statement ('has been integrated into the Intel 0-day test suite'); the visible 0-day output I verified is kismet (2026-02-05 report), not klocalizer. Do not write that 0-day uses klocalizer.",
              "Dossier timing: 'kclause 2-3 min, kmax 10-15 min' is per kernel revision and caching brings most runs under a minute (93.8%); do not present kfold's one-minute whole-tree run as faster than a cached klocalizer repair without measuring both on one machine.",
              "Issue evidence must be cited by number only (double-blind); the pages show user handles, do not copy them."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "rq3_task",
            "summary": "The problem is already solved for patches by klocalizer/krepair (kmax suite): same input (base .config plus unit or patch), same kind of output (repaired config), strong published numbers (98.5% line coverage on 507 patches, under 1.53% of defconfig options changed in 99% of patches), active maintenance through Aug 2026, one visible independent CI user (issue #308) and downstream reuse of its Kbuild formulas by CIP kernel-cve-triage. The paper must not present config-for as new or as filling a gap in capability. What honestly remains open, all about quality or form and not about existence: (1) object-exact Kbuild semantics measured against ground-truth builds, since the incumbent documents inexact formulas, wrong cases, no multi-arch, composites not handled, CONFIG_BROKEN skipped, and a live arch-specific false-satisfiable bug (#308), and publishes no accuracy figure for its Kbuild constraints; (2) explanation: no source shows klocalizer producing a failing-conjunct chain, while kfold's 'why' engine does; (3) a lightweight pipeline with no SuperC/Java or make.cross for the Kbuild half, whole-tree table in about a minute (per-revision cost; incumbent caches to under a minute for most runs, so only claim this if measured on one machine); (4) patch-level multi-file object coverage verified by olddefconfig and compilation on a full stable release (384 of 402 commits); (5) possibly other Kbuild trees, but only after checking whether kextract already supports BusyBox/coreboot; (6) minimality: krepair's own algorithm is core-guided removal with non-minimal unsat cores, so a provably or measurably smaller change set would be a real, checkable claim, but only if kfold's search actually optimises it. kfold cannot claim line-level or #ifdef coverage, which is the incumbent's strength. Recommended framing: related work plus an RQ3 comparison on the same 402 commits at object level (baselines: the four existing builds; head-to-head klocalizer on a small sample needs user approval and one process at a time), reporting quality, option-change counts, failures by cause, and explanation, and never 'first'. Without the klocalizer head-to-head, demote D2 to related work and a limitation. Caveats: web search budget was exhausted after one query; adoption evidence is the mail-archive absence check (0 hits for klocalizer, krepair, koverage), a single issue-tracker user, and CIP triage reuse; absence on a partial archive is weak.",
            "sources_checked": [
              {
                "url": "https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax",
                "opened": true,
                "quote_verbatim": true,
                "note": "README: klocalizer --repair / --include / --include-mutex, -a ARCH, DIMACS/SMT export; limits 'some lines may still be omitted, for instance if they are dead code' and multiple configs for #ifdef arms; SuperC and make.cross needed; 72 stars, 29 forks, 1,289 commits; credits Intel 0-day for kismet. Page shows only Linux examples."
              },
              {
                "url": "https://pypi.org/project/kmax/",
                "opened": true,
                "quote_verbatim": false,
                "note": "Latest 4.10 released 2026-08-18; 55 releases (21 stable, 34 pre); classifier Beta; GPLv2+; one maintainer; no download counts shown."
              },
              {
                "url": "https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax/commits/master",
                "opened": true,
                "quote_verbatim": true,
                "note": "15 latest commits all 2026: Aug 18 (v7.1 Kconfig parser in kextract), Jul 10, Jan 15-16 (kextractlinux LLVM support; 'Disable tristate support from kclause when using klocalizer')."
              },
              {
                "url": "https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax/blob/master/docs/advanced.md",
                "opened": true,
                "quote_verbatim": true,
                "note": "'The extracted formulas may not be exact.'; 'Klocalizer's formulas were wrong in some cases.'; 'Passing multiple architectures is not supported.'; composites not built directly; CONFIG_BROKEN skipped by default; Boolean default, tristate experimental via -T; --approximate removes conflicting constraints successively."
              },
              {
