            "lens": "Fit and experiment designer for D2 (find a .config that builds a given file or patch with minimal change to a base config)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "summary": "FIT. D2 is mainly an object-selection problem at its core, and kfold's per-object build condition addresses that core. The pipeline \"patch -> touched .c/.S -> objects -> conjoin conditions with K -> solve with minimum Hamming distance to a base config\" is exactly the Kbuild half. Four parts of the problem lie outside kfold, and the paper should say so. (1) Line coverage inside a file is a C-preprocessor problem. krepair and Vampyr measure changed lines including #ifdef arms (I re-opened the krepair paper; it reports line-level patch coverage, not file or object coverage). 'The object is built' therefore does not imply 'the changed lines compile'. (2) Whether a requested option survives Kconfig expansion (olddefconfig silently drops or overrides options, as Tartler 2011 reports) is a Kconfig-specification problem. kfold only handles it by over-approximating K and then verifying. (3) Cross-compiler availability and multi-architecture patches are a toolchain problem; krepair reports about 11% of patches needing cross-compilation. (4) Header-only patches have no object. The problem is also not new: undertaker (2011) and klocalizer/krepair (2022-2024) solve it, and kmax is still maintained (issue #308 was filed and answered in August 2026). So the paper must present this as a task that kfold's condition table supports, not as a new problem. Competing on line-level coverage is a losing position. The defensible claim is object-level: a whole-tree condition table computed once in about a minute, an explanation (failing conjuncts) attached to each answer, and a verified result on real stable-update data, including non-Linux trees. EXPERIMENT. The strongest design uses only assets already on hand: the 402 .c/.S-touching commits of v7.2.7..v7.2.8, four fresh builds with object inventories, the saved Kmax run, and the config-for results. The primary question is: how often do standard configs leave a patch's touched objects unbuilt, and does config-for close that gap with few option changes, verified by actual compilation? DEMAND. Evidence is indirect (policy text, COMPILE_TEST patches, one external user of kmax in #308). Do not claim developers want it. Frame it as 'a patch that no tester config builds is untested'.",
            "issues": [
              "Prior-art status: patch-to-config is solved by undertaker (2011) and klocalizer/krepair (FSE 2024: 98.5% mean line coverage, 21.7% defconfig, 29.2% one randconfig, 88.5% allyesconfig, <1.53% of defconfig options changed in 99% of patches; I re-opened arxiv.org/html/2404.17966v1 and these numbers match). A PLDI reviewer will ask 'why not klocalizer?'. kfold cannot win on line coverage and must not compare on it.",
              "Granularity mismatch: krepair's 98.5% is line coverage including #ifdef arms. kfold's number is object coverage. The two must not appear in the same table without saying so. Even a 100% object-level result says nothing about hunks inside #if CONFIG_X regions.",
              "Ground truth is available for the object-level claim: an object counts as covered only if make actually produces the .o under the returned config. This is non-circular because the build is independent of kfold's formulas. It is not available for line-level coverage, since that needs preprocessor-aware coverage (SuperC/koverage). Do not claim anything at line level.",
              "Selection bias: 7.2.7..7.2.8 stable commits are backported fixes concentrated in x86-buildable drivers and core code. They are not representative of new-feature patches, arch/SoC patches, or non-x86 work. krepair sampled 507 patches from a full year; this experiment uses a single point release.",
              "The 402 patches may be dominated by objects already built by allmodconfig or Debian config. If so, the headline gap between baselines and config-for will be small and the paper gets a weak 'it works' result rather than a strong 'baselines miss X%' result. Measure this first (step 1 below, about half a day) before investing in the rest.",
              "Over-approximated K: a returned config can fail olddefconfig or fail to build the object. The 18 of 402 with no config, and any of the 384 that fail compilation, must be reported by cause (non-x86, header-only, unsat under K, K over-approximation, parse gap). Report configs for which olddefconfig changed requested options as a separate failure class.",
              "Tristate: the dossier says klocalizer writes y only (issue #253, Jan 2026 commits). Whether kfold preserves =m from the base config was not checked. Do not list tristate as a difference until it is tested on a few drivers.",
              "Base-config comparability: krepair's median 0.27% is relative to x86 defconfig (about 15k options per its slide; LWN reports 32k for Linux 6.16). kfold's percent will depend on how 'set options' is counted. Report absolute counts of changed options and the percent under a stated definition, and say the numbers are not directly comparable.",
              "Timing comparison is not apples to apples: krepair's 12-18 min is constraint collection per kernel version (kclause 2-3 min plus kmax 10-15 min, from the paper, different machine and kernel version), while kfold's table takes about 1 min on Linux 7.2.8. State both as measured on different machines. A fair claim needs running kmax on the same machine (stretch step).",
              "Dossier wording to soften: issue #308 shows klocalizer saying an arch-specific unit is buildable on x86_64. I confirmed the title, open state, and the maintainer's agreement that ps3disk.o 'should not be buildable in x86'. I did not confirm the dossier's claim that it 'returned a config that omitted the option'; I only saw 'reported that x86_64 could build'. Cite by issue number only (double-blind, and the reporter handle is public).",
              "Demand risk: the only user-survey evidence (KernelCI 2020) ranks per-patch testing first and does not mention build configuration; the Hubaux 2012 survey is old. Maintainers rely on allmodconfig, linux-next and 0-day. Position the tool as predicting and explaining what bots will not build, not as replacing bots."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Keep the krepair 'ten randconfigs' figure as ~74.4-74.6%. Section 5.6 says 74.4% and the intro text I fetched says 74.6%, so quote one with a footnote, not a loose 'around 75%'.",
              "krepair allyesconfig build time: the paper says 'around four hours on average' vs about 20 minutes for defconfig and krepair configs (confirmed in the fetched HTML). The OSS 2022 slide's 3 h should not be cited.",
              "The dossier's long quote for issue #308 ('You are indeed right that ps3disk.o should not be buildable in x86') was not reproduced verbatim by my fetch, which returned a summary-style paraphrase. Use the short phrase 'ps3disk.o should not be buildable in x86' only after one more manual look, or paraphrase.",
              "who_has_it item (5) says Hubaux survey respondents were 'mostly non-kernel-developers', while the evidence block says the survey was posted to 11 distro forums plus LKML. Say 'mixed, distro users and some developers' and flag it as a 2012 survey.",
              "Compare 'files covered' (a rate of patches whose touched objects are all built), not 'lines covered'. Do not call the object-level analogue of krepair's 21.7%/88.5% a reproduction; label it 'object-level coverage of the same kind of baseline'.",
              "384/402 = 95.5% is a rate over .c/.S-touching commits. It is not a rate over the 437 commits (header-only, Documentation, Kconfig-only, and non-code commits are excluded by construction). State the denominator in every sentence that gives the percentage."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "rq3_task",
            "sources_checked": [
              {
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.17966v1",
                "opened": true,
                "note": "Re-opened (first 100,000 of 109,319 characters). Confirmed: patch coverage is measured at line level; krepair 98.5%, defconfig 21.7%, allyesconfig 88.5%, one randconfig 29.2%, ten randconfigs 74.6% (Sec 5.6 says 74.4%); less than 1.53% of defconfig changed in 99% of patches, median 0.27%, max 9.52%; one config for 97% of patches, five or fewer for more than 99%, 23 worst case; header-only unsupported (~2%); ~11% need cross-compilation; 7 unbuildable due to tooling; 2 unsupported-architecture patches; most runs a few minutes, outliers ~1 hour; caching cut max runtime 71.4% to ~19 min; allyesconfig build ~4 h vs ~20 min. Not re-checked: kclause/kmax timings and Table 2 allnoconfig figures (in the unread final 9k characters or later sections).",
                "quote_verbatim": true
