      {
        "id": "D3",
        "name": "Stable and backport build breakage in specific arch/config pairs",
        "dive": {
          "id": "D3",
          "name": "Stable and backport build breakage in specific arch/config pairs",
          "refined_description": "PROBLEM STATEMENT (narrowed by this deep-dive). Backports to old stable trees sometimes break the build for one architecture or one config family. In every incident where the cause is visible, the fault is not Kbuild object selection. It is (a) a backport that missed prerequisite commits, (b) a toolchain issue (old gcc-8, moving clang-nightly), or (c) a global option such as CONFIG_WERROR=Y. The (arch, config) pair is the detection surface, not the fault. The Kbuild question left over is a coverage question that nobody computes per backport: which builds of the 150-400-build stable matrix actually compile the .o files this backport touches, and which touched objects does no build in the matrix compile? The current process catches most breakage at the -rc stage, where Greg Kroah-Hartman drops the offending patches. Some leaks into releases. LWN reported build-fix-only stable releases in Jan 2025 (6.6.71), May 2025 (6.1.137, LoongArch backport error) and Mar 2026 (6.12.76/6.6.129/6.1.166, a backport missing patches, seen with CONFIG_WERROR=Y on x86): three in about 14 months. None of the three is shown to be an object-selection problem, and for 6.6.70 the cause is not stated. Fit with kfold is therefore weak to moderate and speculative: config-for and why could choose and explain the builds that exercise a backport's touched objects, but they cannot predict the error. The problem's existence is well supported; kfold's relevance to it is not yet shown.",
          "who_has_it": "Stable maintainers (Greg Kroah-Hartman, Sasha Levin); stable-rc testers who run fixed build matrices (Guenter Roeck; Linaro LKFT with Naresh Kamboju and others; arch testers such as Matthew Rosato for s390); distro and CIP kernel maintainers who backport to old trees; downstream users of a broken point release (Peter Schneider reported the Mar 2026 one). Not a problem for mainline developers, who rely on linux-next allmodconfig and the 0-day robot.",
          "evidence": [
            {
              "claim": "Linaro LKFT report for 5.15.197-rc1 (Dec 2025): three backported commits broke three arches. arm: 15 of 101 builds failed (fuse-tegra30.c, Tegra114 nvmem cells, commit b9c01adedf38). mips: 22 of 22 failed (tlb-r4k.c, tlb_vpn kmalloc backport). powerpc: 3 of 22 failed (mm/mempool.c, gcc-8-allnoconfig and ppc6xx defconfigs). The powerpc cause is the old gcc-8 toolchain and C99 for-declarations, the mips one fails in all 22 configs, so none of them is an object-selection fault.",
              "quote": "'for' loop initial declarations are only allowed in C99 or C11 mode",
              "url": "https://lkml.iu.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/2512.0/07713.html",
              "date": "2025-12-04",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "Linaro LKFT stable-rc reports",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Outcome of the 5.15.197-rc1 regressions: Greg Kroah-Hartman answered each of the three reports with a one-word reply and removed the offending patches from the rc queue. Detection at -rc time followed by dropping is the normal path.",
              "quote": "dropped.",
              "url": "https://lkml.iu.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/2512.0/06985.html",
              "date": "2025-12",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "Linaro LKFT stable-rc reports",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "6.1.136-rc1 (Apr 2025): s390 defconfig and allmodconfig builds failed in drivers/s390/virtio/virtio_ccw.c because the backport of 's390/virtio_ccw: fix virtual vs physical address confusion' lacked two prerequisite commits. The s390 tester named the missing commits (1bcf7f48b7d4 and 8b19e145e82f). This is a missing-prerequisite fault, visible in 5 of the listed configs for that arch.",
              "quote": "Picking the following 2 dependencies would resolve the build error:",
              "url": "https://www.mail-archive.com/linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org/msg2589470.html",
              "date": "2025-04-30",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "6.1.136-rc1 review thread",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Greg Kroah-Hartman's response to the s390 report: he dropped the whole series and asked for a tested series, instead of picking the dependencies himself.",
              "quote": "I'm just going to drop all of these now and wait for a tested series to be sent.",
              "url": "https://www.mail-archive.com/linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org/msg2589470.html",
              "date": "2025-05-01",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "6.1.136-rc1 review thread",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "The same 6.1.136-rc1 review had three LKFT build regressions: x86_64 clang-nightly (include/net/ip.h), s390 and mips clang-nightly (arch/mips/kernel/branch.c). Two of three were toolchain related; Nathan Chancellor said clang-nightly is a moving target and that stable-rc reports should focus on issues that appear only with the stable patches.",
              "quote": "There are three build regressions and two build warnings.",
              "url": "https://www.mail-archive.com/linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org/msg2589428.html",
              "date": "2025-04-30",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "6.1.136-rc1 review thread",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Chancellor on the clang-nightly regression in 6.1.136-rc1: toolchain noise is separate from the stable patches, and the issue also shows on the base 6.1.136. The new clang warning (-Wdefault-const-init-var-unsafe) is intentional.",
              "quote": "clang-nightly is always a moving target",
              "url": "https://www.mail-archive.com/linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org/msg2589454.html",
              "date": "2025-04-30",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "6.1.136-rc1 review thread",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "6.1.137 (May 2025) is a build-fix-only release. Per LWN, it fixes a LoongArch build problem in 6.1.136 that came from a backport (commit 'LoongArch: Fix build error due to backport', arch/loongarch/mm/hugetlbpage.c, 2 lines). Whether LoongArch was in the pre-release matrix is not known to me. LWN groups it with 6.12.27 as 'build fixes only'.",
              "quote": "LoongArch: Fix build error due to backport",
              "url": "https://lwn.net/Articles/1020133/",
              "date": "2025-05-05",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "LWN build-fix-only stable releases",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "6.6.71 (Jan 2025) replaced 6.6.70 because a common configuration failed to build. Two commits were reverted: an x86/hyperv sched_clock fix and 'x86, crash: wrap crash dumping code into crash related ifdefs'. The announcement states no cause. The revert title suggests C-preprocessor gating, which is my inference, not stated.",
              "quote": "only needed if you could not properly build 6.6.70",
              "url": "https://lwn.net/Articles/1004703/",
              "date": "2025-01-10",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "LWN build-fix-only stable releases",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Mar 2026: 6.12.76, 6.6.129 and 6.1.166 were released only to repair build failures in 6.12.75, 6.6.128 and 6.1.165. LWN gives no cause.",
              "quote": "an upgrade is only necessary for those who have observed a build failure with the 6.12.75, 6.6.128, or 6.1.165 kernels.",
              "url": "https://lwn.net/Articles/1061524/",
              "date": "2026-03-05",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "LWN build-fix-only stable releases",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Cause of the Mar 2026 breakage as seen on LKML: a user reported x86 builds with CONFIG_WERROR=Y failing in arch/x86/kernel/setup.c (implicit declaration of ima_validate_range), traced to an x86/kexec IMA sanity-check commit. The trigger is a global option (WERROR), not an object selected by a CONFIG condition.",
              "quote": "none of these build on x86 with CONFIG_WERROR=Y",
              "url": "https://lkml.iu.edu/2603.0/07304.html",
              "date": "2026-03",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "6.1.165 incident (Schneider and Garg)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Aditya Garg's analysis of the same incident (user analysis, not maintainer confirmation): the three older branches received only part of a three-patch set, a missing-prerequisite fault.",
              "quote": "got only one part of the three patches sent",
              "url": "https://lkml.iu.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/2603.0/07179.html",
              "date": "2026-03",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "6.1.165 incident (Schneider and Garg)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "6.12.53-rc1 (Oct 2025): LKFT reported 13 failing s390 configs (clang and gcc, defconfig, allmodconfig, hardening and LTO variants) in arch/s390/net/bpf_jit_comp.c. The s390 maintainer replied that the prerequisites should be backported. Again a missing-prerequisite fault, visible in every s390 config.",
              "quote": "'struct bpf_jit' has no member named 'frame_off'",
              "url": "https://lkml.iu.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/2510.1/10232.html",
              "date": "2025-10",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "Linaro LKFT stable-rc reports",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Roeck's stable-review matrix, recent form: 155 builds, all pass, for 6.0.15-rc1; also 500 qemu tests with 2 failing (arm:xilinx-zynq-a9, seen all the way back to v4.19.y). The matrix is a fixed per-arch list of configs. Failures per release are rare in recent years.",
              "quote": "total: 155 pass: 155 fail: 0",
              "url": "https://lkml.iu.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/2212.2/04008.html",
              "date": "2022-12",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
              "source_group": "Guenter Roeck stable test matrix",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Older Roeck data point (3.4.97-rc, 2014): 137 builds, 20 skipped, 6 failed, all non-x86 (alpha:allmodconfig, arm:spear6xx_defconfig, score:defconfig, sparc64:allmodconfig, unicore32:defconfig, xtensa:allmodconfig). Shows the long-standing pattern that failures concentrate in non-x86 allmodconfig/defconfig builds.",
              "quote": "total: 137 pass: 111 skipped: 20 fail: 6",
              "url": "https://lkml.iu.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/1407.0/03297.html",
              "date": "2014-07",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
              "source_group": "Guenter Roeck stable test matrix",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "5.15.126-rc1 review (Aug 2023): one tester reported no problems on x86_64 and aarch64, and Roeck reported that aarch64:allmodconfig does not compile. Testers with different config sets reach opposite verdicts on the same rc. The page has no error output or cause.",
              "quote": "fwiw, aarch64:allmodconfig doesn't compile.",
              "url": "https://lkml.iu.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/2308.1/03550.html",
              "date": "2023-08-10",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "Guenter Roeck stable test matrix",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Roeck's public test dashboard (snapshot 2026-10-16, as read through a page summarizer, so counts are approximate) has build tests for 26 arch groups across 14 branches (stable 5.10 to 7.2, master, next and others), and qemu tests for 31 arch/config groups. Two build rows fail: sparc32 and sparc64 on 6.12; qemu rows for sparc and sparc64 6.12 also fail. A persistent tolerated failure, not a surprise.",
              "quote": "failed v6.12.113",
              "url": "https://kerneltests.org/builders",
              "date": "2026-10-16",
              "evidence_type": "tool",
              "source_group": "Guenter Roeck stable test matrix",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Linaro LKFT scale. Its stats page lists stable-RC counts (2025: 292 total RCs, 41 cancelled; 2024: 366 RCs, 34 cancelled; 2022: 497 and 33), kernels built per year (2024: 431,099; 2025: 417,178) and regressions per year (2024: 116; 2025: 83). The 'Regressions' column covers all regressions, not only build breakage, and the page gives no reason for cancelled RCs and no build-failure statistics.",
              "quote": "RC Cancelled",
