              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Linaro slide for 2024: 271 LTS releases tested, about 400 builds per kernel trigger, about 430k builds per year. The same slides name 'Limited access to broad test coverage' as a reliability problem and say the testing target is cloud-parallel build/test plans written as YAML build combinations. The matrix is chosen by hand.",
              "quote": "LTS Releases: 271",
              "url": "https://foss-north.se/2025/slides/aroxell-kerneltesting-slides.pdf",
              "date": "2025",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
              "source_group": "Linaro LKFT stable-rc reports",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Stable rules treat a build error as a valid stable fix category and impose no explicit multi-config compile requirement on backports; the onus is on the submitter. This counters the idea of a policy burden for backport coverage.",
              "quote": "a build error (but not for things marked CONFIG_BROKEN)",
              "url": "https://docs.kernel.org/process/stable-kernel-rules.html",
              "date": "n.d.",
              "evidence_type": "policy",
              "source_group": "Kernel documentation",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "2025 Maintainers Summit linux-next session (LWN, as read through a summarizer): Torvalds said some trees get no testing at all, and that allmodconfig disables some code and can hide problems; Ojeda did not want people testing only canned configurations. Supports that one named config misses objects, but it concerns linux-next, not stable.",
              "quote": "some repositories clearly are not seeing any testing at all",
              "url": "https://lwn.net/Articles/1050027/",
              "date": "2025-12",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "LWN Maintainers Summit",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "The closest tool (krepair/klocalizer) was evaluated only on mainline patches (507 of about 71,000 from one year) and never on stable backports or old trees. Its 11% cross-compile share and the 7 patches it could not build for toolchain reasons show that non-x86 builds are where its tooling is weakest. Whether it works on stable-era Kbuild is untested.",
              "quote": "We have taken a random sample of 507 patches out of the approximately 71,000 patches from one recent whole year",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.17966v1",
              "date": "2024-04",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
              "source_group": "krepair FSE 2024",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            }
          ],
          "existing_solutions": [
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Roeck's stable-rc build and qemu matrix (kerneltests.org)",
              "what_it_does": "Builds every stable-rc on a fixed per-arch list of defconfigs and allmodconfig (155 builds for 6.0.15-rc1; 26 arch groups across 14 branches in Oct 2026) plus qemu boot tests, and posts pass/fail per arch:config.",
              "limits": "The config list is fixed by hand and is not derived from what a backport touches. It does not say which touched objects the matrix compiled. Persistent failures (sparc 6.12) are tolerated.",
              "url": "https://kerneltests.org/builders"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Linaro LKFT / TuxSuite / TuxMake stable-rc builds",
              "what_it_does": "About 400 builds per trigger over arches, gcc/clang versions and config variants; mails regressions per (arch, toolchain, config), usually with the suspected commit.",
              "limits": "Matrix and plans are hand-written YAML. Toolchain noise (clang-nightly, gcc-8) mixes with real regressions. Reports name configs, not objects. Cause and fix remain manual.",
              "url": "https://foss-north.se/2025/slides/aroxell-kerneltesting-slides.pdf"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Maintainer dropping of patches at -rc time (Kroah-Hartman, Levin)",
              "what_it_does": "Offending patches are dropped from the queue when a regression is reported (5.15.197-rc1, 6.1.136-rc1), and the author is asked to resend a tested series.",
              "limits": "Reactive and cheap; works whenever a matrix member exercises the patched object. Some breakage still reaches releases (6.6.70, 6.1.136, 6.1.165).",
              "url": "https://lkml.iu.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/2512.0/06985.html"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "klocalizer / krepair / koverage (Kmax suite)",
              "what_it_does": "Finds or repairs a config so a patch's lines build, checks patch coverage of a config, one architecture per invocation, with #ifdef constraints via SuperC.",
              "limits": "Evaluated only on mainline patches, not on stable backports or old trees. Header-only patches unsupported. Kbuild formulas 'may not be exact'. No explanation chain.",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.17966v1"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Debian buildd (src:linux on 17 arches)",
              "what_it_does": "Builds the linux source package for 17 architectures per upload (7.2.11-1 snapshot: 15 installed, 2 building, none failed).",
              "limits": "Distro-specific configs and release cadence; not a pre-release check for stable backports; only a snapshot was read.",
              "url": "https://buildd.debian.org/status/package.php?p=linux&suite=unstable"
            }
          ],
          "remaining_gap": "What is missing is any statement, per stable backport, of which tested (arch, config) builds compile each touched object, and which touched objects no build in the pre-release matrix compiles. This is a modest gap. Counter-evidence is strong: every cause I could see was upstream of Kbuild (missing prerequisites, toolchain, WERROR), the process already catches most of it at the -rc stage, and maintainers drop patches rather than debug them. No data show how often a stable release breaks because a touched object was outside the matrix. The three build-fix-only releases in 14 months are the only direct count, and their causes are only partly known (6.6.70 unknown; 6.1.136 LoongArch matrix membership unknown).",
          "kfold_contribution": "Modest and specific: (1) given a backport's touched .c/.S files, list for each touched object its build condition, so a tester or a CI plan can see which of the existing matrix builds compile it (config-for/why on the target tree); (2) flag touched objects that none of the matrix's configs builds (a blindspots-style report per backport) and propose the fewest extra (arch, config) builds; (3) give a 'why' chain for a failing or silent pair. It does not predict or explain the compile error, does not detect missing prerequisites, and does not address toolchain or C-preprocessor causes. It would need a per-arch run for non-x86 objects and older-tree Kbuild support (unverified).",
          "quantitative_facts": [
            "5.15.197-rc1 (Dec 2025): arm 15 of 101 builds failed, mips 22 of 22, powerpc 3 of 22; Greg Kroah-Hartman dropped all three offending patches (lkml 2512.0/07713, 06985).",
            "6.1.136-rc1 (Apr 2025): 'three build regressions and two build warnings'; two of the three were clang-nightly, one was an s390 missing-prerequisite backport; the final 6.1.136 release still needed 6.1.137 for a LoongArch backport error (LWN 1020133).",
            "6.12.53-rc1 (Oct 2025): 13 s390 configs failed in one file (bpf_jit_comp.c).",
            "Build-fix-only stable releases reported by LWN: Jan 2025 (6.6.71), May 2025 (6.12.27 and 6.1.137), Mar 2026 (6.12.76/6.6.129/6.1.166): three episodes in about 14 months (a lower bound: only those I found).",
            "Roeck matrix: 155 builds (6.0.15-rc1, Dec 2022) all passing; 137 builds with 6 failures, all non-x86 (3.4.97, 2014); kerneltests.org Oct 2026: 26 arch groups across 14 branches, sparc 6.12 failing.",
            "LKFT: 271 LTS releases, about 430k builds, about 400 builds per trigger in 2024; 2025 stable RCs: 292 with 41 cancelled (14%); 2024: 366 with 34 cancelled (9%); cancellation reasons not given. The 'Regressions' counts (116 in 2024, 83 in 2025) cover all test types and must not be cited as build breakage.",
            "krepair (mainline only): 507 of about 71,000 patches sampled; 11% need cross-compilation; 7 patches unbuildable for toolchain reasons; not evaluated on stable.",
            "mail-archive match counts (35, 21, 20 results for stable-list queries) came from a word-based matcher and are noise; do not cite them."
          ],
          "proposed_experiment": "Single best experiment on available assets: treat v7.2.7..v7.2.8 itself as a stable update. For the 402 commits that touch .c/.S files (384 already have a config-for result), map each touched file to its object and its kfold build condition, then evaluate it against the four x86 object inventories (i386 tinyconfig, x86-64 defconfig, Debian config, x86-64 allmodconfig). Report one table: for each commit, whether every touched object is built by (a) defconfig only, (b) allmodconfig only, (c) the union of the four builds; the number of commits that touch at least one object built by none of the four; how many of those config-for solves (and with how many changed options); and how many touch non-x86 arch/ directories (outside what the x86 run can judge). Add a spot check of 20 'built by none' objects against kfold's why output and, where cheap, against make olddefconfig/object compile results already collected. Optionally run klocalizer on a 30-commit subset for a head-to-head. Prediction to test: most commits are exercised by allmodconfig on x86, a small residual needs a different config, and non-x86 files are a separate class. If the residual is under about 2-3% of commits, the stable-coverage story is weak and should be dropped as a motivation. Do not run kfold on 5.15 or 6.1 trees: no builds exist for them and kfold's entry settings for older Kbuild are unverified. Run the work single-process per CLAUDE.md.",
          "risks": "1) Search budget: WebSearch was exhausted after the first three queries, so I did not run the 15 new searches requested. The new evidence comes from direct WebFetch reads of known mirrors (lkml.iu.edu, mail-archive.com, LWN, kerneltests.org, stats.lkft.linaro.org, Linaro slides via pdftotext). lore.kernel.org returned 403 and DBLP was blocked. 2) Fault classification rests on my reading of five or so incidents; the cause of the 6.6.70 break is unknown, and the 6.1.165 trigger (WERROR) was reported by users, not confirmed by a maintainer. 3) Several numbers (kerneltests.org, stats.lkft, LWN Maintainers Summit) were read through a page summarizer; exact wording and counts are medium confidence. Mail-archive search counts are word-matching noise. 4) The claims from the previous gap-scout (G0-G99) were not re-opened except where listed. 5) Detection by the -rc matrix plus dropping already works; the value of better selection is small and unmeasured. 6) kfold has Linux 7.2.8 x86 only; non-x86 objects, old-tree Makefiles (5.15, 6.1) and the entry settings for them are not covered. 7) Maintainers value runtime testing over compile-only evidence (see G13 'Compile testing isn't sufficient'), and the stable rules impose no coverage requirement. 8) Single-source clustering: most evidence is Linaro LKFT, Roeck, or LWN, so independence is limited (four source groups). 9) Danger of overclaiming: kfold would not have prevented any incident I examined.",
          "confidence": "low"
        },
        "verdicts": [
          {
            "lens": "EVIDENCE AUDITOR for D3 (stable/backport build breakage in specific arch/config pairs)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "Most cited incidents re-verify. I re-opened 18 URLs. They are LWN 1004703, 1004704, 1005546, 1020133, 1061524 and 1050027; lkml.iu.edu 2512.0/07713, 06985, 2510.1/10232, 2603.0/07304, 07179, 2212.2/04008 and 2308.1/03550; mail-archive msg2589470, 2589428 and 2589454; docs.kernel.org stable rules; and the krepair arXiv page. I also opened kerneltests.org, stats.lkft and the Linaro slides. The 5.15.197-rc1 numbers, the 'dropped.' replies, the s390 dependency quotes, the Schneider and Garg quotes, the 155-build line and the Chancellor quote match the sources.",
              "The count of 'three build-fix-only releases in about 14 months' is an undercount. My own LWN fetch found LWN 1005546 (19 Jan 2025). It says of 6.1.126: 'Only upgrade if 6.1.125 did not build properly for you.' Ron Economos is credited with the fix. That is a fourth episode, in the same month as 6.6.71. Build-fix releases are therefore more frequent than the dossier says. The dossier also calls its number a lower bound, so this is consistent, but the figure cannot be quoted as 'three'.",
              "The claim that LWN 1020133 'groups it with 6.12.27 as build fixes only' is not supported by that page. The fetched page covers only 6.1.137 and does not mention 6.12.27. Treat the 6.12.27 claim as unverified. The 6.1.137 text itself is verified: 'This release fixes a problem building the Loongarch target in the 6.1.136 release.' The changelog entry reads 'LoongArch: Fix build error due to backport.'",
              "The date on the stats.lkft.linaro.org claim is wrong. The page says it was generated 2026-02-01, and the 2026 row is partial (19 RCs, 19 cancelled). The dossier dates it 2026-10-17. The figures themselves are right: 2025 has 292 RCs and 41 cancelled, 2024 has 366 and 34, 2022 has 497 and 33. Kernels built are 417,178 for 2025 and 431,099 for 2024. The slides label their 2024 'Regressions: 116' as part of LKFT Linux Stats, with no breakdown. The page gives no reason for cancelled RCs, so the dossier's 14% and 9% cancellation shares cannot be read as build breakage.",
              "The kerneltests.org snapshot is more nuanced than 'two build rows fail'. The page was built on 16 Oct 2026. Four rows are marked 'failed' (build sparc32 and sparc64, qemu sparc and sparc64), all on v6.12.113. Warnings and partial failures also appear: i386 on next (2 fails), x86_64 on pending-fixes (1 fail), and several qemu rows on arm64, parisc and arm-aspeed. The row counts are my own tally from a summarizer, so they are approximate.",
              "The 6.1.136-rc1 'three regressions' claim is only partly checkable. The mail-archive page for msg2589428 details only one of the three, x86_64 clang-nightly in include/net/ip.h. The s390 failures were reported with gcc-13, gcc-8, clang-20 and clang-nightly together. So the s390 failure was not a clang-nightly toolchain artefact, which fits the missing-prerequisite reading. The statement that two of the three were toolchain-related is not confirmed by that page. The report author appears to be Naresh Kamboju (LKFT), but the page does not say so for the build-failure block.",
              "The 6.1.165 trigger is not unambiguous. Peter Schneider reported 'none of these build on x86 with CONFIG_WERROR=Y' on 4 Mar 2026. Aditya Garg named arch/x86/kernel/setup.c, 'implicit declaration of function ima_validate_range', and said the branches 'got only one part of the three patches sent'. Both are user reports, not maintainer confirmation, as the dossier says. The file is x86 core, built under any config. The error is an implicit declaration, which -Werror turns into a hard failure. So this is not an object-selection fault, and WERROR is the detection condition rather than the cause.",
              "The 12 Dec 2025 LWN piece on the Maintainers Summit (Torvalds and Ojeda on allmodconfig) is reported speech by Corbet, not transcript. The dossier correctly flags 'some repositories clearly are not seeing any testing at all' and the allmodconfig remark as summarizer-read. I did re-open it and the wording matches. It concerns linux-next, not stable, and cannot support a stable-coverage claim.",
              "Independence is low. The strongest evidence comes from about four clusters: LKFT reports and slides (Kamboju and Roxell), Roeck's matrix, LWN (Corbet and daroc), and one 6.1.165 thread. Several cited incidents are one group's reports of the same rc. Counting by independent source, the dossier has about four groups, as it admits.",
              "Each of the 18 pages above was opened. The slides URL returns an unreadable PDF through the fetcher. I downloaded it and ran pdftotext. The page title is 'Faster Linux Kernel Testing with Linaro's Open-Source Tools and LKFT Automation', and the stats header is 'LKFT ... Linux Stats - 2024'. The slide text confirms 'LTS Releases: 271', 'Kernel triggers: 1,229', 'Builds: ~400 builds, ~2500 boots', 'Builds: ~430k' and 'Limited access to broad test coverage'. The slides call the YAML a plan 'describing a combination of builds and tests'. The claim 'The matrix is chosen by hand' is an inference. Also note the slides state the reliability problem in general terms, not as stable-rc build breakage.",
              "The remaining gap is not closed by any source. The load-bearing step is 'kfold would help'. No incident shows a touched object left unbuilt by the matrix. The dossier says so itself. The evidence supports existence of occasional stable build breakage, not kfold relevance. WebSearch budget ran out, so I could not search for further contradicting or supporting incidents or for the 6.12.27 release."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Build-fix-only stable releases: the list is not three. Add 6.1.126 (LWN 1005546, 19 Jan 2025, build failures introduced by 6.1.125, fix credited to Ron Economos) beside 6.6.71 (LWN 1004704 'A straggling kernel update', 10 Jan 2025; Chris Clayton reported a build problem with 6.6.70). Also 6.1.137 (5 May 2025, LoongArch, LWN 1020133) and the 6.12.76/6.6.129/6.1.166 set (LWN 1061524, 5 Mar 2026). That is at least four episodes in about 14 months. Say 'at least four, found by non-exhaustive search'.",
              "LWN 1004703 (6.6.71) is the page I opened, and its date of 10 Jan 2025 and the quotes 'It's only needed if you could not properly build 6.6.70' and 'there is a configuration that is pretty common that would fail to build properly.' are verified. The dossier's quote begins 'only needed if you could not properly build 6.6.70', which is a substring and fine. The 'Revert x86, crash: wrap crash dumping code into crash related ifdefs' title is verified. The cause remains unstated.",
              "LWN 1061524 attributes the statement to Levin: 'Levin said that an upgrade is only necessary for those who have observed a build failure ...'. The dossier's quote is a paraphrase of the LWN text with a slight reword ('an upgrade is only necessary for those who have observed a build failure with the 6.12.75, 6.6.128, or 6.1.165 kernels.'). The sentence is LWN's own prose, not Greg's announcement. Date is 5 Mar 2026 (verified). Quote fragments are accurate up to the version list, but cite it as LWN prose.",
              "stats.lkft.linaro.org: date should read 'page generated 2026-02-01', not 2026-10-17. The 2026 row is partial.",
              "6.12.27 'build fixes only' grouping on LWN 1020133: remove or mark unverified. The page I fetched does not mention it.",
              "kerneltests.org: describe as 'four rows marked failed, all sparc on v6.12.113 (build sparc32, sparc64; qemu sparc, sparc64), plus partial fails on i386/next and x86_64/pending-fixes'. Do not say only two rows fail. The 26 arch groups and 14 branches count is my approximate tally from the page and is also approximate in the dossier.",
              "6.12.53-rc1: the verified reply is by Ilya Leoshkevich (14 Oct 2025). The page confirms 13 s390 configs, file arch/s390/net/bpf_jit_comp.c, and the message \"error: 'struct bpf_jit' has no member named 'frame_off'\". The attribution 'the s390 maintainer' is acceptable but I did not verify the wording of his advice. Mark 'prerequisites should be backported' as unverified.",
              "6.1.136-rc1 s390 report: the text 'Picking the following 2 dependencies would resolve the build error:' was written by Matthew Rosato on 30 Apr 2025, and Greg's reply of 1 May 2025 quotes it. The dossier's attribution to 'the s390 tester' is right. The failing configs were defconfig and allmodconfig with gcc-13, gcc-8, clang-20 and clang-nightly, so it is not toolchain noise.",
              "5.15.126-rc1: Harshit Mogalapalli said 'No problems seen on x86_64 and aarch64'. That does not mean aarch64:allmodconfig was tested by him. His config was probably defconfig-style. Roeck wrote 'fwiw, aarch64:allmodconfig doesn't compile.' on 10 Aug 2023. The dossier's reading 'opposite verdicts on the same rc' is plausible but is an inference. The page gives no cause."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "limitation",
            "summary": "The anecdotes and numbers are mostly accurate against the primary pages I opened: the rc thread quotes, the 155-build line and the LWN release notes. The quotes are verbatim or near-verbatim. Three dates and counts need correction: the stats.lkft date, the build-fix release count, and the unverified 6.12.27 grouping. The problem exists, and my check turned up a fourth build-fix episode (6.1.126), so these episodes are not as rare as 'three' suggests. Every visible cause is a missing prerequisite, a toolchain issue or a global option such as CONFIG_WERROR=Y. None is an object-selection fault. The process also already catches most breakage at the -rc stage, where the maintainer drops the patches. The dossier is right that the evidence for kfold's relevance is weak. I could not search for contradicting studies because the WebSearch budget ran out. The verdict is 'partly': the existence of stable build breakage is supported, but kfold's role has no evidence. Use this at most as a limitation or discussion paragraph, not as a motivating problem or an RQ task. Cite only the LWN pages and the specific lkml.iu.edu and mail-archive threads that I re-opened, with the corrected counts. Do not cite the stats.lkft figures as evidence of build breakage, and do not claim that kfold would have prevented any incident.\"",
            "sources_checked": [
              {
                "url": "https://lwn.net/Articles/1004703/",
                "opened": true,
                "note": "6.6.71, 10 Jan 2025. Quotes verified, reverts verified, cause not stated.",
                "quote_verbatim": true
              },
              {
                "url": "https://lwn.net/Articles/1004704/",
                "opened": true,
                "note": "LWN 'A straggling kernel update', 10 Jan 2025. Chris Clayton reported the 6.6.70 build problem.",
                "quote_verbatim": true
              },
              {
                "url": "https://lwn.net/Articles/1005546/",
                "opened": true,
                "note": "6.1.126, 19 Jan 2025, fixes 6.1.125 build failures. Not in the dossier. Shows the build-fix-only count is higher than three.",
