          {
            "lens": "Hostile competitor analyst, D3: stable and backport build breakage in specific arch/config pairs",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "The existing process already does the job. Hand-written build matrices (Roeck, Linaro LKFT/TuxSuite, KernelCI) catch most backport breakage at the -rc stage, and Greg Kroah-Hartman drops the offending patch. No incident I re-read has a cause in Kbuild object selection: missing prerequisite commits (6.1.136 s390; 4.9.y ufs i_blocksize in 2017; 5.15.197 arm/mips), toolchain (gcc-8 C99 for-loops, clang-nightly), or a global option (WERROR).",
              "No competitor computes, per backport, which builds of the matrix compile each touched object. I found no such feature in TuxSuite, TuxMake, KernelCI or the stable rules. This is an absence observation from the pages I opened, not proof. klocalizer/koverage is the nearest, at line level on one architecture per run, and nothing shows it was ever used on stable.",
              "klocalizer already covers the 'config that builds this patch' and 'does this config cover this patch' use cases (--repair, --include foo.o, koverage --check-patch). It also handles #ifdef arms through SuperC, which kfold does not. On a stable-coverage story kfold is therefore a re-implementation with a different engine, not a new capability. Its differentiators are speed, object-level exactness, the why chain and blindspots grouped by MAINTAINERS, and these are not specific to stable.",
              "Config-dependent surfacing does happen, but the matrix handles it. In 5.15.197-rc1 only 15 of 101 arm builds failed (Tegra fuse object), so 86 builds did not compile the touched object. In the 2017 4.9.y report 12 of 203 builds failed on fs/ufs/balloc.c. In both cases the hand-written matrix still included a builder that compiled the object. The open question, whether some touched objects are compiled by no matrix member, has no data. The only direct count is three build-fix-only release episodes in about 14 months, with causes known only in part.",
              "kfold has Linux 7.2.8 x86 only. Stable trees are 5.10 to 6.12, and breakage concentrates on non-x86 builds (arm, mips, s390, LoongArch, powerpc), where kfold has no runs and no verified entry settings for old Kbuild. The proposed experiment on v7.2.7..v7.2.8 therefore tests x86 coverage of a mainline update, not stable-backport safety.",
              "Adoption evidence for the Kmax suite is modest: the paulgazz/kmax page shows 72 stars, 29 forks, 1,289 commits, and its Releases section is empty. The same page lists no stable-kernel or backport support, and I found no list thread using klocalizer on stable. Absence of evidence only, since search was exhausted.",
              "Evidence is clustered in four source groups (Linaro LKFT, Roeck, LWN, Garg/Schneider thread). Search budget is exhausted (200 WebSearch calls shared), so no new competitor discovery was possible this round; findings come from direct fetches. Fetched text passes through a small summarizer, so 'verbatim' quotes carry some risk."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Dossier claim 'Kbuild formulas may not be exact' (and the claims about CONFIG_BROKEN and composite objects) attributed to klocalizer docs: NOT found on the paulgazz/kmax GitHub page (which says only 'some lines may still be omitted, for instance if they are dead code or in header files'), nor on the PyPI kmax page, nor in the krepair arXiv paper (the paper does not state Kbuild inexactness and reports no accuracy measurement for the constraints). Mark unverified or drop.",
              "The krepair paper's stated limits, as seen: header-only patches unsupported (about 2%), 'these tools only collect constraints from the build system', make.cross does not support loongarch, 7 patches unbuildable, Linux-only evaluation. It does not evaluate stable trees or older kernel versions (confirmed from the full text, first 100,000 characters). The paper reports kclause 2-3 minutes and kmax 10-15 minutes.",
              "The kmax PyPI page (version 4.10, released Aug 18, 2026) gives these limits: 'some lines may still be omitted, for instance if they are dead code or in header files'; if a patch modifies both arms of an #ifdef, multiple configs are needed. The examples use one architecture per run (-a x86_64 or -a arm) and Linux 6.10 and 5.16 sources. It says nothing about stable kernels or backports. The repository is github.com/paulgazz/kmax (appleseedlab/kmax and paulgazzillo/kmax return 404).",
              "LWN 1020133 (5 May 2025) quotes 'This release fixes a problem building the Loongarch target in the 6.1.136 release.' and the commit title 'LoongArch: Fix build error due to backport'. The fetched page does not mention 6.12.27, so the dossier's grouping of 6.12.27 with 6.1.137 is unverified from that page.",
              "LWN 1061524 (5 Mar 2026) says the releases 'address a regression reported by Peter Schneider' and gives no technical cause. The WERROR and partial-patch-set explanations come only from the LKML user threads (user analysis, not maintainer confirmation).",
              "Recheck of 5.15.197-rc1 (lkml 2512.0/07713): arm 15 of 101, mips 22 of 22 and powerpc 3 of 22 are confirmed. The regression list names only 14 arm configs. The powerpc failures are gcc-12-ppc6xx_defconfig, gcc-8-allnoconfig and gcc-8-ppc6xx_defconfig. The mips error shown is a memblock_free argument mismatch ('passing argument 1 of memblock_free makes integer from pointer without a cast'), not a tlb-r4k message as the dossier text implied.",
              "The LKFT 6.1.136-rc1 report (mail-archive msg2589428) as fetched shows only x86_64 clang-nightly lines. LoongArch is not mentioned there, so whether LoongArch was in the pre-release matrix remains unknown.",
              "The KernelCI blog (12 Sep 2024) says 'We now support over 220 build configurations' and does not mention stable-rc or per-patch config selection. TuxSuite docs say plan files are hand-written YAML ('The plan file describes the list of builds along with the tests to run for each successful build'). TuxMake picks configs only via --kconfig-add, and I found no patch-based config selection in either.",
              "docs.kernel.org stable-kernel-rules, as fetched, mentions no build-testing, config or architecture requirement. It names AUTOSEL and Fixes-tag scripts for backport selection, and says -stable-rc is 'to be consumed by CI systems'."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "limitation",
            "summary": "The gap is real but thin, and the claim that the problem is open mostly fails. Hand-built CI matrices (Roeck, LKFT/TuxSuite, KernelCI) plus patch-dropping at -rc already catch most stable build breakage, and in every incident I re-read the cause was upstream of Kbuild object selection (missing prerequisites, gcc-8 or clang-nightly, WERROR). Nothing I opened (TuxSuite, TuxMake, KernelCI, the stable rules) maps a backport's touched objects to the builds that compile them. klocalizer and koverage come closest but work at line level, one architecture per run, with no stable evaluation. I found no evidence of kfold-style coverage being used, and no data on how often a touched object falls outside the matrix. Recommendation: do not use D3 as a motivating problem or as 'solved a gap'. Mention it as a limitation: kfold would not have prevented any examined incident, and it has no non-x86 or old-tree runs. The v7.2.7..v7.2.8 coverage table can stay as a small supporting analysis. What kfold can honestly claim here is limited to compared, measured properties: object-level build conditions in about a minute for a whole tree, the why chain, and patch-level multi-file reporting. If that table is kept, run klocalizer on it as a head-to-head. Never say 'first'. New adjacent prior art found: PortGPT (arXiv 2510.22396, LLM patch backporting using compiler feedback) and PatchLens (arXiv 2606.25863, FSE 2026, static recovery of configuration predicates for patches with lightweight build-system analysis). Cite both in related work.",
            "sources_checked": [
              {
