          },
          {
            "lens": "FIT AND EXPERIMENT DESIGNER for D3 (stable/backport build breakage in specific arch/config pairs)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "FIT. Every incident with a visible cause is not Kbuild object selection: 5.15.197-rc1 (toolchain C99 gcc-8; mips fails in 22 of 22 configs), 6.1.136-rc1 s390 (missing prerequisite commits named by the tester), 6.12.53-rc1 s390 bpf_jit_comp.c (missing prerequisites), 6.1.137 LoongArch (backport error in arch/loongarch/mm/hugetlbpage.c, LWN says 'Fix build error due to backport'), 6.1.165 (backport received only part of a patch series). The (arch, config) pair is the detection surface, not the cause. kfold cannot predict, explain, or prevent any of these; claiming otherwise would be the main reviewer objection.",
              "The Mar 2026 incident should not be labeled a CONFIG_WERROR problem. I re-opened lkml 2603.0/07179: the quoted error is 'implicit declaration of function ima_validate_range [-Werror=implicit-function-declaration]' at arch/x86/kernel/setup.c:380. Kbuild enables -Werror=implicit-function-declaration unconditionally (my knowledge of the Kbuild flags, not verified in this session), so WERROR is probably not the trigger. arch/x86/kernel/setup.c is always built on x86, so object selection is irrelevant; the likely mechanism is a declaration missing or gated in a header, i.e. a missing-prerequisite or preprocessor/Kconfig-header problem (inference, unverified). I did not re-open 2603.0/07304, the source of the WERROR quote.",
              "The dossier says the 6.1.137 LoongArch fix is 2 lines; my fetch (through a summarizer) said one line changed in arch/loongarch/mm/hugetlbpage.c. Minor, medium confidence. LWN 1061524 does not use the words 'build-fix' for the Mar 2026 releases; it says an upgrade is 'only necessary for those who have observed a build failure with the 6.12.75, 6.6.128, or 6.1.165 kernels' and gives no cause. The count 'three build-fix-only episodes in 14 months' is therefore an interpretation, a lower bound, and must be worded carefully.",
              "The coverage question (which tested builds compile each touched object, and which objects no build compiles) is real but already answerable without kfold for any config that was built: the .o inventory of a finished build is the oracle. kfold adds value only (a) for configs that are not built (prediction from the .config in seconds), and (b) for uncovered objects (a verified config via config-for). The experiment must be framed on (a) and (b), not on the union-coverage table alone.",
              "Existing assets already show what the residual looks like (results/devtasks/config_for.json, base = x86-64 defconfig, 402 commits). 384 rows succeed; 95 of those have an empty fragment (all touched objects already built by defconfig); 289 need a non-empty fragment (median 2 options, max 26). 18 rows fail: about 7 'no requested object can be built' (excluded objects whose files are non-x86 platform drivers such as mmci, exynos-pmu, sunvdc, hp_sdc, k3-udma-glue, phy-rcar-gen3-usb2), and about 11 'no input mapped to an object kfold knows' (arch/arm64, arch/powerpc, arch/mips files, two tools/testing/selftests files, and kernel/sched/ext/ext.c, which is probably included from a unity-build file, kfold gap to note, unverified). So roughly 72% of commits (289/402) are NOT covered by a defconfig-only matrix, and about 4.5% (18/402) are outside what an x86 run can judge. This is a measured fact from local files; the number for allmodconfig and the four-build union is still to be computed.",
              "Mapping touched .c to .o is object-level. A touched .c that is built may still have its changed lines under an #ifdef that no build compiles; kfold does not see this. Header-only commits (headers_not_compiled lists them) cannot be judged by object selection."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Replace 'trigger is a global option (WERROR)' for Mar 2026 with 'a missing declaration/prerequisite in a backported series; WERROR role unverified'.",
              "Say 'LWN reports releases whose notes say an upgrade is only necessary for those who hit a build failure', not 'LWN reports build-fix-only releases' for Mar 2026.",
              "6.1.137 LoongArch diff size: one line per my fetch, two per dossier; avoid citing the size.",
              "Do not write 'three episodes in about 14 months' as a rate; it is a count of those found, with unknown causes for two.",
              "The proposed experiment in the dossier is correct in spirit but omits the key baseline question (the inventory already gives union coverage) and the prediction-accuracy check; both are added below."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "limitation",
            "summary": "VERDICT: not an object-selection problem; a missing-prerequisite, toolchain and header/preprocessor problem that is detected per (arch, config). Only a thin coverage question remains for kfold. Do not make it an RQ3 task and do not use it as introduction motivation; give it one or two sentences in the limitations or discussion, and keep the measured v7.2.7..v7.2.8 data under the existing config-for/RQ3 patch-to-config task (framed as 'configuration for a patch', not 'stable breakage').\n\nEXPERIMENT (single strongest, assets only, about 3-4 person-days, no new builds, one process at a time per CLAUDE.md):\n\nInputs: results/devtasks/config_for.json (402 commits v7.2.7..v7.2.8, base defconfig, 384 solved, 18 failed); the four object inventories in evidence/inventories (i386 tinyconfig, x86-64 defconfig, Debian, x86-64 allmodconfig) with their .config files in evidence/configs; the kfold build conditions for Linux 7.2.8 (one whole-tree run, about a minute); results/devtasks/config_for_compile.json (olddefconfig plus object compilation checks); results/devtasks/why.json and results/agreement/linux.full.json (400 why verdicts) for the accuracy baseline.\n\nStep 1 (prediction accuracy, the part that needs kfold). For every touched object of the 402 commits (about 465 objects in the solved rows), evaluate the kfold build condition under each of the four .config files and compare with the real inventory of that build. Metric: per-(object, config) agreement, with every disagreement classified (kfold bug, unity-build/include, inventory artifact). This is real ground truth that exists. Baseline: the same question answered by (i) a grep of the Makefile for the CONFIG symbol in obj-$(CONFIG_X) lines (naive developer method) and (ii) the saved Kmax result (results/kmax/linux.json) where it covers the object. Expected form: 'kfold agrees on X of N pairs (99.x%), grep-style on Y, Kmax on Z'.\n\nStep 2 (coverage table, descriptive). For each commit classify by smallest set that builds all its touched objects: defconfig only; allmodconfig only; union of the four; none of the four but solvable by config-for under K (record the number of changed options and whether olddefconfig plus compile verified it); unsolvable on x86 (non-x86 arch, excluded platform driver, unmapped). Known anchors from existing files: 95 of 402 are already covered by defconfig, 289 need extra options (median 2), 18 are unsolvable on x86. Headline form: 'a defconfig-only matrix leaves 72% of the update's commits uncompiled; allmodconfig leaves a%; the four-build matrix leaves b% (n commits); config-for closes c of them with median d changed options; 4.5% are other-arch and need per-arch runs'. Do not call this a bug-finding result.\n\nStep 3 (cheapest extra builds). Greedy set cover over the residual commits: concatenate the residual patches into one config-for call, split when unsatisfiable under K. Metric: number of extra configs needed versus one per commit (k extra builds instead of r). Spot check 20 residual objects with the why chain and the existing compile results.\n\nStep 4 (optional, only if the effort budget allows): run klocalizer single-process on 30 residual commits for a head-to-head on config size and success; skip if it needs more than one day.\n\nFalsification: the D3 coverage claim is dropped if (a) allmodconfig, or the four-build union, already covers at least 97% of solvable commits and every residual object is non-x86 or unsolvable (then x86 coverage is a non-problem), or (b) kfold disagrees with the real inventories on more than about 1% of touched-object pairs without a documented, fixable cause (then prediction without building is not supported). A third falsifier for the claim 'config-for helps': verified-compile success of the generated configs below the 384-solved/compiled rate already measured.\n\nGround truth check: no unavailable ground truth is needed because the inventories and compile results are built artifacts. What is NOT available and must not be claimed: which commits would have broken a build, which stable-rc matrix members exist for old trees, and any 5.15/6.1 results (no builds; kfold entry settings for older Kbuild unverified).\n\nTHREATS TO VALIDITY: (1) v7.2.7..7.2.8 is a mainline-era stable update on one tree, not an old-tree backport; Kbuild is the same as mainline, so it says nothing about 5.15/6.1. (2) The four x86 builds are a proxy, not the real LKFT/Roeck matrix (about 155 to 400 builds across arches); the true matrix covers far more. (3) The union inventory gives coverage without kfold, so the table alone cannot demonstrate kfold; Step 1 and the config-for step carry the claim. (4) Object-level coverage ignores #ifdef inside files; a built object can still have uncompiled changed lines. (5) Header-only commits and unity-build files (kernel/sched/ext/ext.c) are outside the mapping. (6) The 18 unsolvable commits (about 4.5%) show kfold is x86-only here; a per-arch Kconfig model is not available. (7) Circularity: config-for results and the evaluation share the same kfold conditions; independence comes only from the real inventories and olddefconfig/compile checks. (8) The external incident evidence is anecdotal, comes from four source groups (LKFT, Roeck, LWN, a few LKML threads), and kfold would have prevented none of the examined incidents.\n\nPLDI-REVIEWER REASONING FOR PLACEMENT: As RQ3 task or intro motivation, reviewers will say 'the faults are missing prerequisites and toolchains, not Kbuild', 'the -rc matrix plus dropping already works', 'stable rules impose no coverage requirement', and 'no old-tree evaluation'. As related work, it adds little beyond what Kmax/klocalizer/koverage coverage already cover (those were evaluated only on mainline patches per the krepair paper). As a limitation or discussion sentence it is honest and cheap: 'kfold tells which objects a configuration compiles; it does not detect missing prerequisites, toolchain or preprocessor faults, as the recent stable build-fix releases illustrate'. The data (95/402 covered by defconfig, 289 need a median of 2 extra options, 18 unsolvable on x86) are already usable inside the config-for RQ3 evaluation without referring to stable breakage as a motivation. Confidence is low-to-moderate: the web claims I re-opened (LWN 1061524, LWN 1020133, lkml 2603.0/07179) came through a summarizer; local counts were read directly from results/devtasks/config_for.json.\"",
            "sources_checked": [
