      },
      {
        "id": "D4",
        "name": "Blind spots: objects that no standard or CI configuration ever builds",
        "dive": {
          "id": "D4",
          "name": "Blind spots: objects that no standard or CI configuration ever builds",
          "refined_description": "Problem statement. In Kbuild-based projects, \"tested\" in practice means \"built under a few named configurations\" (x86 defconfig, allmodconfig or allyesconfig, a CI matrix, sampled randconfigs). Whether a given object file is built by none of them is not reported by any tool we found; it is found by hand, one driver at a time, usually when a maintainer happens to notice. Consequences seen in the evidence: code lands in the tree that no bot compiles; a bug sits for months because the guarding option is off in the fuzzer's config; a driver is later deleted because \"no board enables it\". The precise question for kfold: given a tree and a user-supplied set of configurations (a CI matrix, the standard x86 trio, a board list), which objects have a build condition that no configuration in the set satisfies, why (which conjunct fails), and which MAINTAINERS entry owns them.\n\nScope and honesty notes. (1) Search budget: WebSearch was exhausted after 2 successful queries in this deep dive (both returned only 2010-2015 material), so the requested 15 new web searches were NOT run. As a substitute I ran 7 text searches through mail-archive.com's own search pages (linux-kernel: \"enable compile testing\" 204 matches, \"never built\" driver 30, \"compile testing\"+\"build coverage\" 36, \"isn't built by any\" 0; u-boot: \"not enabled by any\" 51, \"build coverage\" 227; barebox: \"build coverage\"; coreboot: \"not used by any board\" 0). These are word-matching counts, not curated, and are used only as frequency impressions. (2) I re-opened about 25 pages and 2 PDFs (Tartler PLOS 2011, Gazzillo OSS 2022 slides, read as images) this session; each evidence item says whether it was re-opened. Items carried forward from earlier sweeps and not re-opened are labelled in existing_solutions or risks. (3) Several LKML/LWN quotes came back through the fetch summarizer; I kept only short strings that the tool returned in quotation marks. (4) The 2025-26 Hovold patches are the best independent recent signal: at least three drivers (bcm2835 dmaengine, tegra iommu, k3-socinfo) were outside compile-test coverage until one person noticed; this shows existence, not prevalence. (5) Counter-evidence is real: maintainers' own gates are allmodconfig/allyesconfig (netdev doc, submit-checklist), Torvalds describes his own testing as configuration-limited but still relies on it, and many blind spots are intentional (arch-specific, mutually exclusive, hardware-only) rather than neglect. Evidence for maintainer demand for a tool is anecdotal; no maintainer survey on build coverage exists that we could find.\n\nOwn-data fact (not web evidence). The saved blindspots run (results/devtasks/blindspots.json, Linux 7.2.8) used only three x86 configurations (allmodconfig, allyesconfig, defconfig): 3,209 of 32,877 objects (9.8%) are built by none of them, spread over 510 MAINTAINERS entries. Of the 3,209 unique objects, 349 carry a non-x86 arch symbol in their condition (trivially expected), 2,196 are labelled \"not enabled by any provided config (needs CONFIG_X=...)\", and 664 have no single pivot option. This is \"not built by three x86 configs\", not \"never built by any config\"; it must not be quoted as an effect size. The interesting subset is objects reachable on x86 under the Kconfig model that allyesconfig still misses (Kconfig choices, promptless options, ARCH_*-gated drivers without COMPILE_TEST).",
          "who_has_it": "Subsystem maintainers of drivers/SoC/media/sound/clk/irqchip trees (the Hovold, Golaszewski, Kitt, Herring patches); linux-next and top-level integrators (Rothwell, Torvalds); CI and bot operators choosing configuration matrices (0-day, KernelCI, LKFT, syzbot); build-testers such as Bergmann and Roeck; maintainers of other Kbuild-style projects with board/defconfig-based builds (U-Boot, barebox, coreboot, BusyBox) who currently remove or cover code by hand; static-analysis operators (Coverity sees only what builds on x86); downstream users who want to know whether a component is built.",
          "evidence": [
            {
              "claim": "Hovold campaign: a dmaengine driver was never in compile-test coverage; a one-line patch enabling COMPILE_TEST 'for wider build coverage' was applied by the subsystem maintainer (Vinod Koul, applied as d3e1935fba8d). Page gives no reason it was missed earlier.",
              "quote": "There seems to be nothing preventing the driver from being compile tested so enable that for wider build coverage.",
              "url": "https://lkml.iu.edu/2511.2/04659.html",
              "date": "2025-11-22",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "Hovold compile-testing patch series 2025-26",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Same campaign, Tegra IOMMU driver; patch posted 2025-09-25, Joerg Roedel (IOMMU maintainer) replied 'Applied, thanks.' on 2025-10-27. Independent subsystem, same author.",
              "quote": "nothing preventing this driver from being compile tested so enable that to increase build coverage.",
              "url": "https://lkml.iu.edu/2510.3/03324.html",
              "date": "2025-10-27",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "Hovold compile-testing patch series 2025-26",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
