          ],
          "existing_solutions": [
            {
              "tool_or_process": "COMPILE_TEST (|| COMPILE_TEST dependency convention)",
              "what_it_does": "Lets drivers for exotic hardware be enabled on common architectures so allmodconfig/allyesconfig and bots compile them. Maintainers add it by hand (Hovold 2025-26, Golaszewski 2024-25, Kozlowski 2016-2020, Davis 2024).",
              "limits": "Opt-in per driver; no report of which drivers lack it; can itself fail (Octeon choice case, k3-socinfo promptless option). A mail-archive text search for 'enable compile testing' on linux-kernel gives 204 matches (word match, not a count of patches).",
              "url": "https://docs.kernel.org/kbuild/kconfig-language.html"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "allmodconfig / allyesconfig on one or few architectures (Torvalds, Rothwell daily linux-next, netdev policy, submit-checklist)",
              "what_it_does": "Cheap proxy for 'builds the most code'; the accepted gate for patches and merges.",
              "limits": "Mutually exclusive options, choices, other architectures, promptless options; Tartler measured 78.49% of selectable #ifdef blocks for allyesconfig on 2.6.35 x86 (block level, old). Torvalds calls his testing very limited in the configuration sense.",
              "url": "https://lkml.iu.edu/2502.3/06618.html"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Intel 0-day kernel test robot (many randconfigs plus allno/allyes/allmod/def)",
              "what_it_does": "Post-hoc build bot over sampled configurations; of 36,115 reports in the krepair study 63% were randconfig.",
              "limits": "Sampling, delayed, no per-object coverage statement; reports list 'configs tested/skipped' counts only (report format carried forward from earlier sweeps, not re-opened this session).",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.17966v1"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Bergmann's private randconfig loop (talk page dated 2026-09-28, Kernel Recipes account)",
              "what_it_does": "15 years of custom linux-next build testing with random configurations; claims thousands of bugs fixed.",
              "limits": "Stochastic exploration; page gives no coverage measure. Page re-opened: it names no conference and gives only the one-sentence description plus slide fragments.",
              "url": "https://speakerdeck.com/ennael/large-scale-kernel-build-testing-on-a-desktop"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "KernelCI (220+ build configurations per Sept 2024 blog), TuxSuite/LKFT matrices, syzbot hand-crafted configs",
              "what_it_does": "Hand-written YAML config lists and fragments built in the cloud.",
              "limits": "No statement of which objects the matrix leaves unbuilt; syzbot config staleness caused the multi-month leak in the krepair motivating example (KernelCI blog carried forward, not re-opened this session).",
              "url": "https://kernelci.org/blog/2024/09/12/bring-your-tree-to-the-new-kernelci/"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Kmax suite (kextract/kclause formulas, klocalizer, koverage, kismet)",
              "what_it_does": "Per-compilation-unit Kbuild constraints; klocalizer builds a config that includes a unit or repairs a config for a patch; koverage reports 'how much of patch is covered by a given config file'. CIP kernel-cve-triage queries Kmax formulas per object (carried forward, not re-opened).",
              "limits": "README and PyPI (re-opened) describe patch-scoped coverage; no feature reports whole-tree objects unbuilt by a config set, grouped by owner. The formulas could feed such a report (a script over satisfiability per unit would do it), so kfold's claim must be about the report and its explanations, not about a new capability in principle. Docs say extracted formulas may not be exact.",
              "url": "https://pypi.org/project/kmax/"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "undertaker / Vampyr (Tartler, Dietrich, Hengelein)",
              "what_it_does": "Dead-block detection and partial configurations to cover #ifdef blocks (94.40% vs 78.49% for allyesconfig on 2.6.35 x86).",
              "limits": "Block-level, built around an extracted Kconfig model of the 2010s; described as unmaintained in the krepair related work (carried forward, not re-opened). Not a per-object report by owner.",
              "url": "https://www.sigops.org/s/conferences/sosp/2011/workshops/plos/02-tartler.pdf"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "U-Boot buildman/qconfig and CI (builds every board defconfig; qconfig queries CONFIG combinations across defconfigs); barebox sandbox allyesconfig CI",
              "what_it_does": "Board-complete CI, and a config database to find boards with a given option; barebox added allyesconfig in 2025 to compile otherwise unbuilt drivers (barebox part re-opened).",
              "limits": "One defconfig per board; docs describe no mapping from source object to boards nor a report of options no board enables (U-Boot docs from earlier sweep, not re-opened this session). Removal of unused drivers is done by hand (Rini 2022).",
              "url": "https://docs.u-boot-project.org/en/latest/build/buildman.html"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "FreeBSD LINT kernel generated from sys/conf/NOTES",
              "what_it_does": "A configuration that compiles all kernel code in one go for QA, at the cost of not being runnable.",
              "limits": "Not re-opened this session; FreeBSD only, no per-object report.",
              "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/freebsd/freebsd-src/main/sys/conf/NOTES"
            }
          ],
          "remaining_gap": "No tool we found, in the pages we opened, takes a tree plus a set of configurations and reports, per object, that no configuration builds it, names the failing conjunct, and groups the result by maintainer entry. Today the finding is manual and anecdotal (Hovold, Golaszewski, Kitt, Rini, Simek). Still unknown: (a) how many Linux objects are reachable under the Kconfig model on some architecture yet missed by allyesconfig plus COMPILE_TEST plus the 0-day/KernelCI matrices (the actionable effect size); (b) whether maintainers want such a report (no survey; demand is inferred from patches and two summit remarks; maintainers who reject compile-only evidence were seen in earlier sweeps but not re-opened); (c) how much of the blind-spot set is intentional (arch- or hardware-gated, BROKEN, staging) versus neglect; (d) architecture handling: the saved run covers x86 configs only.",
          "kfold_contribution": "Modest and specific: a per-object report over a user-supplied configuration set that lists objects whose Kbuild condition no configuration satisfies, names the failing conjunct (the existing 'why' chain, e.g. 'needs CONFIG_ACLINT_SSWI=y'), and groups by MAINTAINERS entry so owners can add COMPILE_TEST or a CI configuration. Conditions come from whole-tree symbolic execution, so the denominator (all objects) is exact for Kbuild, which sampling cannot give. It also applies to U-Boot, barebox, coreboot and BusyBox, where Glass's 2012 question and Rini's by-hand removals show the need. It does not claim a new capability over Kmax in principle; it does not look at #ifdefs inside files, Kconfig defects (the k3-socinfo root cause), or runtime behaviour; and an object being unbuilt is not a bug.",
          "quantitative_facts": [
            "Own data (Linux 7.2.8, results/devtasks/blindspots.json, three x86 configs only): 3,209 of 32,877 objects (9.8%) built by none of allmodconfig, allyesconfig, defconfig; 510 MAINTAINERS entries have at least one; unique-object split: 349 non-x86 arch symbol, 2,196 'not enabled by any provided config', 664 no single pivot option. Not an effect size for 'never built'. Examples by entry: IRQCHIP DRIVERS 115 of 152 objects (75.7%), ARM/QUALCOMM 138 of 590 (23.4%), COMMON CLK FRAMEWORK 119 of 465 (25.6%).",
            "Own data: experiments/STATUS.md reports Linux whole-tree median 138.7 s on an idle machine; use the measured value rather than the task's 'about a minute'.",
            "krepair paper: 36,115 0-day build reports (2019-10 to 2022-08) were 63% randconfig (22,702), 15% allyesconfig (5,551), 7% defconfig (2,708), 7% allmodconfig (2,446).",
            "OSS 2022 slides: v5.13, 13,411 commits; defconfig covers 1,688, one randconfig 2,189; not covered 8,853 and 8,333; 1,296 non-x86 and 1,177 header-only excluded.",
            "Tartler 2011: Linux 2.6.35 x86, 10,365 .c files analysed, 3,163 with variability; allyesconfig selects 11,511 of 14,666 selectable blocks (78.49%), undertaker coverage 94.40%, 1,778 dead blocks.",
            "LWN 2025-09-10: 32,468 Kconfig options on x86_64 (6.16).",
            "Mail-archive text-search counts (word matching, not curated): linux-kernel 'enable compile testing' 204; 'compile testing' + 'build coverage' 36; u-boot 'build coverage' 227; u-boot 'not enabled by any' 51 (first nine are the 2022 Rini removals).",
            "Hovold campaign: three drivers re-opened (bcm2835 dmaengine 2025-11-22, tegra iommu 2025-10-27, k3-socinfo fix posted 2025-11-27, still pending 2026-01-14), plus the meson-sm default fix of 2025-07-25."
          ],
          "proposed_experiment": "Propose only, not run here. On Linux 7.2.8 with the existing blindspots output (3,209 objects blind to allmodconfig, allyesconfig and x86 defconfig), classify each object with the Kconfig model K into: (a) arch-gated (needs a non-x86 architecture symbol; 349 by the current label), (b) unreachable under K on x86 even with COMPILE_TEST (candidate Kconfig defects or dead options, the k3-socinfo shape), (c) reachable on x86 under K but absent from the three configs (actionable: COMPILE_TEST or CI-config candidates, e.g. choices and ARCH_*-gated drivers). Report counts per class and per MAINTAINERS entry. Then validate a random sample of class (c) (for example 40 objects, same harness as the 384/402 config-for run): produce a config with config-for, run olddefconfig, compile the object, and check it. Run strictly one process at a time on this machine. Secondary and cheaper: U-Boot blindspots over all board defconfigs (needs defconfig expansion, not builds) to answer Glass's 2012 question, cross-checked against the objects Rini removed in 2022 and Simek in 2025.",
          "risks": "1) Blind spot is not never built: randconfig plus forced COMPILE_TEST sampling over months (Bergmann, 0-day) and distro/vendor configs may cover many of the 2,196 objects; the report is relative to the config set given. 2) Arch conflation: the saved run uses x86 configs only, so many blind spots are simply other architectures; presenting 3,209 or 9.8% as the headline would be misleading. 3) Intentional dead code, BROKEN, staging, hardware-only drivers, and maintainers who reject compile-only evidence mean 'unbuilt' often should stay unbuilt; demand for the report is shown only by anecdotes and two summit remarks, with no maintainer survey. 4) Kconfig root causes (k3-socinfo, choices) are outside kfold's Kbuild model; its Kconfig model is an over-approximation, so the (b)/(c) separation inherits that error. 5) Kmax formulas could produce the same report with a short script, so novelty must rest on the report, explanations, ownership grouping and non-Linux projects, not on feasibility; a head-to-head on the same objects is advisable. 6) Search shortfall: only 2 WebSearch queries succeeded; independent 2023-2026 sources beyond the mail archives, LWN and project docs, and any survey data, were not searched for. 7) Quotes from LKML/LWN pages passed through a fetch summarizer; the Tartler and OSS 2022 numbers were read from page images and are the most reliable. Tartler's metric is #ifdef-block level and 2010-era, finer-grained than objects. 8) Items carried forward and not re-opened (0-day report format, KernelCI blog, U-Boot buildman and CI docs, FreeBSD LINT, undertaker maintenance status) must be re-checked before citing.",
          "confidence": "medium"
        },
        "verdicts": [
          {
            "lens": "EVIDENCE AUDITOR (D4: blind spots, objects no standard or CI configuration builds)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "Quotes held up. I re-opened 21 pages and 2 PDFs (the Gazzillo OSS 2022 slides and the Tartler PLOS 2011 paper, both read as page images). All quoted strings in the dossier's evidence items are verbatim or correctly split fragments, the dates match, and the numbers (36,115 reports and the percentages, the OSS 2022 bar chart, Tartler Table 1, 32,468 options) are correct. The weakness is what the evidence proves, not whether the quotes are real.",
              "Existence versus prevalence. The pile of Hovold, Golaszewski, Kitt, Rini, Simek and Fatoum anecdotes shows that blind-spot objects exist and that people fix them by hand. Nothing in the dossier measures how common this is among objects that are reachable and meant to be built. The only whole-tree number is the dossier's own 9.8% (3,209 of 32,877 objects) for three x86 configs, which the dossier itself warns not to quote as an effect size. Most of the real-world anecdotes are ARCH-gated drivers (bcm2835, Tegra, k3, Octeon, pic32, rb4xx, rt2x00). Those are built by the arm or mips configs that their own maintainers use. They are outside the x86 trio, not outside every configuration. The D4 headline should be 'not built by the given config set', never 'never built'.",
              "Over-read: 'we have \"allyesconfig\" for that'. The sentence is in Hovold's meson-sm 'fix compile-test default' patch (Reviewed-by Neil Armstrong, applied to the Amlogic tree). Its full sentence is 'Enabling compile testing should not enable every individual driver (we have \"allyesconfig\" for that).' It is about the default value of a driver symbol under COMPILE_TEST. The dossier presents it as a community norm that allyesconfig is the build-coverage catch-all. That is a stretch from one commit message.",
              "Omitted counter-evidence in the krepair paper (arXiv 2404.17966). The paper reports that allyesconfig achieves 88.5% average patch coverage on its 507-patch linux-next sample, against 21.7% for defconfig and 29.2% for a single randconfig. Section 5.1 says that although mutual exclusion prevents covering all code, allyesconfig 'still covers the large majority of code (and therefore patches)'. The dossier cites only the 63%/15%/7%/7% report mix and the 'mutual exclusion' point. An honest reading is that allyesconfig, the standard gate, covers most code, so blind spots are a minority tail. A reviewer who knows this paper will notice the omission.",
              "Octeon thread is mischaracterized. In the thread (lkml.rescloud.iu.edu/2306.2/07959.html) Rob Herring's COMPILE_TEST patch broke mips:allmodconfig. Roeck explained that CONFIG_CAVIUM_OCTEON_SOC is a choice and mips:allmodconfig selects a different alternative. Le Moal's stated reason for dropping the patch is that he does not 'see how to allow COMPILE_TEST without CONFIG_CAVIUM_OCTEON_SOC being set'. The dossier says he dropped it 'as not worth the effort at rc7'. I could not verify that wording; the reason shown on the page is technical impossibility. The thread shows that a patch broke a build, not that a config silently missed code. It is weaker than the dossier presents it.",
              "Misattribution in G38. 'Let's seize the opportunity and add it to CI, so we easily detect breakage around allyesconfig in future.' is in Ahmad Fatoum's own cover letter (PATCH 0/6, 2025-03-26), not a maintainer welcoming the change. The maintainer's reply (Sascha Hauer, 2025-03-27) is just 'Applied, thanks!'. The cover letter also says 'With a one-liner fix remaining, sandbox now builds allyesconfig sans SDL/libftdi to completion', so the 'had been broken and untracked' inference in the dossier is reasonable but is inference. The barebox page does not say that previously unbuilt drivers are now covered.",
              "Glass 2012 is a question, not a request for a tool. The message (msg98954, 2012-11-07) is Simon Glass's reply in a thread on '[PATCH 3/3] Add stricmp() and strnicmp()'. He asks 'is there any easy way to obtain build-coverage information for U-Boot?' and 'can we easily find code that is not enabled by any existing board?', then adds 'That might be an interesting investigation.' The dossier's 'asked for a tool' is too strong. G26 also gives the post date as 2012-11-03 (msg98556) while the evidence item says 2012-11-07. These need reconciling, and I did not open msg98556 or the Aribaud reply.",
              "Simek 2025 (msg550803) supports less than claimed. The patch is '[PATCH 2/3] fpga: Remove ancient ACEX1K support'. The reason is that code was ancient and not enabled by any real platform. It is a deletion of dead code, not a case of a bug surfacing in code CI never builds. Rini 2022 (msg436281) is verified, patch 1/10 of the net-driver removals, but the reason given also includes 'not converted to DM_ETH' (an obsolete-API removal, not a coverage problem).",
              "Independence. The evidence is a few clusters. (a) Hovold: one author, three drivers, all from the same 2025-26 campaign, so one source. (b) Gazzillo/Yildiran/Oh: the krepair paper and the OSS 2022 slides come from the same group and data lineage, so one source. The LWN Alden article (32,468 options, 'hard to tell how many configurations are covered') mostly restates their work. (c) Torvalds: the 2025 LKML post, the 2025 Maintainers Summit and the 2022 Maintainers Summit are the same person speaking in three settings. (d) U-Boot: Glass, Wood, Rini and Simek are different people over 13 years but all the same project. Counting distinct independent sources, the dossier has roughly 6 to 8, not 25.",
              "Torvalds quotes cut both ways. The Feb 2025 post says 'my build testing is *very* limited in the configuration sense', but the next clause is 'so if something fails to build for me, I think it's a pretty big failure', and he says allmodconfig on x86-64 is likely to compile the most code. The LWN 2025 line 'basic configurations just haven't been tested' is verbatim but is Jonathan Corbet's narration, not a Torvalds quote. The allmodconfig-hides-code clause is also Corbet's paraphrase. The 2022 line is Corbet reporting that Torvalds 'pointed out that this testing doesn't really cover drivers' about KernelCI linux-next testing, which is about test coverage, not build coverage. The G62/G63/G64 summaries must be re-verified or dropped.",
              "Numbers that came only through counts or summaries. The mail-archive match counts (204, 36, 227, 51, 2,372, 2,133, 68) are word-match counts over many years and include automated mail, so they have no value as frequency evidence and should not be cited. The Bergmann page (verified: dated 2026-09-28, '15 years', 'thousands of bugfixes') is a Speaker Deck description with no coverage measure, and no venue is named on the page. I did not re-open the 0-day report format, the KernelCI blog, U-Boot buildman/qconfig docs, FreeBSD LINT or the undertaker maintenance status, as the dossier already admits. The CI-config claims G34-G36 and G41 (U-Boot CI, barebox workflows) are also from summarizers.",
              "Tartler 2011 is correct but a weak fit. Verified in the PDF: Linux 2.6.35 x86, 10,365 analysed .c files, 3,163 with variability, 14,666 selectable blocks, allyesconfig selects 11,511 (78.49%), undertaker 94.40%, 1,778 dead blocks. Figure 1 shows allyesconfig CC in the 60-70% range over six years. It measures #ifdef blocks, not objects, and is 15 years old. The abstract says 78 percent, which is the corrected figure. It is a motivation sentence at best, and the paper also shows that most of what allyesconfig misses are #ifdef blocks, which kfold does not analyse.",
              "My one WebSearch found no study that lists files never compiled by any standard config. This is consistent with the dossier's 'no tool or measurement' claim but is absence of evidence from a single query. I found no source showing the problem is already handled by an existing tool, and none showing it is negligible. Kernel docs (kconfig-language, netdev) verified: they show COMPILE_TEST is the sanctioned manual mechanism and allyesconfig/allmodconfig are the mandatory patch gate ('At the very minimum your changes must survive an allyesconfig and an allmodconfig build with W=1'). That means maintainers treat the problem as handled at the patch level."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Add krepair's own numbers: allyesconfig 88.5% average patch coverage, defconfig 21.7%, single randconfig 29.2% (aggregate randconfig plateau about 74.4-74.6%), over 507 linux-next patches. State the coverage numbers for all configs, not just the 0-day report mix. The paper says mutual exclusion 'prevents coverage of all code' but allyesconfig 'still covers the large majority of code'.",
              "G38: attribute 'Let's seize the opportunity and add it to CI...' to Ahmad Fatoum's cover letter (2025-03-26). The maintainer's reply is Sascha Hauer's 'Applied, thanks!' (2025-03-27).",
              "Octeon (G0/G1): Damien Le Moal's stated reason is that he does not see how to allow COMPILE_TEST without CONFIG_CAVIUM_OCTEON_SOC being set. Rob Herring's patch caused the mips:allmodconfig failure. Drop the 'not worth the effort at rc7' paraphrase unless it can be sourced.",
              "Glass 2012: it is a question ('is there any easy way to obtain build-coverage information for U-Boot?'), posted in reply to a stricmp patch on 2012-11-07, not a request for a tool. Reconcile the 2012-11-03 (msg98556) date in G26 with 2012-11-07 (msg98954). The Aribaud second is unverified.",
              "Hovold meson-sm: the sentence 'we have allyesconfig for that' concerns whether COMPILE_TEST should default-enable drivers, not a general coverage policy. Reword the dossier claim accordingly.",
              "LWN 1050027: 'basic configurations just haven't been tested' and the allmodconfig-hides-code clause are Corbet's narration. The only direct Torvalds-voiced fragments are the 'weird bugs' remark and 'some repositories ... no testing at all' (the second is a summarizer paraphrase, not verified as a quote). The 2022 LWN item is about KernelCI linux-next testing not covering drivers (a test-coverage statement).",
              "Simek 2025: patch title is 'fpga: Remove ancient ACEX1K support'. Describe it as removal of ancient code, not as a bug found in unbuilt code.",
              "Drop the mail-archive text-search counts (204/36/227/51/2,372/2,133/68) from anything quotable. They are not curated and cover many years.",
              "Dossier item 'x-ref Hovold covers three independent subsystems' should be counted as one source (one author, one campaign) in any 'independent sources' claim.",
              "Own-data note in the dossier already says to use the measured 138.7 s median rather than 'about a minute'. Keep that. The 9.8% figure must always carry 'by three x86 configs'."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "motivation",
            "summary": "The dossier's evidence is real and well sourced: every quote I checked (21 pages and 2 PDFs re-opened) is verbatim, and the dates and statistics are correct. The strongest items are the Hovold/Golaszewski/Kitt anecdotes, the Gazzillo OSS 2022 numbers, the Tartler numbers and the U-Boot/barebox threads. It is still only 'partly' sufficient. It proves that unbuilt objects exist and are fixed by hand, not how many matter. The independent sources number about 6 to 8, not 25. The dossier omits krepair's counter-number (allyesconfig reaches 88.5% patch coverage and covers 'the large majority of code'). Several anecdotes are ARCH-gated drivers that other architectures' configs do build. A few quotes are misattributed or over-read: the barebox 'seize the opportunity' line, Le Moal's reason for dropping the Octeon patch, 'allyesconfig for that', Glass's question and Simek's deletion. Use D4 as a motivation paragraph worded as 'a few named configs build most, but not all, code; maintainers patch the gaps by hand; no report exists for a given config set', backed by the Gazzillo/krepair numbers and 4-5 named threads. Do not use mail-archive counts. Do not present 9.8% or 3,209 as 'never built'. The needed new evidence is the proposed experiment that separates arch-gated, Kconfig-unreachable and actionable-on-x86 objects. A maintainer demand claim stays unsupported by any survey.\"",
            "sources_checked": [
