          },
          {
            "lens": "HOSTILE COMPETITOR ANALYST, D4 blind spots (objects that no standard or CI configuration builds)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "NOT SOLVED AS A PRODUCT, BUT THE PRIMITIVES EXIST. Kmax docs (docs/advanced.md, opened) state: koverage 'checks only the (file, line) targets you supply' via '--check FILE:[LINES]' plus '--check-patch', returns per-target INCLUDED / LINE_EXCLUDED_FILE_INCLUDED / FILE_EXCLUDED / TIMEOUT_* codes, and writes JSON. So 'is this file built by this .config' is already a shipped query. A blindspots report is a loop over all units and all configs in a CI matrix. The docs describe no whole-tree mode and no 'unbuilt by a config set' option (read via WebFetch summarizer, so the absence is the summarizer's reading, not a verbatim doc statement).",
              "klocalizer already has explanation-like features: '--show-unsat-core' (constraints causing unsatisfiability), '--view-kbuild' (Kbuild constraints for a unit and its subdirectories), '--report-all' (architectures where a unit can be configured), '--include FILE.o', '--approximate CONFIG', '--sample N'. kfold's 'why' chain (failing conjunct) therefore overlaps with the unsat core. The claim 'explanation chains are new' is weak. The honest difference is presentation (per-conjunct chain against a given .config) and that it is not an unsat core over SuperC/Kconfig-extended formulas. Nobody has compared the two outputs on the same objects.",
              "CIP kernel-cve-triage (gitlab README, opened) already uses Kmax formulas to run per-file 'is it compiled under this config' checks: 'check_introduced_by_file_compiled' and 'check_fixed_by_file_compiled', with reason 'no file is compiled'. It is the only external consumer of the per-object build-condition primitive that I found. It works for one config and a CVE list, not a tree-wide report. Its own limits: first run on a revision is 'computationally expensive', non-x86 needs ARCH/CROSS_COMPILE, 'some architecture specific objects will fail to build', and it is 'tailored to CIP's use case'.",
              "PatchLens (SBES 2026, Sept 8-11, opened as PDF, read as images) is the closest conceptual competitor to kfold's Makefile analysis: it has a Linux/Kbuild resolver that, for a target file, 'finds the nearest build file, extracts the local predicate that includes the corresponding object, and then ascends through parent directories to collect directory-level inclusion conditions'. Its output is a Boolean Impact Condition per patch. On 1,192 Linux patches it succeeded for 1,057 (88.67%) at about 47.4 ms average per patch. Its Table 1 says Kmax is 'File-level Presence Condition' and PatchLens is 'Patch-level Impact Condition'. It is MIT-licensed, on GitHub (fleap-dev/patchlens) and Zenodo. It does not do whole-tree blindspots or MAINTAINERS grouping (not claimed in the paper), but it overlaps the 'object condition for a path' step and the 'configuration-aware CI' application ('If a job's configuration does not satisfy the Impact Condition, the job ... can be deprioritized or skipped'). That is the inverse use of blindspots (CI skip rather than CI gap).",
              "SPEED IS NOT A SAFE CLAIM AGAINST KMAX. The survey reports Kmax FSE 2017 at a mean 84.15 s on x86 (slides, carried forward from the earlier sweep, not re-opened this session; paper URL fetches failed: paulgazzillo.com/papers/fse17.pdf 404, dl.acm.org 403). kfold's own median on the whole tree is 138.7 s (STATUS.md). The two numbers come from different versions, hardware and tree sizes. Do not claim faster without a same-machine run. kmax 4.10 (released Aug 18, 2026 per PyPI page) caches formulas, so a fair comparison must separate cold and warm runs.",
              "ADOPTION OF KMAX IS REAL BUT NARROW. The GitHub README (opened) shows 72 stars, 29 forks, 77 open issues, 1,289 commits, and thanks Intel 0-day for 'valuable feedback' and says they worked to 'include kismet into the kernel test robot'. LWN (Alden, 2025-09-10, opened) says kmax 'has been integrated into the Intel 0-day test suite'. The robot mails I opened (lkml.iu.edu/2509.2/02067.html) show only 'configs tested: 12 / configs skipped: 119' plus 'More configs may be tested in the coming days', all buildonly-randconfig for i386 and x86_64, with no per-object statement. So the 0-day robot publishes no coverage claim, which supports the gap. But it is not evidence that anyone wants one.",
              "DEMAND EVIDENCE IS ANECDOTAL AND SOME OF IT CUTS AGAINST. Torvalds (LWN 2025-12-12, opened): 'basic configurations just haven't been tested', 'some repositories clearly are not seeing any testing at all', and allmodconfig builds 'cause some code to be disabled, and can thus hide problems'. Ojeda opposed canned configurations. Torvalds' proposed tooling is an email listing repositories disabled from linux-next, not a config-coverage report. krepair (arXiv 2404.17966, opened) says allyesconfig 'still covers the large majority of code (and therefore patches)', 88.5% average patch coverage, with a build of about 4 h. That deflates the idea that allyesconfig leaves large blind spots by line count. The Hovold, Golaszewski, Kitt, Rini and Simek patches show existence, not prevalence.",
              "NO CI/BUILD-FARM DOC I OPENED STATES WHICH FILES ARE UNBUILT. KernelCI blog (opened): 'We now support over 220 build configurations' and nothing about coverage. U-Boot buildman docs (opened): builds all or a subset of boards, reports failures and size changes, and does not report source files or CONFIG options built by no board. Its only related item is a TODO, 'checking commits for changed files and building only boards which use those files'. kernelconfig.io (opened) is a per-module CONFIG lookup by kernel version and architecture. It shows nothing about unbuilt objects. The syzbot coverage page (opened) lists 'Total', 'Repo Heatmap', 'Subsystems Heatmap' views. That is runtime kcov coverage, which is a different notion (it could in principle expose never-executed files, but I could not confirm whether it distinguishes unbuilt from unexecuted).",
              "KCONFIRM IS NOT A COMPETITOR HERE. The v4 cover letter (lkml.iu.edu/2607.3/03886.html, opened) lists dead code, constant conditions, invalid ranges, optional select-visible and dead links. It does not mention COMPILE_TEST or symbols that can never be selected. The k3-socinfo class (promptless option) is plausibly within its reach, but nothing I read says so. Treat as complementary Kconfig-level tooling.",
              "NOT CHECKED, WEB SEARCH BUDGET EXHAUSTED. WebSearch returned 'session budget used up' for both queries, so no new 2023-2026 discovery search ran. Not checked: ConfigFix beyond survey notes, cvehound (PyPI page failed to load), TuxSuite/tuxmake, Yocto, Debian and Fedora config tooling, LKFT, 0-day lkp-tests config lists, Linux Driver Verification, any unpublished klocalizer whole-tree script, GOLEM/KBuildMiner for unbuilt files. A hostile reviewer may know of one. The 'no tool found' claim must be phrased as 'in the documentation we read'.",
              "The dossier's own risks stand and are not undone: the 3,209 / 9.8% figure is relative to three x86 configs and must not be the headline. Many blind spots are intentional (arch, hardware, BROKEN, staging). The Kconfig model is an over-approximation, so classes (b)/(c) inherit error."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Dossier says 'no feature reports whole-tree objects unbuilt by a config set'. Refine to: Kmax docs (docs/advanced.md) describe koverage targets as file:line / patch only, and a whole-tree report is not documented. It is feasible by looping the existing '--check FILE' query over all units, so say 'not provided as a feature' not 'not possible'.",
              "Dossier says Kmax formulas 'may not be exact' as a general note. The verbatim doc line is 'The extracted formulas may not be exact.' (docs/advanced.md), and koverage's FILE_EXCLUDED comes with 'this might be a false alarm due to build issues'. Both are usable as quoted limits, but neither quantifies the error. Do not state Kmax precision as a number.",
              "Dossier says kfold's contribution includes an exact denominator, because whole-tree symbolic execution gives all objects. Kmax also produces per-unit constraints for the whole tree (that is its design, FSE 2017) and 'kreader' reads formulas for a directory. The denominator is not unique to kfold. What can be claimed is exactness relative to measured builds, if the 400 why verdicts and four-build inventories are compared with Kmax on the same objects (not yet done).",
              "Replace 'speed' as a differentiator with 'unmeasured against Kmax'. Kmax FSE 2017 reported 84.15 s on x86 (slides, carried forward), kfold's measured median is 138.7 s on a different tree and machine.",
              "The 'explanation chain' differentiator needs a caveat: klocalizer has '--show-unsat-core' and '--view-kbuild'.",
              "Add PatchLens (SBES 2026) to related work as a Kbuild-resolver tool that computes per-patch Impact Conditions from Makefiles without building. It is the nearest prior art to kfold's 'condition from Makefile' step and to the config-for patch front end. Do not write 'first to derive conditions from Makefiles'.",
              "krepair arXiv abstract wording and numbers I verified this session: allyesconfig 88.5% mean patch coverage and about 4 h build, single randconfig 29.2%, ten randconfigs about 74.6%, 36,115 reports with 63/15/7/7% split. These were summarizer output from the arXiv HTML (first 100,000 of 109,319 characters). Re-check the exact numbers in the paper before citing."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "rq3_task",
            "summary": "The problem is not solved by a product, but I cannot support a strong novelty claim. No tool I opened ships a tree-wide 'objects built by none of a given config set, grouped by owner' report. Intel 0-day mails give only 'configs tested: 12 / skipped: 119'. KernelCI says '220 build configurations'. U-Boot buildman has no coverage report. However, Kmax's koverage already answers 'is FILE included by this config' (FILE_EXCLUDED), klocalizer has --show-unsat-core, --view-kbuild and --report-all, CIP kernel-cve-triage runs per-file 'compiled' checks on Kmax formulas, and PatchLens derives Kbuild presence conditions per patch in about 47 ms. A short script over Kmax would produce the same blind-spot list, so the claim must rest on the report (whole tree, config set as input, failing-conjunct chain, MAINTAINERS grouping), on non-Linux Kbuild projects (U-Boot, barebox, coreboot, BusyBox; Glass 2012 and Rini 2022 show the by-hand status quo), and on a measured same-object comparison with Kmax, which is not yet done. Demand evidence is anecdotal (Hovold, Golaszewski, Kitt, Torvalds remarks) and krepair says allyesconfig covers 88.5% of patch lines, so the effect size is modest. Use this as a case-study task in the evaluation (with the (a)/(b)/(c) classification and a sampled validation), plus one motivation sentence. Do not use 3,209 / 9.8% as a headline. Do not claim speed over Kmax. Web search budget was exhausted, so discovery of newer competitors was not done.",
