          },
          {
            "lens": "Fit and experiment designer for D4 (blind spots: objects that no standard or CI configuration builds)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "FIT: the question 'which object files does this configuration set build, and which conjunct stops the others' is an object-selection question over Kbuild Makefiles, which is what kfold computes. The cause of many blind spots is not: Kconfig choices (Octeon), promptless options (k3-socinfo), arch gating and BROKEN/staging flags are Kconfig-specification or intent questions, and whether an unbuilt object matters is a human judgement. kfold supplies the list and the failing conjunct; it does not supply 'should be built'.",
              "The saved result (3,209 of 32,877 objects, 9.8%, 510 MAINTAINERS entries) is 'not built by three x86 configs', not 'never built'. 349 of the unique objects carry a non-x86 arch symbol. Quoting it as a headline will draw the first PLDI objection (arch conflation). I opened results/devtasks/blindspots.json: its top entry 'THE REST' lists objects such as crypto/crypto.o, crypto/crypto_algapi.o and .tmp_vmlinux1.btf.o labelled 'other', which look like composite or intermediate objects that Kbuild links rather than compiles independently. The 3,209 figure may therefore contain inventory noise and must be re-derived before any use.",
              "Ground truth: there is no ground truth for 'should be built', and none is needed if the claim is scoped. Real ground truth does exist for the 'built' side, because object inventories (evidence/inventories/linux/{allmodconfig,defconfig,debian,tinyconfig}.txt) come from real builds. Note that allyesconfig has a config file (evidence/configs/linux/allyesconfig.config) but no build inventory, so its side can only be checked by kfold evaluation or by compiling sampled objects.",
              "Novelty risk: Kmax formulas (kextract/kclause, saved run on Linux) plus a short script that tests satisfiability per unit under each config would give the same blind set. koverage already reports patch coverage by a config. The claim has to be the whole-tree report, the failing-conjunct explanation, grouping by owner, and use on U-Boot/barebox/coreboot/BusyBox, not a new capability. A head-to-head with Kmax formulas is required to defend this.",
              "Demand evidence is anecdotal (Hovold 2025-26 patches, Golaszewski, Kitt, Rini removals, Simek, Glass 2012) plus two summit remarks. I re-opened lkml.iu.edu/2511.2/04659.html (Hovold bcm2835 dmaengine: 'enable that for wider build coverage'), lkml.iu.edu/2502.3/06618.html (Torvalds: build testing 'very limited in the configuration sense', allmodconfig on x86-64) and arxiv 2404.17966v1 (leak 'remained in the kernel for months'; 0-day reports 63% randconfig, 15% allyesconfig, 7% defconfig, 7% allmodconfig). Prevalence is not shown. Counter-evidence: netdev policy and the submit checklist treat allyesconfig/allmodconfig as the accepted gate, and Hovold's own commit text says 'we have \"allyesconfig\" for that' (taken from the dossier, not re-opened by me).",
              "PL-venue risk: a PLDI reviewer will ask what the program-analysis insight is beyond running a SAT query per object on formulas already produced for config-for/why. The honest answer is that the whole-tree exact denominator and the per-conjunct explanation come for free from the same symbolic execution; this is a client of the core technique, not a contribution. The task is therefore a supporting RQ3 item, not a headline.",
              "Other projects (U-Boot, barebox, coreboot, BusyBox) are strong for generality because Glass 2012 asked this exact question and Rini removed unenabled drivers by hand in 2022. However, we have only one build each and defconfig expansion would be needed for U-Boot, so they cannot carry the main experiment."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Do not present 3,209 / 9.8% as an effect size or as 'never built'. State it as 'unbuilt by this specific 3-config x86 matrix' and recompute after removing arch-gated objects and composite or intermediate artifacts.",
              "The dossier says Linux whole-tree takes 138.7 s median (experiments/STATUS.md), while the task text says about a minute. Use the measured value (I did not re-measure it).",
              "A sound denominator for the headline is 'objects satisfiable under the Kconfig model K with ARCH=x86', not all 32,877 objects. K is an over-approximation, so state that classes (b) and (c) inherit its error.",
              "The dossier's mention of 'Kconfig defects' as outside scope should be stated more precisely: kfold can report that an object is unreachable under K, but it can only call this a defect if K encodes input prompts and defaults faithfully (unverified whether the current K does). Check this before using k3-socinfo as an example of detection.",
              "Quote hygiene: the Torvalds string is verbatim only up to 'limited in the configuration sense' (the fetch tool truncated the quote). Use that short fragment, not the longer sentence. The Corbet LWN 2025 clause about allmodconfig hiding problems is paraphrase, not a Torvalds quote."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "rq3_task",
            "summary": "VERDICT: partly fits. The 'which objects does a config set miss, and which conjunct blocks them' part is object selection and kfold answers it from the same build conditions it already computes. The 'why is it missed' (choices, promptless options, arch gating) is partly Kconfig/intent, and 'is it a problem' is human judgement; there is no ground truth for either.\n\nSTRONGEST EXPERIMENT (single experiment, three stages, only existing assets).\nInputs: Linux 7.2.8 tree; kfold build conditions for all objects; Kconfig model K; configs in evidence/configs/linux (allmodconfig, allyesconfig, defconfig, debian, tinyconfig); real-build inventories in evidence/inventories/linux; the saved Kmax formulas for Linux; the existing config-for/olddefconfig/compile harness (384 of 402 commits). Matrix M = {allmodconfig, allyesconfig, defconfig, debian} on x86. Re-run blindspots with Debian added and with tinyconfig excluded (tinyconfig builds a subset, so it adds almost nothing; keep it only for the soundness check).\n\nStage 1, soundness against real builds (no new ground truth needed). Classify the output objects after removing composite and intermediate artifacts (crypto/crypto.o, .tmp_vmlinux1.btf.o style). Metric: false-blind rate = |objects kfold lists as blind that appear in any real inventory (allmodconfig, defconfig, debian)| / |blind list|. Expected form: 0, or a handful traced to named causes. Also report per-config predicted-vs-actual agreement for the 3 built configs (precision and recall of 'built').\n\nStage 2, classification and actionability. Split the blind set B into (a) arch-gated (non-x86 symbol, ~349 in the old run), (b) unreachable on x86 under K even with COMPILE_TEST (certificates; candidates for dead options or Kconfig defects), (c) reachable on x86 under K but missed by M (actionable: COMPILE_TEST or CI-config candidates). Report counts per class, per failing-conjunct type (arch, choice, ARCH_*-gated, missing dependency, other) and per MAINTAINERS entry. Draw 40 objects at random from (c), stratified by conjunct type; for each run config-for, olddefconfig, then compile the single object, one process at a time. Metric: witness success rate (verified config + object compiles). Manually triage the 40 as BROKEN/staging/hardware-only/intentional versus neglect (one person, written criteria) to estimate the share of (c) that is plausibly neglect. Expected headline form: 'M leaves X objects (x% of the K-reachable x86 objects) in Y MAINTAINERS entries; Z% of the non-arch remainder has a verified witness config; W of 40 sampled objects compile'. I do not know X, Z or W; the old run suggests thousands before filtering and I will not guess the filtered number.\n\nStage 3, baselines. (i) Sampling: generate 50 x86 randconfigs one at a time (make randconfig with fixed KCONFIG_SEED, conf only, seconds each), evaluate kfold conditions on each .config (no builds), and plot the cumulative fraction of class (c) covered versus number of randconfigs, to show what sampling leaves uncertified and that it cannot say an object is unreachable. (ii) Kmax: apply the saved Kmax per-unit formulas to the same four configs with a short script; report the blind-set overlap (Jaccard) and adjudicate every disagreement against the real-build inventories and the compile check. Also compare the explanation quality (named failing conjunct versus a bare UNSAT).\n\nOptional extension if time: apply to U-Boot with all board defconfigs (needs defconfig expansion only) and check against Rini's 2022 and Simek's 2025 manual removals; recall of those named drivers is a ground-truth-like check. A second optional check: confirm that the drivers fixed by Hovold's COMPILE_TEST patches (bcm2835 dmaengine, tegra iommu) are not in the 7.2.8 blind set, which is only a consistency check because the tree is post-fix; a real before/after test needs an older tree that is not among the listed assets.\n\nBASELINE to compare with: x86 randconfig sampling (stage 3i) and the Kmax-formula script (3ii); the status quo is the manual per-driver discovery documented in the dossier.\n\nEFFORT: about 7 person-days (classification and artifact filtering 1.5; rerun with Debian and soundness check 1; Kmax head-to-head 1.5; randconfig curve 1; 40-object validation harness runs 1; manual triage 1). Machine constraint: strictly one process at a time; the long step is the 40 sequential compiles and the whole-tree kfold runs (about 2.3 minutes each by the dossier's STATUS.md figure).\n\nFALSIFIES THE CLAIM: (1) false-blind rate above about 1% against the real inventories; (2) fewer than about half of sampled class (c) objects get a verified witness (conditions or K not actionable); (3) the Kmax script reproduces the same blind set and a comparably useful explanation, which would reduce the contribution to packaging; (4) after removing class (a) and artifacts, class (c) is negligible (a few dozen objects), so the task shows nothing; (5) 50 randconfigs already cover nearly all of class (c), so sampling suffices.\n\nGROUND TRUTH CHECK: the experiment does not need 'should be built' labels. The 'built' side is verified by real inventories and olddefconfig+compile; the 'unbuilt' side is a certificate under K. Whether blind objects are neglected is explicitly left as a small manual triage, reported as an estimate, not as ground truth.\n\nTHREATS TO VALIDITY: x86-only configs, so arch-gated blind spots dominate and multi-arch would change the denominator; blind relative to M, not never built (Bergmann's randconfig loops, 0-day and KernelCI cover more); intentional blind spots (arch, hardware, BROKEN, staging) are not defects; K is an over-approximation so classes (b)/(c) inherit its errors; allyesconfig has no build inventory, so its side relies on kfold evaluation and sampled compiles; composite and intermediate objects can inflate counts; the 40-object sample is small and the triage is single-rater; single kernel version; Kmax formulas may be inexact, so the head-to-head must report both tools' errors, not only kfold's.\n\nPLACEMENT AND REVIEWER OBJECTIONS: use it as a compact RQ3 developer task (third client alongside why and config-for), reporting only soundness, the three-class split with a witness rate, and the two baselines. Add one sentence of introduction motivation citing the Hovold bcm2835 patch, Torvalds' 'very limited in the configuration sense', and the krepair months-long leak. Put the x86-only and 'blind is not buggy' caveats in the limitations. Likely PLDI objections: (1) 'Kmax already gives this' (answer with the head-to-head and the explanation/owner report); (2) 'unbuilt is not a bug' (answer with the triage and by claiming only a worklist); (3) 'where is the PL contribution' (answer that it is a client of the exact whole-tree conditions, so keep it short); (4) 'anecdotal demand' (do not claim demand; cite the patches only as existence evidence). Do not drop it: it is cheap, uses assets already present, and the soundness check against real builds is a strong, falsifiable result. Do not promote it to a main contribution.\""
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "D5",
        "name": "Dead objects and Makefile bugs: entries guarded by undefined or unsatisfiable symbols",
        "dive": {
          "id": "D5",
          "name": "Dead objects and Makefile bugs: Makefile entries guarded by undefined or unsatisfiable symbols",
          "refined_description": "Problem: a Kbuild Makefile can list an object whose guard can never be true, so the object is silently never compiled and no build fails. Three sub-classes were seen in primary sources. (a) Undefined guard: the Makefile names a CONFIG_ symbol that no Kconfig defines (leds as3668, gpib tnt4882, serial 8250_of). (b) Structurally orphaned entries: a composite module defined but never listed under any obj-*, a duplicated assignment, or a missing obj- rule (tegra-utils, cmedia, r8152). (c) Defined guard that is unsatisfiable under the Kconfig model (depends on BROKEN, arch-locked with no COMPILE_TEST, conflicting selects). Class (c) has no instance in my evidence that was found by a Makefile-level tool; it is the part that needs a solver. Related but different: a source file with no Makefile or Kconfig entry at all (rt1016, mm/percpu_km.c, .dts files outside the dtb list). Evidence of recurrence: 2007, 2010, 2014, 2015, 2020, 2022, 2023, 2024-25, 2026. Each is found one patch at a time, usually by a human reading a diff. In April 2026 an AI-assisted Makefile lint ('kconfiglint', Sasha Levin) landed fixes for classes (a) and (b) in mainline, which narrows the gap. Method note: the WebSearch budget was exhausted before this deep-dive started, so discovery used the GitHub commit-search API on torvalds/linux (and a few other repos) plus about 35 direct page fetches. Counts from that API are not curated frequencies. Several fetches went through a small summarizer; quotes below are the ones the fetch returned in quotation marks.",
          "who_has_it": "Patch authors and janitors who find these by reading Makefiles (Bulwahn, Levin, Herring, Yamada); subsystem maintainers who merge a new driver whose Makefile line is wrong (leds, staging, ASoC); Kbuild maintainers; downstream users who assume a CONFIG option enables a driver. In other trees: U-Boot maintainers (Rini removed code 'not enabled by any board'), barebox and coreboot maintainers. Evidence of demand is a trickle of cleanup patches, not a survey or a mailing-list ask for a tool.",
