            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "undertaker dead-block analysis and KBuildMiner (Nadi and Holt)",
              "what_it_does": "Finds dead and undead #ifdef blocks (1,778 dead of 14,666 selectable in Tartler's study) and Kbuild anomalies by fuzzy Makefile parsing.",
              "limits": "2011-2013 work; undertaker is #ifdef granularity; KBuildMiner parses heuristically; maintenance for current kernels not verified.",
              "url": "https://cris.fau.de/publications/118685644"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Manual comparison and patch reading (Herring dts-vs-dtb, Bulwahn, Rini)",
              "what_it_does": "Humans diff source lists against build output or read Makefile diffs.",
              "limits": "Unsystematic; one case at a time; about 3 months for as3668, years for tegra-utils.",
              "url": "https://github.com/torvalds/linux/commit/86684c2481b6e6a46c2282acee13554e34e66071"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Build bots and linux-next (allmodconfig, randconfig)",
              "what_it_does": "Catch compile and link failures.",
              "limits": "An object that no config builds produces no failure, so a dead entry is invisible to them. Inference from the cases above (no bot report found for any), not a measured result.",
              "url": "https://github.com/torvalds/linux/commit/dad701bdb74368e6da30177b1d9e5529e3381591"
            }
          ],
          "remaining_gap": "After kconfiglint and checkkconfigsymbols, undefined Makefile symbols and several syntactic Makefile defects are covered in principle on Linux. What remains: (1) objects whose guard names a defined symbol but whose condition conjoined with the Kconfig model is unsatisfiable (BROKEN, arch-locked without COMPILE_TEST, conflicting selects or choices); I found no instance reported by any tool, so the yield is unknown. (2) Defects that need variable-state and directory-guard tracking rather than line patterns (a parent directory guard that is itself dead; assignments spread over included files). (3) A check that runs on a patch: does this change make an object unreachable? (4) The other trees (BusyBox, barebox, U-Boot, coreboot), where the Makefile-side checks I found are text-level at best. (5) Intent: many unbuilt objects are deliberate (BROKEN, out-of-tree use), and no source quantifies how many.",
          "kfold_contribution": "Modest and specific. kfold already computes a symbolic build condition per object over the whole tree in about a minute. Two derived reports follow without new machinery: (i) objects whose condition mentions a symbol absent from the Kconfig symbol set; (ii) objects where condition AND K is unsatisfiable. If K over-approximates the valid configurations (as described), an unsat result is a sound proof that no valid configuration builds the object, while sat proves nothing. That asymmetry is the one defensible claim. Reports can be grouped by MAINTAINERS and run on four non-Linux trees. It does not replace checkkconfigsymbols or kconfiglint for class (a); position it as a superset check plus the unsat class, not as the first Makefile lint.",
          "quantitative_facts": [
            "as3668: driver authored 2026-01-18 (c7dd343a3756), Makefile fix authored 2026-04-25 (d92e7ca6): about 97 days between authoring dates; merge dates not checked.",
            "gpib tnt4882: Makefile rewrite 2024-12-12 (79d2e1919a27, 16 Makefiles touched), symbol fix 2025-01-07: 26 days.",
            "tegra-utils orphan composite line: orphaned in 2021, rewritten by a 2024 treewide -objs to -y conversion, removed 2026-04-25.",
            "10 mainline commits dated 2026-04-25 credit kconfiglint (3 with X001). Of those 3, only as3668 was a real dead object (the SBRMI line 'has no effect'; ncr53c8xx were -D defines). Counts of landed patches, not tool accuracy.",
            "Herring 2023: 8 .dts files never built (arch/arm/boot/dts/Makefile, 8 insertions).",
            "Tartler et al. (PLOS 2011): 1,778 dead blocks, 14,666 selectable blocks, allyesconfig 78.49% of selectable blocks (block-level).",
            "El-Sharkawy et al.: 80 mismatches in Linux 4.4.1, about two-thirds possibly harmful.",
            "Kconfirm: 1,282 default-check alarms on x86-64 at v7.2-rc4; 38 mainline commits cite it by 2026-10-17 (GitHub commit search, not curated).",
            "Rini: 10-patch U-Boot series removing unenabled net drivers, 2022-03-31.",
            "Do NOT cite as frequency: GitHub search totals for 'never compiled' (5,846, includes unrelated matches) or 'checkkconfigsymbols' (86, includes doc typo fixes).",
            "Corrections to the earlier dossier: the 2010 percpu thread reports 1 case, not 33/10/3 counts; the 2021 sti ':=' patch changed three single-object Makefiles and the page does not say any object was dropped, so it is a style fix, not a dead-object case."
          ],
          "proposed_experiment": "Web-research task, so proposed, not run (and per machine rules, one process at a time). On Linux 7.2.8 run kfold blindspots with K and partition the roughly 32.9k objects into (i) guard references a symbol undefined in K; (ii) guard symbols all defined but condition AND K unsatisfiable; (iii) satisfiable under K but built by none of the four inventories (tinyconfig, defconfig, Debian, allmodconfig). Check (i) against scripts/checkkconfigsymbols.py on the same tree as a superset test: kfold should find every Makefile hit that script finds; report extras and misses. Report (ii) as the new yield and hand-classify a sample into bug, intentional (BROKEN, out-of-tree use), and model artifact. Replay the 2026-04-25 kconfiglint list on the pre-fix parent commits as a recall check on the defect classes it names (as3668 is fixed in 7.2, so it cannot count as a hit on 7.2.8). Repeat (i) and (ii) on BusyBox, Barebox, U-Boot and coreboot, where no Makefile lint was found. Expected runtime about a minute per Linux run; no builds needed.",
          "risks": "1) Low salience: no mailing-list thread asks for a tool, and the cleanup trickle is small (about 2 real dead-object fixes per year in my sample; a sample, not a rate). 2) kconfiglint already lands patches cheaply and checkkconfigsymbols exists, so the marginal yield of a solver is unknown until class (ii) is measured; it could be near zero. 3) Many unbuilt objects are intentional or harmless (percpu_km, BROKEN, SBRMI line 'has no effect', cmedia duplicate), so reports need triage and maintainers may treat them as cleanup, not bugs. 4) Entry settings are per architecture (x86), so objects unbuilt on x86 but valid elsewhere are not dead; arch-wide claims need every arch. 5) Soundness depends on K being a true over-approximation; state the unsat claim only under that assumption. 6) Nadi et al. say typos are not the common cause of anomalies; the Yamada 2014 'Simpler makefiles seem better' quote is a reply about removing obj-n, not a policy statement, so it is weak counter-evidence. 7) Several quotes came through a fetch summarizer; kconfiglint's source and design are unverified; the Tartler paper title was not confirmed. 8) Do not claim novelty over kconfiglint or Kconfirm.",
          "confidence": "medium"
        },
        "verdicts": [
          {
            "lens": "Evidence auditor, problem D5 (dead objects / Makefile entries guarded by undefined or unsatisfiable symbols)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "Everything I opened exists and the quotes are verbatim or near-verbatim (as3668 fix, tegra-utils M002, gpib tnt4882, 8250_of, r8152, Day 2007, Dietrich 2010, Herring 2023, rt1016, Rini, Wood, Kconfirm cover letter, Nadi MSR13, El-Sharkawy, Kmax docs, Tartler). The weakness is not fabrication but what the evidence supports: it is a handful of anecdotes about class (a) and (b), and nothing at all for class (c), which is the only part that needs kfold's solver.",
              "Independence: the 'at least 10 mainline commits cite kconfiglint' are one author, one sitting. The GitHub commit search returns total_count 10, with author timestamps from 20:02:49 to 20:03:32 on 2026-04-25 (43 seconds), from one tool run and one batch of patches. This counts as one source, not ten. Of the 10, only as3668 is a real dead object by the dossier's own reading. SBRMI 'has no effect' (a stale line for a source that moved), ncr53c8xx is -D defines, and several others are Kconfig dependency fixes.",
              "kconfiglint's existence outside these commit trailers is unverified. The trailer reads 'Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-4-6 kconfiglint'. I could not locate a repo or doc, and WebSearch was out of budget. The paper must not describe it as a released tool with check codes beyond what the commit messages say.",
              "The as3668 timeline is overstated. The driver commit is authored 2026-01-18 but appears in mainline history (GitHub file history) dated 2026-02-04. The fix is authored 2026-04-25. Time in the tree is about 80 days at most (the fix's merge date was not checked), not '97 days'. The 97 days compares two author dates, and the first of them is before the driver was merged.",
              "gpib is weak as 'persisted for 26 days'. Bergmann's 2024-12-12 'fix Makefiles' commit itself introduced the wrong symbol while converting obj-m to obj-$(CONFIG_...). The bug is a regression of a fix in a staging driver, found within 26 days by one expert reader (Bulwahn), with no release mentioned. That shows quick manual detection, not long persistence.",
              "The dossier presents the recurrence list 2007/2010/2014/2015/2020/2022/2023/2024-25/2026 as a trend. These are different defect types. The 2007 Day post is a list of about 30 CONFIG variables in Makefiles with no method, no count and no triage (my tally of headings), and many such variables are deliberate. The 2010 thread is one case in mm/ with the poster himself asking whether it is intentional. Rini 2022 and Wood 2016 are code removal and build coverage, not Makefile defects. Yamada 2020 (binderfs) concerns hostprogs/userprogs, which are not kernel .o objects and so are outside kfold's object granularity. Herring 2023 is .dtb entries, also not .o. rt1016 has no Makefile entry at all. Only as3668, 8250_of, gpib, r8152, tegra-utils and the Day/Dietrich lists are actual Makefile-guard cases.",
              "Rini's series (patch 01/10 confirmed, text 'This driver is not enabled by any board and not converted to DM_ETH. Remove.') says nothing about how he found the drivers. 'Found by hand' and 'same finding as a blindspots report' are the dossier's inference. He may have used U-Boot's qconfig or grep. Treat it as a U-Boot code-removal anecdote.",
              "Nadi et al. (MSR 2013) cuts against the anecdotes. The abstract says typos in feature names do not commonly cause anomalies and that they arise mostly from incomplete patches. as3668, 8250_of and gpib are exactly symbol-name mismatches. The dossier mentions this only in the risks section. It also means the 2013 paper is the prior art that quantified the phenomenon, with counts the dossier did not retrieve. The paper needs those counts, and the 2013 abstract alone gives none.",
              "Tartler et al. is not just 'block-level, outside scope'. The paper (PLOS 2011, title 'Configuration Coverage in the Analysis of Large-Scale System Software', Linux 2.6.35, arch-x86 only) defines dead blocks as blocks whose presence condition contradicts the Kconfig model. So the measured 1,778 dead of 16,444 conditional blocks is an existing Kconfig-conjoined unsatisfiability result at block granularity, from 2011. This is the nearest precedent for class (c) and the dossier should cite it as prior art for the method, not only as a statistic. The numbers (1,778 / 14,666 / 78.49%) are exact. They come from a 2010-era kernel and are 16 years old.",
              "Soundness claim needs a second assumption. 'Unsat of condition AND K proves no valid configuration builds the object' needs the Makefile-derived build condition to be an over-approximation too, not only K. The Kmax docs say 'The extracted formulas may not be exact', and that no result suggests the formulas are overconstrained. kfold's own condition extraction has not been shown to be over-approximate for every construct. State both assumptions or measure false positives.",
              "klocalizer already covers per-unit class (c). The Kmax docs state it detects a CONFIG_BROKEN dependency and stops, and offers --show-unsat-core when no configuration exists. The distinction the dossier draws (tree-wide, MAINTAINERS-grouped, other trees) is real but is a scale and packaging difference.",
              "checkkconfigsymbols.py is not shown to be unmaintained or ineffective. The last commits are 2021 and 2023, which says it is stable, not that it fails. 'Bugs it should flag were found by other means after 1-3 months' is not shown: Bulwahn, the author of the gpib fix, is the author of checkkconfigsymbols, so the gpib find was very probably made with it, and the commit simply does not name it. This makes 'no tool is named' misleading as evidence for the gap.",
              "Demand evidence is thin, as the dossier admits. No survey, and no mailing-list request for a tool. About 2 real fixes a year in a sample. Kconfirm cover letter: 1282 alarms on x86-64 at v7.2-rc4 confirmed, but its changelog mentions 'makefile improvements' for the tool's own Makefile, which is unrelated to analyzing kernel Makefiles, so 'says nothing about Makefiles' is right in the relevant sense.",
              "G-claims from the gap scout (G62): LWN's 'Best practices for linux-next' (Corbet, 2025-12-12) says Torvalds 'added that' allmodconfig builds 'cause some code to be disabled, and can thus hide problems'. This is Corbet's report, not Torvalds' words, and the 'allmodconfig' quote in the claim is a single word. Ojeda said he did not want people testing with canned configurations (confirmed). G9 GPIO (Golaszewski 2025-04-08, 'gpio: extend build coverage with COMPILE_TEST=y') is confirmed: it supports the idea that some drivers are not built by allmodconfig, but this is a Kconfig-depends choice, not a Makefile bug, and so is not D5 evidence."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "As3668 interval: the driver was added to mainline 2026-02-04 per GitHub file history (authored 2026-01-18); the fix was authored 2026-04-25. Say 'about 80 days after the driver entered mainline (fix merge date not checked)' or avoid a number.",
              "kconfiglint: 10 commits is one batch, 43 seconds apart on 2026-04-25 (20:02:49 to 20:03:32 -0400), one author. Cite it as one source, with Assisted-by 'Claude:claude-opus-4-6 kconfiglint'. Only as3668 is a real dead object. Replace 'check codes X001/M002/M004/M008' with 'cited in commit messages; tool not located'. The M002 quote is verbatim from the tegra commit.",
              "Tegra-utils timeline from the commit text: 2011 line added (a3cd50deef7b, Warren), 2021 (8c1b3b159300, Osipenko) removed the obj- line but left the composite definition, 2024 (51a50d6ad727, Iwai) converted -objs to -y. Note the defect is a leftover definition with no effect on the build (nothing was dropped), so it is a lint finding, not a dead object. The dossier's heading calls it 'dead composite-module line'. Do not count it among bugs that left a driver unbuilt.",
              "gpib: the unbuilt state was introduced by the 2024-12-12 Makefile rewrite itself (16 files, 16+/16-). Bulwahn's fix is dated 2025-01-07 and the symbol is replaced by GPIB_NI_PCI_ISA. Describe it as a one-month-old regression in a staging driver.",
              "Tartler et al.: add the title, Linux 2.6.35, x86 only, and that dead blocks include Kconfig-model contradictions. Table values confirmed: 16,444 conditional blocks, 1,778 dead, 14,666 selectable, 11,511 selected by allyesconfig, 78.49% (dead-corrected), 70.00% uncorrected; 94.40% for undertaker.",
              "Nadi et al. MSR 2013: add the title 'Linux Variability Anomalies: What Causes Them and How Do They Get Fixed?', authors Nadi, Dietrich, Tartler, Holt, Lohmann, pp. 111-120, and that it found typos uncommon.",
              "El-Sharkawy et al.: SPLC 2017 pp. 19-28, title 'An Empirical Study of Configuration Mismatches in Linux', 80 mismatches, Linux 4.4.1. The arXiv posting is 2021. It is not specific to dead Makefile entries.",
              "Do not use Yamada 2020 (binderfs, userprogs) or Herring 2023 (dtb entries) as dead .o objects. They concern non-.o build artifacts.",
              "Rini 2022: change 'found by hand, outside any CI signal' to 'method not stated in the patch'.",
              "Kmax docs: the quoted sentence ends with a colon and an example, not a period. The page also says 'The extracted formulas may not be exact' and that klocalizer stops at CONFIG_BROKEN by default.",
              "Day 2007: confirmed that the post lists Makefile references to non-existent CONFIG variables (RXRPC in net/Makefile appears). Count about 30 is my tally of headings, not the post's own statement. Keep it as 'at least 19 years old' only if 2007 is checked against the current symbol set, which nobody did."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "rq3_task",
            "summary": "The cited primary sources exist and the quotes check out, so no fabrication. The case for D5 as a motivating pain point is weak: it rests on about six real Makefile-guard anecdotes over 19 years, two of them (as3668, gpib) found within months, and the kconfiglint 'at least 10 commits' is one batch from one author in 43 seconds, with the tool itself not located. Class (c), which is where kfold's solver would add something, has no reported instance, and klocalizer and Tartler 2011 already do per-unit or block-level Kconfig-conjoined dead detection. Nadi 2013 and the likely use of checkkconfigsymbols by Bulwahn weaken the 'found only by reading diffs' framing. I would not use D5 as a motivation. I would keep it as a cheap measured RQ3 task, framed as a yield measurement on the 32.9k objects and four other trees: undefined-symbol, unsat-under-K and never-built partitions, hand-classified into bug, intentional and model artifact. The soundness claim must state that both the build condition and K have to be over-approximations. Prior art to cite: kconfiglint, checkkconfigsymbols.py, klocalizer (--show-unsat-core, BROKEN detection), Tartler et al. 2011 dead blocks, and Nadi et al. 2013. Confidence in the evidence is medium. Gaps I could not close: WebSearch budget was exhausted, so I did not search for contradicting data (for example whether 0-day or linux-next runs checkkconfigsymbols, or a CI lint), and I did not open the coreboot, Zephyr or barebox claims.\"",
            "sources_checked": [
              {
