            ]
          },
          {
            "lens": "Hostile competitor analyst for D5 (dead objects, Makefile bugs: guards on undefined or unsatisfiable symbols). Goal: show the problem is already solved or the claimed gap is wrong. Caveat: WebSearch budget was exhausted mid-task, so all evidence comes from WebFetch of first-party pages (GitHub commit-search API, kernel commits, kmax docs, the Kmax 2017 PDF, LKML mirrors). Absence claims below are therefore weak.",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "PRIOR ART ON THE CORE CLAIM (strongest finding). The Kmax paper (Gazzillo, FSE 2017, read page by page from the PDF) already claims to find never-compilable files. Intro: 'The Linux kernel contains source that can never be compiled, either due to bugs in the Makefiles or infeasible combinations of configurations.' Contributions: 'Kmax is able to identify dead and orphaned code due to infeasible configurations.' Its Table 2 classifies all 20,804 C files of Linux v3.19 by hand-verified category: Orphaned 49, No configuration option 13, Not built with Kbuild 2. BusyBox 1.25.0 (633 C files): Orphaned 18, No configuration option 0. Text: 'Several C files are referenced by Kbuild, but, lacking a definition of their configuration options, cannot be compiled into the kernel by any configurations (row 8).' It also names the phony option ACPI_FUTURE_USAGE 'not defined in Kconfig' (hwtimer.c). So sub-classes (a) undefined guard and the 'source with no Makefile entry' case (set difference of find *.c against Kmax output) were measured on Linux and BusyBox in 2017. The dossier's gap that no whole-tree report exists must be restated as: no continuously-run, patch-time, object-level, explained report exists. It is not 'nobody has counted'.",
              "Class (c), unsatisfiable under the Kconfig model, is not an untouched area either. The Kmax suite (klocalizer) already reports 'no configuration possible' per compilation unit and offers --show-unsat-core. Its docs (fetched via a summarizer) say 'The extracted formulas may not be exact.' and 'No resulting configuration is a sign that the formulas are overconstrained.' They also say klocalizer by default stops when a unit depends on CONFIG_BROKEN. Two consequences: (1) an unsat verdict is a bug report only if K is a true over-approximation, and the Kmax authors themselves read 'no config' as possibly their own error; (2) a tree-wide loop over klocalizer is possible but slow (the FSE 2024 krepair paper reports kclause at 2-3 min and kmax at 10-15 min, per the earlier survey and not re-verified here). The Kmax docs state the one-unit, one-architecture usage; no tree-wide dead-object report is documented there.",
              "Class (a) is solved technically and is in use by hand. scripts/checkkconfigsymbols.py has no Makefile special case but scans every tracked file for CONFIG_ references and reports referenced-but-undefined symbols (header: 'Find Kconfig symbols that are referenced but not defined.'). It has -c (check a commit) and -d (diff two commits). Bulwahn's 2021 commits cite it for Makefile lines: hwmon amd_energy (b3a7ab2d) and crypto rmd320 (ff1469a2), each with './scripts/checkkconfigsymbols.py warns:'. It would have flagged as3668 (CONFIG_LEDS_AS3668 undefined) the day it was merged. So the 97 days (author-date difference) is a process gap (nobody or no bot ran it), not a tool gap. I found no evidence that 0-day or linux-next runs it: a GitHub commit search in intel/lkp-tests returned 0 hits for checkkconfigsymbols, which is not conclusive. kfold's undefined-symbol report would be a re-implementation of a 60-line script unless it is wired into a patch-time bot.",
              "kconfiglint is real but opaque. Mainline commit d92e7ca6 quotes 'kconfiglint reports:' and 'X001: CONFIG_LEDS_AS3668 referenced in Makefile but not defined', with the trailer 'Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-4-6 kconfiglint'. GitHub commit search finds exactly 10 mainline commits citing it, all with author date 2026-04-25 and committer dates from 2026-04-27 to 2026-07-31, with none after. GitHub repository search for 'kconfiglint' returns 0 results. Treat it as one author's one-off batch with unpublished source: no adoption evidence, no false-positive data. Nothing in the evidence says it does solver reasoning. It cannot be cited as a baseline for comparison, and cannot be dismissed as absent either.",
              "The kernel is adding in-tree lints now, which is both adoption evidence and a competitor. (1) kconfig-sym-check (Andrew Jones, authored 2026-05-27, commit f58316a4, merged in the kbuild-7.2-1 pull, merge authored 2026-06-15; Acked-by Shevchenko, Dunlap, Schier; Tested-by Dunlap, Braha, Schier): finds Kconfig symbols referenced in expressions but never defined. Per the fetch it scans only Kconfig files, not Makefiles. (2) Kconfirm (Braha), a Kconfig-side dead-code checker with a make kconfirm target: 'aims for zero false-positives' (cover letter), 1282 default alarms on x86-64 v7.2-rc4, 38 mainline commits cite it by 2026-10-17 (GitHub commit search, uncurated; the 13 titles visible are Kconfig fixes: dead select, unmet dependency). Neither covers Makefile guards. The community accepts small Perl/Rust in-tree lints; a Z3-based tool would face a heavier acceptance bar.",
              "The defect class is wider than the dossier's (a)-(c), and the two newest cases fall outside undefined-symbol checks. (d) Guard names a DEFINED but wrong symbol: iio mlx90635 had 'obj-$(CONFIG_MLX90632) += mlx90635.o' from its introduction (author date 2023-12-06) until commit 63a76e3a (author date 2026-06-25, Fixes tag, Cc stable), about 2.6 years. The object was built as a side effect of the MLX90632 option and the new MLX90635 option had no effect. checkkconfigsymbols and X001 cannot see this. An unsat check cannot either (the condition is satisfiable). (e) Misspelled Kbuild variable: samples/pidfd had 'usertprogs := pidfd-metadata' from commit 60fb0b12 (author date 2020-04-29); fixed by Sasha Levin on 2026-10-11 (f1365f97): 'the sample hasn't been built since the conversion to userprogs' (summarizer paraphrase; the fixed line is usertprogs-always-y to userprogs-always-y). About 6 years; found 6 days before today. Note it is a userspace sample program, probably outside kfold's kernel-object scope. What kfold might add here is reasoning, not verified by any run: report Kconfig symbols with a prompt that appear in no object condition (mlx90635 would surface), and report Makefile assignments to variables the Kbuild semantics never consume. Neither check is shown to exist for Linux. coreboot's kconfig_lint does warn on 'Unused symbols' (fetch summary), so the reverse check is not new as an idea.",
              "Class (c) instances are mostly self-declared dead code, so an unsat report mostly duplicates BROKEN labels. pps_gen_parport 'has been marked BROKEN for almost its entire existence' (removal commit 0bb23328, 2026-01-22; the summarizer's added gloss was: introduced Jan 2011, BROKEN Mar 2011, removed Jan 2026). olpc_dcon 'still depends on BROKEN since 2022' (removed 2024-10-16). gpib: 'no drivers in Kconfig depend on BROKEN any longer' (2025-05-21). GitHub search for 'depends on BROKEN' + remove returns 26 commits (uncurated). I found no instance of an unlabeled, unintentionally unsatisfiable guard found by any tool, so the yield of class (c) beyond BROKEN is still unknown, as the dossier says. klocalizer deliberately stops at CONFIG_BROKEN.",
              "Process, not tool, explains the cleanup trickle. Fixes come one at a time from a few people (Bulwahn, Levin, Yildiran, Yamada, Mork). The mantis orphan shows persistence: mantis_core.c removed 2020-09-22 (message: 'There is no Makefile rule to have drivers/media/pci/mantis/mantis_core.o in build') while mantis_core.h survived until 2024-10-24 (0278d6c1). Kmax's own bugs_found.md lists about 75 kismet and about 50 0-day entries (13 lore links plus about 37 kbuild-list links; approximate, from a summarizer), mostly Kconfig unmet-dependency bugs, with only two dead-code/orphan entries (mantis_core.c, regmap REGCACHE_COMPRESSED unused symbol) and no entry labelled as a Makefile bug. This supports 'Kmax suite has not been aimed at Makefile bugs' but also 'demand for such a report is not documented'.",
              "Adoption evidence for the incumbent suite is modest. kmax GitHub: 72 stars, 29 forks, 1,289 commits; PyPI kmax 4.10 released 2026-08-18; README says a full kismet run takes 'about an hour' and 'some lines may still be omitted, for instance if they are dead code or in header files'. The 0-day robot runs kismet (README credits 'Found by Intel's kernel test robot running kismet'; 2026 kernel commits fix kismet reports), so the route from a Kmax-family check into a bot exists. No evidence that any bot runs a Makefile-level dead-object check. Reminder: the Kmax suite's tools are Linux-focused; the PyPI page does not mention BusyBox, U-Boot, coreboot or barebox, although the 2017 paper evaluated BusyBox and cites coreboot as a Kbuild user.",
              "Speed is not a clean differentiator against Kmax's Makefile extractor. Kmax 2017 (Python, 4.2 GHz 4-core, 16 GB): mean 77.52 s per architecture on Linux v3.19 (min 63.28, max 357.65), 84.15 s mean on x86, KBuildMiner 45.00 s, GOLEM 3.42 h; 2,325.55 s for 30 architectures. kfold's whole-tree run of about a minute on 7.2.8 (x86) is the same order. The speed edge is only over the kclause/Kmax pipeline used by krepair (2-3 and 10-15 min per the earlier survey, not re-verified here).",
              "Other trees: I could verify little. coreboot util/lint/kconfig_lint (fetch summary) flags undefined symbols, 'Unknown CONFIG_ options used in code' and 'Unused symbols', and explicitly scans only payloads/Makefile.mk and payloads/external/Makefile.mk. U-Boot CI docs say 'try and build all possible platforms' and mention no unused-code or Kconfig check. Nothing was opened for BusyBox or barebox tooling. 'No Makefile-semantic lint exists for U-Boot/barebox/coreboot' is plausible but not established (absence claim under an exhausted search budget).",
              "Not examined, so do not claim anything about them in this lens: kernel-cve-triage, cvehound, PatchLens, tuxmake/TuxSuite, KernelCI, syzbot (these do not address dead Makefile entries by their stated scope, but I did not open them for D5), Yocto/Debian/Fedora tooling, ConfigFix (a GitHub commit search in torvalds/linux returned 0 hits, no page opened). arXiv searches (title 'Kconfig': only She and Berger, 'Formal Semantics of the Kconfig Language', arXiv 2209.04916; 'Kbuild': none) are not evidence of absence. I could not search for a 2023-2026 paper specifically on dead Kbuild objects. Nguyen and Nguyen (ICSME 2020) could not be opened (dblp returned an Anubis block); I cannot say whether it reports dead files."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Dossier claim 'kconfiglint tool could not be located': confirmed. GitHub repository search for 'kconfiglint' returns 0 results; commit search finds exactly 10 mainline commits (author date 2026-04-25 each), none later. Keep 'availability unverified'.",
              "Dossier dates for the 2026-04-25 batch are author dates. Committer dates differ: as3668 fix d92e7ca6 committed 2026-06-17, SBRMI 1a1414c6 2026-04-30, tegra-utils dad701bd 2026-04-27, cmedia b795666e 2026-04-27, ncr53c8xx 7787588d 2026-04-30. The 'about 97 days' for as3668 is an authoring-date difference only. The driver's merge date was not retrieved, so no merge-to-fix interval can be stated.",
              "Dossier remaining_gap (1) says class (c) was 'found by no tool'. Add: klocalizer already reports 'no configuration' per unit with --show-unsat-core, and Kmax 2017 claims it can identify 'dead and orphaned code due to infeasible configurations'. The accurate statement is 'no tree-wide, continuously run, explained dead-object report is documented'.",
              "Dossier says a source file with no Makefile entry has 'nothing to analyze' for a Makefile tool. Kmax 2017 handled exactly this by set difference (find *.c minus Kmax's collected files: 'Orphaned' 49 on Linux v3.19, 18 on BusyBox 1.25.0). kfold can do the same (all .c/.S minus conditions' source set); it is not a separate unreachable class.",
              "The dossier's kfold_contribution says kfold 'should not be positioned as first Makefile lint'. Agree, and extend: it cannot be positioned as first for dead or orphaned file detection either; cite Kmax Table 2 (Linux v3.19: 49 orphaned, 13 'no configuration option', 2 'not built with Kbuild').",
              "Add to the evidence list: in-tree kconfig-sym-check (merged via kbuild-7.2-1, merge authored 2026-06-15) covers Kconfig-file references only and was merged after the dossier's April snapshot; Bulwahn's 2021 commits show checkkconfigsymbols in actual use on Makefile lines.",
              "Add two recent cases of classes not covered by symbol checks: mlx90635 (defined-but-wrong guard, 2023-12-06 to 2026-06-25) and samples/pidfd (misspelled Kbuild variable, 2020-04-29 to 2026-10-11).",
              "Dossier 'checkkconfigsymbols ... Not shown to run in CI' stays correct but is an absence claim: one empty commit search in intel/lkp-tests, no CI configuration inspected.",
              "Tool-reliability note for the paper: the klocalizer quotes ('The extracted formulas may not be exact.', 'No resulting configuration is a sign that the formulas are overconstrained.') were returned in quotation marks by a fetch summarizer; re-read docs/advanced.md in a browser before printing them. The Kmax 2017 quotes and Table 2 numbers were read directly from the PDF images and are reliable."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "rq3_task",
            "summary": "Verdict: partly solved, and the novelty claim is much narrower than the dossier implies. What remains open: (i) a current, whole-tree, per-object dead-object report with an explanation chain (which conjunct is undefined or unsatisfiable) and MAINTAINERS grouping; (ii) a patch-time check 'does this change leave an object unreachable' (only checkkconfigsymbols -c covers undefined symbols); (iii) reverse checks for a guard that names a defined but wrong symbol, or a Makefile assignment to a variable Kbuild never consumes (mlx90635, 2023-12 to 2026-06; pidfd, 2020-04 to 2026-10), where I found no existing tool; (iv) Makefile-level runs on U-Boot, barebox and coreboot (BusyBox was already done in 2017), unverified by search. What is not open and must not be claimed: undefined-symbol guards (checkkconfigsymbols, in use for years, and kconfiglint X001, a one-batch tool whose source I could not find), orphaned and never-compiled file counts (Kmax FSE 2017 Table 2, which also says Kmax 'is able to identify dead and orphaned code due to infeasible configurations'), per-unit unsat reporting (klocalizer --show-unsat-core), and speed (Kmax 2017 took 77 s mean per architecture on Linux v3.19, so one minute for 7.2.8 is no edge). Class (c) yield is unknown. Visible unsatisfiable guards are mostly maintainer-declared BROKEN code, so an unsat report may mostly list known-BROKEN objects. Soundness holds only if K over-approximates, and the Kmax docs read 'no configuration' as possibly their own formula error. Recommended use: do not headline D5. As an evaluation task: run kfold on Linux 7.2.8 and the other trees to partition objects into undefined guard, unsat under K (split BROKEN from other), and reverse-check hits; use checkkconfigsymbols.py on the same tree as the baseline for a superset test; hand-triage a sample; replay the 10 kconfiglint commits' parents and mlx90635/pidfd as recall tests; report the yield honestly, even if near zero. Cite Kmax Table 2, checkkconfigsymbols, kconfiglint (as unverified), Kconfirm, kconfig-sym-check and klocalizer in related work. The motivation anecdotes (as3668, mlx90635, gpib, tegra-utils, pidfd) are usable if framed as 'found one patch at a time, with persistence from months to years', not as a frequency. Caveats: the WebSearch budget ran out, so absence-of-tool claims are weak, and I could not open Nguyen and Nguyen 2020, ConfigFix, or any pages on cvehound, PatchLens, CIP triage, KernelCI or TuxSuite for this lens.",
            "sources_checked": [
