          },
          {
            "lens": "Fit and experiment designer, D5 (dead objects and Makefile bugs)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "FIT. Classes (a) undefined guard, (b) orphaned or missing rule, and (c) unsatisfiable guard are all object-selection problems. They are not C-preprocessor, runtime or toolchain problems. But they differ a lot in what kfold adds.",
              "Class (a), undefined symbol in a Makefile guard, is already covered by an existing tool. I opened scripts/checkkconfigsymbols.py. It runs git ls-files, parses files named *Kconfig* for definitions, and searches every other tracked file (Makefiles included) for CONFIG_ references. It reports referenced-but-undefined symbols. So kfold would add no detection power here beyond what that script gives. The as3668 bug (obj-$(CONFIG_LEDS_AS3668) with the symbol LEDS_OSRAM_AMS_AS3668) is exactly what it flags. The defensible kfold claim for (a) is only that kfold lists the affected objects and the exact condition. The dossier's 'superset check' framing is weak because the script is a text match and has no unit of 'object' to compare against.",
              "The dossier overstates class (b). I opened the tegra-utils commit (dad701bd). The message says the leftover line serves no purpose because the source file was already deleted. It reports no lost object. So tegra-utils and the cmedia duplicate are harmless leftovers, not dead objects. The only true class (b) evidence of a lost object is r8152 (2014: missing 'obj-$(CONFIG_USB_RTL8152) += usb/' rule). That case is a missing directory descent, and kfold would never see the object at all. It is found by a set difference between .c files and kfold's object inventory, not by a build condition.",
              "Class (c), unsatisfiable under the Kconfig model, is the only place kfold has a distinctive capability. The dossier has no real-world instance of it found by any tool, so the yield is unknown. The kfold unsat claim is sound only if (1) the build condition is exact and (2) K over-approximates the valid configurations. K is checked in results/kconfig_check.json: 18,749 defined symbols, empty smt_violations on 5 real configs (tinyconfig, defconfig, debian, allmodconfig, allyesconfig). That supports K but does not prove it.",
              "Biggest threat to class (c): arch-locking dominates. The Linux entry is x86, but drivers/ is walked on x86. Thousands of objects that depend on ARM, ARM64, PPC and so on are unsatisfiable under an x86 K without being bugs. A naive 'N dead objects' number would be mostly this and would draw an immediate reviewer objection. The unsat set must be split by cause (arch literal, BROKEN, other), using the Z3 unsat core or a per-cause relaxation. Only the 'other' bucket is a candidate defect.",
              "The assets already contain the denominator. results/devtasks/blindspots.json shows 3,209 blind-spot objects out of 32,877 considered, in 510 MAINTAINERS entries. These are the objects that allmodconfig, allyesconfig and defconfig all fail to build. The honest framing of D5 is 'of the 3,209 uncovered objects, how many are provably dead and how many have a witness configuration'. That framing needs no new infrastructure and needs no external ground truth.",
              "Ground truth. Some validation cannot be done with the listed assets: maintainer confirmation of 'is a real bug' (it needs sending patches, which breaks double blind), and recall on historical bugs (as3668 and gpib are fixed in 7.2.8, and replaying them needs older trees that are not in the asset list). Recall must therefore come from mutation testing on 7.2.8 (planted defects, truth by construction), plus an optional replay on the pre-fix parent commits of d92e7ca6 and 84b172ce. Precision must come from a documented triage rubric, not from maintainers.",
              "Other trees: results/kconfig_check.json contains only Linux. I did not find a Kconfig model K for BusyBox, Barebox, U-Boot or coreboot. Tiers (i) undefined symbol and (iii) unbuilt-with-witness need no K on those trees. Tier (ii) unsat needs K. Treat K for the other four as unverified and say so.",
              "kconfiglint (the April 2026 AI-assisted lint): availability, algorithm and false-positive rate are unverified, and the dossier could not locate its source. It cannot be a baseline in the experiment. Its commit messages can only be cited as evidence of what it reported. Do not claim kfold beats it.",
              "PLDI reviewer objections: (1) 'checkkconfigsymbols already does this' (true for class a). (2) 'This is a bug-finding tool paper, not a PL contribution': the answer is that the analysis is a by-product of the build conditions, not a new analysis. (3) 'Anecdotes, no frequency': the census above produces a frequency. (4) 'Unbuilt is often intentional': the triage must report intentional separately and say so. (5) 'x86 only': say this plainly."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Dossier says the tegra-utils line was a 'dead composite-module line' that survived 2021 to 2026. The commit message I opened (https://github.com/torvalds/linux/commit/dad701bdb74368e6da30177b1d9e5529e3381591.patch) says the leftover line had no purpose because the source file was gone and the obj-* target was already removed. It does not report a missing object. Count it as a Makefile hygiene defect, not a dead object. The same applies to cmedia.",
              "Dossier says kfold with K gives a 'superset check' of checkkconfigsymbols for class (a). The script scans Makefiles as plain text, so kfold cannot be claimed to find more than it. Say 'equal detection, plus an object-level report', and only after measuring.",
              "Dossier says the as3668 'driver can never be built' quote and the fix are verified. Confirmed: the .patch opened shows 'kconfiglint reports:', 'X001: CONFIG_LEDS_AS3668 referenced in Makefile but not defined in any Kconfig', and the one-line change to CONFIG_LEDS_OSRAM_AMS_AS3668. The Assisted-by line on the tegra commit reads 'Claude:claude-opus-4-6 kconfiglint'.",
              "klocalizer docs (opened) say: 'The extracted formulas may not be exact.' and 'No resulting configuration is a sign that the formulas are overconstrained.' So an 'unsat' from the Kmax suite is not itself a proof. kfold must not call its unsat result a proof unless it states the two assumptions (exact condition, K over-approximates).",
              "Kmax has --show-unsat-core, so 'solver-based unsat detection' is not new. What is new is only the tree-wide, MAINTAINERS-grouped, patch-time use. Position it that way."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "rq3_task",
            "summary": "VERDICT: partly fit. D5 is a genuine object-selection problem, but only class (c) (guard defined yet unsatisfiable under K) gives kfold something no existing tool does tree-wide. Class (a) is already served by scripts/checkkconfigsymbols.py, which scans Makefiles as text (opened). Class (b) is mostly harmless leftovers, and the one real instance (r8152) is a missing directory rule.\n\nRECOMMENDATION. Do not use D5 as a standalone problem or as the introduction motivation: the evidence is a trickle of anecdotes, and a PLDI reviewer will answer 'checkkconfigsymbols' and 'kconfiglint'. Fold it into the existing blindspots developer task in RQ3 as a sub-question ('dead versus merely uncovered'). Put class (a) and the tools above in related work. State the x86-only entry and the arch-lock effect as a limitation.\n\nEXPERIMENT (the single strongest one).\nName: Dead-versus-uncovered census of the blindspots.\nInputs, all existing: Linux 7.2.8 (x86); results/devtasks/blindspots.json (3,209 blind-spot objects out of 32,877 considered, in 510 MAINTAINERS entries); the K model (18,749 symbols; no smt_violations on 5 configs); the four build inventories in evidence/inventories/linux; the saved Kmax run in results/kmax/linux.json; and scripts/checkkconfigsymbols.py run on the 7.2.8 tree (it needs a git tree; if the prepared tree is not a git repo this adds some effort).\nProcedure: partition the 3,209 blind-spot objects into four tiers. (T1) condition mentions a symbol absent from K. (T2) condition AND K is unsatisfiable, then split the T2 unsat core by cause: arch literal, BROKEN, other. (T3) satisfiable, and a witness configuration is produced and checked with olddefconfig (reuse the config-for path). (T4) solver timeout or unknown. Then report T2-other and T1 as candidate defects. Triage every candidate (should be tens, not thousands) with a written rubric: intentional (depends on BROKEN, documented unused), model artifact (K or condition wrong), or real defect (a Makefile or Kconfig fix would make it buildable).\nMetrics: (1) the partition counts, headline form 'Of the 3,209 objects that three standard configs miss, D are provably unbuildable on x86 (A arch-locked, B BROKEN, C other), W have a verified witness config, U are unresolved.' (2) T1 compared with checkkconfigsymbols restricted to Makefile hits: agreement, extras, misses. (3) Precision of the 'C other' bucket from triage. (4) Recall by mutation testing: plant about 100 defects in 7.2.8 Makefiles (rename a guard symbol, delete an obj-y line, add a conflicting dependency) and measure the fraction detected. (5) Soundness check, which is free: no object in any of the four inventories may be unsat (expected 0 violations). That check also falsifies a wrong condition or K.\nBaselines: checkkconfigsymbols (class a only); the dynamic baseline 'unbuilt by the standard configs' (the blindspots count itself); optional comparison with the saved Kmax run on the T2 objects.\nExpected headline form: a table of tier counts plus a short list of real defects; I would expect most T2 objects to be arch-locked or BROKEN, with a small 'other' remainder (possibly zero). This is a form, not a prediction of the numbers.\nEffort: about 5 to 7 person-days (2 for partition and unsat-core split, 1 for the checkkconfigsymbols comparison, 1 for mutation harness, 2 for triage, 1 for write-up). The runs are single-process and short (kfold about a minute on Linux), so the machine rules are respected.\nWhat falsifies the claim: (a) any built object comes out unsat (condition or K is wrong); (b) the 'C other' bucket is empty or all of it is model artifacts, in which case kfold finds no dead objects beyond what the existing tools and the arch/BROKEN labels give, and D5 should be dropped to related work; (c) mutation recall on class (a) is below checkkconfigsymbols (then kfold adds nothing there); (d) T1 equals the checkkconfigsymbols Makefile hits exactly and T2-other is empty.\nGround truth needed but unavailable: maintainer confirmation of true defects (do not claim it; double-blind forbids sending patches now) and independent recall on historical bugs (needs older trees; optional).\nThreats to validity: arch scope (x86 K; an object dead on x86 may be live on ARM); K may over-constrain or under-constrain (only 5 configs tested); the condition may be inexact for $(shell), $(call) or generated Makefiles; triage is subjective with few raters (report two-rater agreement); mutation testing measures only the planted defect types; intentional dead code (BROKEN, out-of-tree use) cannot be told from a bug without judgment; the other four trees have no verified K, so only T1 and T3 are possible there.\nPlacement: rq3_task, as above.\"",
            "sources_checked": [
