      {
        "id": "D6",
        "name": "Minimal CI configuration set covering most objects; skipping redundant builds",
        "dive": {
          "id": "D6",
          "name": "Minimal CI configuration set covering most objects; skipping redundant builds",
          "refined_description": "Two related questions that existing practice answers by hand-picked config lists. (a) Covering set: for each architecture, which small set of configurations together builds every buildable object, and which objects does the current CI matrix (allyesconfig, allmodconfig, defconfig, randconfig, vendor fragments) never build? (b) Change impact: given a push, a patch or a pull request that touches a known set of .c/.S files, which entries of the CI matrix can possibly compile any of them, so the others can be skipped without losing coverage? The sources show that (a) was stated as a research problem in 2011 (Tartler) and (b) is solved by hand-written rules in adjacent projects (OpenWrt, Zephyr, GN, Bazel), but no Linux CI source I could open computes either from Kbuild semantics. kfold's whole-tree object-to-condition table makes both an exact object-level computation: set cover over the objects' conditions conjoined with the Kconfig model K (a), and a membership test of touched objects in each matrix entry's conditions (b). Line-level #ifdef coverage and toolchain differences are out of scope, so (b) can soundly skip an entry only when no touched object can be built by it; it cannot claim that an entry is redundant because another one also builds the object.",
          "who_has_it": "CI operators with fixed matrices (KernelCI, about 150 kbuild scheduler jobs; Linaro LKFT, about 430k builds in 2024; Intel 0-day, 12 to 63 configs per report; Red Hat CKI; netdev NIPA; ClangBuiltLinux CI); integrators (Rothwell, Brown, Roeck stable builds); maintainers who must choose configs by hand (KVM x86 profile, netdev profile); submitters who cross-compile themselves; non-Linux Kbuild projects that add allyesconfig CI by hand (Barebox 2025) or keep the file-to-board mapping as an open TODO (U-Boot buildman). Evidence of felt demand is indirect: the cost and hand-selection are documented, but I found no operator or maintainer asking for a covering-set or skip tool.",
          "evidence": [
            {
              "claim": "The covering-set problem (a) was stated in 2011: Tartler et al. say deducing configurations that cover all conditional code is hard, tedious and error-prone, built undertaker to generate them per source file, and measured that allyesconfig reaches 78 percent configuration coverage (CC) against 94 percent for undertaker's set. Block-level inside files, Linux 2.6.36, not whole-tree object coverage. Prior art for D6(a).",
              "quote": "a sometimes difficult, but always tedious and error-prone task",
              "url": "https://sigops.org/s/conferences/sosp/2011/workshops/plos/02-tartler.pdf",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
              "date": "2011-10",
              "source_group": "tartler-undertaker",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "krepair (FSE 2024) searches for a small set of configs covering a patch: a single config for 97 percent of patches, at most five for over 99 percent, 23 in the worst case; build time is proportional to the number of configs. Per patch and line level, so it is the closest prior art to D6(b) at patch scope. The WebFetch summarizer returned the quote; it also reported 9 configs/74.4 percent in one section against about 10 configs/75 percent in another, so verify numbers in the PDF.",
              "quote": "a single configuration for 97% of patches, five or fewer for more than 99% of patches",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.17966v1",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
              "date": "2024-04",
              "source_group": "gazzillo-kmax-krepair",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Gazzillo's OSS 2022 slides (read as PDF text) state that picking a config is hard and arbitrary configs do not cover most patches. They also tell a case where a memory leak introduced in 2016 under CONFIG_MEDIA_CONTROLLER_DVB stayed hidden because syzbot's hand-crafted config had that option off, until a config regeneration in 2020-08 enabled it. A concrete case of code that the operator's config set did not build for years. Same author group as krepair.",
              "quote": "CONFIG_MEDIA_CONTROLLER_DVB is disabled in its mem checking config",
              "url": "https://paulgazzillo.com/files/oss2022.pdf",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "date": "2022",
              "source_group": "gazzillo-kmax-krepair",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "A March 2026 kernel test robot BUILD SUCCESS report for tip/urgent lists 27 tested configs spread over alpha, arc, arm, csky, hexagon, i386, m68k, microblaze, mips, parisc, powerpc, riscv, s390, sh, um and x86_64 (allyesconfig/allmodconfig/allnoconfig, gcc and clang), 48 skipped, elapsed 1152m, and closes with a note that more configs may be tested later. The robot's matrix is wide but sampled, with no per-object statement. The dossier's earlier Feb 2025 report (19 tested, 125 skipped, i386/x86_64 only) differs, so the per-branch config set is not constant.",
              "quote": "configs tested: 27 / configs skipped: 48",
              "url": "https://lkml.iu.edu/2603.0/05564.html",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
              "date": "2026-03",
              "source_group": "zeroday-build-reports",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Intel 0-day team slides (LPC 2022) say the robot builds the branch head under various arch and config kinds and reports within a day. The same slides show the team hand-adding configs to reach newly added code: all Rust samples under samples/rust and Rust options are enabled explicitly, with randconfig giving only partial coverage. Operators extend object coverage by hand, config by config.",
              "quote": "Enable all the Rust samples in samples/rust",
              "url": "https://lpc.events/event/16/contributions/1181/attachments/998/1996/The%20Integration%20of%20Rust%20with%20Kernel%20Testing%20Service.pdf",
              "evidence_type": "tool",
              "date": "2022-09",
              "source_group": "zeroday-lpc2022",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Linaro LKFT 2024 stats (slides read as PDF text): 271 LTS releases, 116 regressions, 1,229 kernel triggers and about 430k builds; a sub-bullet under triggers reads about 400 builds and 2500 boots (apparently per trigger; 430k/1229 is about 350). Per-trigger fan-out into a fixed matrix is the cost that D6(b) targets.",
              "quote": "Builds: ~430k",
              "url": "https://foss-north.se/2025/slides/aroxell-kerneltesting-slides.pdf",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
