          "existing_solutions": [
            {
              "tool_or_process": "undertaker / Vampyr (Tartler et al. 2011; Hengelein and Lohmann 2014)",
              "what_it_does": "Computes, per source file, a small set of partial configurations that cover every #ifdef block (greedy / graph colouring); Vampyr runs the compiler over them. This is D6(a) at block granularity per file.",
              "limits": "Per file, not whole-tree; krepair's related work calls Vampyr unmaintained (dossier claim 275, not re-opened); depends on undertaker's Kconfig extraction; block-level coverage inside files is what kfold does not model.",
              "url": "https://sigops.org/s/conferences/sosp/2011/workshops/plos/02-tartler.pdf"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "klocalizer / krepair / koverage (Kmax suite)",
              "what_it_does": "Repairs a config so a whole patch builds (line level, uses SuperC for #ifdef constraints), emits several NUM-ARCH.config files when a patch needs more than one, and koverage checks how much of a patch a given config covers. Reported: 97 percent of patches need one config, at most five for over 99 percent. Also --include for a compilation unit.",
              "limits": "Per patch, one arch per invocation; the README says some lines (dead code, headers) may stay omitted and the docs say extracted formulas may not be exact; the README/PyPI page describes no whole-tree covering set or matrix-redundancy report (inference from absence). Kmax/kismet is already in Intel 0-day, so a route into CI exists.",
              "url": "https://pypi.org/project/kmax/"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Intel 0-day kernel test robot",
              "what_it_does": "Builds branch heads under a library of arch/config kinds (allyes, allno, allmod, def, rand, rust) and bisects failures; hand-adds configs to cover new areas (Rust samples).",
              "limits": "Sampled; per-report config counts vary (12 to 63 in the reports seen); 'more configs may be tested in the coming days'; no per-object coverage statement.",
              "url": "https://lkml.iu.edu/2603.0/05564.html"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "KernelCI scheduler, TuxSuite plans, LKFT",
              "what_it_does": "Hand-written YAML matrices of arch x toolchain x config/fragment (about 150 kbuild jobs in KernelCI's scheduler; TuxSuite plans list kconfig entries). Scale-out in the cloud.",
              "limits": "Rules select by tree and version, not by changed files (scheduler.yaml as fetched); no stated object-level coverage metric.",
              "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kernelci/kernelci-pipeline/main/config/scheduler.yaml"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "netdev NIPA and other patchwork bots",
              "what_it_does": "Per-patch whole-tree allmodconfig and 32-bit builds with W=1; skips kernel builds only for selftest-only series.",
              "limits": "Hand-coded skip rule; the bot overloads easily per the maintainer doc; builds the whole tree, not touched objects.",
              "url": "https://github.com/linux-netdev/nipa"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "CKI kpet (Red Hat)",
              "what_it_does": "Selects targeted tests from the patch using a pattern database; arch and tree are inputs.",
              "limits": "Chooses tests, not build configs; the page does not say how a config is chosen. Red Hat's ccache-in-S3 cost blog (dossier claim 198) returned 404 when I tried, so that cost figure is unverified.",
              "url": "https://github.com/CKI-project/kpet"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Randconfig sampling, allyesconfig / allmodconfig, COMPILE_TEST convention",
              "what_it_does": "The working practice: random configs with COMPILE_TEST forced on (Bergmann, bots), allyesconfig as catch-all, drivers opted in to COMPILE_TEST by hand.",
              "limits": "Probabilistic; arch-specific and mutually exclusive options stay unbuilt; gaps found one driver at a time (GPIO 2025). Maintainer docs say 'the more the merrier'.",
              "url": "https://docs.kernel.org/process/maintainer-kvm-x86.html"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "syzkaller config fragments",
              "what_it_does": "Hand-written base and subsystem fragments composed by tags into about 41 instances (count by fetch summarizer).",
              "limits": "Hand-maintained; the Gazzillo slides show a bug hidden for years by a disabled option.",
              "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/google/syzkaller/master/dashboard/config/linux/main.yml"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Impacted-target analysis in declarative build systems (GN analyze, bazel-diff, OpenWrt CI, Zephyr test_plan.py, U-Boot buildman TODO)",
              "what_it_does": "GN: 'Analyze which targets are affected by a list of files', reports that no build is necessary when nothing is affected; bazel-diff: 'determine the exact affected set of impacted targets between two Git revisions' (https://github.com/Tinder/bazel-diff). OpenWrt and Zephyr do it with hand-written path rules.",
              "limits": "None computes it for a Kbuild tree whose targets depend on CONFIG_* conditions; U-Boot lists it as a TODO. Evidence is by analogy, not Linux practice.",
              "url": "https://gn.googlesource.com/gn/+/main/docs/reference.md"
            }
          ],
          "remaining_gap": "1) No Linux CI source I opened (KernelCI scheduler, NIPA, 0-day reports, LKFT slides) computes which objects its config matrix builds, and none skips matrix entries from the touched objects; I found no published measurement of the objects that a real CI matrix leaves unbuilt (the kfold blindspots run on three x86 configs is the only such measurement we hold). 2) The covering-set problem was posed by Tartler 2011 and the per-patch small set by krepair; neither computes a whole-tree object-level cover from an exact Kbuild model with the Kconfig model K, across archs, at about one minute of analysis. 3) Demand is not shown: I found no operator or maintainer request for a covering-set or skip tool, and the dossier's survey search found no kernel-developer survey on the topic. 4) Soundness of skipping is untested: whether touched objects built under a config also compile the changed lines (#ifdef) is outside kfold.",
          "kfold_contribution": "Modest and specific. (1) Object-level, whole-tree covering-set computation: greedy set cover (or ILP) over the objects' build conditions conjoined with K, per architecture, giving a small config list and the objects that no config can build (the blindspots side), for Linux and for BusyBox, Barebox, U-Boot and coreboot with their entry settings. (2) A change-impact filter for an existing matrix: given the touched .c/.S files of a push, report which matrix entries build at least one touched object (via why/config-for) and which cannot, so those can be skipped soundly at object level. (3) A measured comparison against a real matrix (the four inventoried configs here, or KernelCI's if the configs can be obtained). It must not be sold as new in idea (Tartler 2011, krepair) and cannot claim line-level coverage, header-only patches, or toolchain coverage. Speed is the practical point: krepair's constraint collection is reported at minutes (kclause 2-3, kmax 10-15, dossier claim 274, not re-opened) against about one minute for the kfold table.",
          "quantitative_facts": [
            "Tartler 2011 (Linux 2.6.36 x86): allyesconfig covers 78.49 percent of selectable #ifdef blocks (70.00 percent not dead-corrected), undertaker's per-file config sets 94.40 percent; 10,365 files analyzed, 3,163 with variability, 4,435 to 4,505 partial configs; kernel/sched_fair.c needs seven configs. Source: https://sigops.org/s/conferences/sosp/2011/workshops/plos/02-tartler.pdf (read as PDF text).",
            "krepair (FSE 2024): a single config suffices for 97 percent of patches, at most five for over 99 percent, 23 worst case; allyesconfig about four hours on a typical workstation versus about 20 minutes for defconfig or a repaired config; of 36,115 Intel 0-day reports with configs, 63 percent randconfig, 15 percent allyesconfig, 7 percent defconfig, 7 percent allmodconfig (fetch summarizer; https://arxiv.org/html/2404.17966v1). One source group with the OSS 2022 slides (3 hours vs 20 minutes there; cite the paper).",
            "0-day report 2026-03 (tip/urgent): 27 configs tested, 48 skipped, elapsed 1152m, 16 architectures; Feb 2025 report: 19 tested, 125 skipped (i386, x86_64 only); Apr 2025: 19 tested, 140 skipped, 1453m; May to Jul 2026 reports from search-result text only: 63/10, 15/1, 12/0, 21/0. Tested count differs by branch from 12 to 63.",
            "Linaro LKFT 2024: 271 LTS releases, 116 regressions, 1,229 triggers, about 430k builds, about 400 builds per trigger (slide sub-bullet); Linaro page claims over 350 release/arch/target combinations built (search-result text, not re-opened).",
            "KernelCI: over 220 build configurations (blog 2024-09-12; the page says nothing on how builds are chosen); about 150 kbuild scheduler jobs (hand tally by fetch summarizer, 2026-10-17).",
            "Linux 6.16 x86_64 has 32,468 Kconfig options and a configuration count of 6,550 digits; LWN says it is hard to tell how many configurations are covered (https://lwn.net/Articles/1034811/, reopened, 2025-09-10).",
            "LWN 2026-09-11: a clean-ccache kernel build takes about 960 s, about 860 s with a new series; with a full ccache about 90 s (https://lwn.net/Articles/1093398/). Context on build cost, not CI.",
            "Bergmann 2016: about 50 builds a day, almost all 32-bit ARM; Roeck stable review: 155 builds per release in 2022-23 and 145 in 2018 (search-result text for the counts; his kerneltests.org page lists 26 architectures and 14 branch columns as of 2026-10-16, fetched).",
            "Kmax paper (from dossier, not re-opened): 1,985 Makefiles controlling 19,651 C files; kfold: 2,225 Makefile instances, about 32.9k objects for Linux 7.2.8 in about a minute."
          ],
          "proposed_experiment": "Offline only, on the existing assets, no new builds beyond olddefconfig checks, one process at a time (CLAUDE.md). Step 1, coverage of the given matrix: with kfold's Linux 7.2.8 x86 object conditions conjoined with K, count the objects that kfold says are buildable on x86 but built by none of the four inventoried configs (tinyconfig, x86-64 defconfig, Debian, allmodconfig); cross-check against the four object inventories (400 why verdicts already checked). Step 2, covering set: greedy set cover over the remaining objects gives the number of extra configs needed to reach all buildable x86 objects (report the curve of configs versus objects covered); each extra config is produced by config-for and verified with make olddefconfig only. Step 3, change impact on the 402 .c/.S commits of v7.2.7..v7.2.8: for each commit and each of the four configs count the touched objects it builds; report the share of commits where all four entries build every touched object (measured redundancy, entries that could be skipped), where exactly one entry builds any touched object, and where none does (blindspot share; 384 of 402 commits get a configuration today, 18 do not, as the baseline). Step 4, optional baseline: run klocalizer on a sample of the same commits if it installs, and compare the number of configs. Report measured facts with the soundness caveat that object-level skipping ignores #ifdef and toolchain.",
          "risks": "Search budget: only about eight WebSearch calls ran in this session before the shared budget was exhausted, so the requested 15 new searches were not done. The new evidence comes from about 40 direct fetches of known documents and from PDFs read as text. Items marked reopened_and_verified true were opened this session, but several quotes came back through a fetch summarizer and must be re-checked against the page before they go into the paper (krepair numbers, KernelCI job count, netdev doc, syzkaller instance count). Demand: operators solve coverage by scaling out and caching (Linaro slides, Red Hat); no operator or maintainer asked for a covering-set or skip tool; netdev and KVM docs show hand-written config advice that works well enough for them; treat D6 as a hypothesis with cost context. Prior art is strong: Tartler 2011 and krepair (patch scope) already pose the problem and a solution at finer granularity, so 'first' claims are not defensible; the only claim is object-level exactness over whole trees and non-Linux projects. Soundness: skipping a build because another entry also builds the object is unsafe (different #ifdef arms, toolchains as in the stable-review clang case, arch-specific code, header-only changes); skipping because no touched object can be built by an entry is sound only if kfold's conditions and K are exact, and K is an over-approximation, so a fallback to the full matrix is needed, as in Zephyr's test_plan.py. A canonical covering set may reduce config diversity (2015 Kernel Summit argument, Ojeda 2025), and COMPILE_TEST means the cover is only as good as the Kconfig annotations. Evidence is Linux-centric and partly old (Tartler 2011, Bergmann 2016); there is no 0-day or KernelCI operator interview; the four-config matrix in the experiment is an x86 stand-in, not a real CI matrix.",
          "confidence": "medium"
        },
        "verdicts": [
          {
            "lens": "EVIDENCE AUDITOR for D6 (minimal CI configuration set covering most objects; skipping redundant builds)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "Numbers and quotes check out. I opened 17 sources (Tartler PDF, krepair arXiv, 0-day tip/urgent report, Linaro slides, Gazzillo OSS 2022 slides, 0-day LPC 2022 slides, KernelCI scheduler.yaml, KVM and netdev docs, NIPA README, Torvalds Feb 2025 mail, LWN 1050027, 908324, 683476 and 1034811, GPIO cover letter, U-Boot buildman docs, OpenWrt commit, Zephyr test_plan.py, kmax PyPI page). The page, quote and date matched in almost every case. The problems are in how the evidence is interpreted and in what is missing.",
              "MISREAD (stable review, Aug 2024; claim 15 and G22): the dossier says Chancellor replied that the CI 'only uses clang', so a named config does not determine the outcome and the toolchain axis splits the matrix. The thread (lkml.rescloud.iu.edu/2408.2/01486.html) says otherwise. Roeck guessed 'Maybe they all build with clang nowadays ?'. Chancellor answered 'I doubt that is the factor because our CI sees it and we obviously only use clang'. He disputes a toolchain explanation, and the page does not name an architecture. The thread shows an unexplained inconsistency across allmodconfig builds and does not support the toolchain-split claim. Drop it or rephrase it.",
              "WRONG KERNEL VERSION (Tartler): the dossier says Linux 2.6.36. The analysis and Table 1 are for Linux 2.6.35, arch-x86 ('CC-Analysis Results with Linux 2.6.35'). 2.6.36 appears only in the dead-block discussion (5,000+ #ifdef blocks). The other Tartler numbers are correct: allyesconfig 78.49% vs undertaker 94.40% after removing dead blocks, 70.00% vs 84.19% without correction, 10,365 files, 3,163 with variability, 4,505 naive and 4,435 greedy partial configs, sched_fair.c needing seven configs. The verbatim quote is 'a sometimes difficult, but always tedious and error-prone task'. The 2011 statement is about a developer checking that every edited line of a file compiles. It is a per-file, block-level problem and does not pose a whole-tree covering set. Venue and date: PLOS 2011, Oct 23, 2011.",
              "OVERSTATED ANECDOTE (Gazzillo OSS 2022 slides): the slide timeline reads 2016 bug introduced, 2020-05 syzkaller capable of finding it, 'CONFIG_MEDIA_CONTROLLER_DVB is disabled in its mem checking config', 2020-08 configs regenerated, 2020-11 bug found, 2020-12 fixed. The config-caused delay is about three months (2020-05 to 2020-08), not 'hidden for years because of the config'. It concerns a runtime fuzzer (syzbot) configuration, not a build-coverage matrix. It is one anecdote from the same group as krepair, so it adds no independent weight.",
              "OVERSTATED INTEGRATION (kmax PyPI page): the page thanks the Intel 0-day team 'for working to include kismet into the kernel test robot'. Kismet checks Kconfig unmet-dependency bugs. The page does not say klocalizer, krepair or koverage run in 0-day. The dossier line 'Kmax/kismet is already in Intel 0-day, so a route into CI exists' must say kismet only.",
              "COUNTER-EVIDENCE the dossier underuses (krepair paper, arXiv 2404.17966v1): the sample is 507 patches out of about 71,000 over one year. defconfig averages 21.7% patch coverage and allyesconfig 88.5%. One random config averages 29.2%, and ten reach 74.6% (Sec 2.1) or 74.4% (Sec 5.6), a discrepancy inside the paper. 97% of the sampled patches need a single repaired config, and the paper reports that most Intel 0-day reports use randconfig (63%) and allyesconfig (15%) (22,702 and 5,551 of 36,115). This suggests that a small cover per patch is easy to find. allyesconfig already gives high average line coverage on the unit that krepair measures. The 2011 allyesconfig figure (78%) predates this and is for 2.6.35. The 'about four hours' vs 'about 20 minutes' build times are in Sec 2.1. The OSS slides say '3 hours vs. 20 minutes', so cite only the paper. This v1 preprint may differ from the FSE 2024 camera-ready.",
              "COUNTER-EVIDENCE on demand: the Linaro slides (read as images) list the pains as 'CI is fragmented', 'Testing setup are painful', 'Feedback takes too long', 'Reproducibility is hard' and 'Many devs still don't test enough'. The problem slide also lists 'Limited access to broad test coverage'. None of this asks for object-level coverage analysis. 'Feedback takes too long' supports a skip tool only weakly and indirectly. The statistics are real: LTS releases 271, regressions 116, total tests 204,487,984, kernel triggers 1,229 with a sub-bullet '~400 builds, ~2500 boots', builds ~430k, boots ~3,000k. The sub-bullet is plausibly per trigger (430k/1229 is about 350). Slide dates are 2024 stats shown at foss-north 2025.",
              "NO DEMAND SIGNAL (confirmed): KernelCI scheduler.yaml has 148 kbuild- entries (147 unique; kbuild-clang-21-x86-android-allmodconfig appears twice; 35 clang-21, 105 gcc-14, 8 gcc-15). Rules filter only by tree and min_version, with no path filter. That matches 'about 150'. The kbuild-clang-21-arm64-mainline quote is present. The dossier's per-arch split (27 arm, 26 arm64, 16 x86) was not re-tallied. Neither this file nor any mail I opened asks for a covering-set or skip tool. U-Boot's buildman TODO and Zephyr's hand-written rules are by analogy, not Linux demand. The LKML anecdotes that 'support' D6 (COMPILE_TEST patches, Torvalds, Bergmann) show config-coverage gaps, which concern D6(a) and the blindspots feature more than CI skipping (D6(b)).",
              "INDEPENDENCE: Tartler, Gazzillo (krepair paper, OSS slides, kmax PyPI) and Linaro (two claims, one deck) each count as one source. The two LWN Maintainers Summit reports (2022, 2025) are both Corbet's session notes, and the Torvalds Feb 2025 mail and the LWN 2025 report express the same Torvalds position. The kernel docs (KVM, netdev) and the Zephyr, OpenWrt and U-Boot items are independent. Independent evidence on actual Linux CI practice reduces to the kernel docs, the 0-day report and slides, scheduler.yaml, NIPA and the Torvalds mail.",
              "NOT RE-OPENED / SUMMARIZER-ONLY: the dossier's Feb 2025 (19 tested, 125 skipped) and Apr 2025 (19/140, 1453m) 0-day reports; the May to Jul 2026 reports (search snippets only); the KernelCI blog 'over 220 build configurations'; Roeck's 155 and 145 builds per stable release; Bergmann's Speaker Deck 2026 and 'make -skj20'; krepair constraint timings (kclause 2-3 min, kmax 10-15 min); Vampyr unmaintained in krepair's related work; the Red Hat ccache cost blog (404); the syzkaller 41 instances count; and all G0 to G73 mailing-list claims except GPIO and the stable review. The remaining 2022 Jan to 2026 LKML anecdotes are unchecked by me. Search budget is exhausted, so I could not run new contradicting-evidence searches (for example whether 0-day already selects configs per patch, or any KernelCI patch-aware selection). That absence is unresolved, not disproved.",
              "MINOR: the March 2026 0-day report matches the dossier (27 tested, 48 skipped, elapsed 1152m, tip/urgent, 3 March 2026, 16 architectures: alpha arc arm csky hexagon i386 m68k microblaze mips parisc powerpc riscv s390 sh um x86_64, with 'More configs may be tested in the coming days'). The report mixes gcc and clang and includes an rhel-9.4-rust config. It tells us nothing about object coverage. NIPA README confirms the selftest skip hint ('When testing purely selftest patch sets it's useful to skip building the kernel, which takes a long time') and describes build_allmodconfig_warn as 'Check if allmodconfig-configured kernel builds with the patch. Catch new errors and warnings with W=1 C=1 flags'. The README does not say the build is whole-tree. That inference is the dossier's. KVM and netdev quotes verified verbatim. LWN 1034811 (Daroc Alden, 10 Sep 2025): 32,468 options and 6,550 digits verified; the '6.16' attribution was not checked.",
              "OpenWrt commit 2023-01-10 verified: 'CI: kernel: build only changed targets', author Christian Marangi, tj-actions/changed-files, and a change under generic builds all targets. The filter matches target names against changed paths. Zephyr test_plan.py verified: full twister run if more than 20 boards match, if more than 20 test roots match (default, configurable), or if a modified file is not resolved. tags.yaml patterns only produce exclusion flags, so 'selects boards and tests via hand-maintained tag patterns' slightly mis-describes it."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Tartler: Linux 2.6.35 (not 2.6.36) for the coverage table; it is per-file, block-level, x86 only. Quote is 'a sometimes difficult, but always tedious and error-prone task'; PLOS 2011, Oct 23, 2011.",
              "Stable review 6.10.5-rc1 (2024-08-16): drop 'toolchain axis splits the matrix'. Chancellor wrote 'I doubt that is the factor because our CI sees it and we obviously only use clang', rejecting Roeck's guess 'Maybe they all build with clang nowadays ?'. G22 repeats the same error.",
              "Gazzillo DVB anecdote: the config delay was 2020-05 to 2020-08 (about three months), with syzbot's runtime memory-checking config. The 2016 to 2020 gap is not due to the config. Slide build time is '3 hours vs. 20 minutes'; the paper says about four hours. Cite the paper.",
              "kmax PyPI: only kismet is thanked for inclusion in the kernel test robot; klocalizer, krepair and koverage are not stated to be integrated.",
              "krepair: 97% of 507 sampled patches (about 71,000 patches in one year), allyesconfig 88.5% average patch coverage and defconfig 21.7%; ten random configs give 74.6% (Sec 2.1) or 74.4% (Sec 5.6). The dossier's '9 configs/74.4' is wrong. Cite the paper's Sec 3 sentence: 'In practice, we find a single configuration for 97% of patches, five or fewer for more than 99% of patches, and 23 in the worst case.'",
              "KernelCI scheduler: 148 kbuild- entries (147 unique), counted from the file; the 'about 150' tally is fine. Rules filter only by tree and min_version.",
              "Zephyr: the full-run fallbacks are >20 boards, >20 test roots (default threshold) and any unresolved modified file; tags.yaml only builds exclusions."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "rq3_task",
            "summary": "The core numbers are real and verbatim: Tartler 78.49/94.40 percent, krepair 97/99 percent, the March 2026 0-day report with 27 tested and 48 skipped, LKFT about 430k builds, KernelCI's 148 kbuild jobs, and the KVM, netdev, U-Boot, OpenWrt and Zephyr texts. Three claims are wrong or inflated. Tartler's analysis is on Linux 2.6.35, not 2.6.36. The stable-review thread rejects a toolchain explanation, where the dossier reads it as proof of one. The kmax PyPI page says kismet, not klocalizer, joined the kernel test robot. The Gazzillo anecdote is a runtime fuzzing config whose gap lasted about three months, and it comes from the same group as krepair. The evidence that CI operators or maintainers want a covering-set or skip tool is indirect and thin. The Linaro slides name other pains, and no source I opened asks for it. krepair's own data (allyesconfig averaging 88.5 percent patch coverage, one config for 97 percent of patches) shows the per-patch problem is largely handled at line level. I could not run new contradicting-evidence searches because the search budget was exhausted, so whether 0-day or KernelCI already select configs per patch stays open. Several dossier items (the 0-day reports from 2025 and 2026, the Roeck build counts, the KernelCI 220-config blog, the Bergmann deck, the krepair timings) were not re-opened and should be marked unverified. Use D6 as a measured-experiment task on the existing assets, with Tartler, krepair and Zephyr/OpenWrt/buildman as related work and the 0-day and KernelCI facts as cost context. Do not use it as a demand-backed motivation, and keep the soundness caveat (object level only, K over-approximate, full-matrix fallback)."
          },
          {
            "lens": "HOSTILE COMPETITOR ANALYST for D6: minimal CI configuration set covering most objects; skipping redundant builds",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "No tool I could open computes D6(a) (whole-tree, object-level covering set of configurations) or D6(b) (skip CI matrix entries from the touched objects). The KernelCI scheduler, NIPA, lkp-tests kbuild.sh, kpet and the Kmax README show no such feature. This is evidence from absence, limited by what could be opened.",
              "The gap is thinner than the dossier suggests. krepair (FSE 2024) already poses the per-patch version: it returns a small set of configs that together cover a patch (1 config for 97 percent of patches, 5 or fewer for more than 99 percent). klocalizer --include-mutex and koverage --check-patch give the per-patch cover and the coverage check. Only the whole-tree and CI-matrix framing is open.",
              "JMake (Lawall and Muller, 2017) is prior art for the patch-to-configs question that the dossier did not list. Gazzillo's OSS 2020 slides list 'given a patch, what configurations does it affect? (jmake, lawall et al)'. krepair's related work says JMake 'tries only a fixed set of standard configurations'. I could not open the JMake repository or paper (Anubis block, 404). Its venue and details are unverified here.",
              "A Kbuild-condition extractor already exists in the same research line. krepair (Section 4.3) relies on kclause (2-3 min) and kmax (10-15 min) for Kbuild constraints. The Kmax per-file presence conditions, plus a greedy set cover, would give D6(a). I did not re-open the Kmax FSE 2017 paper, so the 1,985 Makefiles / 19,651 C files figure is unverified. Any kfold claim has to rest on exactness, speed and portability, not on having an object-to-condition table.",
              "Soundness mismatch with krepair. kfold works at object level and ignores #ifdef. 'Skip an entry if no touched object can be built by it' is sound only at object level, while krepair and koverage reason at line level. An entry that builds the object may still not compile the changed lines, and a header-only patch has no object. krepair itself lists header-only patches as unsupported (about 2 percent). The paper must say this and must not claim line coverage.",
              "Demand is not shown. LKFT slides list the pains as fragmented CI, slow setup and slow feedback, answered by cloud scale-out. KVM and netdev docs handle configs by exhortation. The KernelCI docs I opened say nothing about changed-file selection. I found no maintainer or operator request for a covering-set or skip tool (absence, with search budget exhausted).",
              "Canonical sets are contested in the community. In the 2025 Maintainers Summit report (LWN), Torvalds worried that if particular configs were tested, others would test only those, and Ojeda did not want people using 'canned configurations'. A 'minimal CI set' is therefore a debated proposition, not an obvious win. Use it as a limitation or discussion point.",
              "Adjacent new work (Aug 2026): FCC (arXiv 2608.15225, ISSTA 2026) infers 1-minimal trigger configurations per CVE, which is a competitor for config-for style tasks but not for D6. Its page says nothing about CI matrices or coverage.",
              "Web search budget was exhausted early (200 shared calls). No new searches could be done for 2023-2026 CI-skip tooling beyond direct fetches. Absence claims rest on fetched pages only. Not opened or not conclusive: Kmax GitHub (404 at the two URLs tried), TuxSuite docs (empty fetch), KernelCI pipeline docs on build selection (404), CIP kernel-cve-triage method, PatchLens, cvehound, ConfigFix, Kconfirm internals."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "KernelCI scheduler.yaml (fetched 2026-10-17): the count of 148 uncommented kbuild- jobs is a summarizer count, not a hand tally verified by me. The only rules field on kbuild jobs is 'tree' (8 jobs, include lists such as next:master or an exclusion '!efi'). min_version is NOT used on any kbuild job; it appears only on baseline-* jobs. No paths or files field exists. Fix the dossier statement 'filter by tree name and min_version only'.",
              "KernelCI pipeline config-reference (docs.kernelci.org/components/maestro/pipeline/config-reference/): job filtering uses rules on tree, branch, kernel version, architecture, defconfig and fragments. There is no paths or changes section. This supports 'not selected by changed files' with a documentation source.",
              "LWN 1050027 (2025 Maintainers Summit): the 'allmodconfig ... hide problems' point is attributed by LWN to Torvalds, in indirect speech. The 'canned configurations' remark is attributed to Ojeda, and Torvalds is also reported as worried that testing particular configs would make others test only those. Do not present either as a direct quote. The text I could extract is a summary, not a verbatim string.",
              "krepair 10-random-config figure: the paper gives 74.4 percent (Section 5.6, plateau at 9 configs) and 74.6 percent (Section 2) for 10 configs, and 'around 75%' in the introduction. Quote one value and cite the section.",
              "krepair timing: the allyesconfig build takes 'around four hours on average on a typical development workstation'. The default config and krepair's configs 'take only around 20 minutes on average'. The paper gives no separate defconfig figure. Tool runtimes: kclause 2-3 min, kmax 10-15 min, SuperC about a minute or less (Section 4.3).",
              "krepair 'first' claim: 'To the best of our knowledge krepair is the first technique to repair Linux configuration files for patch coverage.' The kfold paper must not pose config-for as new for patch coverage.",
              "NIPA: the README only offers a manual --disable-test option to skip builds for selftest-only series. It does not say what each build covers, so 'builds the whole tree, not touched objects' (dossier) is an inference I could not confirm. Reword to 'builds an allmodconfig kernel per patch (build_allmodconfig_warn, build_32bit, build_clang, build_clang_rust) with W=1 C=1; skipping is manual'.",
              "kmax PyPI (version 4.10, released Aug 18, 2026): the README documents no timings for klocalizer, koverage or krepair; the only duration is 'about an hour' for kismet. Do not attribute a runtime to koverage. The README has no krepair section (krepair is invoked as klocalizer --repair).",
              "USENIX ATC 2014 page (www.usenix.org/conference/atc14/technical-sessions/presentation/tartler): the fetch confirmed Vampyr and 'systematically increase the configuration coverage (CC)'. It reported hundreds of issues in Linux v3.2, BusyBox and L4/Fiasco. The title and author line were not returned, so cite the title from the dossier only after checking the page.",
              "Scope for kfold wording: the 2011 Tartler 'allyesconfig 78 percent vs undertaker 94 percent' figures are block-level #ifdef coverage on 2.6.36. Do not compare them with object-level numbers."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "motivation",
            "summary": "D6 is not already solved by a tool I could open. It is partly pre-empted at patch scope, and the dossier's framing needs these changes. Nothing I opened computes a whole-tree object-level covering set of configurations, and nothing skips CI-matrix entries from the touched objects. KernelCI's 148 kbuild jobs are filtered by tree only. NIPA skips builds only by a manual flag. lkp-tests kbuild.sh and kpet do not choose build configs from changed files. Patch-scope neighbours are real, though. krepair/klocalizer/koverage give 1 config for 97 percent of patches and at most 5 for more than 99 percent. JMake (Lawall and Muller 2017) answers 'what configs does this patch affect?' but its details are unverified. Vampyr and undertaker do block-level coverage per file. Kmax and kclause already extract Kbuild constraints (10-15 min and 2-3 min per krepair Section 4.3), so a whole-tree table plus a greedy cover is a modest step from existing outputs. Intel 0-day has integrated kmax/kismet (LWN 1034811) and uses sampled matrices (27 configs tested and 48 skipped in the 2026-03 tip/urgent report). Demand is unproven, and Torvalds and Ojeda argue against canned config sets. The honest claim for kfold is narrow. First, a whole-tree, multi-architecture, object-level measurement of what a given config matrix leaves unbuilt, with the blindspot list. Second, an object-level, sound-by-construction filter that says which matrix entries cannot build any touched object, with a fallback to the full matrix when K or the header-only/#ifdef limits apply. Third, speed (about a minute for Linux) and Kbuild trees other than Linux. Evaluate this on the existing assets: the four inventoried configs and the 402 stable commits, with klocalizer as an optional baseline. Present D6 as a measured case study (RQ on matrix coverage and skippable entries), not as a headline contribution, and make no 'first' claims.\"",
            "sources_checked": [
              {
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.17966v1",
                "opened": true,
                "note": "krepair paper. Verified: 97 percent / 5-or-fewer / 23 worst case; header-only unsupported (about 2 percent); 'only collect constraints from the build system'; kclause 2-3 min, kmax 10-15 min; allyesconfig about 4 h vs about 20 min; coverage 98.5/88.5/21.7/29.2; 36,115 0-day reports (63 percent randconfig); JMake 'tries only a fixed set of standard configurations'; Vampyr 'no longer maintained'; 'first technique to repair' claim. The last 9,319 characters were not read.",
                "quote_verbatim": true
