          },
          {
            "lens": "Fit and experiment designer, D6: minimal CI configuration set and skipping redundant builds",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "Fit: D6 is a genuine object-selection problem. Both halves are questions about which .o files a given config or set of configs builds, which is exactly what the build-condition table encodes. It is not mainly a C-preprocessor, toolchain or runtime problem. Those three limit soundness (see below) but do not make the question something else.",
              "Half (b), skipping matrix entries: for a FIXED matrix of already-built configs, the exact answer is available without kfold from the previous build's object inventory, or from 'make olddefconfig && make -n' per config. This is the reviewers' strongest objection, and it is a baseline, not a prior-art citation. kfold only adds value for configs that have not been built or expanded yet (randconfig, fragments, new vendor configs), and for evaluating many configs against one table. The paper must measure kfold's per-config cost against 'make -n' to claim any advantage. I have not measured it, so there is no number.",
              "Half (a), covering set: this is where kfold adds something 'make -n' cannot do, because it synthesizes configs. But on x86 it largely overlaps the existing 'blindspots' command, which already reports objects that none of the given configs build. Set cover over the residue is a thin extension, and the contribution is modest.",
              "Prior art is strong at the problem level. Tartler 2011 posed the covering problem at #ifdef-block level (as quoted in the dossier; I did not reopen it in this pass). krepair (arxiv 2404.17966) gives a small per-patch config set. I re-opened the arXiv page and confirmed 'a single configuration for 97% of patches, five or fewer for more than 99% of patches' and '23 in the worst case' (Section 3). The dossier's alternative figures of 9 configs and 74.4 percent are not what Section 3 states and should be dropped.",
              "krepair's own limits, quoted from the page, are exactly the places where kfold has no advantage: headers ('header file inclusion constraints are also not available from these tools'; 'patches that only modify header files are not supported'), dead code, and non-x86 architectures. Kfold shares the header blind spot, so it cannot claim better patch coverage. The only defensible difference is whole-tree, object-level scope with no per-patch solving. I did not verify the dossier's claim of 'minutes' for kclause and kmax constraint collection, so no speed comparison can go in the paper without measuring it.",
              "The PyPI page for kmax confirms that klocalizer repairs a config to build a patch, that koverage checks 'how much of patch is covered by a given config file', that architecture is chosen per run with -a, and that multiple config files are emitted when both arms of an #ifdef change. It describes no whole-tree covering set or matrix-redundancy report. That is an absence on one page, so say 'we found no' and not 'there is none'.",
              "Demand is not established. The dossier's felt-need evidence is the KVM maintainer doc ('Building every possible combination of Kconfigs isn't feasible') and cost figures, with no operator asking for a skip or cover tool. The Linaro LKFT slides name different pains (fragmented CI, slow feedback), and operators answer with scale-out. A PLDI reviewer will ask who needs it.",
              "Soundness limits for skipping: (1) K is an over-approximation, so 'cannot build' can be trusted but 'can build' cannot. Skipping an entry because no touched object can be built is sound only if the build conditions are exact. (2) Skipping an entry because another entry also builds the object is unsound (#ifdef arms, toolchain, as in the 6.10.5 clang case in the dossier). (3) Header-only changes and files outside the analyzed arch (arch/arm*, arch/riscv ...) are not in an x86-only table. They must fall back to the full matrix, as Zephyr does.",
              "Ground truth is the main design problem. The true set of objects that are buildable under SOME config is not available. The available witnesses give a lower bound only: the union of the four inventories plus the objects compiled in the 384 config-for-verified configs. K gives an upper bound. So report a witnessed and claimed interval for coverage and do not call either 'the' number. For the skip test the ground truth is available and exact: per-config built-object inventories for the four builds.",
              "Biased test set. The 402 stable commits are bug fixes spread over all architectures and are dominated by drivers, fs and net. Many touch non-x86 files and fall outside an x86 table. The matrix of four x86 configs is a stand-in, not a real CI matrix (KernelCI, 0-day), so the headline cannot be generalized to CI practice."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Do not state krepair's numbers as '9 configs / 74.4 percent'. The page states 97 percent with one config, over 99 percent with five or fewer, and 23 in the worst case. These are per patch, line-oriented, and evaluated on Linux only (Section 6: 'only one software system').",
              "The dossier says krepair 'cannot' do whole-tree and that Kmax/kismet is already in Intel 0-day. I could not re-verify the 0-day integration here, so mark it 'unverified'. The only thing I confirmed is that the PyPI page describes no whole-tree cover.",
              "Tartler's 78.49 percent vs 94.40 percent figures are block-level #ifdef configuration coverage on 2.6.36. The paper must not present them as object coverage or compare them directly with kfold numbers.",
              "Describe the experiment's result as 'skip is sound at object level given exact conditions', never 'redundant entries can be removed'. Skipping because another entry also builds the object is not sound and must not be claimed.",
              "The dossier frames (b) as a membership test via why/config-for. Be precise that the test needs evaluating the build conditions under each entry's .config, which is a formula evaluation (the 'why' machinery) and not a K-conjunction. K only enters for synthesizing new configs in (a)."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "related_work",
            "summary": "Honest fit: D6 is an object-selection problem, but only half of it is distinctive. (a) Covering set extends the existing 'blindspots' command, and it is the only part 'make -n' cannot replicate. (b) Skipping entries of a fixed, already-built matrix is answered exactly by build logs or 'make -n', which is the main reviewer objection. Prior art (Tartler 2011 at block level, krepair at patch level) is strong, and demand is not evidenced.\n\nSingle strongest experiment, using only the listed assets. Inputs: the kfold x86 object-condition table for Linux 7.2.8; the four build inventories (i386 tinyconfig, x86-64 defconfig, Debian, allmodconfig) as ground truth; and the 402 .c/.S commits of v7.2.7..v7.2.8, with the 384 verified config-for configs as extra witnesses.\n\nFor each commit and each of the four configs, evaluate whether any touched object's condition holds under that .config. Compare the prediction with the inventory.\n\nMetrics:\n- (1) Unsound skips: kfold says 'cannot build any touched object' but the inventory has one. Must be 0, and each miss is attributed to the table (a defect) or to the commit being out of scope.\n- (2) Skippable share of (commit, config) pairs, and the share of commits where no entry builds any touched object. This is the blindspot share, to be compared with the 18 commits that config-for failed on.\n- (3) Covering curve: greedy set cover of buildable x86 objects, giving the number of extra configs for each coverage level, reported as the interval [witnessed lower bound, K-claimed upper bound]. Each added config is checked by olddefconfig and an object-existence check, never a full build.\n\nBaselines: (i) no skipping, i.e. the full matrix; (ii) a path heuristic in the OpenWrt/Zephyr style (directory to config), which I would construct; (iii) 'make -n' per config on a sample, where kfold's advantage is cost and applicability to unbuilt configs; (iv) optionally klocalizer's config count on a sample of commits.\n\nExpected headline form: 'on N of 402 commits, K of 4 entries could be skipped with zero unsound skips, and M objects of ~32.9k are unbuilt by the matrix but witnessed buildable by c extra configs.' I give no numbers, since none were measured.\n\nFalsification: any unsound skip due to kfold's conditions, a skippable share near zero, or a cost not better than 'make -n'.\n\nEffort is about 5 person-days: 1 for the evaluator, 1 for the harness over 402 x 4, 1 for the greedy cover plus verification, 1 for the baselines, 1 for analysis.\n\nThreats to validity: x86-only table against an all-arch commit set, a four-config stand-in matrix, K's over-approximation, #ifdef/header/toolchain blind spots, and demand not evidenced.\n\nPlacement: keep it out of RQ3 as a headline task. Put the one-sentence cost motivation (KVM maintainer profile, 'testing all Kconfig combinations is infeasible') in the intro, state the design as a 'blindspots'/'config-for' side result if time permits, and cite Tartler and krepair in related work with the stated granularity difference. Put the soundness limits (header-only, non-x86, toolchain) in the limitations. Reasons: PLDI reviewers will object that the exact answer is available from builds or 'make -n', that the contribution is modest against prior art, and that no demand is shown.",
            "sources_checked": [
              {
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.17966v1",
                "opened": true,
                "note": "Re-opened via fetch summarizer; quotes for 97%/five-or-fewer/23 worst case, 'line-oriented', header-only unsupported, one-system evaluation. Last ~9k characters unread. Quotes came via summarizer, so re-check in the PDF before citing.",
                "quote_verbatim": true
