      },
      {
        "id": "D7",
        "name": "Why an object stopped (or started) being built across releases or configs: condition diff",
        "dive": {
          "id": "D7",
          "name": "D7: Why an object stopped (or started) being built across releases or configs (object-level condition diff)",
          "refined_description": "Problem statement: given two kernel trees (or two .config files), a person sees that some object or module is present in one build and absent in the other, or a feature silently vanished after a version bump. Today the question \"which objects changed build condition, why, and in which commit\" is answered by hand with olddefconfig, scripts/diffconfig, git log on Makefiles and trial builds. These tools work on option values, not objects.\n\nThe evidence splits into two bins that kfold serves differently.\n\nBin A, Kbuild condition changed (Makefile logic moved or a file was orphaned). Examples: Yamada's 2019 subdir-y/modpost change, ccp-platform.c becoming an orphan in 2017, and the U-Boot 2023.01 bmp_logo host tool. A per-object condition diff between two trees finds these directly, and blame on the Makefile line names the commit. This is kfold's actual domain. Caveat: in the Yamada case the .o is still compiled while the .ko is not produced, because modpost and modules.order sit downstream of what kfold models at object level. It counts as a modelling fidelity test, not a free win.\n\nBin B, Kbuild condition unchanged but the .config no longer satisfies it (Kconfig renamed, select became depends, a new gate symbol, a distro override). Examples: ARC HSDK (CONFIG_USB lost when the UDL driver stopped selecting it, 2020), i.MX7 RESET (no longer built in by default, 2020), tty PPC_OF (2015), Debian #1118653 (2025). Here kfold's why can name the failing conjunct for each object, and a diff of why verdicts under the old config migrated by olddefconfig lists the objects lost or gained. kfold does not find the Kconfig-level root cause (which symbol flipped, and why); diffconfig and the Kconfig model do that. Do not claim kfold explains these cases end to end.\n\nScope for the paper: an exploratory use case, not a motivating one. The phenomenon is well documented. Demand for a dedicated tool is not documented: nobody in the sources asks for one, and practice is olddefconfig plus diffconfig and move on.",
          "who_has_it": "Distro kernel maintainers on each upstream bump. Debian's kernel team edits dozens of symbols per release (for example five symbol replacements in the 6.10-1~exp1 entry alone). Fedora/RHEL kernel-ark reviews new options each rebase. Yocto, NixOS, OpenWrt and ChromeOS config owners run option-level audits. Android GKI vendors reconcile fragments manually. Regression reporters and bisectors, whose official guide warns that moving between versions can alter .config files in odd ways. End users who notice a missing module after an upgrade (the libvirt/MASQUERADE report). Build-system maintainers (Yamada's Makefile-semantics change confused several people). Firmware projects (U-Boot, Barebox) on version bumps (U-Boot 2023.01 bmp_logo).",
          "evidence": [
            {
              "claim": "Kbuild maintainer states that users cannot tell when a Makefile idiom silently stops producing a module after a Kbuild semantic change. After commit ff9b45c55b26 (modpost reads modules.order), 'obj-m' in a Makefile visited via subdir-y no longer builds the module, and he got questions from multiple people. The patch adds only a warning. Bin A. Note: this concerns module output, not .o compilation.",
              "quote": "However, people will have no idea if their Makefile suddenly stops working.",
              "url": "https://www.mail-archive.com/linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org/msg2072887.html",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "Yamada kbuild subdir-y 2019",
              "reopened_and_verified": true,
              "date": "2019-08-08"
            },
            {
              "claim": "Debian bug #1118653: after moving from 6.16 to 6.17.2 an end user lost netfilter MASQUERADE (libvirt NAT broke). Ben Hutchings first attributed it to an intentional upstream change (new NETFILTER_XTABLES_LEGACY symbol, same day, 2025-10-23). On 2025-10-29 Bastian Blank found 'this problem is actually unrelated' and the real cause was a Debian config override. Final confirmation on 2025-11-05 (only disabled on -rt). The kernel team's own diagnosis of 'why is this object not built' took at least six days and two hypotheses. Bin B. Quote is Blank's verbatim sentence on the cause.",
              "quote": "So it ends up disabled as we also override the default selection with NETFILTER_ADVANCED=y.",
              "url": "https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=1118653",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "Debian kernel team (changelog + bug tracker)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true,
              "date": "2025-10-29"
            },
            {
              "claim": "Official Kconfig documentation: new releases rename symbols, so an old working .config does not reliably give a working new kernel. The recommended remedy is oldconfig plus scripts/diffconfig .config.old .config (option values only).",
              "quote": "new kernel releases may rename config symbols",
              "url": "https://docs.kernel.org/kbuild/kconfig.html",
              "evidence_type": "policy",
              "source_group": "kernel docs: kconfig",
              "reopened_and_verified": true,
              "date": "n.d."
            },
            {
              "claim": "Debian 6.10-1~exp1 changelog (Ben Hutchings, 2024-07-18): one release entry carries five symbol replacements after upstream renames (USB_ONBOARD_HUB to USB_ONBOARD_DEV; CPU_SW_DOMAIN_PAN to ARM_PAN; DRM_DP_CEC to DRM_DISPLAY_DP_AUX_CEC; DRM_DISPLAY_DP_AUX_CHARDEV; ARCH_MICROCHIP/SIFIVE/VIRT). Without these edits the corresponding objects would silently stop being built. Bin B. Only the verbatim 'instead of' lines are cited; the fetch tool's paraphrases of why are not.",
              "quote": "[arm64] Enable USB_ONBOARD_DEV instead of USB_ONBOARD_HUB",
              "url": "https://sources.debian.org/data/main/l/linux/7.3~rc7-1~exp1/debian/changelog",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "Debian kernel team (changelog + bug tracker)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true,
              "date": "2024-07-18"
            },
            {
              "claim": "Debian 6.7.1-1~exp1 changelog: a condition tightened upstream (an SoC option now depends on NONPORTABLE) forced a distro config change for riscv64. Shows the 'stopped being buildable' direction caused by a dependency change. Bin B.",
              "quote": "[riscv64] Disable ARCH_R9A07G043 as it now depends on NONPORTABLE.",
              "url": "https://sources.debian.org/data/main/l/linux/7.3~rc7-1~exp1/debian/changelog",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "Debian kernel team (changelog + bug tracker)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true,
              "date": "2024-01-22"
            },
            {
              "claim": "Debian 6.12~rc6-1~exp1 changelog: upstream made I2C_DESIGNWARE_CORE a gate for the other I2C_DESIGNWARE options, so the distro had to enable the gate or lose those drivers. A new gate symbol moves objects out of reach of existing configs. Bin B. Seen through the fetch summarizer; the line is quoted as returned.",
              "quote": "[arm64,armhf,riscv64,x86] Enable I2C_DESIGNWARE_CORE, now gating the other I2C_DESIGNWARE options.",
              "url": "https://sources.debian.org/data/main/l/linux/7.3~rc7-1~exp1/debian/changelog",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "Debian kernel team (changelog + bug tracker)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true,
              "date": "2024-11-10"
            },
            {
              "claim": "Regression-reporting guidance: a regression only counts if both kernels are built with a similar configuration. The prescribed method is 'make olddefconfig', which sets new options to defaults. So whether an object is built is entangled with config drift and left to the reporter.",
              "quote": "This can be achieved using `make olddefconfig`, as explained in more detail below.",
              "url": "https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/reporting-regressions.html",
              "evidence_type": "policy",
              "source_group": "kernel docs: regressions and bisect (Leemhuis)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true,
              "date": "n.d."
            },
            {
              "claim": "Bisect guide admits that moving between versions can change .config files in odd ways and can make a bisection meaningless. This is the official acknowledgement of config drift across versions, with no object-level tool offered.",
              "quote": "going back and forth between different versions can alter .config files in odd ways",
              "url": "https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/verify-bugs-and-bisect-regressions.html",
              "evidence_type": "policy",
              "source_group": "kernel docs: regressions and bisect (Leemhuis)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true,
              "date": "n.d."
            },
            {
              "claim": "ARC HSDK: a Kconfig change in the UDL driver (select CONFIG_USB replaced by depends) removed the implicit USB enabling, so the board lost USB until hsdk_defconfig set CONFIG_USB=y explicitly (Fixes: 5d50bd440bc2, stable-copied). Bin B: Kbuild unchanged, the Kconfig-level cause is outside kfold's model; kfold would only show USB objects failing a conjunct.",
              "quote": "it is implicitly forcibly enabled by UDL driver which selects CONFIG_USB",
              "url": "https://www.mail-archive.com/linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org/msg2173605.html",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "LKML ARC HSDK 2020",
              "reopened_and_verified": true,
              "date": "2020-05-26"
            },
            {
              "claim": "i.MX7: the reset driver became modular, so a defconfig that relied on the default no longer built it in and had to select it explicitly. Example of a Kconfig default/tristate change changing which objects a standard config builds. Bin B.",
              "quote": "i.MX7 reset driver now supports module build, it is no longer built in by default, need to select it explicitly.",
              "url": "https://www.mail-archive.com/linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org/msg2214210.html",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "LKML i.MX7 RESET 2020",
              "reopened_and_verified": true,
              "date": "2020-06-29"
            },
            {
              "claim": "Guenter Roeck (2015): incomplete removal of a dependency (PPC_OF) meant the serial driver used by powerpc qemu targets stopped being built, and boot tests failed. Found by his qemu boot tests, i.e. by a runtime symptom rather than a build diff. Older than the preferred window; mechanism class is unchanged. Bin B.",
              "quote": "Practical impact is that the serial driver used by powerpc qemu targets is no longer built into the test kernel.",
              "url": "https://www.mail-archive.com/linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org/msg868844.html",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "LKML tty PPC_OF 2015",
              "reopened_and_verified": true,
              "date": "2015-04-16"
            },
            {
              "claim": "A reviewer noticed that a patch left a source file in the tree that the Makefile no longer builds (ccp-platform.c superseded by sp-platform.c). Caught by manual review. A per-object diff of conditions (present in tree, condition false) would flag this class. Bin A. Old and weak.",
              "quote": "the original ccp-platform.c is no longer built.",
              "url": "https://www.mail-archive.com/linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org/msg1344775.html",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "LKML AMD SP ccp 2017",
              "reopened_and_verified": true,
              "date": "2017-03-02"
            },
            {
              "claim": "U-Boot 2023.01: a Fedora packager noticed that a host tool present before 2023.01 was no longer built, and a change that touched its build was reverted. The page does not show the Kconfig or Makefile mechanism, so it is only evidence that downstream packagers detect changed build output between releases by hand. Bin A at best (hostprogs may be outside kfold's object notion). Quote is by the packager (Peter Robinson), 2023-01-19, quoted in the thread.",
              "quote": "It builds fine for me too but that tool is no longer built.",
              "url": "https://www.mail-archive.com/u-boot@lists.denx.de/msg461629.html",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "U-Boot bmp_logo 2023",
              "reopened_and_verified": true,
              "date": "2023-01-19"
            },
            {
              "claim": "Fedora/RHEL kernel-ark: new config options are reviewed by hand on each rebase, with 'pending-*' directories holding unreviewed ones so builds do not block. This is the distro's process for keeping option decisions consistent across releases. Option-level; no object-level report.",
              "quote": "New configuration options are reviewed and set with each release, typically during the -rc1 to -rc3 releases.",
              "url": "https://gitlab.com/cki-project/kernel-ark/-/raw/os-build/redhat/configs/README",
              "evidence_type": "policy",
              "source_group": "Fedora/RHEL kernel-ark",
              "reopened_and_verified": true,
              "date": "n.d."
            },
            {
              "claim": "Yocto ships a task that warns when a requested option does not end up in the final .config. The docs do not say why an option may be dropped (dependencies, missing hardware). Option-level audit; does not say which objects are lost.",
              "quote": "Running this task produces warnings for when a requested configuration does not appear in the final `.config` file",
              "url": "https://docs.yoctoproject.org/kernel-dev/common.html",
              "evidence_type": "tool",
              "source_group": "Yocto kernel-dev",
              "reopened_and_verified": true,
              "date": "n.d."
            },
            {
              "claim": "NixOS kernel build compares requested options with the final .config and prints 'unused option' / 'option not set correctly' errors. Its own comment names the mechanism: later Kconfig answers override earlier ones. Independent confirmation of the silent-drop class by a distro, with option-level detection.",
              "quote": "`make config' often overrides answers if later questions cause options to be selected.",
              "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/master/pkgs/os-specific/linux/kernel/generate-config.pl",
              "evidence_type": "tool",
              "source_group": "NixOS kernel build",
              "reopened_and_verified": true,
              "date": "n.d."
            },
            {
              "claim": "Android GKI FAQ: vendors layer a config fragment on gki_defconfig and must examine options by hand; diffconfig is the suggested comparison. Shows manual reconciliation of configs, option-level only.",
              "quote": "There's no way to avoid some manual examination of configuration options to optimize the fragment.",
              "url": "https://source.android.com/docs/core/architecture/kernel/gki-faq",
              "evidence_type": "policy",
              "source_group": "Android GKI docs",
              "reopened_and_verified": true,
              "date": "n.d."
            },
            {
              "claim": "Nadi et al. (MSR 2013), read from the PDF: variability anomalies introduced by incomplete Kconfig patches stay in Linux for an average of 6 releases before being fixed. Scope: #ifdef-level referential anomalies found by undertaker (1,077 in v3.6, 420 referential), not Kbuild objects. Supports only 'cross-artifact inconsistency persists across releases', not Kbuild-condition drift.",
              "quote": "stay in Linux for an average of 6 releases",
              "url": "http://www4.cs.fau.de/Publications/2013/nadi_13_msr.pdf",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
              "source_group": "Nadi et al. MSR 2013",
              "reopened_and_verified": true,
              "date": "2013-05"
            },
            {
              "claim": "LWN (2025-09): Linux 6.16 has 32,468 configuration options on x86_64, more than twice the count in 2.6.37, and the number of tested configurations is unknown. Scale context for why option-level diffs are large and object-level summaries are attractive.",
              "quote": "it is certain that not all of them have been tested individually",
              "url": "https://lwn.net/Articles/1034811/",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
              "source_group": "LWN Alden 2025",
              "reopened_and_verified": true,
              "date": "2025-09-10"
            }
          ],
          "existing_solutions": [
            {
              "tool_or_process": "scripts/diffconfig, make oldconfig/olddefconfig/listnewconfig",
              "what_it_does": "Compares two .config files and lists changed option values; olddefconfig carries an old config to a new tree with new options at defaults. This is the documented procedure for upgrades and for regression reports.",
              "limits": "Option granularity only. It does not say which objects start or stop building, does not show Makefile condition changes, and cannot attribute a change to a commit. The docs themselves warn that renamed symbols make old configs unreliable.",
              "url": "https://docs.kernel.org/kbuild/kconfig.html"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Yocto do_kernel_configcheck",
              "what_it_does": "Warns when a requested option is missing from the final .config or when a hardware fragment overrides a policy option.",
              "limits": "Option-level; checks only options someone requested; does not map to objects; does not explain the dropped dependency chain.",
              "url": "https://docs.yoctoproject.org/kernel-dev/common.html"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "NixOS generate-config.pl",
              "what_it_does": "Post-checks the final .config against requested answers and reports 'unused option' / 'option not set correctly' (required options are errors unless ignoreConfigErrors is set).",
              "limits": "Per-option, per-build; no cross-release object diff; no Makefile conditions.",
              "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/master/pkgs/os-specific/linux/kernel/generate-config.pl"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "ChromeOS kernelconfig checkconfig",
              "what_it_does": "Runs olddefconfig into a temp dir, diffs each chromeos/config file against the regenerated one, prints a unified diff and exits non-zero on any difference.",
              "limits": "Keeps config files normalized; says nothing about which objects change. Fetched through a summarizer.",
              "url": "https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel/+/refs/heads/chromeos-6.6/chromeos/scripts/kernelconfig"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "U-Boot buildman -K, -B, -S",
              "what_it_does": "-K shows differences in effective configuration between one commit and the next; -B breaks image size change down to function level; -S shows image size change.",
              "limits": "Board (defconfig) granularity and needs builds; -K is option-level; size deltas show effects after compilation, not build conditions. The 'build only boards that use changed files' idea is still a TODO in the docs.",
              "url": "https://docs.u-boot-project.org/en/latest/build/buildman.html"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Fedora/RHEL kernel-ark config review (pending-* directories, evaluate_configs)",
              "what_it_does": "Staged manual review of new options per rebase; evaluate_configs shows how a CONFIG is layered across priority files.",
              "limits": "Manual and option-level; no object-level view of what a rebase changes.",
              "url": "https://gitlab.com/cki-project/kernel-ark/-/raw/os-build/redhat/configs/README"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "kernelconfig.io",
              "what_it_does": "Web lookup of a Kconfig option per kernel version and architecture (help text, depends, 'since version', link to the source file).",
              "limits": "Shows Kconfig metadata only. The pages I opened show no Makefile object name and no 'compiled when' condition; no diff across versions was visible.",
              "url": "https://www.kernelconfig.io/"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "kmax / klocalizer / koverage",
              "what_it_does": "Extracts Kbuild and Kconfig constraints for one tree; klocalizer builds or repairs a config; koverage reports patch coverage for a config.",
              "limits": "The README describes no comparison between two versions or two constraint sets; the tools work on one tree or one config at a time. Usage examples show separate setups per version only.",
              "url": "https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Manual practice: git log/blame on Makefiles, boot tests, reviewer attention",
              "what_it_does": "Reviewers catch orphaned files (ccp-platform.c); Roeck's qemu boot tests exposed the PPC_OF case; Fedora packagers noticed the missing U-Boot tool.",
              "limits": "Detection by symptom and by chance; the cause takes days to isolate (Debian #1118653 took at least six days and two hypotheses).",
              "url": "https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=1118653"
            }
          ],
          "remaining_gap": "No tool in the sources answers, at object granularity, 'for the same configuration, which objects changed build condition between release A and B, and which Makefile or Kconfig change is responsible'. Everything found is option-level (diffconfig, Yocto, NixOS, ChromeOS, buildman -K) or single-tree (kmax suite). I did not verify that nothing exists: artifact-level comparisons of build outputs (for example module lists from two builds) were not checked. Also open and outside kfold: the Kconfig-level root cause (which select or depends flip dropped the option), runtime and boot-time effects, and module-level semantics (modules.order, modpost). Demand for a dedicated tool is not demonstrated: the Linux regression tracker says it publishes no cause statistics, and no survey or maintainer statement asking for such a diff was found. Recent (2023-2026) LKML instances of 'object stopped building after a release' are thin in what I could reach; the best recent cases are Debian's tracker and changelog.",
          "kfold_contribution": "Modest and specific. (1) A 'diff' view on top of the existing symbolic executor: run it on two trees, and for each object (matched by path) classify its build condition as unchanged, removed or added, or with an SMT check old-implies-new and new-implies-old (looser or tighter), listing the objects whose conditions mention a changed symbol. For Bin A this gives per-object answers from the Makefile logic and, if the guard origin is recorded (unverified whether kfold stores source lines), a blame target. (2) For Bin B, a verdict diff of why under a fixed config: objects built under the old config migrated by olddefconfig but not built under the new tree, each with the failing conjunct, which is the object-level answer the Debian #1118653 diagnosis lacked. (3) Whole-tree analysis takes about a minute per tree (2,225 Makefile instances, 32.9k objects at 7.2.8, own data), so running it per release is cheap compared with kclause at 2-3 minutes plus kmax at 10-15 minutes reported by the krepair paper. It would not find Kconfig root causes, would not diff #ifdef blocks, and its Kconfig model is an over-approximation used only by config-for. Position as an exploratory use case that falls out of the engine, backed by a measured recall on a labelled set, not as a motivating need.",
          "quantitative_facts": [
            "Nadi et al. (MSR 2013, read from PDF): referential variability anomalies persist an average of 6 releases before fixing; 10,263 Kconfig patches and 25,410 CPP patches mined over v2.6.37-v3.6; undertaker reported 1,077 anomalies in v3.6, of which 420 (39%) referential. Scope is #ifdef anomalies, not Kbuild objects.",
            "LWN 2025-09: Linux 6.16 has 32,468 options on x86_64, more than twice 2.6.37.",
            "Debian linux 6.10-1~exp1 (2024-07-18) lists five 'Enable X instead of Y' or 'Remove X' symbol replacements caused by upstream renames in a single release entry; 6.11~rc4 (2024-08-21) adds more (for example ACPI_NUMA 'now enabled automatically'). Counts cover only the first ~500 KB of a 2.6 MB changelog that I scanned through a summarizer, so they are lower bounds for that window and not a rate.",
            "Debian #1118653 timeline: reported 2025-10-23 16:26 (6.17.2-1~exp1; 6.16 was fine), first diagnosis the same evening, revised diagnosis 2025-10-29, final confirmation 2025-11-05; archived Jan 2026.",
            "krepair paper (as cited in earlier dossier, not re-opened here): kclause typically 2-3 minutes and kmax 10-15 minutes per tree; kfold whole-tree about 1 minute on Linux 7.2.8 (own data).",
            "kfold assets for the experiment: Linux 7.2.8 (2,225 Makefile instances, about 32.9k objects); 437 commits v7.2.7..v7.2.8 of which 402 touch .c/.S; 400 why verdicts already checked against builds (own data).",
            "Searches: the WebSearch budget was exhausted at the start. About 45 site-native fetches were used instead (mail-archive.com list searches, docs, Debian sources and tracker, raw repo files). The mail-archive linux-kernel full-text index returned no post-2020 hits for six phrase queries about 'no longer built' and similar; treat that as index coverage, not as absence."
          ],
          "proposed_experiment": "Goal: measure whether an object-level condition diff recovers independently labelled build-affecting changes, and how readable its output is. Two extra trees beyond 7.2.8 are needed (checked out from git, not built); each whole-tree kfold run takes about a minute, run one at a time on this machine.\n\n1. Ground truth from Debian changelogs: take the config-affecting entries for upstream bumps 6.9 to 6.10 and 6.10 to 6.11 (for example USB_ONBOARD_HUB to USB_ONBOARD_DEV, DRM_DP_CEC to DRM_DISPLAY_DP_AUX_CEC, ARCH_R9A07G043 'now depends on NONPORTABLE', ACPI_NUMA 'now enabled automatically'; I2C_DESIGNWARE_CORE gating from 6.12~rc6 is a third pair). Label each by bin: A (Makefile or file-selection change) or B (Kconfig change with unchanged Kbuild).\n2. Run kfold on the two trees and diff per-object conditions with SMT implication checks. Report: counts of objects added, removed, looser, tighter and unchanged; for each labelled entry, whether some object's condition change mentions the old or new symbol (recall); how many changed objects there are per release pair (if thousands, the diff needs grouping by Makefile commit to be readable); percentage attributable to one Makefile or Kconfig commit via git log -S or blame.\n3. Bin B check: take the Debian 6.9 config, migrate it with olddefconfig onto the 6.10 tree, and use why to list objects true under the old config on 6.9 but false under the migrated config on 6.10, each with its failing conjunct. Compare against the labelled entries (this is the object-level answer for cases like #1118653).\n4. Control: v7.2.7 to v7.2.8 (stable point release, assets already at hand): expect near-zero condition changes; any non-zero is either a real Makefile or Kconfig change in the stable queue or a false positive worth inspecting.\n5. Validation without new builds: for 30 sampled changed objects, check the predicted verdict against the existing 400-verdict method; physical builds are not rerun here (CLAUDE.md requires asking first). Fallback using only current assets: diff why verdicts across the four 7.2.8 builds (i386 tinyconfig, x86-64 defconfig, Debian, allmodconfig) and report the discriminating conjunct per object, which covers the 'or configs' half of D7 but is weaker as evidence.\n6. Yamada subdir-y case as a fidelity test: check whether kfold's model distinguishes 'object compiled' from 'module listed in modules.order'. If not, report it as an out-of-scope limitation.",
          "risks": "Demand risk: the evidence shows the phenomenon, not requests for a tool. The regression tracker publishes no cause statistics, and practice is olddefconfig plus diffconfig. Reviewers may call it a re-run of the engine on two trees. Scope risk: Bin B causes live in Kconfig and are outside kfold; the HSDK and i.MX7 cases would only show failing conjuncts. Modelling risk: Kbuild semantics such as modules.order or hostprogs may be outside the object notion (Yamada 2019, U-Boot bmp_logo). Evidence quality: the Debian changelog and bug are one source group; changelog scan covered only the first ~500 KB of 2.6 MB through a fetch summarizer; several quotes are summarizer clauses, so re-check against the page before printing. U-Boot bmp_logo mechanism not shown on the page. Nadi 2013 is about #ifdef anomalies, not Kbuild. Coverage: WebSearch budget was exhausted, so no new open-web searches; mail-archive index returned nothing after 2020 for these phrases; lore.kernel.org remains unreachable; recent LKML instances are therefore under-sampled. Earlier dossier items dropped or not re-opened: Gentoo forum [169] (unattributable), Passos 2015 [102] and the 2016 MICROCODE thread [218] (not re-opened, treat as unverified). Novelty: do not claim first; kmax-family tools already export per-tree constraints and a user could diff them, which I did not test.",
          "confidence": "medium"
        },
        "verdicts": [
          {
            "lens": "Evidence auditor for D7 (why an object stopped or started being built across releases or configs: condition diff)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "Debian #1118653 narrative is wrong as written. I re-opened the tracker: the report is 2025-10-23, and Hutchings's first diagnosis (new NETFILTER_XTABLES_LEGACY symbol) was acted on. The bug was closed as fixed in 6.17.5-1~exp1 on 2025-10-25 via 'netfilter: Enable NETFILTER_XTABLES_LEGACY'. Blank reopened it on 2025-10-29 with 'this problem is actually unrelated' and the NETFILTER_ADVANCED=y override theory. Bonaccorso then showed that 6.17.6 has CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_TARGET_MASQUERADE=m (via select from IP_NF_TARGET_MASQUERADE). Hutchings (2025-11-05) answered 'Right. It is only disabled on -rt' and called that 'a separate issue'. So Blank's quoted sentence was refuted in the thread, not confirmed. The reporter's actual libvirt NAT failure was never explained, and the reporter's update-alternatives output pointed to iptables-nft, which weakens the legacy-symbol theory. Dossier claims 'the real cause was a Debian config override' and 'final confirmation 2025-11-05 (only disabled on -rt)'. They are misleading. The case does not show a six-day diagnosis converging on a cause. It shows an unresolved or misattributed diagnosis. That is arguably a stronger 'hard to diagnose by hand' anecdote but cannot be cited as a resolved Bin B case.",
              "Blank's quote is verbatim but split across a line break in the page ('So it ends up disabled as' / 'we also override the default selection with NETFILTER_ADVANCED=y.'). The message is #54 in the tracker (the dossier's message numbering is not used here).",
              "Debian changelog (a single source group; the page is 2.6 MB and I could read only the first 500 KB through a summarizer): I verified verbatim '[arm64] Enable USB_ONBOARD_DEV instead of USB_ONBOARD_HUB' (6.10-1~exp1, Hutchings, Thu 18 Jul 2024), '[riscv64] Disable ARCH_R9A07G043 as it now depends on NONPORTABLE.' (6.7.1-1~exp1, Bastian Blank, 22 Jan 2024), and '[arm64,armhf,riscv64,x86] Enable I2C_DESIGNWARE_CORE, now gating...' (6.12~rc6-1~exp1, 10 Nov 2024). The dossier's 'five symbol replacements in the 6.10-1~exp1 entry' was NOT checked (only the USB_ONBOARD line was). The R9A07G043 entry is by Blank, not Hutchings, which matters only for attribution. The URL names version 7.3~rc7-1~exp1, a changelog snapshot that is unstable by construction; cite a fixed snapshot or the git commit.",
              "The three changelog items prove only that Debian edits option symbols after upstream changes. They are all option-level facts and say nothing about objects stopping to build. 'Without these edits the corresponding objects would silently stop being built' is an inference, not in the source.",
              "ccp-platform.c (2017): the quote is by Mark Rutland in an RFC v2 review of a patch series (Brijesh Singh's 'Introduce the AMD Secure Processor device'), reproduced in Andy Shevchenko's reply of 2017-03-03. The patch under review itself dropped ccp-platform.o from the Makefile and left the file in place. It is a review-time catch within one patch, not a release-to-release regression. Brijesh replied 'Good catch'. It never reached a release. It is weak support for a cross-release diff; it is a lint-style 'file present, no object' case (kfold blindspots-like, not diff).",
              "U-Boot bmp_logo: the page is Peter Robinson, dated Fri 20 Jan 2023 00:30 -0800 (dossier says 2023-01-19; likely a timezone or archive-date difference, minor). The thread is Fabio Estevam's 'Revert \"config: tools only: add VIDEO to build bmp_logo\"'. The page does not show the Kconfig or Makefile mechanism, so Bin A classification is not supported; bmp_logo is a hostprog, outside the object notion. The dossier already admits this.",
              "Yamada subdir-y patch: verified (Thu 8 Aug 2019, '[PATCH v2] kbuild: show hint if subdir-y/m is used to visit module Makefile', cites ff9b45c55b26, quote verbatim). It is a Kbuild semantics change affecting .ko output, not .o compilation, and the thread is one maintainer's view (single source). It is not evidence of demand for a diff tool; the fix chosen was a build warning.",
              "Independence: the Debian items (changelog and bug) are one team/source group. The three 2020 LKML items (ARC HSDK, i.MX7, plus the 2015 PPC_OF) are three different submitters but all are Bin B Kconfig-default or select changes fixed by one-line defconfig edits, so they show the fix is cheap and known once noticed, not that diagnosis is hard. i.MX7 (Anson Huang, 2020-06-29) and HSDK (Paltsev, patch 2020-05-26 as seen in Greg KH's stable [PATCH 5.6 002/126] mail) are verbatim and correctly dated. Note the HSDK page is a stable backport mail, not the original patch.",
              "Roeck PPC_OF (2015-04-16): verbatim and correct (the quote is in a reply on '[PATCH] tty: Remove leftover dependencies on PPC_OF'), caused by commit 'powerpc: kill PPC_OF'. Out of the preferred window, found by qemu tests; a symptom-level detection, as the dossier says.",
              "Nadi et al. MSR 2013: I read the PDF directly (pages 1-4). Verified: 'stay in Linux for an average of 6 releases', 1,077 anomalies in v3.6, 420 (39%) referential, 10 releases v2.6.37-v3.6. These are #ifdef referential anomalies (dead blocks on missing Kconfig features), and the PDF is by Dietrich, Tartler, Lohmann (undertaker group), so it counts as one prior-art source and not as Kbuild-object drift. I did not check the 10,263 and 25,410 patch counts (not on pages 1-4). The 2013 date and the nature of the anomalies make it weak motivation for D7.",
              "Policy quotes verified: kconfig.html (paraphrase in dossier: 'new kernel releases may rename config symbols' is verbatim; the page recommends 'make listnewconfig' or 'make oldconfig' plus 'scripts/diffconfig .config.old .config | less'), bisect guide ('going back and forth between different versions can alter .config files in odd ways', plus 'in some cases render the result of your bisection meaningless', so the dossier's 'can make a bisection meaningless' is accurate, with 'in some cases'), reporting-regressions ('This can be achieved using `make olddefconfig`, as explained in more detail below.'), kernel-ark README (quote verbatim; pending-* directories confirmed), Yocto do_kernel_configcheck (verbatim), NixOS generate-config.pl (comment and 'unused option' / 'option not set correctly' strings verbatim), Android GKI FAQ (verbatim and diffconfig suggested). LWN 2025-09-10 (Daroc Alden): 32,468 options on x86_64, 'more than twice as many ... compared to ... 2.6.37', and the quote are verbatim. These all show practice is option-level; none shows anyone asking for object-level diff. The dossier's own admission that demand is undemonstrated stands, and I found nothing to contradict it or support it.",
              "Not verified (budget): WebSearch is exhausted for this session, so I could not search for contradicting prior art (for example tools or posts that diff modules or object lists across kernel versions, kmax-based diffs, or distro scripts comparing modules.order or built-in.a between builds). The dossier itself admits it did not check artifact-level comparisons of build outputs (module lists), which is the most likely existing 'solution'. Debian's kernel packaging, for example, has an ABI check (abi-whitelist/ABI reference comparison) and Fedora/CKI compare built outputs; I did not open these and make no claim. This gap weakens 'no tool answers this'.",
              "krepair timing ('kclause 2-3 min, kmax 10-15 min') is not re-opened by the dossier and I did not re-open it. Do not print without checking. The CIP IRC figure in the other claims (5-10 min) is a different data point and conflicts in range.",
              "The anecdote set is thin for D7 in the 2023-2026 window: only Debian #1118653 (2025), whose resolution undermines it, the Debian changelog lines, and the U-Boot 2023 note. The 2015-2020 LKML cases are old and all single-symbol Kconfig changes.",
              "Gap-scout claims G0-G25 were not audited here beyond a read-through: they concern allmodconfig/COMPILE_TEST coverage, not cross-release condition changes, and none gives evidence for D7. G62 (Maintainers Summit, Torvalds on allmodconfig) is flagged by its own entry as summarizer-sourced; re-open before citing."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Debian #1118653: replace the dossier narrative. Correct version: reported 2025-10-23 for 6.17.2-1~exp1 (standard amd64 flavour); Hutchings proposed enabling the new NETFILTER_XTABLES_LEGACY symbol the same day; closed 2025-10-25 as fixed in 6.17.5-1~exp1 by that enabling; reopened 2025-10-29 when Blank claimed it was unrelated and blamed NETFILTER_ADVANCED=y; 2025-11-02 Bonaccorso observed the masquerade option is =m in 6.17.6 via select from IP_NF_TARGET_MASQUERADE; 2025-11-05 Hutchings confirmed 'It is only disabled on -rt'. The reporter's libvirt failure was not conclusively explained. Do not write 'the real cause was a Debian config override' and do not cite Blank's sentence as the finding.",
              "'The kernel team diagnosis took at least six days and two hypotheses' should read: the thread went through at least two competing hypotheses between 2025-10-23 and 2025-11-05 without a confirmed cause for the reporter's symptom (and 13 days, not six, to the last message).",
              "6.10-1~exp1 'five symbol replacements': only USB_ONBOARD_DEV instead of USB_ONBOARD_HUB was verified verbatim; re-open the entry for the other four before printing. Attribute the 6.7.1-1~exp1 R9A07G043 line to Bastian Blank (22 Jan 2024), not Ben Hutchings.",
              "U-Boot bmp_logo date: page header shows Fri, 20 Jan 2023 00:30:58 -0800, not 2023-01-19 (use the list date or state the timezone). The thread is a revert request for 'config: tools only: add VIDEO to build bmp_logo'.",
              "ccp-platform.c: say 'Mark Rutland, in a review of an RFC patch (v2 19/32), 2017-03; page date 2017-03-03 (Shevchenko reply)', and that it was caught before merge, not across releases.",
              "HSDK: the cited mail is Greg KH's stable backport '[PATCH 5.6 002/126] ARC: [plat-hsdk]: fix USB regression' (2020-05-26), with the patch by Eugeniy Paltsev and Fixes: 5d50bd440bc2 ('drm/udl: Make udl driver depend on CONFIG_USB'). Cite as a stable backport, not the original posting.",
              "Nadi 2013: label the 6-release figure as the average lifetime of #ifdef variability anomalies (dead code blocks referencing missing Kconfig features) in v2.6.37-v3.6, not Kbuild-object drift; do not use it for D7 beyond 'cross-artifact inconsistency persists'."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "limitation",
            "summary": "Most quotes check out verbatim (Yamada 2019, i.MX7, HSDK, PPC_OF, kconfig, bisect and regression docs, kernel-ark, Yocto, NixOS, GKI, LWN, Nadi 2013, three Debian changelog lines), but the headline anecdote is misdescribed: Debian #1118653 ended with Hutchings saying the option is only disabled on -rt, after Blank's override theory was challenged and the reporter's own failure was never explained. The dossier's 'six days to a confirmed Debian config override' is therefore wrong. The other anecdotes are old, option-level, one-symbol Kconfig fixes that were fixed cheaply once noticed, or catches during patch review, and all Debian items come from one source. Nothing shows demand for an object-level diff tool, and I could not search for prior artifact-level comparisons because the WebSearch budget is spent. Verdict: partly. Use D7 only as an exploratory limitation or use case (a labelled-recall experiment), not as motivation, and cite none of the Debian bug narrative until reworded to the verified facts.",
