          {
            "lens": "Hostile competitor analyst for D7 (object-level condition diff across releases or configs)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "Dossier says artifact-level module-list comparisons 'were not checked'. They now are. Ubuntu's kernel packaging ran a post-build 'missing modules' ABI check against the previous build, with a modules.ignore list. Evidence: Haefliger's 2021 patch text 'In order to prevent a subsequent 'missing modules' ABI check failure' (https://lists.snapcraft.io/archives/kernel-team/2021-May/119745.html), and the cover letter 'Changing module configs from 'm' to 'y' currently requires the removal of the modules from the ABI' (https://lists.snapcraft.io/archives/kernel-team/2021-May/120142.html; the series subject appears as NACK in the list, only the subject was seen). In March 2024 the in-tree checks were removed because 'linux-buildinfo packages are now externally compared by swm' (https://lists.snapcraft.io/archives/kernel-team/2024-March/149288.html). So the symptom 'a module disappeared between releases' is already detected in a distro process, but after a build, at module granularity, with no cause. The commit body does NOT say why module-check was removed; a search summarizer claimed a reason that I did not verify and do not use. The 2021 patch message never names 'module-check'; that name appears only in the diff.",
              "FEVER (Dintzner, van Deursen, Pinzger, MSR 2016, DOI 10.1145/2901739.2901755) is direct prior art for Bin A at commit level. It builds MappingEdit entities from before/after models of Makefiles ('MappingEdit entities are created based on the differences of reverse engineered models of a Makefile, before and after the change'). It extracts symbol pairs by scanning for '+=' and ':='. It states 'FEVER only captures a part of the presence condition of each file' and 'FEVER only considers changes on a file level, and so is unlikely to resolve such complex constructs'. It also says 'FEVER does not capture those structures' for obj-y lists set in a parent Makefile. Reported accuracy is 82.6% on 300 commits (abstract, per search snippet and consistent with the pdf header text; the 82.6% figure was read in the paper abstract lines of the pdftotext output). Its repo (https://github.com/NZR/SPLR-FEVER) shows 1 star and the README says the implementation is 'extremely sensible to the environment'. Dataset covers 15 releases, 3.10 to 4.4 (search snippet, not re-opened). Kfold's semantic symbolic execution of the full Makefile hierarchy is a refinement of this problem, not a new problem; cite FEVER and Passos et al. (EMSE 2015, 500+ commits catalogued by hand) as related work.",
              "Kmax-family tools do not offer a cross-version diff, but building one is trivial. The kmax README (https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax) has no section on comparing versions; the only 'diff' there is a shell diff of two coverage JSON files. The Kmax paper (FSE 2017) ran on Linux v3.19 and v2.6.33.3 only to compare against KBuildMiner and GOLEM, not to study evolution. krepair (arXiv 2404.17966, Sec 4.3) states 'Kbuild Makefiles, which define constraints on source files, only need to be collected once for a given file until the Makefile source code changes', so per-file Kbuild constraints are already cached and amortized across patches and versions. 'Run the engine on two trees and diff the output' is therefore not a contribution by itself. At most the contribution is SMT implication classification (looser/tighter/added/removed) plus why-style conjunct attribution under a migrated config.",
              "Speed is a weak differentiator. krepair says 'kclause typically taking 2-3 minutes, kmax 10-15 minutes' per constraint collection 'depending on the Linux kernel version and the target architecture'. Kmax 2017 reports 'On average, Kmax takes 77.52 seconds for one architecture' on v3.19 (min 63.28 s, max 357.65 s; 2,325.55 s for 30 architectures). Different kernel versions and hardware, so kfold's about 1 minute for x86 on 7.2.8 is the same order as Kmax's 2017 number and must not be sold as a win without a same-machine measurement. Kmax also covers 30 architectures while kfold is evaluated on x86 here.",
              "In-tree precedent exists for commit-range diffs at the symbol layer. scripts/checkkconfigsymbols.py has '-d DIFF, --diff=DIFF Diff undefined symbols between two commits' and '-c' (Rothberg, 2015, https://lkml.iu.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/1503.2/00190.html; option text is from the 2015 post, current script may differ). It diffs symbol references, not object conditions. U-Boot buildman has '-K' (differences in effective configuration between one commit and the next), '-B' (function-level bloat) and '-S' (image size) (https://docs.u-boot-project.org/en/latest/build/buildman.html); all need builds and work per board. Its docs list the TODO 'checking commits for changed files and building only boards which use those files'. kernelconfig.io (https://www.kernelconfig.io/) shows no diff or object information on the page I opened (page may be incomplete).",
              "Dossier case Debian #1118653 is weaker support than stated. I re-opened the bug (https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=1118653). The root cause was a Kconfig select/default interaction ('So it ends up disabled as we also override the default selection with NETFILTER_ADVANCED=y', Blank, 2025-10-29). The bug was auto-closed on 2025-10-25 by an upload that enabled NETFILTER_XTABLES_LEGACY, reopened on 2025-10-29, and closed again on 2025-11-05. The diagnosis was an option-level problem that kfold's Kbuild conditions would not explain; at most why would show the failing CONFIG symbol. Treat it as a Bin B illustration of the phenomenon, not as a kfold use case. The Yamada quote is verified: 'However, people will have no idea if their Makefile suddenly stops working. In fact, I received questions from multiple people.' (2019-08-08, https://www.mail-archive.com/linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org/msg2072887.html), but it concerns module output, not .o compilation, as the dossier already notes.",
              "Demand evidence is absent. No source asks for an object-level cross-release diff. The bisect guide says 'going back and forth between different versions can alter .config files in odd ways' and offers no object tool, and distro practice is diffconfig, olddefconfig, and build-then-compare. Unsearched because WebSearch budget was exhausted mid-task: Debian ABI script contents (salsa and sources.debian.org paths returned 404, so 'Debian ABI check not opened; Ubuntu evidence used'), Fedora/Yocto/ChromeOS module-list diffs, kmax per-version pre-extracted formulas in older kmaxtools docs, PatchLens, 0-day and KernelCI behaviour on lost objects, and any 2023-2026 paper on build-condition evolution. Absence of hits is not evidence of absence.",
              "Source-quality notes: the FEVER, Kmax 2017, krepair, Passos and Rothberg quotes were checked in pdftotext output or fetched page text. The Ubuntu, Yamada, Blank, bisect-guide and buildman quotes come from page fetches. The search-snippet statements (FEVER dataset span, Passos '500 commits') were not re-opened beyond the pdf abstract."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Replace 'artifact-level comparisons of build outputs (for example module lists from two builds) were not checked' with: Ubuntu kernel packaging ran an in-tree post-build 'missing modules' ABI check against the previous release until March 2024, then moved the comparison to an external service. Module granularity, build required, no cause reported.",
              "Add FEVER (MSR 2016) and Passos et al. (EMSE 2015) to prior art for Bin A. The claim 'no tool does a Makefile-mapping diff' must be narrowed to 'no tool found does a semantic, full-hierarchy, per-object condition diff with SMT implication'. FEVER is text-level and single-Makefile.",
              "Soften 'running kfold per release is cheap compared with kclause 2-3 min plus kmax 10-15 min'. krepair says that collection time depends on kernel version and architecture. Kmax 2017 reported 77.52 s average per architecture on v3.19. Per-file caching already exists. Speed is not a differentiator without a same-hardware comparison.",
              "State that Debian #1118653 was a Kconfig select/default override, was auto-closed on 2025-10-25, reopened 2025-10-29 and re-closed 2025-11-05. 'At least six days and two hypotheses' is fair, but the diagnosis was option-level, so kfold would not have named the cause.",
              "Do not state a reason for Ubuntu's removal of module-check. The only verified reason is the swm external comparison of linux-buildinfo.",
              "The claim that kfold is 'the object-level answer' for Bin B is overstated. A why-diff names failing Kbuild conjuncts, but the Kconfig reason is out of scope. The Kbuild-only cases (Bin A) are the honest domain."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "related_work",
            "summary": "Verdict partly. The specific gap, a pre-build, per-object, semantic condition diff between two releases with SMT implication classes, has no direct existing tool in what I could reach. But the problem is not unaddressed, and the dossier overstated the gap. (1) Module disappearance across releases was caught post-build by Ubuntu's in-tree module-check until March 2024, when the comparison moved to an external service (swm over linux-buildinfo). (2) FEVER (MSR 2016) and Passos et al. (EMSE 2015) already extract Makefile mapping changes per commit, though textually, per file and without full presence conditions (FEVER 82.6% on 300 commits). (3) The Kmax family has no cross-version diff but trivially permits one and already caches per-file Kbuild constraints; so 'run twice and diff' is not a contribution. (4) Speed is a weak claim: Kmax 2017 reports 77.52 s per architecture on v3.19 and krepair reports 10-15 min depending on version and architecture. (5) Demand is undocumented, and the best recent case (Debian #1118653) was a Kconfig-level override that kfold would not explain. What kfold could honestly claim: object-level exactness of Makefile semantics (validated against builds), SMT-checked looser/tighter/added/removed classification, conjunct attribution under a migrated config, and portability to non-Linux Kbuild projects. Do not claim first. Use as related work, or as an exploratory task only if two extra trees are run for the Debian-changelog and v7.2.7 to v7.2.8 control experiments, with the Bin B limitation stated. WebSearch was exhausted mid-task, so the sweep is incomplete: Debian ABI scripts, Fedora and Yocto module-list diffs, PatchLens, 0-day and KernelCI behaviour, and recent 2023-2026 papers remain unsearched.",
