          {
            "lens": "FIT AND EXPERIMENT DESIGNER for D7 (why an object stopped or started being built across releases or configs: condition diff)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "use_in_paper": "rq3_task",
            "summary": "FIT. Only half of D7 is an object-selection problem that kfold's build conditions address. Bin A (the Makefile logic changed, a file was orphaned, a parent-directory gate moved) is object selection, and a per-object condition diff between two trees covers it. Bin B (the Kbuild condition is unchanged but the .config no longer satisfies it because of a Kconfig rename, select->depends, a new gate symbol or a distro override) is a Kconfig-specification and configuration-management problem. A condition diff reports ZERO change for Bin B by construction, because the Kbuild conditions are identical. Most of the documented evidence is Bin B: Debian #1118653, ARC HSDK, i.MX7, the Debian R9A07G043 and I2C_DESIGNWARE_CORE entries. kfold can add only a 'why' under a fixed config, which names the failing conjunct and not the root cause. The Yamada 2019 case is a downstream modpost/modules.order semantic, not object selection, so it tests model fidelity and is not a win. The C preprocessor and toolchain are not involved in either bin.\n\nREPO FACT NOT IN THE DOSSIER. tools/diff_build_evolution.py already implements the added/retired/unchanged/generalized/restricted/diverged classification with Z3 and differential witnesses. PLAN.md M5.3 says it was run on BusyBox and U-Boot. DEVTOOL_PLAN.md says 'kfold lint --diff' reports before/after conditions. The experiment is therefore mostly harness and validation work, not new engine work. I did not run the tool or verify those PLAN.md numbers. The M10.3 GNU Make precision/recall figures in PLAN.md look old and inconsistent with the '400 verdicts checked against builds' asset, so treat them as unverified.\n\nSTRONGEST EXPERIMENT (E-REL: semantic vs textual Kbuild diff across a major release pair, with a stable-update control).\nInputs: git checkouts (not builds) of the previous mainline release R-1 (assumed 7.1; check that the tag exists in the local clone), of 7.2, and of the stable pair 7.2.7 and 7.2.8. Four whole-tree kfold runs of about 1 minute each, run one at a time. 7.2.8 is already analyzed. Reuse tools/diff_build_evolution.py.\n- Step 1: match objects by path, using git diff -M to carry file renames and moves, and report moved objects as their own class. Classify each object as added, retired, equivalent, looser, tighter or diverged (SMT). Neutralize Kconfig symbol renames with a rename map taken from git log of the Kconfig files, and report rename-only changes as a separate class.\n- Step 2: attribute each changed condition to a commit. This requires recording the source line of the guarding Makefile statement. I found no evidence that kfold stores line numbers during symbolic execution. why.py recovers lines by text search (_find_line), which is a heuristic. Use git log -S / git blame on the Makefile as the first attribution and report its precision.\n- Step 3: baseline. A textual diff of 'obj-$(CONFIG_X) += foo.o' lines, scoped to the Makefiles that git diff shows as changed. Optionally also the Kmax constraint diff (tools/compare_kfold_with_kmax.py and the saved Kmax run exist for 7.2.8; one more Kmax run on R-1 is needed).\n- Step 4: audit a stratified sample of 100 changed objects, 25 per class, against the raw Makefile diff. Use two raters, a rubric written in advance, and a reported kappa. Cross-check the 7.2 side against the existing inventories and the 400 verdicts. For the older tree, use either the GNU Make evaluation tool or, if the user approves, one extra x86-64 defconfig build of R-1 so the inventory diff between R-1 and 7.2.8 becomes build-based ground truth. I estimate (not measured) that this build takes on the order of 1 to 3 hours at -j1, and CLAUDE.md requires asking first.\n- Step 5: control. Run 7.2.7 to 7.2.8 and expect the changed set to be explained exactly by the few Makefile/Kconfig-touching commits among the 437.\nMetric: (a) the number of objects with a semantically changed condition, as a fraction of about 32.9k; (b) the share of those objects whose own Makefile text did not change (an ancestor gate, an include, a composite-object rewrite or a variable indirection), which the textual baseline misses by construction; (c) the number of textually changed objects whose condition is SMT-equivalent (baseline false alarms); (d) precision on the 100-object audit; (e) commits needed to explain 90% of the changed objects.\nExpected headline form: 'Of N objects whose condition changed between R-1 and 7.2, x% are in Makefiles whose text never mentions them, and a textual diff of the changed Makefiles misses these and also raises F false alarms from condition-preserving rewrites; k commits account for 90% of the changes; 0 or near-0 changes on the 7.2.7 to 7.2.8 control other than the identified Makefile/Kconfig commits.' I cannot predict the numbers.\nFalsifiers: (1) a textual diff with simple parent-directory handling reaches within a few points of kfold's recall and precision, so symbolic execution is not needed for the diff; (2) the Kmax constraint diff gives the same set, leaving only speed as an advantage; (3) the audit precision is below about 90%, or the build-based inventory diff disagrees with the predicted diff on more than a few percent of the sampled objects; (4) most changed objects are Kconfig-rename-only or file-move-only; (5) the stable control shows changes with no corresponding Makefile or Kconfig commit, which means a false positive or nondeterminism.\nGround-truth check: the Debian-changelog labelled set proposed in the dossier (steps 1 to 3) is NOT a usable recall oracle. Those entries are Bin B config edits that a condition diff cannot detect, they do not name objects, and the Debian configs for 6.9 and 6.10 are not among the assets. Using it would give near-zero recall by construction and would be read as a failure. The only ground truth that truly exists for another tree is none, because all builds and the 400 verdicts are for 7.2.8. The audit sample is therefore human-labelled and, optionally, one extra build.\nFallback with zero new builds (config axis, weaker): diff the 7.2.8 inventories of the four builds (tinyconfig, defconfig, Debian, allmodconfig) against kfold's verdict diff. Ground truth is exact, but it is essentially the existing why check run at scale, and a reviewer will see it as a corollary.\nEFFORT: about 5 to 6 person-days. Harness and orchestration 1, rename/move handling 1, attribution 1, baselines 1, audit 1.5, control and write-up 0.5. Add 1 for a Kmax run on R-1, and 0.5 plus approval for the optional defconfig build.\nTHREATS. (T1) Demand is not documented, so this is exploratory. (T2) Bin B is out of scope, and it must be stated as such so the experiment is not read as end-to-end explanation. (T3) Matching by path is wrong under moves and renames; Kconfig renames create spurious condition changes. (T4) Differing entry settings, goals or arch per release can masquerade as condition changes; fix them across both trees. (T5) A kfold model error appears on both sides and cancels when the Makefile is unchanged, so the diff is more robust than a single-tree verdict, but errors in the changed Makefiles do not cancel. (T6) The 7.x release pair is an n=1 data point (one release pair). (T7) The audit is subject to rater bias. (T8) The over-approximate Kconfig model K must not be used in the diff itself. (T9) modules.order, modpost and hostprogs semantics are outside the object notion.\nPAPER PLACEMENT AND PLDI REVIEWER OBJECTIONS. Put it in RQ3 as one compact exploratory row restricted to Bin A, with a sentence in Limitations about Bin B. Do not use it as introduction motivation: the dossier itself says no source asks for such a tool and practice is olddefconfig plus diffconfig, and the Debian evidence is a single source group. Likely objections: (1) 'This is just running the engine twice' -- answer by measuring the textual baseline and the ancestor-gate share. (2) 'No demand' -- do not claim demand. (3) 'Kmax or klocalizer could do this' -- do not claim first, and compare directly. (4) 'No ground truth for old trees' -- state the audit and optional build. (5) 'Does not explain Kconfig-level causes' -- scoped out explicitly. If the baseline falsifier (1) fires, demote it to a one-paragraph limitation or drop it.",
            "issues": [
              "Fit: Bin B (Kconfig change, Kbuild unchanged) is NOT detectable by a condition diff, because the conditions are identical across the two trees. Most of the documented evidence (Debian #1118653, ARC HSDK, i.MX7, Debian R9A07G043 and I2C_DESIGNWARE_CORE entries) is Bin B. A why-diff under a migrated config needs the over-approximate Kconfig model K and a migrated Debian config for the older release, which is not among the assets.",
              "The dossier's proposed experiment steps 1 to 3 use Debian changelog entries as recall ground truth. That set is mostly Bin B, does not name objects, and its configs and trees are not in the assets. Recall measured on it would be near zero by construction.",
              "Step 5 of the dossier validates changed objects 'against the existing 400-verdict method'. Those verdicts and the four builds are for 7.2.8 only, so they do not validate conditions in any other tree. The older-tree side needs an audit, the GNU Make evaluation tool, or one new build, and the build requires user approval under CLAUDE.md.",
              "The dossier lists the diff view as a contribution still to be built. tools/diff_build_evolution.py already implements the added/retired/unchanged/generalized/restricted/diverged classification with Z3 and witnesses. PLAN.md M5.3 says it was run on BusyBox and U-Boot. I did not verify those results. A reviewer who knows the repo or artifact will see the diff as already existing.",
              "Attribution to a commit needs the source line of the guarding statement. I found no evidence that kfold records it during symbolic execution. why.py recovers lines by a text search (_find_line), which is a heuristic. Attribution would therefore be git log -S / blame on the Makefile, and its precision is unmeasured.",
              "The dossier says Blank found the real cause to be a Debian config override. The thread (opened) is less conclusive: on 2025-11-02 Salvatore Bonaccorso noted that NETFILTER_XT_TARGET_MASQUERADE=m is still present on 6.17.6 through a select by IP_NF_TARGET_MASQUERADE, and on 2025-11-05 Ben Hutchings said MASQUERADE is disabled only on the -rt flavour, because of an xtables/rt locking issue. The thread does not confirm that the reporter's libvirt setup was fixed. Do not cite it as 'the cause was a config override'. It is at most an example of two competing hypotheses within about six days (2025-10-23 to 2025-10-29).",
              "Debian USB_ONBOARD_HUB to USB_ONBOARD_DEV in 6.10-1~exp1: I could NOT re-verify this line. The changelog is 2.6 MB and I read only the 0-100k and 400k-500k windows, and the second fetch did not reach the 6.10 entry. The first window returned a different line, 'Enable USB_DWC3, USB_ONBOARD_DEV and PHY_SPACEMIT_K1_USB2' (6.19.2-1~exp1, 2026-02-17). Treat the dossier's 'instead of USB_ONBOARD_HUB' quote as unverified until the 6.10-1~exp1 entry is opened.",
              "Related work gap: the dossier's novelty check omits the Kbuild/Kconfig co-evolution literature (Passos et al., EMSE 2015, and FEVER, MSR 2016, which extracts changes in Kconfig, preprocessor C code and Makefile mappings from commits). The dossier also omits GOLEM, which the survey says works over the whole git history. These are the closest prior art for 'what changed in Kbuild conditions between versions' and must be cited. I opened FEVER; I did not open the Passos full text.",
              "Matching objects by path across releases breaks under file moves and renames, and Kconfig symbol renames create spurious condition changes. Without the rename handling described in the experiment, the changed-object count is inflated and the audit will show it.",
              "The Yamada 2019 case needs care. The object is still compiled while the .ko is not produced. It tests whether kfold distinguishes obj-y/obj-m from modules.order, and should be reported as a fidelity limit, not as a detection success."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Debian #1118653 first-report time: the bug page shows 2025-10-23 15:01 UTC (not 16:26). Ben Hutchings' first diagnosis (new NETFILTER_XTABLES_LEGACY option, intentional upstream) is at 18:21 UTC the same day. Bastian Blank reopened on 2025-10-29 and wrote 'And this problem is actually unrelated.' and 'So it ends up disabled as we also override the default selection with NETFILTER_ADVANCED=y.' (both verbatim from the page I opened). The final state on 2025-11-05 is MASQUERADE disabled only on -rt. The archive date is 2026-01-25.",
              "Yamada quote verified verbatim: 'However, people will have no idea if their Makefile suddenly stops working.' Author Masahiro Yamada, posted 2019-08-08. The patch adds a build-time warning when a Makefile reached through subdir-y or subdir-m sets obj-m, as a consequence of commit ff9b45c55b26. The dossier's reading matches this.",
              "Debian changelog entries re-verified verbatim in this session: '[riscv64] Disable ARCH_R9A07G043 as it now depends on NONPORTABLE.' under linux (6.7.1-1~exp1), Mon, 22 Jan 2024; and '[arm64,armhf,riscv64,x86] Enable I2C_DESIGNWARE_CORE, now gating the other' under linux (6.12~rc6-1~exp1), Sun, 10 Nov 2024. The I2C line wraps in the changelog, so the quote ends mid-sentence. The 6.10-1~exp1 USB_ONBOARD_HUB line is unverified (see issues).",
              "FEVER (Dintzner, van Deursen, Pinzger; MSR 2016, ACM, pp. 85-96) extracts mappings (Makefiles); its abstract says 'Our evaluation on 300 randomly selected commits, from two different releases, shows our results are accurate in 82.6% of the commits.' (verbatim from the TU Delft record I opened). This is prior art for commit-level feature changes, and the dossier does not mention it.",
              "The dossier's krepair timing comparison (kclause 2-3 minutes, kmax 10-15 minutes per tree) was not re-opened by the dossier or by me. Do not use it as a speed comparison for the diff until the arXiv 2404.17966 text is checked.",
              "Scope wording: replace 'kfold would show USB objects failing a conjunct' for HSDK with an explicit statement that the failing conjunct would be the USB option and that kfold cannot say that the UDL driver's select was replaced by depends."
            ],
            "sources_checked": [
