      {
        "id": "D8",
        "name": "Distro, Android, and embedded configuration maintenance",
        "dive": {
          "id": "D8",
          "name": "Distro, Android and embedded configuration maintenance: which objects does a config (or a config change) build?",
          "refined_description": "Problem statement. Downstream kernel integrators (Debian, Fedora/RHEL, Ubuntu, SUSE, Android vendors on GKI, ChromeOS, Yocto, OpenWrt, Buildroot, CIP) maintain many per-architecture or per-flavour configs by hand and by fragment. Sources show five recurring chores. (1) Reviewing every new Kconfig symbol on each upstream rebase (Fedora kernel-ark opens one merge request per source directory; Ubuntu refreshes every .config). (2) Answering requests of the form 'please enable CONFIG_X' or 'which drivers does flavour Y ship', still filed against Debian in 2025-26. (3) Keeping hand-written package-to-option lists in step with the kernel (OpenWrt kmod FILES lists, Buildroot LINUX_CONFIG_FIXUPS, Gentoo CONFIG_CHECK, Docker check-config, SUSE supported.conf, RHEL removed-driver tables). (4) Learning why a requested option or module did not take effect (Yocto configcheck, NixOS 'unused option', OpenWrt 'module is missing'). (5) Deriving per-config 'used source' lists for compliance and CVE triage; CIP does this with a full compiler-wrapped build that its README says takes hours.\n\nWhere kfold touches it. Only chores 2, 3 and 5 are really object-level questions. kfold's per-object conditions answer them statically: which objects a config or config delta adds or drops, which Kbuild conjunct blocks a requested object, and which minimal options bring an object in. Chore 4 is mostly Kconfig resolution (unmet depends/select), which the existing audits already detect after the fact. kfold's `why` starts from the final .config, so it complements those audits and does not replace them. Chore 1 is a per-symbol human-judgement process that kfold does not remove.\n\nHonest scoping. Every pain statement found for this problem is a process page, a bug title or an unanswered request. No operator was found asking for object-level output, and no survey of distro maintainers exists. Android GKI's constraint is the KMI/ABI, which depends on exported symbols and struct layout, not on object lists. The D8 pitch should therefore be a modest utility claim (cheap, explainable object deltas, replacing build-derived source lists) and not a claim of widely felt need.",
          "who_has_it": "Distro kernel packagers (Debian kernel team, Fedora/RHEL kernel-ark, Ubuntu kernel team, SUSE), who must decide which drivers each flavour ships. Android SoC/OEM vendors who layer a fragment on gki_defconfig. ChromeOS kernel config maintainers (6 flavours in the example guide). Embedded build-system integrators (Yocto/OpenEmbedded, OpenWrt, Buildroot, NixOS, Gentoo packagers). CIP and Siemens-style long-term embedded maintainers, who need per-config 'used source' lists for CVE triage and CRA compliance. Users filing 'please enable CONFIG_X' wishlist bugs.",
          "evidence": [
            {
              "claim": "Android GKI: vendors keep a device fragment on top of gki_defconfig and trimming it is manual; diffconfig is the suggested aid, which compares option values and not objects.",
              "quote": "There's no way to avoid some manual examination of configuration options to optimize the fragment",
              "url": "https://source.android.com/docs/core/architecture/kernel/gki-faq",
              "date": "current docs (n.d.)",
              "evidence_type": "policy",
              "source_group": "AOSP kernel docs",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Android GKI: a vendor-requested CONFIG change that touches the frozen KMI is rejected. The gating factor is ABI, not object membership, so kfold supplies only a partial input here (the objects a change adds).",
              "quote": "Any `CONFIG` change that affects the Kernel Module Interface (KMI) after it's frozen is rejected.",
              "url": "https://source.android.com/docs/core/architecture/kernel/kernel-code",
              "date": "current docs (n.d.)",
              "evidence_type": "policy",
              "source_group": "AOSP kernel docs",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Android compatibility: required kernel configs are enforced by VTS and a compatibility matrix, and some requirements are conditional on other options. This is a hand-maintained, Kconfig-level constraint set with no object semantics.",
              "quote": "Other requirements only apply if some other kernel config option has a particular value.",
              "url": "https://android.googlesource.com/kernel/configs/+/refs/heads/main/README.md",
              "date": "current docs (n.d.)",
              "evidence_type": "policy",
              "source_group": "AOSP kernel docs",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Yocto ships a post-hoc audit because fragment options can fail to reach the final .config. This is a Kconfig-resolution check on option values, not on objects.",
              "quote": "Running this task produces warnings for when a requested configuration does not appear in the final `.config` file",
              "url": "https://docs.yoctoproject.org/kernel-dev/common.html",
              "date": "current docs (n.d.)",
              "evidence_type": "tool",
              "source_group": "Yocto kernel-dev docs",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Yocto list 2017: after a branch upgrade the OP's fragments stopped applying and the audit task did not flag it. The maintainer's first answer was that the cause 'could be either' the recipe or the lower-level class. Older, but a direct pain statement.",
              "quote": "The kernel_configcheck task doesn't catch the error either.",
              "url": "https://docs.yoctoproject.org/pipermail/yocto/2017-October/038236.html",
              "date": "2017-10-01",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
