              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Hubaux, Xiong, Czarnecki (VaMoS 2012) survey of 97 Linux users: 20% need at least a few dozen minutes to activate an inactive option, and respondents complain of poor support for finding options and minimal configurations. It also says kernel users are mainly kernel developers and distro packagers. This is the only configuration-specific user survey found; it is 2012 and mostly Kconfig, not Kbuild.",
              "quote": "20% of the Linux users report that, when they need to change an inactive option, they need at least a “few dozen minutes”",
              "url": "https://gsd.uwaterloo.ca/sites/default/files/vamos12-survey.pdf",
              "date": "2012-01",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
              "source_group": "Hubaux et al. VaMoS 2012",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "LWN 2012: during the debate on distro Kconfig profiles, Ben Hutchings (Debian) said he sometimes does not know what depends on an option. Torvalds wanted a comment explaining why each option is in a distro config. A reader comment in the same thread says one Debian release can produce 30-40 kernel configs; that figure is a comment, not the article. Foundational and old.",
              "quote": "doesn't mean I know what's using or depending on it now.",
              "url": "https://lwn.net/Articles/507239/",
              "date": "2012-07-18",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "LWN 2012 distro-config thread",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "RHEL 9 documents removed hardware support as hand-written driver and device tables (for example liquidio, HNS-RoCE, several v4l/dvb devices) and tells users to compare lspci -nn IDs against them. The page offers no way to list which drivers a kernel enables.",
              "quote": "The following devices (drivers, adapters) have been removed from RHEL 9.",
              "url": "https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html/considerations_in_adopting_rhel_9/assembly_hardware-enablement_considerations-in-adopting-rhel-9",
              "date": "current docs (n.d.)",
              "evidence_type": "policy",
              "source_group": "Red Hat RHEL 9 docs",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Counter-evidence: LWN's 2025 overview of the kernel configuration space says popular distro kernels are well tested and that other option combinations are 'uncharted territory'. Distro configs are therefore the safe, reviewed corner, and the unreviewed risk lies elsewhere. The article is about Kconfig and Kmax and says little about distro workflows.",
              "quote": "The kernels used by popular distributions are well-tested",
              "url": "https://lwn.net/Articles/1034811/",
              "date": "2025 (dossier says 2025-09-10)",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "LWN 2025 Kconfig-space article",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Kernel CNA policy: the kernel team will not judge whether a CVE applies to a given configuration, so downstream distros and embedded vendors must do it. The CNA page itself does not mention kernel config options. This is the demand driver behind per-config object lists, and it is mostly D10 territory.",
              "quote": "the applicability of any specific CVE is up to the user of Linux to determine, it is not up to the CVE assignment team.",
              "url": "https://docs.kernel.org/process/cve.html",
              "date": "current docs (n.d.)",
              "evidence_type": "policy",
              "source_group": "Kernel CNA docs",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Siemens/CIP (OSS 2025 slides): triage that asks 'is this file compiled for my config' (Kmax formulas evaluated against the use-case config) excluded 90-95% of 2024 CVEs for the CIP defconfig. This is the best quantified payoff of object-level conditions for an embedded distro, but it comes from the tool authors, with no independent validation. Cross-reference to D10.",
              "quote": "For CIP kernels /w CIP defconfig 90% - 95% of CVEs reported in 2024 were deemed \"not relevant\"",
              "url": "https://opensource.siemens.com/events/2025/slides/Christoph_Steiger__Triaging_CVEs_for_the_Linux_Kernel.pdf",
              "date": "2025-06-04",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
              "source_group": "Siemens/CIP kernel-cve-triage",
              "reopened_and_verified": false
            }
          ],
          "existing_solutions": [
            {
              "tool_or_process": "scripts/diffconfig, scripts/kconfig/merge_config.sh",
              "what_it_does": "diffconfig compares two .config files by option value (Android's GKI FAQ recommends it); merge_config.sh merges fragments and warns about overridden values and requested values missing from the final .config.",
              "limits": "Option-level only. Neither shows which objects a fragment adds or drops, and neither explains why an option was dropped beyond naming the symbol.",
              "url": "https://source.android.com/docs/core/architecture/kernel/gki-faq"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "make localmodconfig / localyesconfig",
              "what_it_does": "Builds a smaller config from the current config plus the modules loaded (lsmod); LMC_KEEP preserves chosen paths.",
              "limits": "Driven by runtime-loaded modules of one machine, so unloaded but needed modules are lost unless kept. The docs name no caveats; how it maps modules to options is unverified here (the script could not be fetched).",
              "url": "https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/README.html"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Yocto do_kernel_configcheck (kernel config audit)",
              "what_it_does": "Reports requested fragment options that are absent from or differ in the final .config, with a Requested value / Actual value listing.",
              "limits": "Detects the drop after the fact and does not explain the Kconfig or Kbuild reason. A 2017 thread reports a case it missed.",
              "url": "https://docs.yoctoproject.org/kernel-dev/common.html"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "NixOS generate-config.pl; Gentoo linux-info.eclass CONFIG_CHECK; Buildroot LINUX_CONFIG_FIXUPS; Docker check-config.sh",
              "what_it_does": "Packagers hand-maintain the kernel options their software needs. NixOS prints 'unused option' or 'option not set correctly' after make config; Gentoo warns on '~' options; Buildroot's docker-engine package enables 31 options through fixups.",
              "limits": "Hand-written option lists checked post hoc on .config values. None maps options to objects or explains a miss. Buildroot's linux.mk showed no visible post-check on the resulting values (medium confidence, read through a summarizer).",
              "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/master/pkgs/os-specific/linux/kernel/generate-config.pl"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Android VTS / compatibility matrix and android-base-conditional.xml",
              "what_it_does": "Enforces required kernel configs, including conditional ones, at test time and at boot.",
              "limits": "Checks option values against a fixed requirement list. It does not compute what objects a vendor fragment builds.",
              "url": "https://android.googlesource.com/kernel/configs/+/refs/heads/main/README.md"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Distro hand-maintained module and driver tables (SUSE supported.conf, RHEL removed-device tables, OpenWrt kmod FILES lists, Debian kconfigeditor2 for sorting config files)",
              "what_it_does": "Record which modules or drivers are shipped or supported, and keep config files tidy. SUSE's supported.conf alone is about 168 KB of text.",
              "limits": "Hand-maintained and checked only at package or release time. kconfigeditor2 sorts and edits config files and does not report objects. Debian's own tooling for this was not examined in depth.",
              "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/openSUSE/kernel-source/master/supported.conf"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "CIP cip-kernel-config .sources generation (get_used_sources.py)",
              "what_it_does": "For each board config, runs a full kernel build with a compiler wrapper and records the source files seen, so a 'potentially used source' list lives in git per config and per branch.",
              "limits": "Full builds ('can take hours'), a toolchain per architecture, and a regeneration step run by hand. Includes headers and conservatively adds non-source files. Object conditions are not symbolic, so a config change needs a rebuild. The 7.2.y directory has no .sources files, though the 6.1.y-cip listing has 20 (a summarizer count; verify).",
              "url": "https://gitlab.com/cip-project/cip-kernel/cip-kernel-config/-/raw/master/scripts/get_used_sources.py"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "kernel-cve-triage (CIP/Siemens) using Kmax; cvehound; PatchLens",
              "what_it_does": "Decide whether a CVE-touched file is compiled for a given .config. kernel-cve-triage evaluates Kmax formulas against the config, cvehound uses its own Kbuild parser, PatchLens resolves conditions per patch.",
              "limits": "Kmax formulas take 5-10 minutes per kernel revision (author, 2024 IRC). No Kconfig dependency model in the first two, and cvehound is line-based. Claims of 90-95% exclusion are from the tool authors. See D10 for details; none of these tools reopened for this dossier.",
              "url": "https://gitlab.com/cip-project/cip-kernel/kernel-cve-triage/-/raw/main/src/cve_triage/triage/kmax.py"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "klocalizer / krepair / koverage (Kmax suite)",
              "what_it_does": "klocalizer produces a .config that builds a given compilation unit or repairs a config to cover a patch; koverage reports patch coverage of a config. This is the incumbent for 'which options bring object X in'.",
              "limits": "Per the dossier, it works per architecture through SuperC, with heavy setup. Its Kbuild formulas 'may not be exact', and no distro or embedded use was found. Taken from earlier dossier claims; not reopened here.",
              "url": "https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "kernelconfig.io; kernel-hardening-checker",
              "what_it_does": "kernelconfig.io is a symbol-level search for config information about kernel modules. kernel-hardening-checker audits Kconfig, cmdline and sysctl settings against hardening recommendations.",
              "limits": "Symbol or value level, not per-object Kbuild conditions. The hardening checker's example run reported 'OK' - 168 / 'FAIL' - 184 for one system.",
              "url": "https://github.com/a13xp0p0v/kernel-hardening-checker"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "ConfigFix and similar Kconfig conflict resolvers",
              "what_it_does": "Propose option changes that satisfy Kconfig constraints when a requested option cannot be enabled (ICSE SEIP 2021).",
              "limits": "Kconfig-only; it has no model of Makefile conditions. Whether it is in use at any distro was not found.",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2012.15342"
            }
          ],
          "remaining_gap": "Gap one: nobody exposes a fast, static, explainable answer to 'which objects does config A build that config B does not', with the options responsible. Distros and embedded projects now answer it by full builds (CIP .sources, 'can take hours'), by comparing .ko inventories after a build (OpenWrt kernel.mk), or by hand-written tables (RHEL, SUSE). Gap two: for a requested driver (a Debian 'please enable' bug), the minimal set of options and the blocking Kbuild/Kconfig conjunct are found by hand. Gap three: for a fragment, only option-value audits exist.\n\nWhat stays open for anyone, kfold included: (1) whether any maintainer wants this, since no operator quote requests object-level output; (2) Kconfig resolution of dropped options, which is the larger share of the pain in the Yocto, ChromeOS and NixOS sources; (3) ABI/KMI impact for Android, which needs exported-symbol and type data; (4) per-symbol review judgement in kernel-ark; (5) header-only and #ifdef-level effects, which object-level analysis cannot see.",
          "kfold_contribution": "Modest and specific. (a) Static per-config object lists and deltas. From the Kbuild condition table (about a minute for Linux 7.2.8, 32.9k objects) one can evaluate any .config, or diff two, without a build. This replaces the build-derived lists in the CIP style for the .o level, but not header-level dependency tracking. (b) Object-level explanation: `why OBJ --config .config` names the failing conjunct from Kbuild, for example a parent directory condition, whereas Yocto, NixOS and Gentoo checkers report only that an option value differs. (c) `config-for` as the answer to a 'please enable' request: given the objects for DLN2 or GPIB, return the minimal option set against a distro base config and verify with olddefconfig. (d) A Kbuild-condition check of hand-maintained module lists (OpenWrt FILES, SUSE supported.conf, Debian installer lists) against a target config, flagging entries whose object cannot be built or is only built in. This is a proposal and has not been measured. (e) Non-Linux Kbuild-style projects (BusyBox, Barebox, U-Boot, coreboot) are in kfold's evaluation, and nothing in D8 distinguishes it there. What kfold does not supply: KMI/ABI effects, Kconfig dependency repair beyond its over-approximate K model, or runtime/boot behaviour.",
          "quantitative_facts": [
            "Debian src:linux debian/config has 15 architecture trees (alpha, amd64, arm64, armhf, hppa, kernelarch-powerpc, loong64, m68k, powerpc, ppc64, ppc64el, riscv64, s390x, sh4, sparc64) plus config, config.cloud, config.rt, config.test and defines.toml at the top level (GitLab API listing, read 2026-10-17). Directory count only; it does not give the number of option settings.",
            "ChromeOS kernel-config guide example: 6 flavours (3 ARM, 3 Intel), edited through a layered hierarchy (generic, chrome-specific, architecture, board); the earlier dossier's 'about 10' is not supported by the page.",
            "Fedora kernel-ark: per-directory 'New configs in <path>' merge requests still appear in Oct 2026 (for example MR 4778, 2026-10-05, listing one symbol). MR 3665 dates from 2025-01-30. The MR numbers count all merge requests, so they do not measure the config-review rate.",
            "CIP cip-kernel-config: 7.2.y has 26 _defconfig files (x86: 8, arm: 9, arm64: 6, riscv: 3) and no .sources files; 6.1.y-cip has 20 .sources files for 28 defconfigs (summarizer counts, read 2026-10-17). The Makefile says .sources lists 'take a very long time to regenerate' and the README says generation needs a complete kernel build that 'can take hours'.",
            "Hubaux et al. 2012: 97 Linux users answered, 20% need at least a few dozen minutes to activate an inactive option (re-read from the PDF text).",
            "Buildroot docker-engine package: 31 KCONFIG_ENABLE_OPT lines, plus two conditional fixup sets; the file notes it is 'based on contrib/check-config.sh'.",
            "SUSE kernel-source supported.conf is a hand-maintained module-support file of about 168,549 characters (fetched size); entry count not seen.",
            "Siemens/CIP slides (tool authors, 2025): 90-95% of 2024 CVEs 'not relevant' for the CIP defconfig; about 5-10% of ~85,000 kernel files typically used. KCVEPruner slides (June 2026): Ubuntu 24.04 config leaves 1,807 CVEs (12.11% FP) versus 176 for tinyconfig. Both are from D10 and were not reopened.",
            "LWN 2025: Linux 6.16 has 32,468 options on x86_64 and a configuration count with 6,550 digits.",
            "Kmax run time: 5-10 minutes per kernel revision in the CIP triage (author, Aug 2024 IRC); kfold analyzes Linux 7.2.8 whole-tree in about one minute (own data).",
            "Counts found in the search budget: only 3 WebSearch calls succeeded. The dossier rests on about 45 direct page fetches (WebFetch plus one local pdftotext), not on 15 searches."
          ],
          "proposed_experiment": "Experiment: config-delta prediction against two real builds, plus two Debian requests. No new builds are needed. (1) Take the x86-64 defconfig and Debian-config object inventories from the existing builds of Linux 7.2.x. Have kfold evaluate each .config and predict the object set, then the added and dropped objects of defconfig to Debian (and, as a second pair, tinyconfig to defconfig). Report precision and recall of kfold's predicted .o sets per pair, split by directory, and list each false positive or false negative with its cause (composite objects, tristate, arch overlay, Kconfig model). Also report the time per config evaluation. (2) For each added or dropped object, name the responsible options from the chain and check them against `scripts/diffconfig` output, to show how many option flips change no objects and how many objects are gated by several options. (3) Worked examples from Debian BTS #1126062 (DLN2) and #1112317 (GPIB): run `why` against the Debian config for each requested object, run `config-for` to get the minimal option set, and compare it with the five or six options the reporters listed by hand. If Linux 7.2.8 lacks a requested symbol, say so and report it. (4) Optional external oracle: CIP .sources exist for 6.1.y-cip (x86: 7 lists in the summarizer count) but not 7.2.y, so using them needs a second kfold run on a 6.1 tree. Compare only the .c/.S-derived subset, since the lists add headers and all non-source git files conservatively. Expected cost: no builds, a few kfold runs; run them one at a time per the machine rules. Success is high recall and precision on object sets with explainable misses. Failure would show that composite objects or the K model dominate the errors.",
          "risks": "1) Search constraint: the shared WebSearch budget was exhausted after 3 successful queries, against the required 15. The new evidence comes from about 45 direct page fetches, including local text extraction of one PDF, and one search class (mailing-list full text) could not be run at all. Do not count the fetches as 15 searches. 2) Thin and mostly documentary evidence: nearly all items are docs and process pages, bug titles or unanswered requests. The only direct pain statements are from 2012 (LWN, Hubaux) and 2015-2017 (OpenWrt, Yocto), plus two Debian wishlist bugs with no visible replies. No operator asks for object-level output, and no distro-maintainer survey exists. 3) Mismatch of layer: most failures (dropped options, vanished configs, KMI) are Kconfig or ABI problems that kfold's Kbuild conditions only partly cover. A paper that sold D8 as 'config maintenance' would overclaim. 4) Counter-evidence: popular distro kernels are described as well tested; AOSP and RHEL keep working with fragments, diffconfig and hand tables; the Debian BTS shows requests, not a pain with the current process. 5) Source quality: many page quotes passed through a fetch summarizer and may be trimmed (the OpenWrt forum error path shows '...'), and I could not open Yocto bugzilla (403), so bug 15424 ('fragments with no defaults if previous build had defconfig') is only a list-page title with an uncertain date and is left out of the evidence. The CIP .sources counts are summarizer counts. The SUSE entry count, Debian udeb module lists and Fedora check-configs.pl behaviour were not verified. 6) Independence: the AOSP, Yocto, CIP and OpenWrt items each form one group; counting items as independent sources would inflate the weight. 7) The CIP get_used_sources.py analysis, the Siemens slide figures and the G74-G98 Kmax-based triage claims for D10 come from other agents' fetches (reopened false), and the CIP triage tool is a direct Kmax user, so an overlap claim against it needs a head-to-head. 8) The experiment depends on the Debian config in the assets matching the Debian version of the requests (6.12-6.19), so symbols may differ at 7.2.8.",
          "confidence": "medium"
        },
        "verdicts": [
          {
            "lens": "Evidence auditor, problem D8 (distro, Android, embedded configuration maintenance)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "Quotes and dates mostly check out. I opened about 20 URLs. The AOSP GKI FAQ, AOSP kernel-code, AOSP configs README, CIP README, CIP get_used_sources.py, Debian #1126062 and #1112317, kernel-ark MR 3665 and MR 4778, OpenWrt kernel.mk, ChromeOS guide, Yocto docs, Yocto 2017 post, LWN 507239, LWN 1034811, RHEL 9 docs, kernel CNA docs and the Hubaux PDF all exist and support the quotes as phrased. The Siemens slides support the 90-95% quote. The trouble is weight, not accuracy.",
              "The dossier itself concedes that no operator asks for object-level output, and my audit found nothing more. The evidence is documentation, process pages, two Debian wishlist bugs, and 2012-2017 anecdotes. It does not show that the pain is widely felt. The honest-scoping paragraph in the dossier is correct and should be kept.",
              "The Debian bugs are weaker than presented. Both pages show no reply, but the Debian kernel team did act on GPIB. Debian's changelog entry linux (6.19-1~exp1), dated 12 Feb 2026, reads '[arm64] drivers/gpib: Enable GPIB_GPIO as module'. It does not reference bug 1112317, and I did not find an amd64 GPIB entry in the changelog span I read (about Oct 2024 to Feb 2026). So 'no reply' may reflect the BTS page, not maintainer inaction. DLN2 is still '# CONFIG_MFD_DLN2 is not set' in debian/latest config, so the DLN2 request appears unhandled. The two bugs are also one pattern from two reporters, and they show requests, not a missing tool.",
              "OpenWrt-devel 2015 is partly rebutted by its own reply. The reply says the build log already flags built-in modules with a notice and packages with missing config options with a warning. It says the build deliberately does not fail, because some modules must be built in for ext4 root boot and small-flash routers use squashfs. So this is a design choice with existing diagnostics, not an undetected mismatch. The page lists the author as Dirk Neukirchen (replier), and the quoted phrase \"trying to load a module which doesn't exist\" is his description, not the original poster's. The dossier's reading of it as a pain statement is a stretch.",
              "OpenWrt forum thread 61768: the page shows 7 replies (8 posts), not six. The error text is truncated in the page, so the quote is not fully verbatim as given (the dossier marks it with '...'). The only cause the thread identifies is a code problem (an out-of-tree driver failing under -Werror on a newer kernel), and one user's `make defconfig clean` fix worked for them but not for another. The dossier already labels the cause 'mixed', but this thread is weak support for a Kbuild-condition problem.",
              "Hubaux 2012 is mis-framed in two ways. (a) The 20% figure comes from the subset of 81 usable answers; the dossier ties it to '97 users'. 97 filled the survey and 81 were used for quantitative results, so the denominator is 81. (b) The statement that kernel users are 'mainly the developers of the kernel and the packager of the kernel packages' is the authors' own claim in the text. The respondents' use was 'primarily for personal purpose', across 39 distributions, so the survey sample is mostly not distro packagers. The survey is also about Kconfig activation, not Kbuild objects. The supported use is background motivation only.",
              "Siemens/CIP slides: the 90-95% quote is verbatim, and the slide gives the date 2025-06-04. The slides contain no mention of Kmax (my text extraction found none), so the claim that Kmax formulas are evaluated against the config rests on kernel-cve-triage's kmax.py, which I did not reopen. The slides give no validation of 'No false negatives', which is listed only as a goal. Also the slide says 'For better results a better description of the use-case is required'. This is the tool authors' own result, one source, single-sourced from one talk/one project. Note it is the best quantified payoff in the dossier, but it supports D10 (CVE triage), not D8 config maintenance.",
              "CIP get_used_sources.py (now opened): it runs make mrproper, swaps AS/CC/HOSTCC for a wrapper, runs make oldconfig and a full make, and then adds other tracked files from git ls-tree. The dossier says it adds 'all non-C/H/S files conservatively'. The script actually skips dotfiles, C/asm sources, docs, samples, user-space tools and other architectures, so 'all' overstates it. The README says 'can take hours for the full set of configurations'. That is for the full set of configs, not per config. The dossier's 'full build, hours' framing should say 'for all configurations'. The README does not mention get_used_sources.py (the script is in the repo).",
              "ChromeOS: the page confirms 6 flavours (3 ARM, 3 Intel) and the 'If you miss one...' quote. Only one flavour is named in the text (chromeos-tegra2); the rest are in an image. The page is an old developer guide, so it is a weak current-practice source.",
              "kernel-ark: MR 3665 (2025-01-30) and MR 4778 ('New configs in kernel/irq', open, 2026-10-05, symbol REFCOUNT_INTERRUPT_KUNIT_TEST) both verified. The MRs are bot-generated and show the process, not pain. The dossier already notes this limit. Treat as a process description from a single project (Fedora/RHEL ARK).",
              "Independence: AOSP (GKI FAQ, kernel-code, configs README) is one group. CIP (cip-kernel-config, kernel-cve-triage, Siemens slides) is one group and also the same people as the CVE-triage tool. OpenWrt (kernel.mk, 2015 list, 2020 forum) is one project. Yocto docs and the 2017 post are one project. Counting them as 15+ separate items would overstate support. By independent sources, there are about 8 communities: AOSP, Yocto, ChromeOS, kernel-ark, Debian BTS, OpenWrt, CIP/Siemens, RHEL docs.",
              "Counter-evidence: LWN 1034811 (Daroc Alden, 2025-09-10) does say 'The kernels used by popular distributions are well-tested' and warns that other configurations are 'uncharted territory'. That is a Kconfig/Kmax overview and not a statement on distro workflows. The OpenWrt build already warns on built-in and missing modules. Yocto has kernel_configcheck. The kernel CNA page does not mention Kconfig at all. The 2012 LWN thread (Ben Hutchings: 'doesn't mean I know what's using or depending on it now') is verbatim in part, and the Torvalds 'comment explaining why it is needed' is the article's paraphrase, not a direct quote. The '30-40 configs per Debian release' number is a reader comment (user dlang), so it must not be cited as fact.",
              "Search budget: WebSearch was unavailable (shared budget exhausted), so I could not run new queries for contradicting evidence. I only checked adjacent facts by direct fetch (Debian changelog and config). Wider counter-evidence (Ubuntu annotations files, Debian tooling, Fedora scripts) is unchecked.",
              "Items I did not reopen: Buildroot, NixOS, Gentoo, SUSE supported.conf, localmodconfig, kernel-hardening-checker, ConfigFix, Hubaux's follow-ups. The dossier's Buildroot count of 31 options is flagged as summarizer-sourced and should be dropped or re-checked. The CIP '.sources counts' (20 for 6.1.y-cip, none for 7.2.y) are summarizer counts and unverified."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Hubaux: say 'of 81 usable answers, 20% need at least a few dozen minutes'. 97 is the number who filled the survey. Respondents' use was primarily personal, across 39 distributions. The 'mainly kernel developers and packagers' sentence is the authors' claim about who configures the kernel, not a respondent statistic.",
              "Debian GPIB: do not say the request was ignored. Debian changelog linux (6.19-1~exp1), 12 Feb 2026, has '[arm64] drivers/gpib: Enable GPIB_GPIO as module'. Say 'no reply visible on the BTS page' and note partial maintainer action; the amd64 outcome is unverified. DLN2: debian/latest config has '# CONFIG_MFD_DLN2 is not set', so that request is still open as of the 2026-10-16 changelog head (7.3~rc7-1~exp1). I read the debian/latest 'config' file, not every per-architecture config.",
              "OpenWrt-devel 2015: the reply (Dirk Neukirchen) says the build log gives a notice for built-in modules and a warning for missing config options, and that failing the build was avoided on purpose. Drop the framing 'config-versus-object mismatch found by a human, build did not fail' as a pain point, or add the reply. The quoted phrase is the replier's.",
              "OpenWrt forum 61768: 7 replies (8 posts), not six. The error line is truncated in the page. The only identified cause was a code problem (warnings treated as errors for an out-of-tree driver).",
              "CIP get_used_sources.py: it excludes dotfiles, C/asm sources, docs, samples, user-space tools and other-arch files when adding git-tracked files. It does not add 'all non-C/H/S files'. The 'can take hours' phrase is 'for the full set of configurations'.",
              "Siemens slides: the slides do not name Kmax. Attribute Kmax use to kernel-cve-triage's kmax.py, not to the slides. The 90-95% figure is for the CIP defconfig on 2024 CVEs with conservative checks and no independent validation. Slide scale facts: ~85.000 files, ~40.000.000 LoC, 'Typically only about 5% to 10% are used', 'Around 60 CVEs each week', 56% of ~1000 unfixed 4.19-EOL CVEs excludable.",
              "LWN 2012: the 30-40 configs figure is a reader comment by dlang, not the article. The Torvalds requirement is the article's paraphrase ('a comment explaining why it is needed').",
              "Yocto 2017: the author is Jonathan Haws, original post 2017-09-30 (reply by Bruce Ashfield dated 2017-10-01), subject 'Configuration fragments not making it into kernel configuration'. The dossier dates it 2017-10-01 (the reply date). Ashfield said the cause could be the recipe or the lower-level class, or Haws' configuration; he offered no fix. Running menuconfig fixed it for the poster.",
              "ChromeOS flavours: confirmed as 6 (3 ARM, 3 Intel), but only chromeos-tegra2 is named in the text.",
              "kernel-ark MR 4778 title is '[redhat] New configs in kernel/irq' (open, opened 2026-10-05). MR 3665 is '[redhat] New configs in lib/Kconfig' (2025-01-30, symbol CRC_OPTIMIZATIONS). The dossier's statement that the bot MRs are one per directory is supported."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "rq3_task",
            "summary": "I opened about 20 URLs. The quotes, dates and numbers in the dossier mostly verify (AOSP GKI FAQ and kernel-code, CIP README, Debian bugs 1126062 and 1112317, kernel-ark MR 3665 and 4778, OpenWrt kernel.mk, ChromeOS guide, Yocto docs and 2017 post, Hubaux, LWN 507239 and 1034811, RHEL 9, kernel CNA, Siemens slides). The evidence is accurate but thin, and the dossier admits this. It is all documentation and process pages, two unanswered wishlist bugs, and old anecdotes (2012-2017, plus a 2015 list post whose reply says the build already warns). Hubaux's 20% is of 81 usable answers and the respondents were mostly personal users, not packagers. The 90-95% result is single-source (CIP/Siemens, the tool authors) and belongs to D10. The Debian GPIB request got partial maintainer action (arm64 GPIB_GPIO enabled in 6.19-1~exp1), and DLN2 is still not set. I could not search for contradicting evidence because the WebSearch budget was exhausted. By independent source there are about 8 communities, not 15+ items. Verdict: partly. D8 should not be cited as proof of a widely felt need. It can serve as a worked-example task for the evaluation (config delta defconfig to Debian config, why/config-for on the DLN2 and GPIB requests, comparison against CIP .sources built the slow way), with the CIP build-time cost as the one concrete comparison point."
          },
          {
            "lens": "Hostile competitor analyst, D8 (distro, Android and embedded configuration maintenance). WebSearch was exhausted, so everything below comes from direct page fetches.",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "PER-FILE QUESTION ALREADY SOLVED, IN USE. D8 'gap one' says nobody exposes a static answer to 'which objects does config A build'. At per-file level that is wrong. CIP/Siemens kernel-cve-triage (https://gitlab.com/cip-project/cip-kernel/kernel-cve-triage, 134 commits, Apache-2.0, created 2024-07-11) runs `kmaxall` over arch/ and default paths, then `kreader --kmax-formulas <kmax> --show-constraints <file>.o`. It parses the And/Or formula and evaluates it against the enabled-option list (src/cve_triage/triage/kmax.py, read in full). Its README says 'kmax formulas must be generated internally' on the first run per kernel revision and caches them. That is an embedded-distro production use of per-object Kbuild conditions evaluated against a config. The same project's cip-kernel-config instead builds the lists with a full compiler-wrapped build.",
              "NOT ALL OF THE 90-95% IS FROM FILE-LEVEL KMAX. The Siemens slides (OSS 2025, read verbatim via pdftotext) say the tool runs 'a number of checks' per CVE, short-circuiting on 'not relevant'. These include 'simple checks based on introducing/fixing commits' and 'more sophisticated checks assess if files affected by the CVE are used'. The slides never name Kmax. The 90-95% is therefore the total of all checks. The slides add that for 4.19 EOL, of ~1000 unfixed CVEs, '56% can be excluded'. The slides' goal 'No false negatives' is a design goal, not a measurement. The dossier's attribution of 90-95% to Kmax formulas alone is not supported.",
              "'WHICH OPTIONS BRING OBJECT X IN, STARTING FROM A DISTRO CONFIG' IS SOLVED BY klocalizer. The kmax README example: 'klocalizer --repair .config -o allnoconfig_repaired --include drivers/usb/storage/alauda.o'. krepair (FSE 2024, arXiv 2404.17966) takes a starting config and changes few options: 'less than 1.53%' of options changed for 99% of patches, 98.5% mean patch coverage, build times '10.5x faster than maximal configuration files'. This is exactly what the D8 pitch (c) 'config-for as the answer to a please-enable request' proposes. The Debian DLN2 and GPIB requests are one `klocalizer --repair <debian .config> --include drivers/mfd/dln2.o` away, in principle. I did not run it.",
              "klocalizer HAS AN EXPLANATION AND A VIEW MODE. `klocalizer --view-kbuild --include <unit>` prints the Kbuild constraints for a unit and its parent directories, e.g. `[And(CONFIG_SQUASHFS, CONFIG_SQUASHFS_DECOMP_MULTI)]` (kmax docs/advanced.md). `kmax <dir>` prints the symbolic condition per unit. So the 'condition chain' is partly there. What it lacks is evaluation against a .config that names the failing conjunct. Naming that conjunct is a small step on top of an And() formula, so kfold's `why` is an incremental front-end over an existing formula, not a new capability. I found no tool that prints an object-level 'why not built' chain from a .config.",
              "NO WHOLE-CONFIG ENUMERATION OR DIFF FRONT-END WAS FOUND. advanced.md says there is no documented command to list the units a config builds; `koverage` checks specific file/line pairs, and `klocalizer --report-all` lists architectures, not units. cip-kernel-triage evaluates one file at a time (each call starts a new kreader process, no caching in kmax.py). I found no tool that outputs 'objects added/dropped from config A to config B' without a build. That is the genuine, small gap. It is trivially assembled from kmaxall output plus an evaluator, so the novelty is the packaging and speed, not the idea.",
              "THE DEBIAN BUGS ARE NOT OBJECT-KNOWLEDGE PROBLEMS. #1126062 (2026-01-21, DLN2) and #1112317 (2025-08-28, GPIB) both list the exact CONFIG_ options themselves. Both are wishlist, open, with no reply on the page. #1126062 notes 'Most major distributions support it in their current versions, e.g., Fedora, OpenSUSE, Arch, Ubuntu.' The bottleneck is a policy decision (size, security, support), not a missing tool. kernelconfig.io already shows, for CONFIG_MFD_DLN2, the module `dln2.ko`, depends (HAS_IOMEM, USB), selects (MFD_CORE), selected-by (GPIO_DLN2, DLN2_ADC), the source file drivers/mfd/dln2.c and the version range. LKDDb (cateee.net) shows the source file, dependencies and USB IDs. Neither shows a Makefile condition or a minimal option set.",
              "WHAT THE INCUMBENT'S CODE ADMITS (this replaces the dossier's unsourced 'may not be exact' quote). In kmax/klocalizer.py: 'Since this is not a Kbuild makefile, kmax does not process and cannot capture the constraints' (net/ and samples/ are hard-coded); 'If no kmax formula can be found, the path is assumed to be unconstrained'; 'TODO: tristate is disabled, because this is not properly integrated with how kclause models tristate options'. The maintainer, in issue #308 (2026-08-16), says klocalizer 'processes one architecture at a time'. So an arch-foreign unit like drivers/block/ps3disk.o is reported buildable on x86_64 because CONFIG_PS3_DISK is defined only in the PowerPC Kconfig. Important: the first two are Kbuild-side gaps. The tristate and per-arch model gaps are Kconfig-model gaps, which kfold's over-approximate K model does not obviously fix. Do not claim these as a kfold advantage without a test.",
              "THE DOSSIER'S 'KMAX NEEDS SUPERC AND HEAVY SETUP' IS PARTLY WRONG. SuperC is needed for #ifdef constraints in klocalizer's repair mode. Per-object Kbuild formulas come from kmaxall/kreader, which cip-kernel-triage uses without SuperC. Setup does need system dependencies and environment variables (PyPI page), and the repo shows Arch-Linux/GCC-version build issues (#272, #303). Speed: the krepair paper gives 'kclause typically taking 2-3 minutes, kmax 10-15 minutes, and SuperC about a minute or less on commodity hardware'. Formulas are cacheable per kernel version in .kmax/, and klocalizer 'will also look for prebuilt formulas to download'. So for a distro, a per-revision cost of 10-15 minutes once, then cached, is not a pain. kfold's one-minute whole-tree figure is an advantage in seconds-vs-minutes, not a qualitative change. A head-to-head on the same machine has not been measured.",
              "NON-LINUX KBUILD PROJECTS ARE NOT UNIQUE TO KFOLD. kmax docs/advanced.md show BusyBox commands: `kmaxall $(find | grep \"Kbuild$\" ...) | tee .kmax/kmax` and kextract/kclause into .kmax/. D8's contribution (e) is therefore not a differentiator by itself. It would have to be backed by accuracy on Barebox, U-Boot and coreboot (builds exist in the assets).",
              "ADOPTION OF THE INCUMBENT: MODEST BUT REAL. kmax on GitHub: 72 stars, 29 forks, 1,289 commits, 77 open issues, 0 dependent repositories; PyPI 4.10 released 2026-08-18, 330 downloads in the last month (pypistats 'recent', mirrors handling not checked; kmaxtools 10), no Debian/Yocto/Android mention in the README. An issue filed 2026-10-14 shows it still maintained. LWN 2025 says kmax 'has been integrated into the Intel 0-day test suite' (primary source not opened; only the LWN statement). One embedded-distro user (CIP/Siemens) is confirmed. Verdict on adoption: a research tool with one embedded-distro downstream, not an unused tool.",
              "STRONG IN-TREE PRECEDENT FOR OBJECT-TO-OPTION MAPPING. scripts/kconfig/streamline_config.pl (behind localmodconfig) walks every Makefile/Kbuild, joins continuations, expands simple $(VAR) assignments, and maps obj-$(CONFIG_X) += foo.o to CONFIG_X, then closes over Kconfig depends/select. It does not handle foo-objs/foo-y composites or ifeq/ifdef, per the summarizer read (not byte-verified), and its own comments say 'we do not care about operands (like: &&, ||, !)' and 'This script does not enable configs that are not already enabled.' Verbatim quotes are unverified (summarizer). So a crude Makefile-regex mapper has been in the kernel for years, driven by lsmod (runtime state). This weakens any claim that mapping objects to options statically is new. It also gives kfold a clean comparison point on composites, conditionals and parent-directory conditions.",
              "CHORES 1 AND 4 REMAIN OUTSIDE KFOLD. Fedora kernel-ark documents `evaluate_configs`, which shows how one CONFIG is layered across directories (kernel-ark redhat/configs/README), not which drivers a config enables. Android Kleaf (kernel_config.md) has `check_defconfig` and fragment checks: 'if an item is not declared in `Kconfig`, then `make ..._defconfig` silently drops it', plus verifying that fragment entries appear in .config. Yocto kernel_configcheck flags requested options that did not reach .config. These Kconfig-level audits are mature and in-pipeline. kfold's `why` starts from the final .config and complements them. I did not open Yocto's symbol_why.py (403/404 on all tries), so whether Yocto has a Kconfig-level 'why not set' explainer is UNVERIFIED. Treat chore 4 as covered at Kconfig level until checked.",
              "RELATED CHECKERS ARE CLOSE TO THE OBJECT-LEVEL IDEA. cvehound (https://github.com/evdenis/cvehound; 138 stars, 17 forks, 1,204 commits) has `--kernel-config`, which 'infers the configuration needed to build the affected code from Kbuild/Makefiles' and checks a .config. Its stated limits: 'ifdefs are not checked', and files it cannot map are reported as unknown and counted as affected. PatchLens (arXiv 2606.25863, FSE 2026) recovers a Vulnerability Impact Condition per patch via 'lightweight build system analysis' with 1.84 variables per predicate on Linux; the abstract page gives no runtime or precision figures. Both target CVE triage (D9/D10). For D8 they show that a second and third group already do Kbuild-derived file conditions.",
              "ANDROID/GKI IS MOSTLY AN ABI PROBLEM. The GKI FAQ quote 'There's no way to avoid some manual examination of configuration options to optimize the fragment' and the diffconfig suggestion are confirmed. The FAQ also says mismatched ABIs 'represents an implicit module config dependency', and Kleaf's docs note that flags like --debug and --kasan turn off TRIM_UNUSED_KSYMS and MODULE_SIG_PROTECT, which affects the KMI. Object membership is not the gating factor. kfold supplies a minor input here only.",
              "BUILDROOT/OPENWRT FACTS FOR 'HAND LISTS CHECKED LATE'. Confirmed: Buildroot linux.mk applies LINUX_KCONFIG_FIXUP_CMDS with no re-read of the result; its only hard check is a CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_BTF/host-pahole grep. The docker-engine package has 36 KCONFIG_ENABLE_OPT calls total (31 + 3 + 2), not 31. OpenWrt kernel.mk, in its install loop, checks modules.builtin (NOTICE 'is built-in'), then file existence, else `echo \"ERROR: module '$$$$$$$$mod' is missing.\"` and exit 1, so mismatches surface at package time, after the kernel build. This is the one place where a static pre-build check of FILES lists against Kbuild conditions has no existing equivalent I found. It is a proposal, not evidence of demand, and it would need the tristate (=y vs =m) distinction that klocalizer's code says it has disabled for config-file constraints.",
              "SOURCE-QUALITY CAVEATS. Several quotes come from a fetch summarizer, not raw text; verbatim ones are marked in sources_checked. The CIP .sources counts in the dossier, SUSE entry counts, and the Fedora/Ubuntu tool behaviours were not re-verified. Debian's packages.debian.org file list for the signed linux-image meta-package shows only 3 paths, so it is NOT a module inventory; do not cite it as one. Debian handbook ch-common-tasks says nothing about config checks."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Dossier gap one ('nobody exposes a fast, static, explainable answer to which objects config A builds that config B does not'): narrow to 'nobody ships a whole-config enumeration and A-to-B object diff front-end'. Per-file evaluation of Kbuild formulas against a config is already done by kernel-cve-triage (kmaxall + kreader --show-constraints + And/Or evaluator).",
              "Dossier gap two (minimal option set for a requested driver found by hand): wrong as stated. `klocalizer --repair <config> --include <unit>.o` does it, per architecture, with Kbuild and Kconfig constraints (krepair paper: <1.53% options changed for 99% of patches). kfold can only claim speed, patch-level multi-file with olddefconfig verification, and measured object-level exactness, and only if a head-to-head confirms it.",
              "Dossier contribution (e) (BusyBox, Barebox, U-Boot, coreboot): kmax docs already show BusyBox. Rephrase as 'also evaluated on' with a measured accuracy table, not as an advantage.",
              "Dossier says Kmax formulas 'may not be exact' (klocalizer docs). I did NOT find this phrase in the kmax README, advanced.md, PyPI page or klocalizer.py. Mark it UNVERIFIED and do not quote it. Use the verified code-level caveats instead (non-Kbuild top Makefile constraints hard-coded; missing formula treated as unconstrained; tristate disabled in config-file constraints; one architecture at a time, per issue #308).",
              "Dossier says the 'Kmax run time is 5-10 min per kernel revision (author, 2024 IRC)'. Use the krepair paper's figure instead: 'kclause typically taking 2-3 minutes, kmax 10-15 minutes, and SuperC about a minute or less on commodity hardware' (arxiv.org/html/2404.17966, Section 4.3). Compare it with kfold's about one minute for the whole tree only after timing both on the same machine and tree.",
              "Dossier says the Siemens 90-95% is Kmax-formula triage. Correct: it is the result of all checks combined (introducing/fixing commit checks plus file-usage checks), and the slides do not name Kmax. Add the slides' '4.19 EOL for the unfixed ~1000 CVEs 56% can be excluded'. Source is now opened (pdftotext of the downloaded PDF); reopened_and_verified for this item should be true.",
              "Dossier says klocalizer 'works per architecture through SuperC, with heavy setup'. Correct: per-architecture is true (issue #308), SuperC is used only for #ifdef constraints in repair mode; the formula step (kmaxall/kreader) is what cip-kernel-triage runs.",
              "Buildroot: raise confidence from 'medium (summarizer)' to confirmed that linux.mk has no post-check of fixup options (direct raw-file read, summarizer answer was explicit). Fix the docker-engine count to 36 total (31 in the main set).",
              "ChromeOS: keep the correction to 6 flavours (3 ARM, 3 Intel) for the example in the guide, not '~10'.",
              "kmax adoption: replace 'no distro or embedded use was found' with 'one embedded-distro downstream (CIP kernel-cve-triage, created 2024-07-11); 0 GitHub dependents; 330 PyPI downloads last month'.",
              "streamline_config.pl: dossier says 'how it maps modules to options is unverified'. Now: it regex-parses obj-$(CONFIG_X) lines in Makefile/Kbuild files (summarizer read of github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/scripts/kconfig/streamline_config.pl; composite handling and quotes still unverified).",
              "Dossier cites Debian packages file list nowhere, but if used: the signed linux-image meta-package filelist has only 3 paths. Do not present package file lists as a module inventory."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "related_work",
            "summary": "D8 is not already solved as a whole, but its centre is covered. Per-file 'is this object built under config C' has an incumbent in production: Kmax formulas (kmaxall + kreader) evaluated against a .config, as in CIP/Siemens kernel-cve-triage, and `klocalizer --view-kbuild` prints the per-unit conditions. 'Which minimal options bring object X in from a base config' has an incumbent too: `klocalizer --repair <config> --include foo.o`, with krepair reporting 98.5% mean patch coverage and under 1.53% of options changed for 99% of patches. CIP also holds the build-derived alternative (cip-kernel-config .sources, a full build that 'can take hours'). Symbol-level lookup (kernelconfig.io, LKDDb) and Kconfig-level audits (Yocto configcheck, Kleaf check_defconfig, merge_config.sh, diffconfig) are mature. The two Debian wishlist bugs show a policy bottleneck, not an object-knowledge gap, since the reporters list the options themselves and no reply is visible. What I found no tool for is (a) whole-config object enumeration and an A-to-B object diff front-end, (b) a failing-conjunct explanation from a .config (klocalizer prints the formula but not which conjunct fails), (c) verified multi-file patch configs against a Kconfig model with olddefconfig, and (d) a static check of hand-written module lists (OpenWrt FILES) against Kbuild conditions, which is unmeasured and would need tristate handling that klocalizer's code says is disabled for config-file constraints. kfold can honestly claim: faster formula extraction (about one minute whole-tree vs the krepair paper's 'kmax 10-15 minutes', once timed head-to-head on the same machine), object-level exactness measured against real builds, naming the blocking conjunct, and patch-level multi-file config-for with verification. It cannot claim novelty for per-file conditions, config-for, or BusyBox support (kmax docs show BusyBox). Recommended paper use: related work and a framing sentence that distro/embedded settings are a use case, not the motivation, because no operator ask for object-level output exists. If the paper plans a head-to-head against kmax formulas (kreader/koverage) on the Debian and defconfig inventories, D8's config-delta experiment is sound and needs no new builds, and could be promoted to an RQ task. Without that head-to-head, keep it to related work and limitation. Run any kmax job one process at a time per CLAUDE.md.",
