          {
            "lens": "Fit and experiment designer, D8 (distro, Android, embedded configuration maintenance)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "FIT. Of the five chores in the dossier, only the object-level questions are Kbuild-condition problems: 'what does config A build that B does not' (chore 5, CIP-style source lists), 'which options bring object X into this config' (chore 2, Debian 'please enable' bugs) and hand-written module lists (chore 3). Chore 4 (an option did not take effect) is mostly Kconfig resolution. Chore 1 (per-symbol review in kernel-ark) is human judgement. Android GKI is gated by KMI/ABI. #ifdef and header-only effects are invisible to kfold. D8 is therefore a thin application of kfold's existing why/config-for/delta machinery, not a new problem class.",
              "NEED IS UNDEMONSTRATED. The sources I re-opened show process documentation and unanswered wishlist bugs, not an operator asking for object-level output. Debian #1126062 (2026-01-21, wishlist) and #1112317 (2025-08-28, wishlist) show no maintainer reply on the fetched pages. I did not re-verify the other dossier items (Fedora MR, OpenWrt, Yocto, ChromeOS). A PLDI reviewer will ask who would use it and will not accept a bug title as demand.",
              "Debian does not ship these drivers for reasons that are not object selection. Possible reasons are security posture, maintenance cost, size, signing and ABI policy. Nothing I saw says why the two bugs are open. kfold can show what enabling would add, but not whether Debian should enable it.",
              "REDUNDANCY WITH RQ1. The dossier's proposed experiment (predict object sets for defconfig, Debian and tinyconfig, then diff them) is already measured. In results/agreement/linux.full.json the full tool has precision 100.0% and recall-within-universe 100.0% for tinyconfig (528 objects), defconfig (3,133), Debian (17,191) and allmodconfig (29,625). Outside the analysed universe there are 74 Debian objects and 31 allmodconfig and defconfig objects (tinyconfig has none). A delta between two exactly predicted sets is exact within that universe, so it adds no new evidence and cannot go wrong in an interesting way. Repeating it as a D8 result would look padded.",
              "The Kmax baseline for such deltas already exists in results/kmax/linux.json (kmaxall as shipped, scored the same way). On the Debian config Kmax scores precision 88.6% and recall-within-universe 61.2% under the 'local' scoring, and 90.1% and 59.1% under 'with_dirs'. Deltas computed from Kmax inherit those errors. These are our own saved results, not yet in the paper's D8 text. The comparison is also costly for Kmax: the saved run took 6,275 s and 28.5 GB RSS, against about 58 s and 212 MB for kfold (analysis_seconds 57.96, peak_rss_mb 212.2).",
              "GROUND TRUTH. Two things can be checked and two cannot. Can: (a) whether an object is built under a config, using the four Linux inventories; (b) whether a returned option set is sufficient, by olddefconfig plus compiling the object, as the existing config-for pipeline already does for 384 commits (results/devtasks/config_for_compile.json). Cannot: whether a returned set is minimal (no oracle), and whether a maintainer would accept the change. The experiment must claim sufficiency, option counts and explanation, never 'what the distro should do'.",
              "Asset limits. The Debian asset is /boot/config-7.2.6+deb14-amd64 after olddefconfig, not the 6.16-6.18 kernels the bugs were filed against. Four Debian-only options are dropped. Everything is x86 and one kernel version. Cross-architecture flavours (the 15 Debian arch trees) are out of reach.",
              "The dossier's step (4), the CIP .sources oracle, needs a second kfold run on a 6.1 tree and covers only a conservative superset, since the lists add headers and non-source files. This is expensive and gives a muddy oracle; I recommend dropping it. The CIP evidence is better used as motivation: the README states that the lists are generated by a full build that 'can take hours'.",
              "Dossier fact check. Debian #1126062 asks for CONFIG_MFD_DLN2, GPIO_DLN2, DLN2_ADC, I2C_DLN2 and SPI_DLN2 (I saw the symbol DLN2_ADC, not an IIO_ name). #1112317's page title is 'linux-image-6.16.3+deb14-amd64: Please enable the GPIB driver, CONFIG_GPIB=y, CONFIG_GPIB_COMMON=m, etc' and lists GPIB, GPIB_COMMON, GPIB_NI_PCI_ISA, GPIB_AGILENT_82357A, GPIB_NI_USB, GPIB_GPIO. The dossier's quote 'please consider enabling the GPIB interface drivers...' did not appear in my fetch summary; mark it unverified and use the title instead.",
              "Local check that the two requests are still live at the asset version. In evidence/configs/linux/debian.config, '# CONFIG_MFD_DLN2 is not set' and '# CONFIG_GPIB is not set'. All requested symbols exist in the 7.2.8 allmodconfig (DLN2_ADC=m, GPIB_NI_PCI_ISA=m, GPIB_NI_USB=m, GPIB_GPIO=m). The allmodconfig inventory contains the target objects: 5 DLN2 objects (drivers/mfd/dln2.o, drivers/gpio/gpio-dln2.o, drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-dln2.o, drivers/iio/adc/dln2-adc.o, drivers/spi/spi-dln2.o) and 21 under drivers/gpib. The request-driven worked examples are therefore feasible without new builds. Debian has 17,191 objects and allmodconfig 29,625; the set difference is 12,541 objects in allmodconfig but not Debian, and 107 in Debian but not allmodconfig.",
              "EXPERIMENT (single strongest design), 'Enable-request sweep on the Debian config'. Inputs: evidence/configs/linux/debian.config (base), evidence/inventories/linux/{debian,allmodconfig}.txt, the cached Linux 7.2.8 condition table, the existing devtasks config-for and compile harness (experiments/devtasks.py, which currently uses defconfig as base). Part A: take the two real Debian requests above. For each, give config-for the target objects (a synthetic one-line patch touching the .c files) with the Debian base, then run olddefconfig and compile the objects, and compare the returned option set with the reporters' hand-written lists. Part B: take D = allmodconfig minus Debian (12,541 objects) and draw a sample of about 300, stratified by top-level directory. For each object run config-for against the Debian base. Metrics: (i) success rate = the fragment survives olddefconfig and the object compiles; (ii) distribution of options flipped (1, 2, 3 or more) and how many differ from a naive guess; (iii) seconds per request; (iv) failure causes (K model over-approximation, unmet depends, composite object, arch). Baselines: (a) the naive rule a person would use, namely enable the CONFIG symbol on the object's own obj-$(CONFIG_X) line (and its Kconfig symbol if different) and run olddefconfig; this takes about a day to implement from kfold's origin data; (b) Kmax-derived delta accuracy from the saved results/kmax/linux.json (exact formulas may not be saved; unverified). Headline form: 'for N of 300 drivers Debian does not ship, config-for returns a verified option set (median k options, t s each), where the naive leaf-option rule succeeds for only M of 300; for the two open Debian requests it returns the reporters\\' lists plus/minus Z options'. Secondary cheap metric: for the options in the Debian-to-allmodconfig diff, the fraction whose single flip changes no object (solver evaluation only, no build). Falsifier: if the naive leaf-symbol rule succeeds on about 90% or more of the sample, or kfold's success is below about 90% with K-model and Kconfig errors dominating the failures, then the claim reduces to 'Kconfig resolution', not Kbuild conditions, and D8 should be dropped to a limitation.",
              "EFFORT. Roughly 5 to 6 person-days: 0.5 for Part A, 1 to harness changes (Debian base, per-object requests), 1 for the naive baseline, 1 for a Debian tree with modules_prepare (single job, per machine rules), 1 for analysis and failure triage, 0.5 for the write-up. Compute at one process: per-object compile was about 10 s in the existing run (seconds field in config_for_compile.json), so 300 objects take about 1 to 2.5 hours, plus a one-time tree preparation; ask the user before the long step.",
              "THREATS TO VALIDITY. (1) Circularity: kfold chooses the options and is also scored by its own conditions; the compile oracle and olddefconfig mitigate this for sufficiency only, and Kconfig resolution uses kfold's own over-approximate model (kconfiglib plus compat layer), so a verified fragment is evidence about Kbuild-plus-model, not about the real distro. (2) Selection bias: allmodconfig minus Debian is the set of objects Debian chose not to ship, so it is a synthetic request population, not real demand; only 2 real requests exist. (3) Single kernel (7.2.x), single arch (x86-64), one Debian config. (4) Object granularity: no #ifdef, no header-only, no link or module-load test; compile of the .o is not 'the feature works'. (5) Success rate depends on the base config; Debian is large (9,881 CONFIG lines), so results may not carry to Yocto or Android fragments. (6) The naive baseline is something I invented, so reviewers may call it a strawman; justify it as the Yocto/OpenWrt-style practice and also report klocalizer if it can be run (not run here; machine rules). (7) The 100% kfold agreement is mostly within a universe that excludes 74 Debian objects; report that count."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Drop the dossier's step (1) as a separate result: per-config object prediction for defconfig, Debian and tinyconfig is already the RQ1 agreement experiment (100.0% precision and recall within universe on all four Linux configs), and deltas of exact sets are exact. Only mention that delta accuracy follows from RQ1, with Kmax's Debian 88.6% precision and 61.2% recall as the contrast.",
              "Drop the dossier's step (4) (CIP .sources oracle on a 6.1 tree): needs a second analysis, covers only a conservative superset, and adds little. Keep CIP only as motivation.",
              "Replace 'DLN2 ADC' by the actual symbol DLN2_ADC and cite the GPIB bug by its page title; mark the dossier's quoted GPIB phrase as unverified.",
              "Do not describe the Debian bugs as 'unanswered by a maintainer' without qualification; I only saw the page without a reply at fetch time (page last modified 2026-10-16). Say 'no reply visible'.",
              "Do not say D8 shows a felt need. The honest claim is a modest utility one: a cheap, explainable, build-free answer to the 'what would this config add and which options do I need' question, verified by compilation on a sample.",
              "The dossier's Kmax slow-run claim is from authors' IRC (5 to 10 min); our own saved run was 6,275 s (about 105 min) with 28.5 GB RSS in results/kmax/linux.meta.json, which is a different Kmax invocation. Quote our own measurement, not the IRC figure, with the invocation named."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "rq3_task",
            "summary": "D8 is only partly an object-selection problem. The question 'which objects does config A add over B, and which options bring object X in' is a Kbuild-condition question that why, config-for and the object table answer. Most of the pain in the cited sources (dropped options, unmet depends, KMI/ABI, per-symbol review, #ifdef effects) is Kconfig, ABI or human judgement, and no operator was found asking for object-level output. The two Debian wishlist bugs I re-opened (#1126062 and #1112317) still show no reply at fetch time and are still unmet in the Debian 7.2.6 config: both '# CONFIG_MFD_DLN2 is not set' and '# CONFIG_GPIB is not set'. I recommend a small subsection in RQ3, not a new RQ and not the main motivation: 'config-for from a distro base config', with the two real requests as worked examples plus a sampled sweep of about 300 objects from allmodconfig minus Debian (12,541 objects) against a naive leaf-symbol baseline, verified by olddefconfig and compile. Cost is about 5 to 6 person-days and 1 to 2.5 hours of single-process compute. The proposed delta-prediction experiment is redundant with RQ1. The experiment depends on no unavailable ground truth if the claim is limited to sufficiency, option count and speed (minimality and maintainer acceptance have no oracle). Use one sentence of introduction motivation from CIP's README (a full build 'can take hours' per config set). State the Kconfig, KMI and #ifdef limits explicitly. Falsifier: the naive rule succeeding on about 90% or more of the sample, or kfold failures dominated by the Kconfig model. PLDI reviewers will likely object that demand is shown only by bug titles, that the sample population is synthetic, and that klocalizer is the incumbent for 'config that builds unit X', so state the claim narrowly and compare on measured facts.",
            "sources_checked": [
