      {
        "id": "D9",
        "name": "CVE applicability: is the file touched by this fix built in my configuration",
        "dive": {
          "id": "D9",
          "name": "CVE applicability: is the file touched by this fix built in my configuration",
          "refined_description": "Problem: the Linux kernel CNA assigns a CVE to almost every bug fix and says that deciding whether a CVE matters for a given system is the user's job. A downstream operator (CIP/LTS maintainer, distro, embedded integrator, SBOM/VEX producer) holds a list of fix commits, each touching some files, plus one concrete .config, and must decide per CVE whether any touched file is compiled into that kernel. If none is, the CVE can be marked not_affected with the OpenVEX justification vulnerable_code_not_present. Today this is done by hand (Chainguard writes per-CVE advisories naming the unset CONFIG option), by building and inspecting the tree (compile_commands.json), or with Kmax-based tooling (CIP kernel-cve-triage) or a line-based Makefile parser (cvehound). The reusable core is a per-object build condition for the whole tree, evaluated against a .config, with a human-readable reason for a 'not built' verdict. This holds only at object/file level: header-only fixes, #ifdef arms inside a built file, built-in vs module, loaded modules and runtime reachability are outside it. Honest framing: demand is strong, recent and compliance-driven (EU CRA), but the closest competitor (Kmax-based kernel-cve-triage, Siemens/CIP, OSS Europe talk 2026-10-07) already computes exactly this verdict. New finding from re-opening its README: in the README's example run the two file-compiled checks account for only about 374 of the 2,658 CVEs filtered (about 14 percent, my arithmetic); the rest are removed by commit/version checks. So the '90-95 percent not relevant' headline is not evidence for config analysis alone. The unresolved points are accuracy (no published false-negative measurement), cost, explanation, and coverage of non-Linux trees.",
          "who_has_it": "CIP/LTS kernel maintainers and Siemens product security (kernel-cve-triage users); distro and container-image vendors that triage kernel CVEs per shipped config (Chainguard does it per CVE, by hand); Yocto/embedded integrators (Ericsson/Yocto talk, Garmin SBOM work); SBOM/VEX producers facing the EU CRA (Article 14 reporting from 2026-09-11, general application 2027-12-11); vendors of custom or BSP kernels whose commercial scanners do not match their kernel (BeagleBoard forum thread, 2026-07-31, from earlier dossier, not re-opened). Not hyperscale operators running one fixed config.",
          "evidence": [
            {
              "claim": "Kernel CNA policy puts applicability on the user and assigns CVEs to essentially every bug fix.",
              "quote": "the applicability of any specific CVE is up to the user of Linux to determine, it is not up to the CVE assignment team.",
              "url": "https://docs.kernel.org/process/cve.html",
              "evidence_type": "policy",
              "source_group": "kernel-cna-team (Kroah-Hartman)",
              "date": "n.d. (current docs, 2026)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "The CNA refuses to gate CVEs on config options. Maintainer Sterba said the btrfs code was not enabled in distro kernels (CONFIG_BTRFS_DEBUG), and Kroah-Hartman answered that users should do the config check themselves.",
              "quote": "We do not know people's use case, and can not \"gate\" CVE ids based on difference config options like this.",
              "url": "https://lkml.iu.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/2407.3/08754.html",
              "evidence_type": "policy",
              "source_group": "kernel-cna-team (Kroah-Hartman)",
              "date": "2024-07-30",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Siemens/CIP report that with the CIP defconfig 90-95% of 2024 CVEs were judged not relevant, and that better use-case description is needed for better results. The headline combines commit/version checks and file-compiled checks (see the README item).",
              "quote": "For CIP kernels /w CIP defconfig 90% - 95% of CVEs reported in 2024 were deemed \"not relevant\"",
              "url": "https://opensource.siemens.com/events/2025/slides/Christoph_Steiger__Triaging_CVEs_for_the_Linux_Kernel.pdf",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
              "source_group": "Siemens-CIP kernel-cve-triage",
              "date": "2025-06-04",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "The same slides show the exact output kfold would produce: per-object built:false plus a formula, turned into a VEX not_affected / vulnerable_code_not_present entry. Design goals on the slides are 'Be fast' and 'No false negatives'. Each check is described as conservative.",
              "quote": "\"config\": \"CONFIG_MTD_NAND_ATMEL && CONFIG_MTD\"",
              "url": "https://opensource.siemens.com/events/2025/slides/Christoph_Steiger__Triaging_CVEs_for_the_Linux_Kernel.pdf",
              "evidence_type": "tool",
              "source_group": "Siemens-CIP kernel-cve-triage",
              "date": "2025-06-04",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "kernel-cve-triage gets a file's condition from Kmax: it runs kmaxall once per tree, calls kreader per object, and evaluates the printed formula as a Boolean over the list of enabled CONFIG names. There is no SAT/Z3 call and no Kconfig model (reading through a summarizer, medium confidence on code behaviour).",
              "quote": "cmd = [\"kreader\", \"--kmax-formulas\", kmax, \"--show-constraints\", str(path)]",
              "url": "https://gitlab.com/cip-project/cip-kernel/kernel-cve-triage/-/raw/main/src/cve_triage/triage/kmax.py",
              "evidence_type": "tool",
              "source_group": "Siemens-CIP kernel-cve-triage",
              "date": "n.d. (repo main, read 2026-10-17)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "README example run: 'Filtered 2658/2953 - 90.01%'. Per-check successes (from a summarizer read of the README): check_ignore 204, check_introduced_by 1721, check_introduced_by_file_compiled 276, check_fixed_by 359, check_fixed_by_file_compiled 98. These sum exactly to 2,658, so the two file-compiled checks removed about 374 CVEs (about 14% of those filtered, 12.7% of 2,953). Kernel and config of the run are not stated. This arithmetic is mine. The README also says the formula step 'can take a noticeable amount of time' and that the tool is tailored to the CIP use case.",
              "quote": "Filtered 2658/2953 - 90.01%",
              "url": "https://gitlab.com/cip-project/cip-kernel/kernel-cve-triage/-/raw/main/README.md",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
              "source_group": "Siemens-CIP kernel-cve-triage",
              "date": "n.d. (repo main, read 2026-10-17)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Failure handling of the file-compiled check (summarizer reading of introduced_by.py, not tested): deleted files, header search-depth overflow, empty introduced-by and unknown status all lean RELEVANT. A ValueError from kreader stores built=None, which the verdict logic does not count as unknown, so such a file can end in 'no file is compiled'. This is a possible false negative against the tool's own 'no false negatives' goal.",
              "quote": "build status of at least one file could not be determined",
              "url": "https://gitlab.com/cip-project/cip-kernel/kernel-cve-triage/-/raw/main/src/cve_triage/triage/checks/introduced_by.py",
              "evidence_type": "tool",
              "source_group": "Siemens-CIP kernel-cve-triage",
              "date": "n.d. (repo main, read 2026-10-17)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Cost and validation in the CIP IRC meeting: formula generation takes about 5-10 minutes on the Siemens developer's machine (the other developer measured about 25 minutes per run in his first prototype, with no cache), and accuracy was checked on only three kernel versions. No false-negative discussion appears in the log.",
              "quote": "Generating them takes about 5 to 10 minutes on my machine",
              "url": "https://ircbot.wl.linuxfoundation.org/meetings/cip/2024/08/cip.2024-08-22-13.02.log.txt",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
              "source_group": "Siemens-CIP kernel-cve-triage",
              "date": "2024-08-22",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "The CIP kernel team filters CVEs by its kernel configuration in an automated first step; for the 6.1 kernel alone it sees about 2,000 CVE entries per month, and the remainder is reviewed by hand. This figure conflicts with 'about 60 per week' (Siemens) and 104 per week (KCVEPruner slides); cite with its source only.",
              "quote": "about 2,000 CVE entries per month",
              "url": "https://lwn.net/Articles/1049604/",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
              "source_group": "LWN-Corbet CIP article",
              "date": "2025-12-17",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "OpenVEX, the format consumers use, defines the justification that a not-built file maps to, and says it typically arises from configuration.",
              "quote": "Typically, this case occurs when source code is configured or built in a way that excluded the vulnerable code.",
              "url": "https://github.com/openvex/spec/blob/main/OPENVEX-SPEC.md",
              "evidence_type": "policy",
              "source_group": "OpenVEX spec",
              "date": "n.d.",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "An operator already does this per CVE, by hand: Chainguard marks kernel CVEs Not affected / 'Vulnerable code not present' and names the unset option (CVE-2026-53308: CONFIG_CHARGER_MAX77705; also CONFIG_SND_SOC_SDCA_IRQ and CONFIG_SCSI_QLA_FC in other advisories). Debian's tracker, by contrast, uses the same label for version differences, with no config note on the page I opened.",
              "quote": "is not enabled in any Chainguard kernel configuration",
              "url": "https://images.chainguard.dev/security/CGA-wffj-qqv4-xpvx",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "Chainguard advisories",
              "date": "2026 (page shows no date)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "PatchLens (FSE 2026), a competitor for patch-level conditions, resolves file inclusion by a per-target upward walk through nearby Makefiles. Its own validation shows a wrong over-approximation (CVE-2023-31436, net/sched/sch_qfq.c): it missed the root Kbuild guard on CONFIG_NET. Kmax gave the correct (CONFIG_NET_SCH_QFQ) & (CONFIG_NET). 27 of 500 reviewed reports (5.4%) were over-approximate.",
              "quote": "Patchlens fails to parse this file correctly and assumes `net/` is always added (over-approximation).",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.25863",
