            },
            {
              "claim": "The compliance driver is real in the Regulation text: Article 14 applies from 11 September 2026 and the whole Regulation from 11 December 2027. Annex I Part II requires manufacturers to 'identify and document vulnerabilities and components'. The Commission guidance (C(2026) 5252) says applicability to the product must be confirmed but gives no method (earlier-sweep claim, not re-opened).",
              "quote": "This Regulation shall apply from 11 December 2027.",
              "url": "https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R2847",
              "evidence_type": "policy",
              "source_group": "EU CRA text",
              "date": "2024-11-20",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Counter-evidence on scope: an embedded-security vendor says a kernel review needs more than the config. It also needs built-in versus module status, loaded modules, runtime availability, and version matching that accounts for BSP backports. Build conditions are the first filter, not the verdict.",
              "quote": "Product kernel config, built-in/module status, loaded modules, and runtime availability",
              "url": "https://www.lynx.com/blog/copy-fail-dirty-frag-embedded-linux-device-risk",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "Lynx blog",
              "date": "2026-05-26",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            }
          ],
          "existing_solutions": [
            {
              "tool_or_process": "CIP kernel-cve-triage (Siemens/CIP, built on Kmax; OSS Europe 2026 talk)",
              "what_it_does": "Runs kmaxall once per kernel revision, then kreader per object and evaluates the formula against the enabled CONFIG names. Also runs commit/version checks (introduced-by, fixed-by). Emits per-object built:false with a condition, and VEX not_affected / vulnerable_code_not_present. Claims up to 95% fewer false-positive CVEs.",
              "limits": "Formula generation is expensive (5-10 min reported on one machine, 25 min in a first prototype). Accuracy was checked on three kernel versions with no published false-negative rate. Needs libclang and pp-trace, and the kmax suite. Tailored to the CIP use case. The condition is printed as a flat formula without a per-conjunct explanation. Linux only. Possible fail-open path on kreader errors (code reading, untested). In the README example the file-compiled checks account for about 14% of the CVEs filtered.",
              "url": "https://gitlab.com/cip-project/cip-kernel/kernel-cve-triage/-/raw/main/README.md"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "cvehound",
              "what_it_does": "Detects CVEs by Coccinelle/grep rules, and with --kernel-config infers needed CONFIG options from Kbuild/Makefiles with its own line-based parser. Unknown files count as affected.",
              "limits": "Does not check #ifdefs. Heuristic parser, no SAT or Kconfig model. No accuracy numbers. Rules cover only CVEs with Coccinelle rules.",
              "url": "https://github.com/evdenis/cvehound"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "PatchLens (FSE 2026)",
              "what_it_does": "Computes a Boolean Vulnerability Impact Condition per patch, combining hunk-level #ifdef presence conditions with a per-target Kbuild upward walk. Runs in about 47 ms per patch. Linux: 1,192 patches, 88.67% success, 5.4% over-approximate in manual review.",
              "limits": "Does not model headers or the Kconfig dependency model, and does not evaluate against a concrete distro .config or emit VEX. Its own listing shows a missed root-Kbuild guard (CONFIG_NET). 135 of 1,192 Linux patches failed.",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.25863"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "KCVEPruner (Ossillate, KubeCon India 2026 lightning talk)",
              "what_it_does": "Maps CVEs to functions and filters by build output. Per the earlier sweep, its build-time filter checks for the .o/.cmd/.ko in a finished build tree (not re-verified in this pass). The talk reports FP rates of 91.43% (tinyconfig), 12.11% (Ubuntu 24.04) and 5.3% (allyesconfig).",
              "limits": "Needs a finished build per config. The repo README says it is a work in progress, with 3 stars and no accuracy validation. File-level build check only; reachability method undocumented.",
              "url": "https://github.com/ossillate-inc/kcvepruner"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Manual per-CVE justification by distro/vendors (Chainguard advisories; Yocto CVE_STATUS; Debian tracker by version)",
              "what_it_does": "A person or script decides per CVE and records 'Vulnerable code not present' with the unset option, or a fixed-version status.",
              "limits": "Does not scale to tens of CVEs per week. Not reusable across configs. oe-core's kernel exclusion list has only 3 not-applicable-config entries, none Kconfig-driven (summarizer read, medium confidence).",
              "url": "https://images.chainguard.dev/security/CGA-wffj-qqv4-xpvx"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Build the tree and inspect (compile_commands.json, .o/.cmd search, KernelSBOM)",
              "what_it_does": "Ground truth for one config, once a build exists.",
              "limits": "Needs a full build per config. KernelSBOM does not parse Makefiles or Kbuild and does not say anything about CVEs.",
              "url": "https://kernel.org/doc/html/latest/tools/sbom/sbom.html"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "klocalizer/koverage/kreader (Kmax suite)",
              "what_it_does": "Gives per-object Kbuild formulas (kreader) and checks patch coverage by a .config (koverage, line level, includes #ifdef via SuperC).",
              "limits": "Docs state 'The extracted formulas may not be exact.' One architecture per run. Header and dead-code lines may be omitted. kmax takes 10-15 min per krepair paper. Not packaged for CVE use except through kernel-cve-triage.",
              "url": "https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax/blob/master/docs/advanced.md"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "ISSTA 2026 FCC (arXiv 2608.15225)",
              "what_it_does": "Infers a minimal Kconfig option set per CVE, using an LLM to link cues to options, then checks the Makefile mapping and olddefconfig. Config success on 88 CVEs rose from 62.5% to 96.6%.",
              "limits": "Answers a different question (which config triggers the bug), needs an LLM and a QEMU run, and covers upstream kernels only. 34 of 66 KernJC CVEs were excluded because they trigger under defconfig.",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.15225"
            }
          ],
          "remaining_gap": "(1) No published accuracy or false-negative measurement of any 'is the fixed file built under this .config' check against real builds (CIP checked three kernel versions informally; PatchLens cross-checked against Kmax only). (2) No measurement of how much the file-built filter contributes beyond version/commit checks (the README example suggests about 14% of those filtered, one run, unknown kernel and config). (3) No tool explains a 'not built' verdict as a per-conjunct chain that can be pasted into a VEX statement. (4) Per-tree cost: whole-tree table versus per-revision Kmax runs has not been compared on the same machine. (5) Out of scope for any object-level tool, so a gap but not kfold's to claim: header-only fixes, #ifdef arms in built files, cross-architecture fixes (the example CVE-2025-37996 touches arch/arm64/kvm/mmu.c), built-in versus module, loaded modules and runtime reachability. (6) No non-Linux evidence: I found no CVE-applicability tooling for BusyBox, Barebox, U-Boot or coreboot. The Buildroot busybox.mk lists five ignored CVEs with no stated reason.",
          "kfold_contribution": "Modest and specific. (a) A whole-tree per-object build-condition table, computed once per source tree (about 1 minute for Linux 7.2.8 on the project's machine) and reused for every .config and every CVE's file list. This is an alternative to per-revision Kmax runs (5-15 min in published reports), but the comparison must be measured on one machine because published figures come from different hardware. (b) `why OBJ --config .config` returns a condition chain naming the failing conjunct (for example, 'CONFIG_MTD_NAND_ATMEL unset'). This is the evidence text a VEX not_affected / vulnerable_code_not_present entry needs, where kernel-cve-triage prints a flat formula. (c) The condition comes from symbolic execution of the Makefiles with state merging, not from a nearby-file parse (PatchLens) or a line-based parser (cvehound), so it can be checked against both on cases like the net/ CONFIG_NET guard. (d) The same engine runs on BusyBox, Barebox, U-Boot and coreboot, where no CVE-applicability tool was found. (e) The strongest contribution is an independent, measured accuracy and false-negative study of the file-built verdict against real builds, which the existing tools lack. kfold should not claim to be first. It does not cover headers, #ifdefs, other architectures (unless entry settings are added), modules or runtime state, so it cannot by itself justify a not_affected verdict where those matter.",
          "quantitative_facts": [
            "Siemens/CIP slides (2025-06-04): kernel CNA since Feb 2024, around 60 CVEs per week; Linux about 85,000 files and about 40M LoC, typically 5-10% used; with the CIP defconfig 90-95% of 2024 CVEs 'not relevant'; at 4.19 EOL about 56% of about 1,000 unfixed CVEs can be excluded. Slides state goals 'Be fast' and 'No false negatives'.",
            "kernel-cve-triage README example (summarizer read, my arithmetic): 2,658 of 2,953 CVEs filtered (90.01%). Per check: ignore 204, introduced_by 1,721, introduced_by_file_compiled 276, fixed_by 359, fixed_by_file_compiled 98. These sum to 2,658, so the file-compiled checks removed about 374 (about 14% of filtered, 12.7% of all). Kernel and config unspecified.",
            "Kmax formula generation: 5-10 min (Siemens developer), about 25 min per run in the first prototype (CIP IRC, 2024-08-22); about 10 s per CVE afterwards; krepair paper reports kclause 2-3 min and kmax 10-15 min. kfold: Linux 7.2.8 whole tree in about 1 minute (own data; different machine and kernel).",
            "CIP LWN article (2025-12-17): about 2,000 CVE entries per month for the 6.1 kernel; over 1,000 patches per month reviewed for backport. Conflicts with about 60 per week (Siemens) and 104 per week (KCVEPruner slides, NVD-derived, mean after Feb 2024, bursts to about 880). Cite one with a footnote, do not combine.",
            "PatchLens (FSE 2026): 1,192 Linux patches, 1,057 resolved (88.67%), 135 failed; about 47.4 ms per Linux patch; 500 reports reviewed (282 Linux), 0 inaccurate, 27 (5.4%) over-approximate; mean 1.84 variables per Linux formula; CVE text names a required option in only about 1% of cases.",
            "KCVEPruner slides (v6.17.0): tinyconfig 176 CVEs remain (91.43% FP), Ubuntu 24.04 config 1,807 (12.11% FP), allyesconfig 2,056 (5.3% FP); adding 195 loaded modules cuts Ubuntu to 814. No accuracy validation shown (earlier sweep, slides read by the earlier agent).",
            "ISSTA FCC (arXiv 2608.15225): 88 CVEs (32 KernJC, 56 KernelCTF); olddefconfig success 62.5% to 96.6%; average 14.72 candidate options per CVE versus 69.00 for KernJC.",
            "EU CRA: Article 14 applies from 2026-09-11, Chapter IV from 2026-06-11, whole Regulation from 2027-12-11 (verified in Article 71(2) text).",
            "Kernel CVE JSON (CVE-2025-37996, bippy-1.2.0): carries programFiles (arch/arm64/kvm/mmu.c) and git version ranges but no CONFIG conditions, so the file list is the available input.",
            "Stable-update proxy on kfold's own assets: 437 commits v7.2.7..v7.2.8, 402 touch .c/.S, 384 already mapped to a configuration. Four builds (i386 tinyconfig, x86-64 defconfig, Debian, allmodconfig) have object inventories."
          ],
          "proposed_experiment": "Single best experiment: a ground-truth accuracy study of the 'touched file built under this .config' verdict, using the stable-update commits as a proxy for kernel CVE fix commits (the CNA assigns CVEs to stable fixes; not every stable commit has a CVE, so call it a proxy). Inputs already available: the 402 .c/.S-touching commits of v7.2.7..v7.2.8, the four builds with object inventories, and the saved Kmax run if it matches 7.2.8. Steps, all sequential and single-process per CLAUDE.md. (1) Smoke test: one config (x86-64 defconfig) and 10 commits. (2) For each commit, map touched .c/.S files to objects. For each of the four configs, take the kfold verdict ('why OBJ --config'), with the ground truth that the object (or its composite parent, or .ko) is in that build's inventory. Report per-object agreement, false 'not built' (the safety-critical error, target zero), false 'built', and 'unresolved'. Then aggregate per commit (not_affected only if every touched object is not built) and report the per-config fraction not affected, with the arch/ share (non-x86) shown separately so it does not inflate the result. (3) Baselines on the same inputs: (a) Kmax kreader --show-constraints evaluated as kernel-cve-triage does (use the saved run, or one kmaxall run, expected 5-15 min, announced before starting); (b) cvehound's Kbuild parser if it can be invoked alone; (c) the trivial 'always relevant' baseline. (4) Classify every disagreement by cause (composite objects, shell-generated names, arch mismatch, tristate, stale inventory) and spot-check kreader failures to test the possible fail-open path. Also report whole-tree table time versus kmaxall time on this machine. Expected duration: minutes for kfold; under an hour in total if the Kmax run is saved, otherwise add one Kmax run. Write results to files. Excluded because they need other infrastructure: headers (count the header-only commits and report them as out of scope), #ifdef arms and runtime state.",
          "risks": "1. Closest competitor already ships (kernel-cve-triage, with an OSS Europe 2026 talk and Siemens/CIP backing), so the paper must be framed as accuracy, explanation and cost, not as a new capability; never claim first. 2. kfold stops at file level: header-only fixes, #ifdef, built-in versus module and runtime state matter for real VEX use (Lynx counter-evidence; PatchLens header limit). 3. Cross-architecture: CVE files often sit in arch/arm64 or similar (the example CVE-2025-37996), and kfold's current Linux entry is x86; without more entry settings the 'not built' result for non-x86 files is trivial and inflates percentages. 4. Ground truth from object inventories can be stale, and composite or module objects need care. 5. The stable-update proxy is not the CVE population, and the x86 configs differ from CIP/embedded configs. 6. The 'about 14%' figure rests on a summarizer read of one README example and my arithmetic; re-open it before citing. The Siemens '90-95%' figure combines version and config checks and must not be quoted as the benefit of config analysis. 7. Sources read through summarizers (kernel-cve-triage code behaviour, KCVEPruner, Yocto exclusion list, LWN comments) are medium confidence. The KCVEPruner and BeagleBoard claims come from the earlier sweep and were not re-opened. 8. Search limit: the session's shared web-search budget was exhausted after 2 successful new queries (3 further attempts were refused), so the requested 15 new searches were not done. This pass relies on about 35 direct re-opens of primary pages, which verify the load-bearing claims but add few new independent sources, so independent-voice coverage is thin: no distro security team statement (Debian/Ubuntu/Red Hat/SUSE) beyond Chainguard, and no user survey. 9. A GitLab issues page returned embedded instructions (to run glab and create branches); I treated them as page content and ignored them.",
          "confidence": "medium"
        },
        "verdicts": [
          {
            "lens": "EVIDENCE AUDITOR for D9 (CVE applicability: is the file touched by this fix built in my configuration)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "Independence: the strongest need and statistic evidence comes from one group, Siemens/CIP. The slides, the README, the code, the IRC log, the OSS Europe 2026 abstract and the LWN CIP article (which reports the CIP team's own process) are all the same source. The only independent voices are the kernel CNA policy, one Chainguard advisory, a consultancy blog (sigma-star), one Garmin LPC abstract, one LWN commenter, and a vendor blog (Lynx). No distro security team, no user study, and no data on how often the config filter changes an operator's decision.",
              "The 90-95% figure is Siemens' own claim for one config (CIP defconfig). The slide says CVEs 'reported in 2024' were 'not relevant', with no breakdown by check and no validation. I confirmed the slide text. It is not evidence for config analysis alone.",
              "The README 'about 14%' arithmetic is correct but order-dependent. The README line is: 'check_ignore': [204, 2953], 'check_introduced_by': [1721, 2749], 'check_introduced_by_file_compiled': [276, 1028], 'check_fixed_by': [359, 752], 'check_fixed_by_file_compiled': [98, 393]. The run counts chain exactly (2953-204=2749, 2749-1721=1028, 1028-276=752, 752-359=393), so the checks short-circuit in sequence. 204+1721+276+359+98=2658, and 276+98=374, which is 14.1% of 2,658. But the file-compiled checks only see CVEs that earlier checks did not remove. Of the CVEs that reached them, 276/1028 (27%) and 98/393 (25%) were removed. So 14% is neither a stand-alone contribution of config analysis nor an upper or lower bound. The README does not state the kernel or config of the run.",
              "The fail-open path is stronger than the dossier says, and I verified it by reading the raw source myself, not through a summarizer. In kmax.py (lines 177-182), kreader printing nothing raises ValueError('no kmax compile information for ...'). In introduced_by.py (lines 162-166), this is caught and stored as built=None, with the comment 'we do not know when this file is built, we have to assume it is'. Lines 171-178 then set NOT_RELEVANT ('no file is compiled') whenever no object has built truthy or == 'unknown'. None is neither, so a mix of False and None objects also ends as NOT_RELEVANT. The code contradicts its own comment and the 'No false negatives' goal. It is still untested at runtime. Whether kreader really prints nothing for objects Kmax does not cover is not verified. The dossier labels this 'summarizer, untested'; it can now be labelled 'read in raw source, not executed'. The similar except at lines 265 and 312 (the fixed-by path) I only grepped, not read.",
              "The Kroah-Hartman quote is cited at the wrong URL. The cited page (2407.3/08754.html) is Sterba's reply of 2024-07-31, which only quotes the sentence. Kroah-Hartman's own message is https://lkml.iu.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/2407.3/06160.html, dated 2024-07-30 and with the verbatim sentence. The following sentence there is 'It's a fix for a vulnerability, so I think it should stay assigned.' The dossier's gloss that he 'answered that users should do the config check themselves' is a paraphrase, not in the quote.",
              "PatchLens' 5.4% (27 of 500) is over all three systems (282 Linux, 160 FFmpeg, 58 PHP), not Linux alone. The dossier's existing_solutions entry attaches it to Linux. The paper reports no Linux-only over-approximation rate. Linux validation used Kmax output as the independent cross-check, so there is no build-based ground truth. The paper reports 0 inaccurate VICs. The over-approximations lean safe for a not_affected verdict, so they do not count as false negatives.",
              "Misquote or misattribution: 'The extracted formulas may not be exact.' sits in the klocalizer troubleshooting section of kmax docs/advanced.md. The page says nothing about kreader's accuracy. The dossier attaches it to klocalizer/koverage/kreader as a group.",
              "Not verbatim: the README does not contain 'tailored to the CIP use case'. The actual text is 'It is currently tailored to our use case, but the effort to make it a more general tool should be minimal.' The README says nothing on accuracy or false negatives, as the dossier states. The KernelSBOM quote in the dossier body ('Parsing these files is considered too complex...') is verbatim, but the G89 quote 'Makefile and Kbuild dependencies are not parsed' is not on the page. KCVEPruner's README entry point is measure.py, and the repo has 3 stars, 8 commits and a 'work in progress' note. The 'filter_build_time.py' claim stays summarizer-level.",
              "The Chainguard claim that it is done 'by hand' is the dossier's inference. The page I opened (CVE-2026-53308, linux-qemu-6.18, Not affected, Vulnerable code not present, CONFIG_CHARGER_MAX77705, no date) says nothing about how the verdict was produced. It is one advisory by a vendor that controls its own config. It shows the verdict format, not a labour cost or a prevalence.",
              "Competitor and counter-evidence: the CIP kernel team already filters automatically by config as its first step ('takes the CIP kernel configuration into account to weed out the CVEs that cannot be applicable', LWN 2025-12-17). The tool is shipped and presented at OSS Europe 2026-10-07 (Siemens, Steiger and Masoudifar). The 'problem unsolved' framing is therefore weak; only 'unmeasured accuracy / cost / explanation' remains. The LWN comment thread (2024) also contains commenters who call kernel CVEs low-quality or doubt their security impact. That is not evidence against the need, but it shows the community is not uniform on it.",
              "Accuracy evidence for the competitor: in the CIP IRC log, iwamatsu__ says 'I have only checked three kernel versions, but so far I have not found any problems.' and 'Yes, I have also checked some for accuracy.' jki asked about accuracy. False negatives are not discussed. Timings: '5 to 10 minutes' is csteiger, '25 minutes per run' is iwamatsu__ (a run = one kernel config). The dossier's wording that the 25 min was 'the other developer, first prototype, no cache' was not verified in my read. These are anecdotal timings from different machines and cannot be compared with kfold's 1 minute on a different kernel.",
              "Claims I did not re-open (still unverified): Debian tracker label, KCVEPruner slide numbers (91.43/12.11/5.3%), BeagleBoard thread, ISSTA FCC arXiv 2608.15225, Yocto cve-exclusion.inc, cvelistV5 CVE-2025-37996 JSON, Commission guidance C(2026) 5252, the OpenVEX not_in_execute_path family. WebSearch budget was exhausted (refused), so I ran no new searches for contradicting evidence and found none by search; that is absence of effort, not absence of evidence."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Cite Kroah-Hartman's sentence at https://lkml.iu.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/2407.3/06160.html (2024-07-30); cite 08754.html only as Sterba's reply (2024-07-31) with his CONFIG_BTRFS_DEBUG argument.",
              "README example: say 'check_introduced_by_file_compiled succeeded on 276 of 1,028 runs and check_fixed_by_file_compiled on 98 of 393 runs; together 374 of the 2,658 filtered CVEs (14.1%), but the checks run in sequence with short-circuit, so this share depends on the order and cannot be read as the contribution of config analysis.' Add that kernel and config are unstated.",
              "PatchLens 5.4% (27/500) is across Linux, FFmpeg and PHP, not Linux-only. 1,192 patches, 1,057 resolved (88.67%), 135 failed and ~47.4 ms per Linux patch are confirmed from the PDF text (47.47 ms is the all-system average). FSE 2026, arXiv 2606.25863 submitted 24 June 2026, publication date July 2026.",
              "Kmax doc quote 'The extracted formulas may not be exact.' belongs to klocalizer, not kreader.",
              "README wording is 'currently tailored to our use case', not 'tailored to the CIP use case'. Drop the quoted G89 string 'Makefile and Kbuild dependencies are not parsed'; use 'Parsing these files is considered too complex for the scope of this project.'",
              "Upgrade the kernel-cve-triage fail-open claim from 'summarizer reading' to 'read in raw source: kmax.py:177-182 and checks/introduced_by.py:162-178', and note it is not executed. State the exact consequence: unresolvable objects get built=None, and None counts as not compiled.",
              "Chainguard: remove 'by hand' unless a second source says so; say 'a vendor advisory names the unset option'. Siemens '90-95%': always attach 'combined commit/version and file-compiled checks, CIP defconfig, own claim'.",
              "Verified and fine: docs.kernel.org CNA quote (verbatim); Siemens slides text and the built:false / 'CONFIG_MTD_NAND_ATMEL && CONFIG_MTD' / not_affected / vulnerable_code_not_present image (CVE-2024-56766); 'Be fast', 'No false negatives', 'Each check is conservative', 'Around 60 CVEs each week', '~85.000 files', 'Typically only about 5% to 10%', 'ignored' 56% of ~1000 at 4.19 EOL (slide wording: 'can be excluded'); LWN 1049604 ('about 2,000 CVE entries per month, just for the 6.1 kernel', Corbet 2025-12-17); OpenVEX quotes; Lynx quote (2026-05-26, a table cell, not a sentence); sigma-star (2024-03-13, compile_commands.json, .o search); LPC Garmin (Joshua Watt, 2025-12-11); EU CRA Art. 71(2): 11 December 2027, Art. 14 from 11 September 2026, Chapter IV from 11 June 2026; OSS Europe 2026 abstract (7 October 2026, Steiger and Masoudifar, 'up to 95%')."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "rq3_task",
            "summary": "Most load-bearing quotes and numbers hold. I opened and verified verbatim: the kernel CNA policy, the Kroah-Hartman 'gate' quote, the Siemens slides (read as extracted text and rendered images), the CIP IRC log, the LWN CIP article, the kernel-cve-triage README and raw source, PatchLens (read from the PDF text), cvehound, OpenVEX, EU CRA Art. 71, Lynx, sigma-star, LPC and the OSS Europe abstract. Three things limit the dossier. (1) Independence: the need and the statistics rest on one group, Siemens/CIP, and the 90-95% figure is an unvalidated own claim for one config that mixes commit and config checks. (2) The README arithmetic checks out, but the 14% is order-dependent because of short-circuiting, so it supports neither a high nor a low contribution of config analysis. (3) Attribution errors: the Kroah-Hartman quote is at the wrong URL; PatchLens' 5.4% is over three systems; the Kmax 'may not be exact' line is about klocalizer; one README phrase is not verbatim; 'by hand' for Chainguard is the dossier's inference. A positive finding: the kernel-cve-triage fail-open path is real in the raw source (a kreader ValueError becomes built=None, which counts as 'no file is compiled' and gives NOT_RELEVANT). It was read, not executed. That makes the proposed ground-truth accuracy and false-negative study the best-supported use of D9 (an RQ3 task), with kernel-cve-triage, cvehound, PatchLens and KCVEPruner in related work as existing tools. D9 is not a strong stand-alone motivation, since a competitor already ships and no search for counter-evidence could be run (search budget exhausted).\"",
            "sources_checked": [
              {
