                "opened": true,
                "quote_verbatim": true,
                "note": "README section 'Filter CVEs based on build-time info'; WIP; 3 stars, 8 commits; no accuracy claims. Slide numbers not re-checked."
              },
              {
                "url": "https://osselceu2026.sched.com/event/2RaWO",
                "opened": true,
                "quote_verbatim": true,
                "note": "Talk 2026-10-07, Steiger and Masoudifar (Siemens); 'up to 95%'; same group as the slides, so not independent."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "lens": "Hostile competitor analyst, D9: CVE applicability (is the file touched by this fix built in my configuration)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "The file-level question is already solved, in public and in use, by at least three independent routes. (a) CIP kernel-cve-triage (Siemens/CIP, Apache-2.0, repo created 2024-07-11, 134 commits) runs kmaxall once per kernel revision and kreader per object, evaluates the formula against the enabled CONFIG names, and emits per-object built:false plus a VEX not_affected / vulnerable_code_not_present entry. I read the slides directly; the Results slide shows 'config: CONFIG_MTD_NAND_ATMEL && CONFIG_MTD' per object and the VEX output. (b) Bootlin sbom-cve-check (github.com/bootlin/sbom-cve-check, 58 stars, 17 forks) with Yocto SPDX_INCLUDE_COMPILED_SOURCES: it takes the list of really compiled source files from debug info and marks a CVE not-applicable-config / 'Source code not compiled by config' / justification 'vulnerable code not present' when no affected file is in it. The dossier does not list this tool at all. (c) cvehound --kernel-config (138 stars, 1,204 commits, no 'Used by' entries) does a Kbuild-only mapping. PatchLens (FSE 2026) adds patch-level conditions including #ifdef.",
              "The sbom-cve-check route is a build-based ground-truth competitor, not a static one. Its stated requirements: CONFIG_DEBUG_KERNEL=y, CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_DWARF_TOOLCHAIN_DEFAULT=y, CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO=y, and 'Yocto relies on debug information to extract the list of compiled source files.' Path matching is by suffix and 'most of the time the compiled file path is prefixed with the build directory'. So it is exact for the config it built, needs a full build with debug info, and cannot answer for a config that was never built. That is the honest gap kfold can claim (answer without building, for many configs), not accuracy.",
              "Dossier correction on headers and architectures. The dossier says header-only fixes are out of scope for any object-level tool. The kernel-cve-triage code (read through a fetch summarizer, not raw source; medium confidence) calls header_impacted_files to expand a changed header into the .o files that include it, with a depth limit that falls back to RELEVANT ('header search depth exceeded'). It also passes every subdirectory of arch/ to kmaxall, so it appears to cover non-x86 fixes. kfold (x86 entry, no header handling) is weaker than the incumbent on both points unless that is added. PatchLens, by contrast, states 'does not model the transitive effect of changes in header files' (verified in the PDF, Threats to Validity).",
              "The 'about 14 percent' figure is confirmed but understates the standalone value of the file-compiled checks. I re-opened the README: 'Filtered 2658/2953 - 90.01%' (verbatim) and per-check successes/runs of 204/2953, 1721/2749, 276/1028, 359/752, 98/393 (the second line was not quotable verbatim). 204+1721+276+359+98 = 2658 and 276+98 = 374. But the slides say 'Short circuit on a not relevant verdict', so each file-compiled check only sees CVEs that earlier cheap checks did not remove (1,028 and 393 runs). When they run, they succeed on about 27 percent (276/1028) and 25 percent (98/393) of their inputs. The 374 is therefore a marginal contribution after commit/version checks, not a measure of how many CVEs config analysis could remove alone. Do not use 14 percent as the benefit of config analysis in either direction. The kernel and config of the README run are not stated.",
              "Possible fail-open path in kernel-cve-triage, now read twice (both reads through the fetch summarizer, neither tested). In introduced_by.py a ValueError from kreader/file_compiled stores built=None with the comment 'we do not know when this file is built, we have to assume it is', but the final check counts only 'unknown' or truthy entries, so None is not counted and the verdict can become NOT_RELEVANT 'no file is compiled'. The README-level quote for the opposite behaviour exists only as that code comment. Against the slide goal 'No false negatives' this is the one concrete weakness to test, not to assert. Also: deleted files, header-depth overflow and missing introduced-by lean RELEVANT, which is the safe direction.",
              "No published false-negative measurement exists for any of these. CIP IRC 2024-08-22: 'I have only checked three kernel versions, but so far I have not found any problems.' No false-negative statement appears in that log. Cost statements from the same log: 'about 5 to 10 minutes on my machine' once per kernel version, '25 minutes per run' with no cache, about 10 seconds per CVE afterwards. The cost argument is weak for a fixed kernel: formulas are cached per revision and reused across configs and CVEs, so kfold's one-minute whole-tree table is faster but not obviously decisive. Compare on one machine before claiming it.",
              "PatchLens facts re-verified from the PDF (arXiv 2606.25863, FSE119). Its Linux resolver walks upward from the target to the nearest Kbuild/Makefile and conjoins directory predicates; on non-standard constructs it marks the condition 'unresolved'. It coerces tristate: 'we coerce y and m to true'. Results: Linux 1,192 patches, 1,057 success (88.67%), 135 failure, about 47.4 ms per patch; 500 reports reviewed (282 Linux), 0 inaccurate, 27 (5.4%) over-approximate. The Linux cross-check used Kmax and PCLocator output, and its own Listing 4 (CVE-2023-31436, net/sched/sch_qfq.c) shows the missed CONFIG_NET guard. It handles #ifdef hunks, which kfold does not. The abstract says it needs no variant builds. The paper cites a public GitHub repo and a replication package ([43], [44]) but the arXiv abstract page gave no URL; I did not locate the repo. Reference [30], PCLocator, is a further related tool I did not open.",
              "CNA stance and vendor practice confirmed as demand evidence, but with weak machine-readability. Debian tracker for CVE-2024-41067 marks bullseye 'not affected' with the note 'Vulnerable code not present' and says nothing about config options. The Red Hat page says RHEL 'does not affect the versions or configurations of the Linux kernel' and names no option. So vendors do decide per CVE but publish no per-object condition. That supports the claim 'nobody publishes the reasoning', but it is a few pages, not a survey.",
              "Two dossier claims could not be reproduced. (1) KCVEPruner: the README, as read, does not say how a CVE is judged not applicable; it mentions a --kernel-build argument, 3 stars, 8 commits, and 'work in progress'. The dossier's statement that it checks .o/.cmd/.ko in a finished build tree is an inference, and the 'function-level' label is from the talk abstract, not the repo. (2) The LWN CIP article (1049604) does not name kernel-cve-triage; it says only that the CIP configuration is 'taken into account to weed out the CVEs that cannot be applicable' and gives about 2,000 CVE entries per month for the 6.1 kernel. Linking that automated step to kernel-cve-triage is an inference.",
              "Search budget was exhausted at the first query of this pass (the shared 200-call limit), so no new independent discovery was possible. This pass is direct re-opens only. Not opened this pass: kernelconfig.io, ConfigFix, Kconfirm, kismet, 0-day, KernelCI, TuxSuite, syzbot; their relevance to CVE applicability stays as in the earlier context file (none of them was shown to decide per-CVE file applicability; that is absence of evidence, not a finding). Fetches of git.openembedded.org, git.kernel.org/vulns and Ubuntu returned 403/504, so oe-core's exclusion script and the kernel CVE JSON repo were not re-read."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Drop or rewrite 'no tool emits a VEX statement for a not-built file': kernel-cve-triage does (slides) and sbom-cve-check does (design.md: status not-applicable-config, justification vulnerable code not present). The open point is the form of the reason, not its existence.",
              "Add sbom-cve-check + Yocto SPDX_INCLUDE_COMPILED_SOURCES (debug-info based, needs a build) to the existing-solutions list. It is the closest deployed alternative that is exact rather than static, and it is not based on Kmax.",
              "Replace 'header-only fixes are out of scope for any object-level tool' with: out of scope for kfold as built; kernel-cve-triage expands headers to including .o files with a depth limit; PatchLens and krepair state header limits.",
              "Replace 'cross-architecture is outside every object-level tool' with: kernel-cve-triage passes all arch/* directories to kmaxall (summarizer read); kfold needs additional per-architecture entry settings to compete.",
              "Restate the 14 percent figure as: 374 of 2,658 filtered CVEs (276/1028 and 98/393 successes of file-compiled checks that run after short-circuiting earlier checks), kernel and config unstated. Never as the share config analysis could remove.",
              "Do not describe the kernel-cve-triage formula as 'flat without explanation' without qualification: the output lists the required options per object ('CONFIG_MTD_NAND_ATMEL && CONFIG_MTD'), which already lets a reader see what is missing. kfold's gain is only that it evaluates the formula against a given .config and names the failing conjunct, which kernel-cve-triage's own eval does not report (it returns a Boolean and the expression, per the code read). State this as a small usability difference.",
              "Do not say 'PatchLens does not evaluate against a .config': its predicates are Boolean and could be evaluated; the paper simply does not do so. Say 'does not ship a config evaluator or VEX output in the paper'.",
              "KCVEPruner: cite only what the README says (work in progress, 3 stars, no validation). Remove the mechanism claim about .o/.cmd/.ko unless the slides are reopened."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "rq3_task",
            "summary": "Not solved by new capability, but not closed either. Verdict: partly (the gap as framed fails; a narrower one holds). As a capability, 'is the touched file built under this .config' is already delivered by (1) CIP kernel-cve-triage (Kmax formulas + VEX output, Siemens/CIP, OSS Europe talk 2026-10-07), (2) Bootlin sbom-cve-check with Yocto SPDX_INCLUDE_COMPILED_SOURCES (exact, from debug info, needs a build), (3) cvehound (Kbuild-only, no #ifdef), and (4) PatchLens (patch-level conditions with #ifdef; Kbuild upward walk; y and m both treated as true). Distros (Debian, Red Hat, Chainguard) record 'not affected' by hand with no machine-readable condition. Adoption evidence is thin everywhere: cvehound 138 stars, sbom-cve-check 58, kmax 72, KCVEPruner 3, kernel-cve-triage unknown (GitLab page showed no stars). The only strong adoption signal is Siemens/CIP's own use and its talks. What remains honestly open for kfold, in order of defensibility: (1) an independent, measured false-negative and agreement study of the file-built verdict against real builds, across kfold, Kmax kreader as kernel-cve-triage uses it (including the ValueError/None path), cvehound's parser and the debug-info route; no such measurement exists in the sources I opened (CIP checked three kernel versions informally; PatchLens cross-checked against Kmax only). (2) Answering for any config without building it, which the build-based route cannot do and which the static routes already do, so this is a cost/convenience claim, not a new one. (3) Cost: a whole-tree table in about one minute versus 5-10 minutes (up to 25 in a first prototype) for kmaxall, but only if measured on the same machine; formulas are cached per revision, so the benefit is moderate. (4) Per-conjunct 'why' against a given .config, a small usability gain over a printed formula. (5) Non-Linux Kbuild trees, where I found no CVE tooling (not re-searched in this pass; budget exhausted). kfold cannot claim first, cannot claim header or cross-arch coverage unless it adds them, and should not claim to beat kernel-cve-triage on accuracy without the measurement. Best use in the paper: an RQ3-style task (stable-update commits as a proxy for fix commits, evaluated against the four builds) positioned as validation and as motivation with the CNA policy quotes, with related work stating the four competitors above plainly.\"",
            "sources_checked": [
              {
