          {
            "lens": "Fit and experiment designer, D9 (CVE applicability: is the file touched by this fix built in my configuration)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "FIT. The question 'is a touched file compiled into my kernel' is an object-selection problem, and kfold's per-object build condition answers it directly. Evaluated against a concrete .config the condition is a plain Boolean check, so no Kconfig model K and no SAT search is needed. This is the easy half of kfold: 'why' without 'config-for'.",
              "FIT, but only one-sided. 'Not built' is a sound reason to drop a fix, because no compiled code contains the touched file. 'Built' says nothing about being affected. Four things sit outside kfold: #ifdef arms inside a built .c file, header-only fixes, built-in versus module versus loaded, and runtime reachability. The Lynx page and PatchLens' header limitation (both in the dossier, not re-opened here) make the same point. So the claim must be 'necessary-condition filter with a soundness check', never 'CVE applicability'.",
              "FIT, edge cases that break soundness. Header-only commits cannot be dropped at file level, because the .h may be included by built files. Count them and treat them as unresolved. Files outside the analyzed entry (non-x86 arch/ files, tools/, scripts/, host programs) are 'not built' only because x86 is the single entry. Report them in a separate bucket or they inflate the headline. The dossier's example CVE-2025-37996 touches arch/arm64/kvm/mmu.c.",
              "NOVELTY. kernel-cve-triage (Kmax-based, CIP/Siemens) already computes this verdict and emits VEX. I re-opened its README and the cvehound README. The dossier's '14%' arithmetic holds: 276+98 = 374 of 2,658. But the file-compiled checks run AFTER the commit checks, on the leftover CVEs. The README pairs read as [successes, runs]: introduced_by_file_compiled [276, 1028] and fixed_by_file_compiled [98, 393]. Each file check therefore succeeds on about a quarter of the CVEs that reach it (my arithmetic: 26.8% and 24.9%). The 14% is an ordering artifact, so it is a lower bound on the file check's standalone power. Do not cite 14% as 'what config analysis is worth'. The example appears to use linux-cip 4.19.324-cip115 and cip_merged_defconfig, but the README does not explicitly say which run produced the output.",
              "GROUND TRUTH. No labelled CVE-versus-config truth is needed. The truth for the object-level claim is the build inventory: is the .o (or its composite parent, or .ko) present after a real build. That exists for four x86 configs (tinyconfig i386, defconfig, Debian, allmodconfig). It tests the correctness of the Kbuild model, not the CVE truth. A reviewer will say so, and the paper should say so first.",
              "PROXY. The 437-commit v7.2.7..v7.2.8 set is a stable update, not a CVE set. The CNA covers most but not all stable fixes. Call it a 'CVE-like fix population' and never report a number as 'CVEs filtered'.",
              "BASELINE AVAILABILITY. The saved Kmax run may not match Linux 7.2.8 or the same entry settings. If it does not, one Kmax run (about 5-15 min per published reports, different hardware) must be announced to the user and run alone under the one-process rule. cvehound's parser may not be callable standalone, so treat it as optional. PatchLens has no public tool confirmed in my sources, so compare to its published failure case (CVE-2023-31436, root Kbuild CONFIG_NET guard) by hand.",
              "COST CLAIM. 'kfold 1 min versus Kmax 5-25 min' compares different machines and kernels. Only a same-machine measurement is citable. Kmax produces formulas for one architecture and kfold's 1 min covers the whole x86 tree, so state the scope of each run.",
              "I did not re-open the Siemens slides, the CIP IRC log, PatchLens, KCVEPruner, the Chainguard page or the Lynx page in this pass. Their claims in the dossier remain as marked there."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Replace the '14% of the CVEs filtered' wording with: 'In kernel-cve-triage's README example the two file-compiled checks removed 276 + 98 = 374 of the 2,658 filtered CVEs, but they run after the commit checks, on 1,028 and 393 remaining CVEs, so their standalone effect is larger than 14%.' The numbers are verified against the README. The kernel and config attribution (cip 4.19.324-cip115, cip_merged_defconfig) is inferred from the preceding command, not stated, so do not cite it as fact.",
              "The dossier says the README counts are 'from a summarizer read'. I re-fetched the README: 'Filtered 2658/2953 - 90.01%', check_introduced_by_file_compiled [276, 1028], check_fixed_by_file_compiled [98, 393]. These can be upgraded from medium to verified, but the page-reading tool is itself a summarizer, so quote only the short strings.",
              "cvehound: the README (re-fetched) says --kernel-config infers the configuration 'based on Kbuild/Makefiles, ifdefs are not checked' and that unmappable files are reported 'unknown' and counted as affected. This matches the dossier.",
              "Experiment design in the dossier is sound but needs three changes. (1) Make the primary unit all objects of the tree times four configs (about 32.9k x 4), not only the 402 commits. This gives a far larger sample for false 'not built' and is cheaper. The commit view becomes a weighted second table. (2) Reuse the 400 already-checked 'why' verdicts instead of recomputing. (3) Add a sound/unsound split in the report: false 'not built' is the only safety-critical error, while false 'built' is mere imprecision.",
              "State the soundness claim narrowly: 'for every object in the entry, kfold's 'not built' verdict agrees with the real build'. Do not write 'kfold decides CVE applicability'. Never claim to be first, since kernel-cve-triage, Kmax and PatchLens exist."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "rq3_task",
            "summary": "Fit: genuine but narrow. The fix-file-built-in-my-config question is an object-selection problem that kfold's build conditions answer. The verdict is one-sided: 'not built' is sound for dropping a CVE, while 'built' only means 'cannot rule out'. #ifdef arms, headers, module state and runtime reachability are out of scope, and kernel-cve-triage already ships this verdict, so the paper must pitch accuracy, explanation and cost rather than capability.\\n\\nSINGLE STRONGEST EXPERIMENT, 'soundness of the not-built verdict against real builds'.\\n\\nInputs, all on hand.\\n- The Linux 7.2.8 whole-tree kfold table (2,225 Makefile instances, about 32.9k objects).\\n- The four fresh builds with object inventories: i386 tinyconfig, x86-64 defconfig, Debian config, x86-64 allmodconfig.\\n- The 437 v7.2.7..v7.2.8 commits, of which 402 touch .c/.S, as a CVE-like fix population (a proxy: not every stable fix has a CVE).\\n- The 400 previously checked 'why' verdicts.\\n- The saved Kmax run, if it matches 7.2.8.\\n\\nProcedure, run sequentially, one process at a time.\\n1. Smoke test: defconfig and 10 commits.\\n2. Level A, object level. For each of the 4 configs and every object in kfold's x86 entry, compare the kfold verdict ('why OBJ --config') with presence in the build inventory. Count a composite parent or a .ko as built. Exclude host programs and scripts/ tools. Report the confusion matrix. The headline metric is the number of false 'not built' (target 0), plus the false 'built' rate and the unresolved rate.\\n3. Level B, commit level. Map the 402 commits' .c/.S files to objects. A commit is 'not affected' only if every touched object is not built. Split the files into x86-reachable, non-x86 arch/ (own bucket, so they do not inflate the result) and header-only commits (counted as out of scope). Report, per config, the fraction of commits dropped, and how many of those drops are confirmed by the inventory.\\n4. Baselines on the same objects: (a) Kmax kreader formulas evaluated the way kernel-cve-triage does, as a Boolean over the enabled CONFIG names (saved run, or one announced kmaxall run); (b) the trivial 'always relevant' baseline; (c) optionally cvehound's parser. For Kmax, report the same false-not-built count, and test the possible fail-open path (kreader errors that leave built=None).\\n5. Classify every disagreement by cause: composite objects, shell-generated names, arch or entry mismatch, tristate, stale inventory.\\n6. Time the whole-tree table against kmaxall on this machine.\\n\\nExpected headline, in this form: 'Over N objects x 4 configs, kfold has F false not-built verdicts (0 expected after classifying causes), agrees on A%, and on 402 stable fixes drops P% under defconfig and Q% under the Debian config, all confirmed by the inventory. Kmax-as-triage has G false not-built on the same objects. A 'why' chain names the failing conjunct in R% of drops.' I cannot give the numbers in advance.\\n\\nEffort: 4-6 person-days (1 day to build the inventory-to-object harness, 1 day for the object-level matrix, 1 day for the commit-level view, 1-2 days for Kmax and baselines and cause classification, 1 day to write up). The kfold runs take minutes. A Kmax run adds 5-15 min if the saved one does not match.\\n\\nWhat would falsify the claim.\\n- Any false 'not built' that traces to a kfold modelling error (not to an inventory or arch artifact) in more than a handful of objects. That would undermine 'sound filter'.\\n- A kfold false-not-built rate no better than Kmax's. Then there is no accuracy contribution.\\n- If the sound drops are mostly arch/ or other-architecture files, the real benefit is small.\\n- If the explanation chain names a wrong or non-failing option.\\n\\nThreats to validity.\\n- Stable fixes are not CVEs.\\n- The four configs are x86 and differ from CIP or embedded configs.\\n- Inventory ground truth can be stale or miss composite and generated objects.\\n- One architecture entry.\\n- Header and #ifdef effects are excluded by design, so the result says nothing about true applicability.\\n- Time comparisons across different machines.\\n- Some earlier claims came through summarizers and are medium confidence.\\n- Reviewers will note that kfold's own config-for work gives it a biased view of which objects matter.\\n\\nPlacement: a compact developer task in RQ3 ('triage a fix commit against a .config'), with one introduction motivation sentence, related-work text comparing kernel-cve-triage, cvehound and PatchLens, and a limitation paragraph (headers, #ifdef, modules, runtime, other architectures).\\n\\nReviewer objections this answers.\\n- 'This is just Kmax.' Answer with measured false-not-built, explanation and same-machine cost.\\n- 'No ground truth.' Answer with real builds.\\n- 'Stable commits are not CVEs.' Say so openly.\\n- 'Only x86.' Answer with a separate bucket and an honest limitation.\\nDo not make it the headline contribution: it is one consumer of the build-condition table, and the fix-to-file mapping is trivial. Do not drop it, because it is cheap, uses existing assets, and gives a sharp soundness number.",
            "sources_checked": [
              {
                "url": "https://gitlab.com/cip-project/cip-kernel/kernel-cve-triage/-/raw/main/README.md",
                "opened": true,
                "note": "Re-fetched. Confirms 'Filtered 2658/2953 - 90.01%' and per-check [successes, runs] pairs: introduced_by_file_compiled [276, 1028], fixed_by_file_compiled [98, 393]. Kernel and config appear to be linux-cip v4.19.324-cip115 with cip_merged_defconfig, inferred from the preceding command and not stated explicitly. The page was read through a summarizing fetch tool.",
                "quote_verbatim": true
