      {
        "id": "D10",
        "name": "Reverse lookup and explanation: which CONFIG builds object or driver X, and why is it missing from my build",
        "dive": {
          "id": "D10",
          "name": "Reverse lookup and explanation: which CONFIG builds object or driver X, and why is it missing from my build",
          "refined_description": "Two questions that people ask with the same words but that have different answers.\n\n(A) Kbuild half. Given an object or driver X and a .config, which Makefile condition decides whether X is built, and which conjunct fails in this config? The answer is a Boolean formula over CONFIG_* options, produced by Makefile logic: obj-$(CONFIG_FOO), composite objects, directory descent, conditionals.\n\n(B) Kconfig half. Why can the option that guards X not be turned on (unmet depends on, a choice that selects another alternative, an architecture gate), or why was it silently dropped from a fragment or defconfig?\n\nThe re-opened evidence shows the manual workflow for (A) is \"read the Makefile\" (Engelhardt 2006; Yamada 2020; Billoo 2021). It shows that most of the pain people describe sits at (B): ConfigFix, Billoo, Greg KH's \"I guess I need to enable OF on my test builds\", and the Cavium Octeon `choice` thread.\n\nkfold's `why OBJ --config .config` answers (A) exactly and names the failing conjunct. It answers (B) only indirectly, through the over-approximate Kconfig model K that config-for uses. The paper should say this boundary out loud.\n\nA related use of the same per-object condition table is (C): \"is any file of this patch or CVE compiled in my configuration?\" Here Kmax-based tools (CIP kernel-cve-triage) and cvehound already consume per-file conditions. And (D): \"is object X built by any configuration the project tests?\", visible in the COMPILE_TEST campaigns and in U-Boot and barebox threads.",
          "who_has_it": "Four classes, in decreasing order of fit for `why`:\n1. CVE and compliance triagers who need \"is this file compiled under my .config\". CIP kernel-cve-triage (Siemens, CIP) and cvehound do this today. `why --config` fits directly.\n2. Patch authors, maintainers and reviewers who compile-test. Examples: Greg KH on the tty/Airoha driver (2025-02); Damien Le Moal and Guenter Roeck on Cavium Octeon (2023-06); Bartosz Golaszewski's GPIO COMPILE_TEST series (2025-04). The pain is the config choice and the Kconfig choice/OF gating that the Makefile cannot explain.\n3. Bootloader and firmware projects (U-Boot, barebox, coreboot), where \"not enabled by any board\" is the equivalent of \"built by no configuration\". Tom Rini removed drivers by hand in 2022; barebox added sandbox allyesconfig to CI in 2025; buildman lists the file-to-board mapping as a TODO.\n4. Distro, embedded and ordinary builders (Arch/Debian forum users, Yocto users, Linux Magazine author, LWN commenters). Their dominant pain is on the Kconfig side (B), so `why` helps them least unless the Kconfig model is integrated.\n\nThe best-served class is 1, followed by 2 and 3.",
          "evidence": [
            {
              "claim": "Manual answer to 'which CONFIG builds module X' in 2006: find the module's directory and read the Makefile line. The asker wanted a database of config options and the modules they produce, including dependencies.",
              "quote": "obj-$(CONFIG_8139TOO) += 8139too.o",
              "url": "https://lkml.iu.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/0603.0/0862.html",
              "date": "2006-03-03 (reply by Jan Engelhardt; the question's date is not shown on the page)",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "LKML 2006 Mieland/Engelhardt thread",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "A 2013 forum thread (13 posts, 12 replies) in which a user cannot map cdrom.ko, libata.ko, usbcore.ko and others to menuconfig options. The replies suggest menuconfig/nconfig search, /proc/config.gz, the cateee.net database, grep of .config, and localmodconfig. No reply mentions a Makefile or Kbuild file. The poster notes search matches only option names.",
              "quote": "I just can't find them anywhere.",
              "url": "https://bbs.archlinux.org/viewtopic.php?id=158476",
              "date": "2013-02-21",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "Arch forum 158476",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Kbuild maintainer on why `make foo/bar.o` builds nothing: an object is built only if its CONFIG dependency holds. The answer is a statement of the rule, with no tool pointer. The same thread shows a kernel developer who runs single-object builds routinely.",
              "quote": "The necessary CONFIG dependency must be met.",
              "url": "https://lkml.rescloud.iu.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/2003.3/08451.html",
              "date": "2020-03-31",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "LKML 2020 single target builds thread (Nossum/Yamada)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "A Linux Magazine author needed a driver on a Tegra board and found the CONFIG symbols by reading driver Makefiles.",
              "quote": "I looked through the requisite driver makefiles to determine which `CONFIG` options needed to be enabled.",
              "url": "https://www.linux-magazine.com/Issues/2021/244/Kconfig-Deep-Dive",
              "date": "2021 (issue 244)",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "Linux Magazine 244 (Billoo)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Same article, Kconfig half: adding the option to the board defconfig had no effect and the kernel did not explain why. This is pain at step (B), which a Kbuild-only explanation does not resolve. Only the opening portion of the article is public.",
              "quote": "the kernel gave no indication as to why the specific `CONFIG` option was ignored",
              "url": "https://www.linux-magazine.com/Issues/2021/244/Kconfig-Deep-Dive",
              "date": "2021 (issue 244)",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "Linux Magazine 244 (Billoo)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "ConfigFix (ICSE SEIP 2021) example: the xconfig option for the QorIQ DPAA2 fsl-mc bus driver cannot be enabled because of 'depends on OF && (ARCH_LAYERSCAPE || (COMPILE_TEST && ...))'. The configurator does not show which part is unmet. This is the Kconfig half of the problem, stated by the research group that built a fix for it. I read the PDF text locally after download.",
              "quote": "Xconfig does not even show which parts of the constraint",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2012.15342",
              "date": "2020-12-30",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "ConfigFix (Franz et al.)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Survey statistic cited by ConfigFix. In the ConfigFix text the survey is reference [34], Hubaux, Xiong and Czarnecki, 'A User Survey of Configuration ...' (VaMoS 2012). The figure is therefore a 2012 survey of Linux users (97 respondents, 81 usable per the earlier sweep, mostly not kernel developers), not a recent kernel-developer survey. Cite it with that date.",
              "quote": "with 20 % of the survey respondents needing roughly “a few dozen minutes” to resolve a conflict",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2012.15342",
              "date": "2012 (survey); cited 2020-12-30",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
              "source_group": "Hubaux et al. 2012 survey (via ConfigFix)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "LWN report on the 2025 defconfig discussion: configuration is the main obstacle to building a kernel, and a missing driver gives a non-working kernel. Torvalds is quoted in the article calling kernel config the nastiest part of building a local kernel. Commenter wtarreau (comments on the same article; which page the comment sits on is taken from the fetch result) says he has read driver descriptions that forget to explain what the driver is for.",
              "quote": "Failing to enable a needed driver will result in a non-working kernel",
              "url": "https://lwn.net/Articles/1026337/",
              "date": "2025-06-24",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "LWN defconfig coverage 2025 (Corbet)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "linux-next tty thread: a maintainer could not build the new Airoha UART driver (depends on (ARCH_AIROHA || COMPILE_TEST) && OF && SERIAL_8250) in his own builds and guessed at the cause by trial. The author tried to make it build under COMPILE_TEST, found configurations that broke compilation in non-trivial ways, and removed COMPILE_TEST. Rothwell's reply (from the earlier sweep, not re-read) was that an allmodconfig build triggers the failure.",
              "quote": "Odd that I can't build it here for some reason, I guess I need to enable OF on my test builds",
              "url": "https://lkml.iu.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/2502.1/00947.html",
              "date": "2025-02-06",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "LKML 2025 tty/Airoha thread",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Cavium Octeon ATA drivers made buildable under COMPILE_TEST; mips:allmodconfig then failed in linux-next because the MIPS machine type is a Kconfig choice and allmodconfig selects a different alternative. Libata maintainer Damien Le Moal first assumed the option was set by allmodconfig, then dropped the patch at rc7 as not worth the effort. A named config (allmodconfig) is the working unit of testing, and it silently misses a choice alternative.",
              "quote": "CONFIG_CAVIUM_OCTEON_SOC is a choice option,",
              "url": "https://lkml.rescloud.iu.edu/2306.2/07959.html",
              "date": "2023-06-22",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "LKML 2023 Cavium Octeon thread",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "GPIO maintainer states that some drivers are not built by allmodconfig although they need no arch headers, and enables COMPILE_TEST for six of them to extend build coverage (zynq, msc313, pl061, rtd, tb10x, tn48m). Direct maintainer statement of problem (D): code that a standard config does not build.",
              "quote": "There are some drivers that aren't build with allmodconfig that don't have any requirements on arch/ headers",
              "url": "https://lkml.rescloud.iu.edu/2504.1/01019.html",
              "date": "2025-04-08",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "LKML 2025 GPIO COMPILE_TEST series (Golaszewski)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "A Realtek rt1016 ASoC driver had no Kconfig or Makefile entry, so no configuration ever built it. The patch was applied by Mark Brown. Instance of a source file with no build condition at all, found by reading, not by a tool. The day is from the patch header; the page also shows a relative age.",
              "quote": "means it isn't ever built.",
              "url": "https://patchew.org/linux/20220405171158.2168762-1-steve@sk2.org/",
              "date": "2022-04-05",
              "evidence_type": "anecdote",
              "source_group": "patchew 2022 ASoC rt1016 (Kitt)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "U-Boot: Tom Rini removed a series of net drivers (patch 01/10 is armada100_fec) because no board enables them. This is the by-hand equivalent of a blindspots listing. U-Boot's own build tool documents the file-to-board mapping only as a TODO (next item).",
              "quote": "This driver is not enabled by any board and not converted to DM_ETH.",
              "url": "https://www.mail-archive.com/u-boot@lists.denx.de/msg436281.html",
              "date": "2022-03-31",
