              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Kernel policy text that creates the demand: a patch should build cleanly with the applicable or modified CONFIG options set to y, m and n, pass allnoconfig/allmodconfig, and build on several architectures. The checklist does not say how to find which options are applicable.",
              "quote": "Builds cleanly with applicable or modified `CONFIG` options `=y`, `=m`, and `=n`.",
              "url": "https://docs.kernel.org/process/submit-checklist.html",
              "date": "n.d. (current docs)",
              "evidence_type": "policy",
              "source_group": "docs.kernel.org submit-checklist",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Downstream consumer of per-file build conditions: Siemens/CIP slides. Linux has about 85,000 files, typically 5-10% used. For CIP kernels with the CIP defconfig 90-95% of CVEs reported in 2024 were judged not relevant. The slides list 'Be fast' and 'No false negatives' as goals and say the use case is described by a Kconfig. Read from the downloaded PDF text.",
              "quote": "Typically only about 5% to 10% are used",
              "url": "https://opensource.siemens.com/events/2025/slides/Christoph_Steiger__Triaging_CVEs_for_the_Linux_Kernel.pdf",
              "date": "2025-06-04",
              "evidence_type": "statistic",
              "source_group": "Siemens/CIP kernel-cve-triage",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "CIP kernel-cve-triage decides 'is this file compiled' by running kmaxall once per kernel revision and then kreader per object. The result is evaluated as a plain Boolean expression over enabled CONFIG names. When the object is not built the function returns False plus the parsed expression; the code does not compute the missing option itself (read through a summarizer, so medium confidence on that last point). This is the closest existing use of the reverse-lookup output.",
              "quote": "cmd = [\"kreader\", \"--kmax-formulas\", kmax, \"--show-constraints\", str(path)]",
              "url": "https://gitlab.com/cip-project/cip-kernel/kernel-cve-triage/-/raw/main/src/cve_triage/triage/kmax.py",
              "date": "n.d. (repo main, fetched 2026-10-17)",
              "evidence_type": "tool",
              "source_group": "Siemens/CIP kernel-cve-triage",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "cvehound's --kernel-config mode infers the config of an affected file from Kbuild/Makefiles with a line-based parser. Files it cannot map are reported 'unknown' and counted as affected. The README gives no false-positive or false-negative rates.",
              "quote": "ifdefs are not checked",
              "url": "https://github.com/evdenis/cvehound",
              "date": "n.d. (README fetched 2026-10-17)",
              "evidence_type": "tool",
              "source_group": "cvehound",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "The kmax docs say extracted formulas may be inexact and describe what a failed result means: an unsatisfiable result is a sign of over-constraint, and a result that lacks the unit is a sign of under-constraint. They describe the formula as the output, with no explanation facility; the README and advanced.md document none.",
              "quote": "The extracted formulas may not be exact.",
              "url": "https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax/blob/master/docs/advanced.md",
              "date": "n.d. (fetched 2026-10-17)",
              "evidence_type": "tool",
              "source_group": "kmax docs (Gazzillo group)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "The official trimmed-kernel guide admits that a configurer cannot anticipate everything needed and that even kernel developers forget things. It documents localmodconfig and modprobed-db but no way to map a needed driver to its option.",
              "quote": "it is hard to think of everything that might be needed",
              "url": "https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/quickly-build-trimmed-linux.html",
              "date": "n.d. (current docs)",
              "evidence_type": "policy",
              "source_group": "docs.kernel.org trimmed-kernel guide",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            }
          ],
          "existing_solutions": [
            {
              "tool_or_process": "menuconfig/nconfig/xconfig symbol search ('/')",
              "what_it_does": "Searches symbol names and jumps to the symbol's location in the menu. The kernel docs say it 'searches for kernel configuration symbol names'.",
              "limits": "Needs the symbol name, not the module or object name (Arch thread: i2c-algo-bit is I2C_ALGOBIT). Does not say which Makefile condition builds an object. I did not see what fields the search result shows, so I assert nothing about them. ConfigFix says xconfig does not show which parts of a constraint are unmet.",
              "url": "https://docs.kernel.org/kbuild/kconfig.html"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "Manual grep of Makefiles (obj-$(CONFIG_X) lines) and Kconfig help ('module will be called ...')",
              "what_it_does": "Gives the controlling symbol for simple cases; this is what the 2006 LKML answer, Billoo (2021) and Yamada (2020) recommend.",
              "limits": "Breaks on composite objects, variables, conditionals and parent-directory gates (the condition is a conjunction over the directory path). Gives no verdict against a given .config. Does not help with the Kconfig half.",
              "url": "https://lkml.iu.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/0603.0/0862.html"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "kernelconfig.io",
              "what_it_does": "Search site advertised as 'search config information for linux kernel modules', with selectable kernel versions (e.g. 6.18.57, 7.3-rc7, 7.2.11) and about 15 architectures.",
              "limits": "I saw only the landing page. Whether it supports reverse lookup from an object or file, or shows dependencies, is unverified. Show HN post in 2023-12 drew 5 points and 0 comments (earlier sweep).",
              "url": "https://www.kernelconfig.io/index.html"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "LKDDb (cateee.net)",
              "what_it_does": "Database generated from kernel sources that lists hardware IDs, the kernel configuration menu needed to build a driver, and the driver filename.",
              "limits": "Stale: newest news entry 2007-11-22 and the page was generated 2014-11-10; its example references a 2.6 tree. Not usable for current kernels.",
              "url": "https://www.cateee.net/lkddb/"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "klocalizer --repair --include foo.o (Kmax suite, kmax 4.10 released 2026-08-18) and koverage",
              "what_it_does": "Forward direction: repairs a config (e.g. allnoconfig) so that a named object such as drivers/usb/storage/alauda.o is built. koverage reports how much of a patch a config covers. The Kmax tool outputs 'the list of configuration conditions for each compilation unit file' as C-style Boolean expressions.",
              "limits": "README and advanced.md document no explanation of why an object is absent from a given config (not documented, not shown to be absent). They state formulas 'may not be exact', that repair may omit dead code or header lines, and that examples run one architecture per invocation. Setup needs SuperC with Java dependencies. Kmax slides (earlier sweep) report 84 s on x86 for formula extraction; krepair says kmax takes 10-15 minutes (not re-verified).",
              "url": "https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "CIP kernel-cve-triage (kmaxall + kreader)",
              "what_it_does": "Whole-tree Kmax formulas once per kernel revision, then evaluates each object's condition against the enabled option list. Reports built false/true and the condition string for CVE filtering.",
              "limits": "Returns the condition but does not compute the missing option (per summarizer reading). No Kconfig model. Generation takes about 5-10 minutes per revision per the author in an Aug 2024 CIP meeting (IRC log from the earlier sweep, not re-read). Accuracy checked only on three kernel versions by the developer.",
              "url": "https://gitlab.com/cip-project/cip-kernel/kernel-cve-triage/-/raw/main/README.md"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "cvehound --kernel-config",
              "what_it_does": "Line-based Kbuild parser maps affected files to plain-string CONFIG conditions and evaluates them against a parsed .config.",
              "limits": "Handles only some conditional forms; ifdefs not checked; unmappable files are counted as affected; no solver, no Kconfig model, no accuracy numbers.",
              "url": "https://github.com/evdenis/cvehound"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "PatchLens (FSE 2026, arXiv 2606.25863)",
              "what_it_does": "Per-patch resolver, 'inspired by KBuildMiner', that walks up the Makefile tree and conjoins directory predicates to give a Boolean vulnerability impact condition.",
              "limits": "From the earlier sweep, not re-opened here. No Kconfig dependency model, does not evaluate against a concrete distro .config, and the paper reports 27 of 500 reviewed reports (5.4%) as over-approximate.",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.25863"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "localmodconfig and modprobed-db",
              "what_it_does": "Derives a config from the modules loaded on a running system.",
              "limits": "Only what was loaded at the time; the kernel guide itself warns the result 'is likely to disable kernel features you did not use since you booted'. Does not explain individual objects.",
              "url": "https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/quickly-build-trimmed-linux.html"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "U-Boot buildman and qconfig.py",
              "what_it_does": "Selects boards by attributes and finds boards by CONFIG combination across defconfigs.",
              "limits": "Board-level, not file-level: the file-to-board mapping is a TODO in the buildman docs. Does not list code that no board enables.",
              "url": "https://docs.u-boot-project.org/en/latest/build/buildman.html"
            },
            {
              "tool_or_process": "COMPILE_TEST annotations, allmodconfig/allyesconfig and randconfig by CI",
              "what_it_does": "Maintainers manually opt drivers into broader configs; CI samples configs.",
              "limits": "Per-driver manual effort (GPIO, SPI rb4xx, rt2x00, pic32 and Hovold threads). Cavium thread shows allmodconfig can still miss a choice alternative. A COMPILE_TEST opt-in tells nothing about drivers that have not yet been annotated.",
              "url": "https://lkml.rescloud.iu.edu/2504.1/01019.html"
            }
          ],
          "remaining_gap": "1. No tool I could verify gives an exact, whole-tree, per-object condition table together with a 'why not' verdict against a given .config that names the failing conjunct. Kmax-based tools output the formula and leave the user to compare; cvehound's parser is heuristic; kernelconfig.io's capability is unverified.\n\n2. The Kconfig half is not closed. Users cannot see why the guarding option cannot be turned on (unmet depends, choice, arch) or why it was dropped. ConfigFix is the research answer for conflict resolution (no 'why object absent' link). A Kbuild-only `why` leaves exactly the step that Billoo, ConfigFix, Greg KH and the Cavium thread complain about.\n\n3. The reverse lookup has no demand data of its own. I found no 2023-2026 survey of kernel developers asking about it, and the earlier sweep found no LKML thread of the form 'which CONFIG builds file X' (absence of evidence, tools blocked). The evidence is a set of independent anecdotes, one 2012 survey figure and a set of workarounds.\n\n4. No quantitative measure of how often the Kbuild-side explanation is the complete answer for a user. That is what the proposed experiment measures.",
          "kfold_contribution": "Modest and specific.\n(1) For every object of a tree, an exact Boolean condition produced by symbolic execution of the Makefiles. On the available assets, Linux 7.2.8 is analyzed in about a minute (2,225 Makefile instances, about 32.9k objects). The same engine runs on BusyBox, Barebox, U-Boot and coreboot with per-project entry settings.\n(2) `why OBJ --config .config` returns a verdict and a condition chain that names the failing conjunct(s). Kmax-based tools give the formula; the CIP code leaves the comparison to the caller (summarizer reading). I do not claim klocalizer cannot explain; I claim its documentation shows no such feature.\n(3) 400 `why` verdicts have already been checked against builds, which gives an accuracy figure the cvehound README and kmax docs do not report.\n(4) The same condition table serves blindspots (objects no given config builds), which is the by-hand work done in U-Boot (Rini 2022), the GPIO/SPI/pic32 COMPILE_TEST campaigns and barebox's allyesconfig CI.\n\nNot claimed: explaining unmet Kconfig dependencies in general (only through config-for's over-approximate model K), #ifdefs inside sources, runtime or boot-time configuration, or any 'first' claim. Prior art is Kmax, klocalizer, CIP kernel-cve-triage, cvehound, PatchLens.",
          "quantitative_facts": [
            "Linux 6.16 has 32,468 configuration options on x86_64; the LWN article says 'it's hard to tell exactly how many configurations are being covered' (LWN, Daroc Alden, 2025-09-10, re-opened). https://lwn.net/Articles/1034811/",
            "Siemens/CIP (2025-06-04, slides read from PDF text): Linux has about 85,000 files and 40M LoC, typically 5-10% used; with the CIP defconfig 90-95% of 2024 CVEs were judged 'not relevant'; at 4.19 EOL 56% of about 1000 unfixed CVEs could be excluded; about 60 CVEs per week. The KCVEPruner slides (earlier sweep) give 104 per week from NVD; cite one with its source.",
            "Hubaux, Xiong, Czarnecki 2012 survey, cited by ConfigFix as ref [34]: 20% of respondents needed roughly 'a few dozen minutes' to resolve a conflict; 97 Linux users answered, 81 usable (earlier sweep read the primary PDF). Not a kernel-developer survey and 14 years old.",
            "Arch forum 2013 thread: 13 posts, 12 replies, zero mention a Makefile (re-opened). Anecdote, not a rate.",
            "The CIP tool needs about 5-10 minutes to generate Kmax formulas per kernel revision and about 10 seconds per CVE afterwards (author, CIP IRC 2024-08-22; earlier sweep). kfold's whole-tree analysis of Linux 7.2.8 takes about a minute (own data).",
            "krepair (FSE 2024), earlier sweep: defconfig covers 21.7% of patched lines, one randconfig 29.2%, allyesconfig 88.5%; krepair 98.5%; 507 patches. Line-level and includes #ifdef arms, so it is not a measure of object-level unbuilt share.",
            "kmax 4.10 released 2026-08-18 (PyPI); its examples use one architecture per invocation.",
            "Archive text-search counts (mail-archive.com, earlier sweep, not re-run): 'compile tested only' 2,372 matches on linux-kernel, 'allmodconfig' 2,133 (inflated by robot mail), 'build coverage' 227 on the u-boot list. Frequency impressions only.",
            "The earlier sweep found no 2023-2026 survey that asks kernel maintainers about compile-testing, build-condition lookup or config coverage; the KernelCI 2020 survey (just under 100 responses) lists per-patch testing as the top need and reports nothing on config coverage."
          ],
          "proposed_experiment": "Single experiment on existing assets: how often is the Kbuild-side explanation the whole answer?\n\nMethod. For each of the four Linux build inventories (i386 tinyconfig, x86-64 defconfig, Debian config, x86-64 allmodconfig), take every object of the Linux 7.2.8 object list that is absent from the build. Run `why OBJ --config .config`, one object at a time (the project rule: one process at a time, Z3 single-threaded; the whole-tree analysis is about one minute and then each query is a formula evaluation; measure the total before stating a duration). Classify the failing conjunct of each verdict as:\n(a) an option that is simply n and that K says can be set without conflict, so the Kbuild chain is the full explanation;\n(b) an option that K shows cannot be set in this configuration (unmet depends, a different choice alternative such as the Cavium case, or an architecture gate), so the Kconfig half is the real answer;\n(c) a directory- or architecture-level gate (the object lives outside the entry directories);\n(d) 'built' in disagreement with the inventory, counted as a kfold error.\n\nReport the fractions per config, check the `why` verdicts against the inventories (the 400-verdict set is already done; this extends it to every absent object), and hand-check about 100 sampled class-(b) cases in menuconfig. The (a):(b) ratio is the number needed to decide how much the Kconfig half limits `why`. Output goes to a results file.\n\nSecondary follow-up (not both): compare `why`'s per-object condition with the saved Kmax formulas on Linux and BusyBox, and adjudicate disagreements against the build inventories.",
          "risks": "1. Search budget: the session's WebSearch limit was exhausted after the first query, so only 1 of the requested 15+ new searches ran (it returned 2006-2013 forum and LKML material). No new independent discovery of 2023-2026 sources was possible. Verification was done by re-opening sources, about 35 WebFetch calls plus local pdftotext of two PDFs, not by new searches. The coverage gaps listed in the earlier sweep (no lore full text, no maintainer survey, Linux-heavy evidence) remain.\n\n2. Demand evidence is mostly anecdote. The strongest D10-specific items are old (2006 LKML, 2013 forum, 2020 LKML) or on the Kconfig side. The 20% survey statistic is from 2012 (Hubaux et al., ref [34] in ConfigFix), not a recent kernel-developer survey. The recent maintainer items (GPIO, SPI, tty, Cavium) are about compile coverage and Kconfig gating, not literally 'which CONFIG builds file X'.\n\n3. The central pain is half outside kfold. A reviewer can say that `why` explains the part users already solve by grepping Makefiles, and not the Kconfig step. The experiment above exists to put a number on this.\n\n4. Counter-evidence: Shuah Khan on staging said 'Compile testing isn't sufficient'; Jens Axboe said compile-only testing is far from functional testing; the KernelCI 2020 survey's top need was per-patch testing; Linaro and KernelCI describe their pain as feedback latency, not config choice. Maintainers' stated 2026 concerns were review load and linux-next test breakage. Incumbents (0-day, Rothwell's daily allmodconfig builds) already catch hard build errors.\n\n5. Competitor claims need care. klocalizer/koverage may document less than they do (I read the README, PyPI page and advanced.md, not the source), so write 'not documented', never 'cannot'. Kmax formula timing figures come from earlier sweeps, not re-verified. CIP code behaviour was read through a summarizer.\n\n6. Source reliability. Several quotes are from LWN reports, not transcripts (LWN 1050027 words are Corbet's). Hovold's bcm2835 patch says 'wider build coverage' only; earlier statistics that it was 'covered by no standard build' are not supported by the re-opened page. kernelconfig.io: landing page only. The wtarreau comment quotes came from a fetch of the article page; the earlier sweep placed them on the 1026717 page, so cite as 'comments on LWN's defconfig coverage'. Kconfig docs fetch did not show the menuconfig search result fields.\n\n7. Double-blind and 'first' claims: avoid both; Kmax, klocalizer, CIP kernel-cve-triage, cvehound and PatchLens are prior art for the per-object condition.",
          "confidence": "medium"
        },
        "verdicts": [
          {
            "lens": "Evidence auditor (refute attempt) for D10: reverse lookup and explanation of which CONFIG builds object X and why it is missing from a build",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "Demand evidence is a handful of independent anecdotes. I found no rate or survey for a 'which CONFIG builds file X' lookup. The dossier admits this, and my checks found nothing that changes it.",
              "The one numeric demand statistic is mis-framed. ConfigFix writes that 20% of respondents needed 'a few dozen minutes' to 'resolve a conflict'. The primary source, Hubaux et al. (VaMoS 2012), says 20% of Linux users need at least a 'few dozen minutes', on average, to figure out how to activate an inactive option. It is a 2012 survey of 97 Linux users (81 usable answers, so about 16 people), recruited from 11 forums and mailing lists. It concerns the Kconfig half (B), not the Kbuild half (A).",
              "Cavium Octeon thread (lkml.rescloud.iu.edu/2306.2/07959.html): the dossier says 'Damien Le Moal ... dropped the patch at rc7 as not worth the effort'. The page attributes the 'not sure it's worth the effort' wording to Rob Herring, the patch author. Damien's stated reason is the schedule: 'given that we are at rc7 already, this effort should not be now anyway'. The Roeck quote is verbatim ('CONFIG_CAVIUM_OCTEON_SOC is a choice option, meaning only one of the choices can be set') and 'mips:allmodconfig selects a different choice' is also on the page. Damien's 'I would assume that CONFIG_CAVIUM_OCTEON_SOC is set ?' is verbatim.",
              "Airoha thread: the dossier's summary omits that Greg KH replied 'No, you should allow COMPILE_TEST to build the code, right?' to the proposal to drop COMPILE_TEST. The author then removed COMPILE_TEST anyway, after finding configurations that 'broke the compilation in non trivial ways', and posted a v4. The Greg quote is verbatim but ends with '...'. The Kconfig line 'depends on (ARCH_AIROHA || COMPILE_TEST) && OF && SERIAL_8250' is on the page. The page shows no Rothwell reply and no allmodconfig mention, so keep that claim out. The dossier date of 2025-02-06 is plausible (URL 2502.1, header '06/02/2025'), but the header could be read as either dd/mm or mm/dd.",
              "The kmax claim 'no explanation facility; README and advanced.md document none' is too strong. docs/advanced.md mentions `klocalizer --view-kbuild --include <unit>`, which shows the Kbuild constraints for a unit and its subdirectories. It also mentions `klocalizer --show-unsat-core`, which shows the constraints behind an unsatisfiable request. koverage reports FILE_EXCLUDED and LINE_EXCLUDED_FILE_INCLUDED, which say whether a file is excluded but not why. The README fetch did not show those flags and returned no version info, so the dossier's 'kmax 4.10 released 2026-08-18' is unverified by me. Do not write 'no explanation' about klocalizer. Safe wording: 'its documentation offers constraint views and an unsat core, but no per-config verdict that names the failing conjunct'. Even that is only from documentation, not source.",
              "CIP kernel-cve-triage: the kmax.py quote is verbatim. Per the fetch summary, the code has no missing-option computation and returns (bool, expression). The summary also says FileCompileStatus sets built='unknown' on None, and a ValueError is raised when kreader prints nothing. That contradicts the earlier-sweep claim G75 that a ValueError could end as 'no file is compiled', a possible false negative. Do not assert that false-negative path. Both readings came through a summarizer, so medium confidence.",
              "The ConfigFix sentence 'Xconfig does not even show which parts of the constraint [are unmet]' is verbatim, and the fsl-mc 'depends on OF && (ARCH_LAYERSCAPE || ...)' example is on the page. It is specific to xconfig in 2020. The dossier says it saw no menuconfig search result fields, so it cannot claim that menuconfig also hides unmet parts. My fetch of docs.kernel.org/kbuild/kconfig.html also shows no 'Depends on' text. From my own knowledge, menuconfig search results do print Depends on and Selected by with current symbol values. I did not verify that on a page I opened, so treat it as an unverified counter-risk: a reviewer may say that part of (B) is already handled in menuconfig.",
              "Source independence: ConfigFix and Hubaux are one group's evidence on the same Kconfig-side problem. Billoo (Linux Magazine) is a single author, and the article is paywalled after the intro. Both of its quotes appear in the public intro, so they are verified. The Arch 2013 thread, the 2006 LKML thread and the 2020 Yamada exchange are three separate anecdotes, 7 to 20 years old. Greg KH, Cavium, GPIO, SPI, rt2x00, pic32 and qcom are all COMPILE_TEST or build-coverage items (problem D), not the (A) question of which CONFIG builds X. Count them as one theme, 'COMPILE_TEST coverage campaigns', not as many sources.",
              "Several items rest on LWN paraphrase. LWN 1050027 ('cause some code to be disabled, and can thus hide problems') is Corbet's narration of Torvalds, not a Torvalds quote. LWN 1026337 contains the 'Failing to enable a needed driver...' quote and the Torvalds 'nastiest part' quote. The wtarreau comment (2025-06-24 18:25 UTC) is about a missing CONFIG_MOTORCOM_PHY on a single-board computer, a Kconfig-side symbol-hunt anecdote. The dossier's paraphrase that he 'read driver descriptions that forget to explain what the driver is for' roughly matches, but I saw only the summarizer text.",
              "Siemens/CIP slides: I confirmed from the downloaded PDF's text that 'Typically only about 5% to 10% are used', '~85.000 files', 'Around 60 CVEs each week', 'Be fast', 'No false negatives' and 'For CIP kernels /w CIP defconfig 90% - 95% of CVEs ... ' are present. The '56% of ~1000 unfixed CVEs' line is also there. The dossier quote spells '85,000', but the slide writes '85.000', so cite the slide's own text. The 5-10% figure says 'used' and is not defined as 'compiled'. It is the authors' estimate and should not be turned into a measured unbuilt share. The 90-95% 'not relevant' figure is the authors' own judgement for one defconfig and has no stated validation.",
              "Policy and tool claims confirmed: U-Boot buildman's TODO list says 'hunting for problems, perhaps by building a few boards for each arch, or checking commits for changed files and building only boards which use those files'. It is a hedged idea ('perhaps') in a 'scope for more' list, not an admission of a missing feature, so soften 'operator-level admission'. Rini's patch 01/10 (2022-03-31) and the Fatoum barebox CI commit message (2025-03-26) are verbatim. The barebox text says 'compile-tested drivers' but never states that normal defconfigs do not build them. That part is inference, as the dossier half-admits.",
              "The cvehound quotes hold. The README says `--kernel-config` works 'based on Kbuild/Makefiles, ifdefs are not checked'. Files the parser cannot map are reported 'unknown' and counted as affected. No false-positive rate is stated. The 'line-based parser' and 'unknown options count as disabled' details came from a summarizer read of the code and were not re-verified here.",
              "The 2006 Engelhardt reply is verbatim and dated Fri 03 Mar 2006 08:33 EST. The Arch thread has 13 posts (original plus 12 replies), from 2013-02-21 to 2013-03-01, and no reply mentions a Makefile or Kbuild file. The rt1016 patch is verbatim ('which AFAICT means it isn't ever built'), dated 5 April 2022, and Mark Brown applied it. All of these are confirmed.",
              "Counter-evidence is already listed in the dossier (Shuah Khan 'Compile testing isn't sufficient', the KernelCI survey, mostly latency and review-load concerns). My one search found no dedicated reverse-lookup tool for 'which CONFIG builds file X'. That is consistent with the gap, but it is a single standard search and not exhaustive."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Hubaux 20% figure: say '20% of 81 usable respondents to a 2012 Linux user survey report needing at least a few dozen minutes, on average, to figure out how to activate an inactive option', not 'to resolve a conflict'. Cite Hubaux, Xiong, Czarnecki, 'A User Survey of Configuration Challenges in Linux and eCos', VaMoS 2012 (confirmed as ConfigFix reference [34]). Cite ConfigFix only for the sentence 'A survey [34] found that users are commonly challenged with conflict resolution...'.",
              "Cavium: attribute 'not sure it's worth the effort' to Rob Herring (the author). Damien Le Moal dropped the patch because 'we are at rc7 already'. Roeck's mail is dated 6/22/23, and Herring's original patch Jun 14, 2023. Damien's reply date is unclear on the page.",
              "Airoha: add that Greg KH first said COMPILE_TEST should be kept. The author (Benjamin Larsson) removed it after finding configurations that 'broke the compilation in non trivial ways'. Drop the Rothwell/allmodconfig claim because it is not on the page.",
              "Kmax/klocalizer: replace 'no explanation facility' with 'documents `--view-kbuild` (Kbuild constraints for a unit) and `--show-unsat-core`, but no per-config verdict naming the failing conjunct (documentation only)'. Drop the 'kmax 4.10, 2026-08-18' release claim unless it is re-sourced to PyPI. The GitHub README page I fetched showed no version or releases.",
              "Do not state that the CIP tool can produce a false negative via ValueError. The code, as summarized, raises ValueError on empty kreader output, and 'unknown' is the status on None.",
              "Buildman wording: it is a TODO idea phrased 'perhaps ... or checking commits for changed files and building only boards which use those files'. Do not present it as an admission that the file-to-board mapping is missing.",
              "Slide quote: the Siemens slide spells '~85.000 files'. Cite it as written, or write '~85k files'.",
              "Date of the Airoha mail: keep '2025-02-06' only with the caveat that the header's '06/02/2025' is ambiguous. The archive URL (2502.1) supports February.",
              "wtarreau comment: the content is a missing CONFIG_MOTORCOM_PHY (a Kconfig-side hunt) with suggested 'config profiles', not specifically 'descriptions that forget what the driver is for'. Quote only what is on the page."
            ],
            "sources_checked": [
