            ],
            "use_in_paper": "motivation",
            "summary": "Partly holds. Of the 19 load-bearing URLs I opened, 18 exist and their quotes are verbatim. For one (kmax README) I found no matching sentence and no version info. The dossier's anecdotes are real, but it makes two attribution errors (Cavium, Airoha) and one mis-framed statistic (Hubaux 20%). It also overstates one competitor claim: kmax advanced.md documents `--view-kbuild` and `--show-unsat-core`, so 'no explanation facility' is too strong. No rate or survey data supports demand for a 'which CONFIG builds X' lookup. Most evidence is about COMPILE_TEST coverage campaigns (problem D) or the Kconfig half (B), so it is thematically one source and not many. Use it as motivation with soft wording, flagged as anecdotal. The Kconfig-side limit of `why` should be stated plainly, and any claim of kfold's novelty over klocalizer must be phrased 'not documented'. D10 is weak as a standalone evaluation task (RQ3) unless the proposed (a):(b) experiment is run."
          },
          {
            "lens": "Hostile competitor analyst, D10 (reverse lookup and explanation: which CONFIG builds object X, why is it missing from my build)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "WRONG CLAIM IN DOSSIER: it says the kmax/klocalizer docs 'document no explanation' of why an object is absent. docs/advanced.md documents `klocalizer --view-kbuild --include <unit>` ('View the Kbuild constraints for a compilation unit and each of its subdirectories'). The example output is `[And(CONFIG_SQUASHFS, CONFIG_SQUASHFS_DECOMP_MULTI)]`. It also documents `--show-unsat-core` ('Use `--show-unsat-core` to see what constraints are causing the issue:'), with an example that forces `--undefine CONFIG_USB` and includes alauda.o, annotated 'no configuration possible because alauda depends on USB'. So per-unit Kbuild constraints and a Kbuild+Kconfig unsat-core explanation already exist in the Kmax suite. Source: https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax/blob/master/docs/advanced.md (read through the fetch tool's summarizer; I could not copy the full sections).",
              "The PyPI page for kmax 4.10 (https://pypi.org/project/kmax/) does not mention --view-kbuild, --show-unsat-core, kreader or kmaxall. The README also does not. These features sit only in docs/advanced.md. Anyone who searches only the README or PyPI will conclude 'no explanation'. A reviewer who opens advanced.md will find them. The paper must not say 'Kmax has no explanation facility'.",
              "koverage documents per-unit status codes. FILE_EXCLUDED means the unit is not included because preprocessing failed. LINE_EXCLUDED_FILE_INCLUDED means the unit was preprocessed but the line is not included. This is a diagnostic of 'why not covered' at line level, not a condition chain. Same advanced.md source.",
              "The klocalizer troubleshooting section lists several causes for a unit that is 'configured but not built': already compiled (make clean), unit cannot be built individually, composite units are not source files, compile-time error in the config, wrong formulas. This is an explanation checklist for the Kbuild half. It is manual, not a computed verdict.",
              "Because klocalizer solves Kbuild plus Kconfig constraints together, `--show-unsat-core` with the user's options pinned could answer BOTH halves (A) and (B) of D10. The dossier's position, that the Kconfig half is out of kfold's reach and only reachable through the over-approximate model K, therefore means klocalizer is stronger than kfold on exactly the half users complain about most. I did not run it. The claim is from documentation only. Its unsat-core output format and cost per query are not documented in what I read.",
              "The dossier's 'remaining gap 1' (no tool gives a 'why not' verdict against a given .config that names the failing conjunct) is only partly true. The CIP kernel-cve-triage code (kmax.py, read via summarizer) runs `kreader --kmax-formulas <kmax> --show-constraints <file.o>`, evaluates the Boolean tree against the enabled options, and returns (False, bool_expr). The caller therefore gets the whole condition, not only a yes/no. Nobody computes the failing conjunct, but the user can read it from the returned expression. The residual claim is narrow: a single command that takes a .config and prints which conjuncts fail. Even that is a small step beyond klocalizer plus the CIP code.",
              "kernelconfig.io is no longer 'unverified'. Its about page says 'Dependencies and automatically selected kernel modules/builtins are displayed' and that it gives links to source files where possible. So a web reverse lookup (module or option to dependencies) exists for the Kconfig side, covering many versions and architectures. I could not verify whether it maps an object path to the Makefile condition. It is a hobby-scale site (a Show HN with 5 points and 0 comments, per the earlier sweep).",
              "In-tree and ecosystem tools already cover pieces: (i) menuconfig/nconfig search shows 'Defined at', 'Prompt', 'Location' and 'Depends on' with current values like `ARM [=n]`. I saw this in a 2019 patch description whose example is U-Boot's Kconfig (arch/arm/mach-stm32mp), not Linux, so the field list for Linux is only partly verified (https://lkml.iu.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/1912.2/03106.html). The kernel docs page I fetched says only that search matches symbol names (https://docs.kernel.org/kbuild/kconfig.html). (ii) scripts/kconfig/streamline_config.pl (localmodconfig) maps module names to CONFIG symbols by regex over Makefile/Kbuild `obj-$(CONFIG_X)` lines and prints a 'config not found!' style warning for unmapped modules. It handles only the simple form, so it fails on composite objects and conditionals (https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/scripts/kconfig/streamline_config.pl). (iii) Yocto's kernel_configcheck task warns 'specified values did not make it into the kernel's final configuration' for dropped fragment options, with a hint such as 'No reference to SCSI_DEBUG found'. It does not name the unmet dependency (https://openembedded.org/pipermail/openembedded-core/2019-July/284554.html). So the 'silently dropped from a fragment' pain is detected (not explained) in Yocto.",
              "Practical 'solved by building' counter: developers answer 'is X built' by `make path/x.o` or `make -n`, and by 0-day, linux-next and allmodconfig CI. The dossier already holds Kbuild-maintainer and Rothwell quotes of this kind. The need for a static answer is argued, not shown.",
              "ConfigFix (Kconfig half): status is unclear. It was posted as 'kbuild, kconfig: Add support for conflict resolution' v6 on 2024-10-27 (11 patches, PicoSAT, xconfig UI). The cover letter reports 'Around 80 % of the conflicts could be resolved' and '99.9 % of the generated fixes resolved the conflict'. I found no confirmation of a merge (unverified). The letter does not clearly say it explains root causes. It proposes fixes. Source: https://lkml.rescloud.iu.edu/2410.3/04900.html. A 2025 VaMoS demo exists (https://research.chalmers.se/en/publication/547485).",
              "Kmax-suite adoption evidence is mixed. LWN says kmax is integrated into Intel's 0-day test suite and found 'a number of bugs' (https://lwn.net/Articles/1034811/). This concerns kismet and Kconfig-bug finding. The krepair paper does not say 0-day adopted kmax, so the earlier line 'kmax in 0-day' must be tied to LWN, not the paper. GitHub: 72 stars, 29 forks, 1,289 commits, 77 open issues (fetched 2026-10-17), no GitHub release listed. PyPI: 55 release entries, 34 of them pre-releases, latest 4.10 on 2026-08-18. cvehound: 138 stars, 17 forks, 1,204 commits (README states no accuracy figures). No developer-facing usage of `--view-kbuild` or `--show-unsat-core` found anywhere. I could not search for it, so absence of evidence only.",
              "Timing evidence is thin. 'kclause typically taking 2-3 minutes, kmax 10-15 minutes, and SuperC about a minute or less' comes from the krepair paper (https://arxiv.org/html/2404.17966v1). It describes obtaining the constraints for a single line's patch workflow on commodity hardware. It is not a head-to-head with kfold's one-minute whole-tree run. CIP's README gives no timing figures ('can take a noticeable amount of time'), so the '5-10 minutes' figure rests on the earlier-sweep IRC log, which is unverified here. A speed claim needs a measured comparison on the same machine.",
              "The krepair paper reports no accuracy measurement for the Kbuild constraints (confirmed in the text I read: it says only 'any limitations of these tools limit krepair'). So kfold's 400 `why` verdicts checked against builds are an actual differentiator, but only if the 400 verdicts are not all easy cases. Report the share of composite, conditional or variable-defined objects among them.",
              "Search budget was exhausted. I could run only 5 searches, of which 3 produced results. I did not find new 2023-2026 tools beyond those in the dossier, and could not check Kconfirm, tuxmake and Fedora or Debian config tooling. The conclusion that no other tool exists is therefore not established. raw.githubusercontent.com returned HTTP 429 for two files. I used github.com blob pages instead."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Replace 'klocalizer documents no explanation' with: klocalizer documents `--view-kbuild` (per-unit Kbuild constraints, including subdirectories) and `--show-unsat-core` (unsat constraints for a failed --include), and koverage documents FILE_EXCLUDED and LINE_EXCLUDED_FILE_INCLUDED. All of it is in docs/advanced.md, not the README or PyPI page.",
              "Replace 'kernelconfig.io capability unverified' with: its about page says it shows dependencies and automatically selected modules or builtins and links to kernel source files. Whether it maps object paths to Makefile conditions remains unverified.",
              "Do not say 'no tool gives a why-not verdict'. Say instead: no tool I found, in what I read, evaluates the per-object condition against a given .config and prints the failing conjuncts in one command. klocalizer shows the constraint and an unsat core, and CIP returns the evaluated expression, so the user compares by hand.",
              "The claim 'CIP does not compute the missing option' is right (the code does no dependency resolution), but the function does return the expression on False. Write 'returns the condition and leaves the comparison to the caller'.",
              "The menuconfig search field list is verified only through a 2019 patch description whose example comes from U-Boot's Kconfig. For Linux, cite only 'symbol-name search' from docs.kernel.org/kbuild/kconfig.html, or test locally by running `make menuconfig` and pressing '/'. A local run is one process at a time and fits the machine rules.",
              "Attribute the 0-day adoption of kmax to LWN 1034811 ('a number of bugs'), not to the krepair paper. Describe it as Kconfig-bug finding (kismet) and do not extend it to klocalizer.",
              "Say ConfigFix is 'posted as v6 in 2024-10, merge status unverified'. Do not say it is merged or in xconfig."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "related_work",
            "summary": "The D10 gap is partly closed by prior art, so the paper must not claim that no explanation facility exists. The Kmax suite documents, in docs/advanced.md only, `klocalizer --view-kbuild --include <unit>` (per-unit Kbuild constraints), `--show-unsat-core` (which constraints block a unit, possibly combining Kbuild and Kconfig), and koverage's FILE_EXCLUDED reasons. The CIP triage code already evaluates the per-object condition against a .config and returns the expression, cvehound does a heuristic version, and localmodconfig does the module-to-option mapping for simple obj-$(CONFIG_X) lines. For the Kconfig half, kernelconfig.io shows dependencies and selects, menuconfig shows depends and location, and Yocto's configcheck flags dropped options. ConfigFix proposes fixes, with unverified merge status. What kfold can still honestly claim, in decreasing strength: (1) object-level exact conditions from symbolic execution of the Makefiles, with an accuracy figure measured against real builds (the 400 verdicts), which the krepair paper does not give for Kbuild constraints (keep the Kmax FSE 2017 and Nguyen and Nguyen 2020 lineage in view); (2) a single command that evaluates the condition against a .config and prints the failing conjuncts, for Linux and also BusyBox, Barebox, U-Boot and coreboot with per-project entry settings; (3) whole-tree speed, but only after a same-machine timing against kmax/kclause/kreader, since the 10-15 minute figure is from the krepair paper (a different, per-line workflow); (4) the same table serving blindspots by MAINTAINERS entry. Do not claim to be first. State openly that the Kconfig half is where klocalizer's unsat core may be stronger than kfold's over-approximate model K. The best use of the proposed experiment is to test klocalizer's `--view-kbuild` and `--show-unsat-core` against kfold's `why` on the same absent objects and to report where each is the complete answer. This turns the competitor into a baseline. Limits of this check: the search budget ran out after 5 searches, several sources were read through a summarizing fetch tool, and I ran no tool locally.\"",
            "sources_checked": [
              {
                "url": "https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax",
                "opened": true,
                "note": "Suite: klocalizer (--repair, --include, --save-dimacs, --save-smt), koverage (--check-patch), kismet. 72 stars, 29 forks, 1,289 commits, 77 open issues. Quoted limits 'some lines may still be omitted' and 'cannot cover all patched lines'. No explain feature on the README.",
                "quote_verbatim": true
              },
              {
                "url": "https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax/blob/master/docs/advanced.md",
                "opened": true,
                "note": "Documents --view-kbuild, --show-unsat-core, --report-all, --undefine, koverage FILE_EXCLUDED and LINE_EXCLUDED_FILE_INCLUDED, and a troubleshooting list. Read via a summarizing fetch (quotes under 125 characters); the example output `[And(CONFIG_SQUASHFS, CONFIG_SQUASHFS_DECOMP_MULTI)]`. Does not say how parent-directory constraints are combined.",
                "quote_verbatim": true
              },
              {
                "url": "https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax/tree/master/docs",
                "opened": true,
                "note": "docs/ holds advanced.md, bugs_found.md, release.md and bugs_found/ (listing may be partial).",
                "quote_verbatim": false
              },
              {
                "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/paulgazz/kmax/master/README.md",
                "opened": true,
                "note": "Same repo content as the README. Gives klocalizer --repair and --include-mutex examples, multiple configs NUM-ARCH.config, koverage JSON output. Limit quote: 'some lines may still be omitted, for instance if they are dead code or in header files.'",
                "quote_verbatim": true
              },
              {
                "url": "https://pypi.org/project/kmax/",
                "opened": true,
                "note": "Version 4.10 released Aug 18 2026; 55 release entries (34 pre-releases, 21 final). Does not mention --view-kbuild, --show-unsat-core, kreader or kmaxall.",
                "quote_verbatim": true
              },
              {
                "url": "https://www.kernelconfig.io/index.html",
                "opened": true,
                "note": "Landing page: choose kernel version (mainline 6.18.57, stable 7.2.11, custom) and architecture; no object lookup mentioned.",
                "quote_verbatim": false
              },
              {
                "url": "https://www.kernelconfig.io/about.html",
                "opened": true,
                "note": "Search for kernel modules or configuration options. States 'Dependencies and automatically selected kernel modules/builtins are displayed' and links source files where possible. Run by one individual. Object-to-Makefile-condition mapping not stated.",
                "quote_verbatim": true
              },
              {
                "url": "https://gitlab.com/cip-project/cip-kernel/kernel-cve-triage/-/raw/main/README.md",
                "opened": true,
                "note": "Uses kmax formulas generated per kernel revision and cached. No benchmarks, no accuracy metrics, tailored to CIP. Does not say whether it names missing options. Needs libclang and the pp-trace binary.",
                "quote_verbatim": true
              },
              {
                "url": "https://gitlab.com/cip-project/cip-kernel/kernel-cve-triage/-/raw/main/src/cve_triage/triage/kmax.py",
                "opened": true,
                "note": "Runs kreader --show-constraints, parses the Boolean tree, evaluates it against enabled options, and returns (False, expr) when not built. Does no dependency resolution. Read via summarizer.",
                "quote_verbatim": false
              },
              {
                "url": "https://github.com/evdenis/cvehound",
                "opened": true,
