            "lens": "Fit and experiment designer for D10 (reverse lookup and explanation: which CONFIG builds object X, why is it missing from my build)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "FIT. D10 is two problems under one phrase. (A) 'which Makefile condition gates X and which conjunct fails in this .config' is an object-selection problem; kfold's build conditions answer it exactly. (B) 'why can the guarding option not be turned on' (unmet depends, a choice alternative, an arch gate, option silently dropped) is a Kconfig-specification and configurator problem. kfold reaches it only via the over-approximate model K. It is not a C-preprocessor, toolchain or runtime problem. The sharpest anecdotes (ConfigFix xconfig example, Billoo/Linux Magazine 2021, Greg KH on Airoha, Cavium Octeon choice thread) all sit at (B) or at 'a named config silently misses a choice alternative'.",
              "CORRECTION TO DOSSIER (competitor claim). The dossier says klocalizer/kmax documentation shows no explanation facility. I re-opened https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax/blob/master/docs/advanced.md and it documents (1) 'klocalizer --show-unsat-core -a x86_64 --undefine CONFIG_USB --include drivers/usb/storage/alauda.o' ('Use --show-unsat-core to see what constraints are causing the issue'), no sample output shown; (2) 'klocalizer --view-kbuild --include <unit>', which prints the unit's Kbuild constraint as one 'And(...)' expression (squashfs example '[And(CONFIG_SQUASHFS, CONFIG_SQUASHFS_DECOMP_MULTI)]'); the doc explains in prose, not tool output, that DECOMP_MULTI conflicts with DECOMP_SINGLE selected by allyesconfig; (3) koverage FILE_EXCLUDED status with no cause. So the accurate statement is: the Kmax suite gives the formula plus an unsat core for a requested forward repair; documentation shows no per-config 'which conjunct of the Kbuild chain fails in this .config' verdict. Write 'not documented', never 'cannot'. The 'no explanation' wording must not be used.",
              "kfold's own `why` is already more than the dossier credits (read src/cli/commands/why.py and _why_kconfig.py in the repo). It returns the guard chain with an ok flag per conjunct, first_failing_conjunct, pivotal_symbols (single-symbol flips that would make the condition true), per-symbol Kconfig explanation (depends on / select / choice via kconfiglib plus Z3) and a Z3 minimal suggested_change against the current .config. So kfold does attempt (B) through K, not only 'indirectly'. The existing results/devtasks/why.json records only verdict correctness and failing_kind, not whether the suggestion works.",
              "Existing evidence is on verdict accuracy only. results/devtasks/why.json: 4 Linux configs (allmodconfig, debian, defconfig, tinyconfig) x 100 sampled objects (50 built, 50 not built, seed 0), 100/100 verdicts correct each, 0 kconfig_errors. Among the 50 unbuilt objects per config the failing kind is: allmodconfig 24 target-list line / 18 directory route / 8 composite membership; debian 23/24/3; defconfig 16/30/4; tinyconfig 6/39/5. pivotal_symbols is empty for 9 (allmodconfig), 10 (debian), 19 (defconfig), 31 (tinyconfig) of the 50 unbuilt objects; the empty cases are those where no single flip suffices (a multi-conjunct condition), so a single-symbol explanation is inadequate there. I did not check the reasons for each case. Directory-route gates account for 18-39 of 50 unbuilt cases, which is the case where grep of one Makefile line is wrong; this is the strongest honest evidence that `why` adds something over the manual workflow.",
              "Verdict accuracy is table stakes and does not distinguish kfold: any Kmax-formula evaluation (CIP kernel-cve-triage does exactly this) returns the same built/not-built verdict. The differentiator is the explanation (named conjunct and a change that works). No existing result tests that. Without this the PLDI reviewer objection is 'you reimplemented formula evaluation and grep'.",
              "Ground truth risk: the build inventories give ground truth for 'is X built under config C' (verdict) and a make-based compile gives ground truth for 'does the suggested change make X build'. There is NO automatic ground truth for 'is the Kconfig-side reason correct' (class b). That needs hand adjudication or a proxy (olddefconfig drops the option). The experiment must therefore claim 'the suggestion works', not 'the explanation is the true cause'.",
              "Scale and cost anchors from local files: kfold whole-tree analysis of Linux 7.2.8 about a minute (results/timing.json has per-subject medians; barebox 1.53 s, BusyBox 0.31 s, coreboot 14.58 s). The saved Kmax run on Linux took 6275 s and 28.5 GB RSS (results/kmax/linux.meta.json, args -T -DSRCARCH=x86 Kbuild lib), BusyBox 73.3 s. The dossier's '84 s' Kmax figure from slides conflicts with the local run and should not be used without reconciliation. Inventory sizes: defconfig 3,133 objects, tinyconfig 528, debian 17,191, allmodconfig 29,625 of 32,877 considered, so about 3,250 objects are absent from allmodconfig. blindspots over allmodconfig, allyesconfig and defconfig reports 3,209 objects in 510 MAINTAINERS entries. config-for compile verification costs about 10 s per case (config_for_compile.json: 40 sampled of 384 eligible, 40/40 compiled, first case 10.1 s).",
              "Re-verified sources: Cavium thread quote (Guenter Roeck, https://lkml.rescloud.iu.edu/2306.2/07959.html): 'CONFIG_CAVIUM_OCTEON_SOC is a choice option, meaning only one of the choices can be set, and mips:allmodconfig selects a different choice.' ConfigFix PDF (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2012.15342, text extracted locally with pdftotext): 'Xconfig does not even show which parts of the constraint are unmet.' A fresh web search was refused (session search budget exhausted), so no new 2023-2026 prior-art discovery was done for explanation tools; the novelty of the explanation feature against unseen tools is unverified."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Replace 'klocalizer documents no explanation' with: 'the Kmax suite documents --view-kbuild (prints a unit's Kbuild constraint) and --show-unsat-core (explains an infeasible forward request); no per-.config verdict that names the failing conjunct is documented'. Source: https://github.com/paulgazz/kmax/blob/master/docs/advanced.md.",
              "Replace 'kfold answers (B) only indirectly' with: 'kfold's why gives a Kconfig-side explanation and a minimal change through the over-approximate model K (depends/select/choice via kconfiglib and Z3). Whether that explanation is correct is validated only by the counterfactual build test below, not against a ground-truth cause.'",
              "Do not state the 400-verdict check as evidence that explanations are good. It checks built/not-built verdicts only (100 per config x 4 configs). The 50 'unbuilt' per config sample is 50 of thousands of absent objects, picked at random (seed 0), not stratified by failing kind.",
              "Drop or demote the '84 s Kmax' number. The saved local Kmax run is 6275 s / 28.5 GB for Linux, 73.3 s for BusyBox, which are different machines and modes from the slide figure.",
              "Do not describe the CIP kernel-cve-triage behaviour (returns condition but does not compute the missing option) as verified; it was read through a summarizer."
