            "use_in_paper": "rq3_task",
            "summary": "Verdict: partly fits. The Kbuild half (A) of D10 is a genuine object-selection problem that kfold's per-object build conditions answer exactly. The Kconfig half (B) is a specification/configurator problem that kfold touches only through the over-approximate model K. The dossier also understates two facts: kfold's `why` already emits first_failing_conjunct, pivotal_symbols, a depends/select/choice explanation and a minimal suggested change (src/cli/commands/why.py, _why_kconfig.py), and the Kmax suite does document --view-kbuild and --show-unsat-core, so the competitor claim must be softened to 'per-.config conjunct verdict not documented'.\n\nSINGLE STRONGEST EXPERIMENT (counterfactual sufficiency of a `why` explanation, on the unbuilt side of the four Linux inventories).\nQuestion: when kfold says object X is absent because of conjunct set S and suggests change D, does applying D make X actually build, and when it does not, is the failure attributable to the Kconfig side? This is the missing piece because verdict accuracy (400/400 already) is shared by any Kmax-formula evaluator; the explanation is what differs.\nInputs (all available): Linux 7.2.8 prepared tree; evidence/configs/linux/{tinyconfig,defconfig,debian,allmodconfig}.config; evidence/inventories/linux/*.txt (absent set = analyzed objects minus inventory); `kfold why --json --config C OBJ` (fields first_failing_conjunct, pivotal_symbols, symbol_explanations, suggested_change, kconfig_error); config-for's apply path (scripts/config, make olddefconfig, make OBJ.o) already used for the 384-commit run; saved Kmax results/kmax/linux.json for the baseline.\nSampling: for each config draw 100 absent objects stratified by failing_kind (target-list line, directory route, composite membership) with the allmodconfig sample taken as the primary result (about 3,250 absent, the Cavium/GPIO/blindspots situation). 400 objects total. Pilot with 10 objects first.\nProcedure, strictly one process at a time (CLAUDE.md): (1) run `why`; (2) apply suggested_change to the config with scripts/config; (3) run make olddefconfig and record which suggested options survive (kept versus dropped); (4) run `make OBJ.o` single-job; (5) record built/not built; (6) if not built, record whether an option was dropped (Kconfig-side block), whether Z3 returned unsat for the suggestion (K says unreachable on this architecture), or neither (a kfold error).\nOutcome classes: (a) suggestion applied, options retained, object compiles (explanation sufficient); (b) options dropped by olddefconfig or K unsat (blocked on Kconfig side, e.g. ARCH_ gate or choice alternative); (c) options retained but object does not compile or is not produced (kfold error or a non-Kbuild gate such as a missing prerequisite, toolchain or a generated header); (d) 'built' disagreeing with the inventory (verdict error).\nBaselines: B1 naive manual workflow scripted: grep the object's basename in the Makefiles, take the CONFIG symbol on the first obj-$(CONFIG_X) line, set it, olddefconfig, make. B2 Kmax: evaluate the saved Kmax formula for X against C (same verdict), and take the symbols of its formula as the 'explanation' set to apply. B3 `why --no-kconfig` (pivotal symbols only, no K) to isolate the value of K.\nMetric and headline form: sufficiency rate = share of absent objects in class (a), per config and per stratum, for kfold versus B1 and B2/B3; plus the share in class (b), which is the measured limit of the Kbuild-only explanation; plus the share of (c)+(d) as the error rate. Expected form: 'For N=400 absent objects, kfold's suggested change makes the object compile for X% (CI), versus Y% when applying the symbol on the nearest Makefile line; Z% are blocked on the Kconfig side and for those kfold names the blocking depends/choice/arch gate.' Prediction from why.json: directory-route and composite cases are 18-39 of 50 unbuilt per config, so B1 should be clearly lower than kfold; B1 and B2 sufficiency should be near kfold for plain target-list-line cases. I will not state numbers because none exist.\nSecondary slice for the paper's D (blindspots) tie-in: report the share of the 3,209 blindspots objects that fall in class (a) (fixable by a legal config on x86) versus (b) (not buildable here), since that is the Cavium/GPIO situation.\nFalsification: the claim 'kfold's explanation names what to change' fails if (i) sufficiency is within a few points of B1 on directory-route and composite strata, (ii) class (c)+(d) exceeds about 10%, or (iii) most of class (b) are options that olddefconfig drops because of kfold's own K being too permissive and K proves nothing, i.e. K's unsat verdicts disagree with a hand check. The Kconfig-side claim is falsified separately if a 50-object hand check of class (b) finds the named blocker (depends/choice/arch) wrong in more than a minority (for example more than 20%).\nGround truth check: all of (a), (c), (d) use ground truth already available (the build or the inventory). Only the reason in (b) needs adjudication; that is a bounded hand check of about 50 cases against menuconfig/`make olddefconfig` output and is explicitly labelled a limitation, not a headline.\nEffort: about 5 person-days. 1 day harness (JSON loop reading why output, apply, olddefconfig, make, log); 0.5 day B1 and B2 baselines; 1 day pilot and fixing why for cases it crashes on; about 0.5 day of machine time (400 cases at roughly 10 to 60 s each, one process at a time, so 1 to 7 hours; estimate from config_for_compile.json 10.1 s for one case; check the total after the pilot and tell the user before the full run); 1 day hand check of 50 class-(b) cases; 1 day tables and write-up. Duration on this 5900X is untested for allmodconfig object builds because a prepared tree and headers may be needed; if make OBJ.o of an allmodconfig case needs a full prepare, cost rises (to be measured in the pilot).\nThreats to validity: (1) x86-only entry settings, so ARM-only drivers appear only as Kconfig-blocked; do not generalize to all architectures. (2) Single Linux version 7.2.8; results hold per release. (3) Compile success at object level does not mean the driver works or that a #ifdef inside the C file is exercised; this is not a claim about code coverage inside files. (4) Kconfig model K is an over-approximation, so 'suggestion exists' can be optimistic; olddefconfig is the arbiter and dropped options are counted against kfold's suggestion, not hidden. (5) Sample bias: stratification by failing kind changes the mix relative to the population; report population-weighted rates as well. (6) kfold's own author selects the baselines; B1 and B2 must be implemented before looking at results and kept simple but fair (B2 uses the real saved Kmax output). (7) Class (b) reason is checked by hand on 50 cases, a small sample from a single annotator. (8) Absent set includes objects needing generated files or prerequisites (for example objects that exist only after earlier build steps), which land in class (c) and must be reported, not dropped. (9) Tool and kernel build time vary with the machine; times come from different CPUs (timing.json lists an i7-7700).\n\nPLACEMENT in the paper. Make it an RQ3 developer task named 'explain why an object is absent and what to change', with a short, scoped claim (A exactly, B only as far as K reaches). Use the CVE/triage consumer (CIP kernel-cve-triage, cvehound) in related work as the nearest existing use of per-file conditions, and the Cavium/GPIO/Airoha anecdotes in the introduction only as motivation for 'a named config silently misses code', not as proof of demand. State the Kconfig-half boundary as a limitation (kfold is not ConfigFix or a Kconfig bug finder). Do not drop it: it is the one place where `why` reveals something beyond verdict accuracy and it feeds directly into the blindspots result. Do not make it a headline contribution: reviewers will say the manual answer is 'read the Makefile', that Kmax already outputs the formula, and that demand evidence is anecdotal (the survey figure is 2012, the real threads are about Kconfig gating, and no survey of kernel developers on 'which CONFIG builds X' exists). The experiment above answers the first two with a measured number; the third remains an acknowledged limitation.",
            "sources_checked": [
