      {
        "id": "D11",
        "name": "Deriving test and fuzzer configurations for the objects under test (KUnit, syzbot)",
        "dive": {
          "id": "D11",
          "name": "Deriving and verifying test and fuzzer configurations for the objects under test (KUnit, syzbot)",
          "refined_description": "Problem statement. A dynamic test (KUnit suite, syzkaller fuzzing session, per-patch fuzzing in syzbot ci) only exercises code if the kernel configuration compiles the object under test. Today the object-to-configuration step is done by hand or heuristically, and nothing checks afterwards that the chosen configuration still builds the intended objects. Three variants appear in primary sources. (1) KUnit: a test author hand-writes a .kunitconfig that lists the test option plus every transitive Kconfig dependency, and the in-tree all_tests.config is documented as \"manually maintained\". When a Kconfig dependency changes (a select is removed, an option becomes invisible), tests silently stop being built or the kunit.py run breaks. Documented cases: PRIME_NUMBERS tests \"neither built nor run\" (2025-04), iommupt kunitconfig broken by GENERIC_PT becoming unselectable (2026-01), --alltests broken on non-UML by wireless tests (2024-03), GPIO tests not buildable under all_tests.config (2026-06). (2) syzbot: configs are generated by syz-kconf from about 541 subsystem options \"bulk copied from a legacy config\", and developers send pull requests to enable single options so that a directory (for example fs/xfs/scrub/) \"can be exercised\". (3) syzbot ci (patch-series fuzzing, 2025-2026): all nine production fuzz targets use one config (upstream-apparmor-kasan.config); from June 2026 an LLM is asked to name the CONFIG_ options needed to \"compile and reach the modified code\", validated only by a check that the names exist; whether the patched code is in the build is detected after the fact by building base and patched kernels and comparing symbol hashes. The kfold-shaped sub-problem is the Kbuild half: given the touched or test objects, say whether a given config builds them, why not (failing conjunct), and the smallest change that does, checked with olddefconfig. It is not the #ifdef half, not reachability from syscalls or hardware, and not Kconfig correctness.",
          "who_has_it": "KUnit test authors and subsystem maintainers who maintain .kunitconfig files (46 in torvalds/linux per GitHub code search, approximate) and the shared all_tests.config; CI operators who run kunit.py --alltests (Mark Brown, Jakub Kicinski threads); the syzbot and syzbot ci team at Google (Nogikh) and external contributors who send PRs to enable options in syzkaller's dashboard configs; vendors scoping CVE triggerability under production configs (FCC, Aug 2026). Demand is shown by operator artifacts and maintainer fix commits. No source found where someone asks for a tool.",
          "evidence": [
            {
              "claim": "syzbot ci now asks an LLM to find the CONFIG_ options needed to build and reach the patched code. The commit message of 2026-06-09 says the extracted configs are injected into the kernel build configuration before compilation. This is an operator building the object-to-config step with an LLM (demand evidence and, equally, a competing approach).",
              "quote": "Identify any specific CONFIG_ options required to properly compile and reach the modified code:",
              "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/google/syzkaller/master/pkg/aflow/flow/fuzzing/patch_triage.go",
              "date": "2026-06-02 (workflow added) to 2026-10-15 (latest change)",
              "evidence_type": "tool",
              "source_group": "Google syzkaller (syzbot ci code and ops)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "Same prompt tells the model to inspect Kconfig files and #ifdef guards. The Go validator (commit 2026-06-19, 'validate Linux kernel config names') only checks that each named option exists in the top-level Kconfig, not that it suffices to build the patched files. Commit 2026-06-09 message: the LLM extracts EnableConfigs 'by actively inspecting Kconfig #ifdefs'. Read via GitHub commits API.",
              "quote": "`EnableConfigs` by actively inspecting Kconfig `#ifdef`s.",
              "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/google/syzkaller/commits?path=pkg/aflow/flow/fuzzing/patch_triage.go&per_page=100",
              "date": "2026-06-09",
              "evidence_type": "tool",
              "source_group": "Google syzkaller (syzbot ci code and ops)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "All nine production syzbot ci fuzz targets (kvm, io_uring, bpf, net x2, fs, usb, mm, default) select the same kernel config. Series are matched to a target by mailing list or path regexps; the config is not chosen per patch. A skip_cover_check flag (set for mm) means 'Don't expect kernel coverage for the patched area'.",
              "quote": "kernel_config: upstream-apparmor-kasan.config",
              "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/google/syzkaller/master/syz-cluster/overlays/gke/prod/global-config.yaml",
              "date": "master as of 2026-10-17",
              "evidence_type": "tool",
              "source_group": "Google syzkaller (syzbot ci code and ops)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "syzbot ci finds out that a patch is not in the fuzzed build only dynamically: it builds base and patched kernels, hashes .text/.data symbols and skips fuzzing if they are equal (shouldSkipFuzzing); if fuzzing never reaches the patched area it returns ErrPatchedAreaNotReached and treats it as fine. A static per-object condition would answer the first case without two kernel builds (object granularity; header-only changes and #ifdef arms are not resolved by Kbuild conditions). Source decoded from GitHub contents API.",
              "quote": "We did not reach the modified parts of the kernel, but that's fine.",
              "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/google/syzkaller/contents/syz-cluster/workflow/fuzz/main.go",
              "date": "master as of 2026-10-17",
              "evidence_type": "tool",
              "source_group": "Google syzkaller (syzbot ci code and ops)",
              "reopened_and_verified": true
            },
            {
              "claim": "syzbot's subsystem fragment (about 541 options by the fetch tool's tally) was copied from a legacy config, and some options are on for reasons nobody remembers. The object-to-option link is not recorded.",
