          "remaining_gap": "No tool, in the sources found, that (a) takes a set of test or touched objects and returns a minimal, olddefconfig-verified configuration change on top of a base fuzz or KUnit config, with a per-object explanation of what blocks each object; (b) re-checks after each Kconfig change that every KUnit test object is still built by at least one maintained test config, so a PRIME_NUMBERS-style silent drop or an iommupt-style breakage shows up as a failing check rather than a later fix commit; (c) tells syzbot ci before building whether the patched objects are in the fuzzed config. The harder parts stay open and are outside kfold: #ifdef-level reachability (the DVB leak), syscall or hardware reachability, UML availability of options, Kconfig correctness, and choosing configs that fuzz well (sanitizers, 'reduced' and 'baseline' variants). klocalizer already offers (a) for Linux, so a gap exists only for exactness, explanation, speed or integration, which has to be measured.",
          "kfold_contribution": "Modest and specific: the Kbuild half of what syzbot ci asks an LLM to do, as a deterministic oracle. (1) why: for each touched or test object, say whether a given base config builds it and name the failing conjuncts. (2) config-for: smallest change to a base config (syzbot or KUnit) that builds the objects, verified with make olddefconfig, which is the check FCC and kunit_tool both need because olddefconfig silently drops options. (3) A blindspots-style regression check: list KUnit test objects that none of the maintained test configs (all_tests.config on UML, per-arch .kunitconfig) builds, usable as a CI gate. (4) A precomputed whole-tree table (about a minute for Linux 7.2.8, 32.9k objects) that can replace the per-series base-versus-patched build used only to learn that the patch is not in the config, for .c/.S patches. kfold does not handle #ifdef inside files, header-only changes, UML-unavailable options, or reachability, and its Kconfig model K is an over-approximation.",
