            "torvalds/linux (GitHub code search, approximate, version unspecified): 46 .kunitconfig files; 88 files matching 'default KUNIT_ALL_TESTS'; 59 Kconfig files matching 'depends on KUNIT'; 143 commits matching 'kunitconfig'.",
            "all_tests.config: 40 CONFIG_ lines (fetch tool count), 'manually maintained'. Fix commits for dependency drift found: 2024-03-26 (DAMON), 2025-04-22 (PRIME_NUMBERS), 2026-01-06 (iommupt), 2026-06-29 (GPIOLIB).",
            "FSE 2024 (Gazzillo group): defconfig 21.7%, one randconfig 29.2%, allyesconfig 88.5%, krepair 98.5% mean patch coverage on 507 of about 71,000 patches; allyesconfig build about 4 hours versus about 20 minutes; the 42% syzbot figure is not in the paper or slides.",
            "FCC (arXiv 2608.15225): 88 CVEs; post-olddefconfig success 62.5% (55/88) to 96.6% (85/88); 14.72 versus 69.00 options per CVE on the KernJC set; Stage I 1977 s and 291.8k tokens per CVE on one set.",
            "syzbot ci dashboard snapshot (ci.syzbot.org, 2026-10-17, read through a summarizer, approximate): about 14 of about 100 listed series showed status 'skipped'; reasons for skipping were not shown.",
            "kfold assets: Linux 7.2.8 whole-tree about 1 minute, 32.9k objects; 437 commits in v7.2.7..v7.2.8, 402 touch .c/.S, 384 get a verified configuration."
          ],
          "proposed_experiment": "Primary (no builds, minutes): fetch syzkaller's upstream-apparmor-kasan.config (the single config of all nine syzbot ci targets), apply the same =m to =y rewrite syz-cluster uses, run make olddefconfig for Linux 7.2.8 x86_64, then run kfold why for every object touched by the 402 .c/.S commits of v7.2.7..v7.2.8 already processed. Report: (a) fraction of commits whose touched objects are all unbuilt, partly built, fully built by that config; (b) histogram of failing conjuncts (which Kconfig symbols block the objects, grouped by MAINTAINERS entry); (c) for unbuilt commits, run config-for with the syzbot config as base and report the number of options changed and the olddefconfig verification rate; (d) agreement with syzbot ci's own criterion on a small sample (about 20 commits, one object each, checked with single-object make targets rather than full builds, one process at a time). Caveats to state: syzbot's LLM EnableConfigs overlay cannot be reproduced; syzbot config is generated for another kernel version; header-only and #ifdef-only patches are out of scope. Secondary: for the 46 in-tree .kunitconfig files and all_tests.config, list KUnit test objects (condition mentions a symbol that depends on KUNIT) that no file builds on x86_64 or UML after olddefconfig, and run a retrospective check on the four known fix commits (4ea404fdbc39, cefd81e76a8c, 34b5c0132952, cfedfb24c9dd) at their parent commits to see whether the blindspots-style check would have flagged them. Expected duration: minutes per pass for kfold; no kernel builds; follow CLAUDE.md (single process, results written to files).",
          "risks": "1) WebSearch was exhausted at the start of this task (shared budget), so the 15 required new searches were not done; they were substituted by direct WebFetch of primary sources, mail-archive and arXiv internal searches, and gh api queries (GitHub code/commit/issue search, commits and contents APIs). Recall is therefore lower than a real search sweep; absence claims ('no klocalizer mention in syzkaller or linux') are only as good as GitHub search. 2) The syzbot ci LLM step is both the best demand evidence and the strongest counter-evidence: the operator has already built a workable heuristic, and a deterministic oracle must beat it on measured facts, not on argument. 3) The headline DVB anecdote is an #ifdef case and is outside kfold's reach; the dossier's earlier '42% syzbot patch coverage' figure (claim 113) could not be found in the slides or the paper and must not be cited. 4) Much KUnit pain is Kconfig dependency and UML availability, where kfold's Kbuild conditions help only partly; K is an over-approximation. 5) No source found where a maintainer asks for such a tool; evidence is fix commits and operator code. Some maintainers do not accept compile-only evidence at all (Shuah Khan, staging, 2026-03, from the gap-scout claims; not re-opened here). 6) klocalizer already provides config-for for named objects; the contribution needs a head-to-head. 7) Several quotes (docs, mail-archive pages, the Go prompt text, YAML tallies, counts like 40 lines or 541 options) came through the fetch tool's summarizer; quotes from pdftotext output, gh api commit bodies, and base64-decoded source are direct. GitHub code-search counts are approximate and version-agnostic. 8) Date caveats: syzbot ci code is a moving target (changed as late as 2026-10-15); description may be stale by publication. 9) Double-blind: the paper must not name the project or authors when citing local assets.",
