        "verdicts": [
          {
            "lens": "EVIDENCE AUDITOR for D11 (test and fuzzer configurations for objects under test: KUnit, syzbot)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "Independence: most load-bearing evidence is operator or maintainer artifacts, not users asking for a tool. The dossier itself says no source asks for such a tool. Four KUnit fix commits come from four different authors, so they are independent, but each is a tiny one-line config fix and together they show drift is occasional, not frequent. The four syzbot ci items (config file, prompt, commit log, main.go) are one source group (Google syzkaller) and count as ONE source. The DVB anecdote and the krepair quote both come from the Gazzillo group (slides and paper) and count as ONE source.",
              "Overcounted statistic: the 'GitHub search 33 issues/PRs 2022-2026 with dashboard/config and enable in the title (7, 9, 3, 7, 7 per year)' figure is not a clean count. The search API returned total_count 54, and I could see only the first 35 items (one page, truncated). The per-year numbers match that visible subset (2022: 7, 2023: 9, 2024: 3, 2025: 7, 2026: 7), but they are a partial tally of a search with 54 hits, and the 2020 items are in the same set. Worse, most of the visible titles are about sanitizers, debug or runtime options (KASAN, PAGE_TABLE_CHECK, FORTIFY_SOURCE, RCU_EXPERT, DEBUG_VFS, audit at runtime, MODULE_SIG_SHA512, BB descriptors), not 'compile this directory'. Only a minority (XFS_ONLINE_SCRUB/REPAIR #7552, XFRM_IPTFS #7792, BLK_DEV_UBLK, SMB_SERVER, UFS_FS_WRITE, KVM_SMM, ANDROID_BINDERFS, binder rust) look like making code reachable. The claim cannot be used as a rate of 'unbuilt code found by contributors'.",
              "PR #7552 verified (title 'dashboard/config/linux: enable XFS_ONLINE_SCRUB and XFS_ONLINE_REPAIR', body quoted verbatim, created 2026-07-01, merged 2026-07-02, author Radon10043). The dossier's 'date 2026-07-01' is the creation date, fine. #7792 verified (created 2026-08-13, merged 2026-08-14). The body says the options let 'generated payloads to reach the IP-TFS packet parser', which is about reachability by fuzzed packets, not about the object being compiled at all. XFRM_IPTFS may well have been missing from the build, but the body does not say 'not compiled'. This weakens it as an 'object not built' example.",
              "Quote error: the dossier says the DAMON commit (2024-03, cfedfb24c9dd, 'kunit: configs: Enable CONFIG_DAMON_DBGFS_DEPRECATED for --alltests') said 'This is breaking all UML alltests'. I read the patch and that phrase is NOT in it. The message says '--alltests UML builds will fail due to the missing config option otherwise.' The cause is a Kconfig symbol rename (DAMON_DBGFS to DAMON_DBGFS_DEPRECATED), not an unbuilt object. This is the least relevant of the four 'dependency drift' cases and a Kbuild-condition oracle would not catch it.",
              "Mischaracterisation: only the PRIME_NUMBERS case (4ea404fdbc39) is the 'object that no standard test config builds' pattern. The iommupt case (cefd81e76a8c) is a hand-written .kunitconfig broken when GENERIC_PT became unselectable. The GPIO case (34b5c0132952) is a one-line GPIOLIB addition whose message just says to ensure the tests 'can be built with this config'. Both are Kconfig dependency/visibility problems, which the dossier's own 'kfold contribution' admits is only partly covered by Kbuild conditions plus an over-approximate K. The Kbuild half (obj-$(CONFIG_X) conditions) is the smaller part of the story in these commits.",
              "Date detail: the GPIO commit is dated 2026-06-29 by the author, but it was committed by Shuah Khan on 2026-07-14. The iommupt commit is authored 2026-01-06 and committed 2026-01-10. The PRIME_NUMBERS commit is 2025-04-22. The dossier uses author dates, which is acceptable but should be labelled 'author date'. 'merged via the AppArmor pull of 2026-02-18' for 2f53da43b203 is unverified: I only saw the patch header (author Ryota Sakamoto, 2026-01-25); the dossier also lists John Johansen as an author, but the patch header names only Sakamoto.",
              "Quote not fully verifiable: PRIME_NUMBERS 'meaning that when run via the KUnit runner the tests are neither built nor run' came to me only as a summary; the fetch tool refused to print the full message (125-char limit), so I confirmed the substance (select removed, nothing added to the KUnit configs, tests 'neither built nor run', fix adds CONFIG_PRIME_NUMBERS=y to all_tests.config, Fixes: 3f2925174f8b) but not the exact wording. The iommupt quote 'Not all Kconfig options selected in kunitconfig were in the generated .config' is verified, but it is the kunit_tool error string (confirmed in kunit_kernel.py validate_config, which reports 'Missing:' and does not repair), not the author's words. The 'doesn't currently build for the' fragment is verified, but I could not see what it refers to; the sentence is attached to removing the 'unneeded' CONFIG_IOMMUFD_TEST and adding COMPILE_TEST, so the dossier's paraphrase 'removes a config that doesn't currently build for the kunit' is plausible but unconfirmed.",
              "Wrong attribution of tool property: the dossier says the kmax docs say the formulas 'may not be exact'. The kmax README does not contain that phrase (I checked). The README only says 'While repair usually covers all lines of a patch, some lines may still be omitted, for instance if they are dead code or in header files.' If 'may not be exact' comes from another kmax doc page, give that URL; otherwise drop it. The klocalizer --include drivers/usb/storage/alauda.o example is verified, and the README mentions no KUnit, syzbot or fuzzing.",
              "Issue 1887: verified quote, author dvyukov, opened 2020-07-01. But it was closed on 2020-10-21 as 'dup of #2171', and #2171 is titled 'dashboard/config: refactor linux config system' (opened 2020-10-04, closed completed 2021-01-28, proposes a kconfig package). The page does not name syz-kconf, so 'closed after syz-kconf was introduced' is an inference. Also the issue asks for fragments/inheritance and does not ask for object-to-option links; the 'why is this option on' reading is the dossier's gloss. The README of the syzkaller config dir says the process is declarative except for USB configs.",
              "FSE 2024 krepair quote verified verbatim in the arXiv HTML (2404.17966): 'These configurations provide no assurance that code in new patches gets compiled before testing.' preceded by the claim that syzbot 'relies on a small, fixed set of configuration files'. The page gives no syzbot patch coverage figure, which confirms the dossier's own warning not to cite 42%. I did not read the last 9k characters of that HTML. This is a 2024 paper's claim about syzbot (as of its writing), not a measurement, and it predates syzbot ci. It is the same research group as the DVB anecdote.",
              "OSS NA 2022 slides (read via pdftotext): the DVB bullets are verified (2016 leak, 2020-05 syzkaller capable of finding it, config disabled in mem checking config, 2020-08 configs regenerated, 2020-11 found, 2020-12 fixed). 'only possible under #ifdef CONFIG_MEDIA_CONTROLLER_DVB' confirms it is a CPP case outside kfold's reach. 'syzbot uses a set of hand-crafted configs ... kept update with changes to defaults' verified. The slide's date is June 22nd, 2022. Note: the slide attributes the finding to 'syzkaller', and the 2020-08 regeneration to 'syzkaller regenerates configs'. Use it as motivation only.",
              "LPC 2023 slides (pdftotext): 'Many kernel revisions do not build/boot with syzbot config.' is verified (line 204 of the extract), and the 'shift-left' text with 'apply incoming patch, build an instrumented kernel' is verified. But the first claim is about cause bisection, i.e. config/revision compatibility, not about whether objects under test are compiled. It is weak evidence for D11 and should not be cited for it. I did not open the slide page for the 'about 25 instances, 150-200 VMs' numbers; they remain unverified.",
              "syzbot ci evidence is verified but its meaning is narrower than the dossier presents. global-config.yaml: exactly 9 fuzz targets, all 'kernel_config: upstream-apparmor-kasan.config'; only the mm target sets skip_cover_check: true. patch_triage.go prompt: 'Identify any specific CONFIG_ options required to properly compile and reach the modified code:' and 'Inspect Kconfig files and #ifdef guards; do not make assumptions.' and 'Check \"depends on\" lines in Kconfig...'. The Go side normalizes the CONFIG_ prefix and validates names against the top-level Kconfig. The commit log (read via the GitHub commits API) shows 9 commits, 2026-06-02 to 2026-10-15; commit 8 (2026-06-09) says configs are 'injected into the kernel build configuration prior to compilation' and the LLM is told to inspect 'Kconfig `#ifdef`s'. Importantly, the workflow's stated purpose (2026-06-02) is to 'statically decide whether patches are worth fuzzing'; config selection is a side output. Prompts were refined on 2026-07-24 and 2026-09-03, so the operator is actively iterating, and the code is a moving target. main.go: shouldSkipFuzzing compares .text and data symbol hashes of base and patched builds and returns true when equal ('binaries are the same, no sense to do fuzzing'). 'We did not reach the modified parts of the kernel, but that's fine.' is verified as a code comment. Conclusion: the facts hold, but they show an operator that has a working (if unvalidated) mechanism, not a measured failure. No data exist on how often the LLM-chosen configs miss the patched objects.",
              "subsystems.yml header verified, with a wording correction: the file reads 'This file enables subsystems that syzkaller can test. Contents are bulk copied from a legacy config, so some of the configs do other things, some are enabled for unknown now reasons.' The '~541 options' is the fetch tool's own manual tally (it said about 540, plus or minus 10). Treat it as 'roughly 540', not 541. The '93 .config files' and '45 upstream ones' counts were not re-opened by me.",
              "FCC (arXiv 2608.15225) verified: title 'Inferring 1-Minimal Trigger Configurations for Assessing Linux Kernel CVE Triggerability', Nanjing University of Science and Technology and Shandong Normal University, submitted 2026-08-15, listed for ISSTA 2026 (PACMSE vol. 3). 62.5% (55/88) to 96.6% (85/88) confirmed in the abstract, Sec 6.2 and the conclusion; the baseline is called an 'injection baseline' in the paper, not an 'olddef-only baseline'. The quote about options 'silently rolled back during make olddefconfig' is verified verbatim in the Introduction. The syzbot reference config has 4,206 enabled entries and is called 'too broad for identifying the options required for a known CVE to remain triggerable' (Sec 6.3). '1977 s and 291.8k tokens' is confirmed for Stage I on KernJC-D, but the sentence does not say 'per CVE'; the per-CVE reading comes from Figure 7 and surrounding text, so say 'per CVE' only with that caveat. No Kmax/klocalizer/KUnit in the visible body; the last 7.5k characters (reference list) were not read. It concerns CVE triggerability, not test-config derivation, so it is adjacent work only.",
              "Kicinski thread (mail-archive msg20977): verified quote and date 2025-04-02 10:36 -0700. Context: he runs kunit.py run --alltests on x86_64 and asks whether the Cirrus tests can be fixed or excluded from KUNIT_ALL_TESTS. It is about failing tests, not unbuilt objects; the dossier already says so. The 'wireless 2024-03 Missing: CONFIG_IWLWIFI=y' item and 'Mark Brown replied everything was fine in -next' were not re-opened and rest on a summarizer.",
              "Barebox msg52399 verified (Ahmad Fatoum, 2025-03-26, patch 5/6 of 'ci: run tests and static analysis on allyesconfig'; quote verbatim). But the sentence is about catching breakage from treewide changes; the dossier's gloss 'so that drivers that default configs never compile are caught' is an interpretation. The same message also notes that compile-only drivers must not break normal startup.",
              "all_tests.config header verified verbatim: 'This config enables as many tests as possible under UML.' and 'The config is manually maintained, though it uses KUNIT_ALL_TESTS=y to enable any tests whose dependencies are already satisfied.' 40 CONFIG_ lines confirmed (via the fetch tool). The 'under UML' scope matters: kfold's x86 Kbuild model would need a UML (ARCH=um) Kbuild/Kconfig run, which the dossier only mentions as a limit. The 'depends on rule' commit 7eac33305174 is verified (David Gow, 2026-06-27, 'suddenly grow unexpected dependencies'). KUnit docs quote ('tests could theoretically depend on incompatible options, so handling that would be tricky') and 'iffy' verified verbatim on running_tips.html; that page does not mention all_tests.config.",
              "Not verified / could not be checked: the '46 .kunitconfig files', '88 files matching default KUNIT_ALL_TESTS', '59 Kconfig files matching depends on KUNIT', '143 commits matching kunitconfig' counts (GitHub code search needs auth, and web search was exhausted); the 'zero klocalizer mentions in syzkaller or linux' absence claim; the ci.syzbot.org '14 of 100 skipped' snapshot; the 'time limit 1 hour and 5M tokens' for ai.go; the LPC 2023 '300 to 1,000/year' bar-chart values. All of these must be marked 'unverified' or dropped.",
              "Contradicting or weakening evidence I could not search for: WebSearch was exhausted (limit shared across the session), so I did NOT look for (a) existing scripts that report KUnit tests not enabled by any maintained config, (b) any KUnit/syzbot use of klocalizer, or (c) data on how often syzbot ci LLM-chosen configs miss patched code. Absence in this audit is therefore not evidence of absence. Note the kernel-side mitigation already shipped: KUNIT_ALL_TESTS with a 'depends on' convention (documented 2026-06-27) is meant to make most tests appear without a per-test config entry, and the four drift commits since 2024-03 suggest roughly one per year to one per several months, which is a low rate."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "DAMON commit cfedfb24c9dd (2024-03-26, David Gow): the phrase 'This is breaking all UML alltests' is not in the commit. Replace with the actual text '--alltests UML builds will fail due to the missing config option otherwise.' and say the cause is a rename (DAMON_DBGFS to DAMON_DBGFS_DEPRECATED, after commit f4cba4bf6777), which is a Kconfig change, not an unbuilt object.",
              "GitHub title-count 'a total of 33 (7, 9, 3, 7, 7)': the search returned total_count 54 and only 35 items were visible, including 2 from 2020. Report it as 'at least 33 in 2022-2026 in the first 35 hits of a 54-hit search' and add that most are sanitizer/debug/runtime options rather than 'compile this directory'. Better still, hand-classify a sample before using any rate.",
              "kmax: the README does not say 'may not be exact'. Keep only the README sentence 'some lines may still be omitted, for instance if they are dead code or in header files', or cite the real source of 'may not be exact'.",
              "Issue 1887 was closed 2020-10-21 as 'dup of #2171' ('dashboard/config: refactor linux config system', closed 2021-01-28). The page does not mention syz-kconf by name, so soften 'closed after syz-kconf was introduced' to 'closed as a duplicate of the config-system refactor issue'.",
              "subsystems.yml: the header reads 'some are enabled for unknown now reasons' (not 'unknown reasons'), and the '541' count is the tool's tally of about 540 plus or minus 10; write 'about 540'.",
              "FCC: the comparison baseline is an 'injection baseline' (not 'olddef-only'); Stage I 1977 s and 291.8k tokens is stated for KernJC-D, and 'per CVE' is read from Figure 7, not from the same sentence. The paper is by Nanjing University of Science and Technology and Shandong Normal University and is listed for ISSTA 2026, which could be added to the citation.",
              "LPC 2023 slide: 'Many kernel revisions do not build/boot with syzbot config' concerns cause bisection, not config choice for the objects under test; do not cite it as D11 evidence.",
              "Commit dates: label them 'author date'. GPIO 34b5c0132952 was committed on 2026-07-14, iommupt cefd81e76a8c on 2026-01-10. The 'AppArmor pull of 2026-02-18' and the co-author John Johansen for 2f53da43b203 are unverified; the patch header I read names only Ryota Sakamoto (2026-01-25).",
              "syzbot ci patch_triage workflow (commit 2026-06-02): describe its stated purpose as statically deciding whether a patch is worth fuzzing, with config enabling as a side output added on 2026-06-09 and name validation on 2026-06-19. The code changed again on 2026-07-24, 2026-08-05, 2026-08-25, 2026-09-03 and 2026-10-15."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "motivation",
            "summary": "Most quotes and URLs check out. I opened 17 sources and read two PDFs through pdftotext. The syzbot ci facts (nine targets with one config, the LLM EnableConfigs prompt, the commit log, the symbol-hash skip) are real and dated correctly. So are the KUnit facts (all_tests.config 'manually maintained', the PRIME_NUMBERS, iommupt and GPIO commits, the KUnit docs quotes, the kunit_tool validate_config that detects but does not repair) and the FCC paper. But the evidence is weaker than the dossier frames it, for four reasons. (1) It is operator and maintainer artifacts, not users asking for a tool, and the syzbot ci items are a single source group. (2) The four 'dependency drift' commits are one-line fixes, spread across 2024-2026, and only PRIME_NUMBERS is truly an unbuilt-object case; DAMON is a rename, and its quote 'This is breaking all UML alltests' is not in the commit. iommupt and GPIO are Kconfig visibility issues. (3) The '33 issues/PRs' count is a truncated partial tally of a 54-hit search, and most of those titles are sanitizer, debug or runtime options. (4) The syzbot ci LLM step is a working operator heuristic whose miss rate nobody has measured. I could not verify the GitHub code-search counts, the klocalizer-absence claim, or several figures, and web search was exhausted, so I did not look for contradicting evidence such as existing scripts or syzbot ci data on missed patches. Use D11 as motivation (the 'config decides what gets tested' pattern, the syzbot ci LLM step as an operator workaround), not as a research task, unless the retrospective experiment in the dossier (the kfold why check on syzbot's config over the 402 v7.2.7..v7.2.8 commits) is run to show a measured rate."
          },
          {
            "lens": "Hostile competitor analyst for D11 (deriving and verifying test and fuzzer configurations for the objects under test: KUnit, syzbot, syzbot ci)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "ALREADY SOLVED AT RESEARCH LEVEL FOR THE SYZBOT HALF. ICSE 2025, 'A Little Goes a Long Way: Tuning Configuration Selection for Continuous Kernel Fuzzing' (Hasanov, Nagy, Gazzillo), runs krepair on syz-kconf output per kernel commit in an emulated syzbot. Reported: syz-kconf mean patch coverage 43.2% (507 patches), 98.6% after krepair; 35 new bugs vs 13 (24 unique vs 2); krepair median 76 options changed (max 1264); builds under 10 min; fuzzer throughput and bootability preserved. It is presented as 'no engineering changes to the fuzzer itself'. The dossier's 'nothing derives the object-to-config step' is therefore false for the derivation step: a published tool does it at line level, including #ifdef (SuperC).",
              "klocalizer is the direct competitor to config-for. Documented: 'klocalizer --repair .config -o allnoconfig_repaired --include drivers/usb/storage/alauda.o' (per-object) and '--include-mutex PATCH.diff' (per-patch, several configs when both arms of an #ifdef are touched). Latest kmax release 4.10 (2026-08-18). Not verified: whether --include accepts several objects at once (README shows one).",
              "Limits of klocalizer to quote: 'The extracted formulas may not be exact.' (docs/advanced.md); 'Passing multiple architectures is not supported.'; repair 'usually covers all lines of a patch, some lines may still be omitted ... dead code or header files'; FSE 2024: 'patches that only modify header files are not supported, which we only encountered in 2% of patches'; the paper reports no separate accuracy study of the Kbuild constraints (end-to-end patch coverage only). Open issue #308 (2026-08-16): klocalizer --include drivers/block/ps3disk.o reports x86_64 satisfiable although the option is PowerPC-only; maintainer reply (summarized) says there is no fully satisfactory fix yet. That is a Kconfig-model/arch-entry-point problem, not necessarily a Kbuild-condition error, so it must not be presented as evidence that kfold's conditions are more exact. Issue #303 (2026-01): kextract hardcodes CC=cc so clang-dependent constraints are removed.",
              "krepair is not minimal and not explanatory. ICSE 2025 case study: CONFIG_FS_VERITY 'is only enabled in the original configuration, while our modified configuration file disables it'. kfold's 'change as few options as possible' claim is therefore measurable against krepair Table VI (median 76, 90th percentile 115, max 1264 options changed). I did not find in klocalizer or koverage documentation any per-object failing-conjunct explanation; that is an inference from absence in README and docs, not a stated fact. koverage reports patch coverage per line (docs mention a missing line 'might be a false alarm due to build issues').",
              "Adoption of klocalizer by the test/fuzz operators is not visible. GitHub issue search: 0 hits for klocalizer/krepair/kmax in google/syzkaller issues and PRs; 0 commits mentioning klocalizer in torvalds/linux. The only list traffic found is a Sept 2023 ext4 report by the paper's first author using a klocalizer-enhanced syzbot config, which Ts'o dismissed (zero-length reproducer; official syzbot offers bisection and patch testing) and Wong answered by noting the kernel was about four months old (summarized thread, lkml.iu.edu 2309.1/05403). Outside academic use exists: kmax has 121 issues matching klocalizer (the 23 visible are all open; reporters from 2024-2026 look external), and a UCF honors thesis (KBootRepair, Spring 2026) combines an LLM with klocalizer to repair boot failures (90% within 20 attempts on 50 random x86_64 configs; abstract via summarizer).",
              "syzbot ci chose a different path in 2026 rather than krepair/klocalizer. Located the injection point: syz-cluster/workflow/build/main.go (fetch summarizer) reads the named base config, does parsed.Set(cfg, kconfig.Yes) for each req.EnableConfigs, converts =m to =y with ModToYes(), serializes it, and passes it to build.Image. No check afterwards that the options survived (Kconfig dependency resolution happens inside build.Image, which I did not open: 'olddefconfig inside build.Image' is unverified). patch_triage.go prompt: 'Identify any specific CONFIG_ options required to properly compile and reach the modified code:' and 'Inspect Kconfig files and #ifdef guards; do not make assumptions.' Validation: strip prefix, require the name to exist in the arch's parsed Kconfig. No mention of klocalizer, kmax, krepair or olddefconfig anywhere in patch_triage.go. So the demand evidence is real and the operator's chosen competitor is an LLM plus an existence check, not Kmax.",
              "The dossier claim that a precomputed static table 'can replace the per-series base-versus-patched build' is probably wrong. fuzz/main.go generates both a base and a patched syz-manager config and imports pkg/manager/diff, i.e. syzbot ci fuzzes base against patched differentially, so both kernels are built anyway; the symbol-hash comparison (shouldSkipFuzzing) only avoids the fuzzing run, not the builds. This is an inference from imports and config generation; I did not read pkg/manager/diff. A static oracle would save fuzzing time (skip or re-target) and would pick EnableConfigs before the build, not save the second build.",
              "KUnit variant is largely a Kconfig-symbol problem, not a Kbuild-object problem, and a trivial symbol-level check competes with kfold. KUnit style guide (docs.kernel.org, summarized): each suite is tied to a Kconfig entry that depends on CONFIG_KUNIT and defaults to KUNIT_ALL_TESTS. So 'is every test built by some maintained config' can be answered by running olddefconfig with all_tests.config and listing *_KUNIT_TEST symbols that stay unset, with no Makefile analysis. The documented failures are Kconfig-side: PRIME_NUMBERS (commit 4ea404fdbc39, Mark Brown, 2025-04-22: a select was removed; fix adds CONFIG_PRIME_NUMBERS=y to all_tests.config) and iommupt (commit cefd81e76a8c, 2026-01-06: the fix adds CONFIG_COMPILE_TEST=y and removes CONFIG_IOMMUFD_TEST=y in .kunitconfig, a visibility/dependency fix). kfold sees these only through its Kconfig model K (an over-approximation) or through why on the resulting .config. The kfold-specific KUnit add-on is narrow: test .c files that no config's object list contains (Makefile wiring) and the #include-the-test-file pattern is the #ifdef half, which kfold does not cover.",
              "KUnit tooling itself (docs, summarized) states the gap but not a demand: kunit_tool 'warns if we have not included the options dependencies'; nested .kunitconfig is 'iffy'; recursive combination is dismissed with 'tests could theoretically depend on incompatible options, so handling that would be tricky.' No source found where a maintainer asks for a tool. all_tests.config could not be re-fetched (HTTP 429), so 'manually maintained' and the 40-line count rest on the dossier's earlier verification, not re-opened here.",
              "Other tools in this space are weaker or off-target. cvehound (GitHub, 138 stars): with --kernel-config it infers the CONFIG options needed to build each affected file from Kbuild Makefiles; 'ifdefs are not checked'; unmappable files are reported 'unknown' and counted as affected; kbuild.py is line/regex based for variable capture with separate processor classes for if/objects/subdirs (I could not read those). It is a shape-similar but heuristic Kbuild reader aimed at CVEs. PatchLens (FSE 2026, arXiv 2606.25863): hunk-level presence conditions with 'lightweight' build-system analysis, no config output, no klocalizer/Kmax mention on the abstract page. FCC (arXiv 2608.15225): LLM plus olddefconfig feedback, CVE-oriented; calls the syzbot reference config upstream-apparmor-kasan.config (4,206 enabled entries) 'too broad' for identifying a CVE's options; I read all but the last 7.5k characters. Kismet runs in the 0-day robot (Kconfig-level only). ConfigFix (ICSE SEIP 2021), Kconfirm, kernelconfig.io, TuxMake/TuxSuite, KernelCI: Kconfig-level, symbol lookup, or build matrices; none maps objects to configs. These come from prior dossier claims and were NOT re-opened (search budget exhausted).",
              "Coverage: WebSearch budget was exhausted after three searches, so recall is limited to targeted fetches. Not searched or opened this pass: a head-to-head of klocalizer on the 402 commits; Kmax's own non-Linux support (the 2017 paper's evaluation set); whether koverage can explain a failing constraint; 0-day or KernelCI use of KUnit; Yocto/Debian/Fedora config tooling; the 'make dir/file.o compiles the file regardless of obj-y' behaviour (believed true, unverified, not cited). GitHub code search needs authentication (HTTP 401), so counts of .kunitconfig files in the tree were not re-checked.",
              "Double-blind and wording hazards: do not write 'first'. ICSE 2025 states 'To the best of our knowledge, no systematic analysis of configuration selection for continuous kernel fuzzers has been conducted previously', so a related-work sentence must credit it for the fuzzing-config problem. The ICSE experiment used 10 random commits for the overhead measurements; the number of commits in the 12-hour bug-finding runs was not extracted (unverified)."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "Dossier statement 'the 42% syzbot patch coverage is NOT in this paper or in the OSS 2022 slides; do not cite it' needs correcting. ICSE 2025 (Hasanov, Nagy, Gazzillo), Fig. 1 and Section III, text extracted with pdftotext: syz-kconf mean patch coverage on the same 507 patches is 43.2%; kAFL's config 24.1%; defconfig 21.8% (prior work); after krepair 98.6% for syzkaller and kAFL. The 42% in a search summary is a rounding of this. Cite ICSE 2025 (and the FSE 2024 paper for the method), not OSS 2022 slides. Quote (verbatim, pdftotext): 'syzkaller's syz-kconf covers more, 43.2%, although it is still less than half of patched code.'",
              "Dossier remaining_gap (a) says no tool returns a configuration change on top of a base fuzz config that builds given objects. Correct: klocalizer --repair with --include (objects) or --include-mutex (patch) does this on top of an existing .config, and ICSE 2025 applied it to syzkaller's own configs. What the sources do NOT show is any per-object failing-conjunct explanation, any exactness validation of the Kbuild constraints, any automatic olddefconfig+build verification step inside the tool (the README runs olddefconfig as a separate make step and tells the user to look for the 'CC ...alauda.o' line; ICSE 2025 says the user runs make olddefconfig), or minimality (median 76 options changed, FS_VERITY disabled).",
              "Dossier 'closest tool: klocalizer ... no sign of use by syzbot' is supported only as absence: 0 matches in google/syzkaller issues and PRs, 0 commits in torvalds/linux. But it overlooks that the klocalizer authors published and evaluated the syzbot use case themselves (ICSE 2025) and filed an LKML bug report in 2023 using a klocalizer-enhanced syzbot config. 'No one has tried' is false; 'no operator adopted it' is what the evidence supports.",
              "Dossier kfold_contribution item (4), that a precomputed table 'can replace the per-series base-versus-patched build', should be reworded: the base build is probably needed for syzbot ci's differential fuzzing (pkg/manager/diff), so the saving is the fuzzing run and the choice of EnableConfigs, not a kernel build. Mark as inference until pkg/manager/diff is read.",
              "Dossier item (b) 'a PRIME_NUMBERS-style silent drop would show up as a failing check' is true, but the same check is achievable without Kbuild analysis (list *_KUNIT_TEST symbols unset after olddefconfig of all_tests.config), because the style guide ties each suite to a Kconfig entry. Do not use the four fix commits as evidence for object-level analysis; only the retrospective test (would the check have flagged them at the parent commit) can, and it should be compared with the symbol-level baseline.",
              "Dossier says the syzbot ci config 'injection' is established by a commit message. Now located in code: syz-cluster/workflow/build/main.go sets each EnableConfigs entry to y, converts =m to =y, and there is no check that options survived; the dependency completion step, if any, is inside build.Image (not opened).",
              "Dossier 'kfold does not handle UML-unavailable options' and 'FCC compares syzbot config as too broad' are fine; but 'Barebox ... klocalizer does not target BusyBox, Barebox, U-Boot, coreboot' (from context file claim 138) is an inference from absence; the 2017 Kmax paper's subjects were not re-opened here. Verify before claiming 'other Kbuild projects' as a differentiator."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "rq3_task",
            "summary": "Verdict: partly solved. The syzbot half of D11 has a published, evaluated solution: ICSE 2025 (Hasanov, Nagy, Gazzillo) applies krepair/klocalizer to syz-kconf output per commit; syz-kconf covers 43.2% of patched lines, krepair raises it to 98.6%, and fuzzing the repaired configs found 35 bugs vs 13. klocalizer itself offers per-object (--include) and per-patch (--include-mutex) config repair, including #ifdef via SuperC, so any claim that 'nothing derives the object-to-config step' must be dropped. Its documented limits are real but are limits, not absences: formulas 'may not be exact', one architecture per run, header-only patches unsupported, no published accuracy study, not minimal (median 76 options changed, FS_VERITY disabled in a case study), no per-object explanation found in the docs (inference). Adoption by operators is not visible: 0 mentions of klocalizer or krepair in google/syzkaller issues/PRs or in torvalds/linux commits; syzbot ci built an LLM step in June 2026 instead. That step sets named options to y, converts m to y, and validates only that names exist in Kconfig, with no check that the options survived dependency resolution (located in syz-cluster/workflow/build/main.go; the build.Image internals were not opened). The KUnit half is weaker for kfold: failures such as PRIME_NUMBERS and iommupt are Kconfig-dependency issues, and a symbol-level check of *_KUNIT_TEST options after olddefconfig would catch them without any Kbuild analysis; I found no tool that does this, and no source where a maintainer asks for one. What kfold can honestly claim: an object-level, deterministic oracle with a failing-conjunct chain and an olddefconfig-verified minimal change, run over the whole tree in about a minute, usable before the build to choose or check EnableConfigs; whether it beats klocalizer on speed, exactness or minimality is only supportable with a measured head-to-head (needs SuperC/Java setup, a new infrastructure cost under the one-process rule), and krepair's per-patch runtimes (a few minutes; under one minute for 93.8% of cached runs; up to an hour in outliers) mean the speed argument must not be overstated. Recommended paper use: an RQ task replicating ICSE 2025's measurement at object level (kfold why for the touched objects of the 402 .c/.S commits against upstream-apparmor-kasan.config after olddefconfig; failing-conjunct histogram; config-for change counts vs krepair's median 76), with ICSE 2025 and klocalizer credited in related work; do not use it as the headline motivation. Recall caveat: WebSearch budget was exhausted after three queries, so absence claims rest on GitHub issue/commit searches and targeted fetches only.",
