          {
            "lens": "FIT AND EXPERIMENT DESIGNER, D11: deriving and verifying test and fuzzer configurations (KUnit, syzbot)",
            "verdict": "partly",
            "issues": [
              "FIT. Half of D11 is an object-selection problem that kfold's build conditions address. Syzbot ci's question 'is the patched object in the fuzzed build' and the KUnit question 'does any maintained test config build this test object' both reduce to evaluating an object's Kbuild condition under a concrete .config (kfold why, blindspots). Neither needs the Kconfig model K, so K's over-approximation does not affect them. The other half is not: the documented KUnit incidents are Kconfig-dependency drift (the PRIME_NUMBERS select removed earlier, GENERIC_PT made invisible, GPIOLIB missing from a hand-kept seed list). The Kbuild condition detects the symptom (test object unbuilt) but does not explain it. Naming the blocking Kconfig symbol needs K, which is an over-approximation.",
              "The strongest D11 evidence is not about kfold's strength. The cleanest incident (iommupt, cefd81e76a8c) is a Kconfig visibility problem and the fix was an edit to .kunitconfig (+COMPILE_TEST, -IOMMUFD_TEST). The GPIO fix (34b5c0132952) is one added line, CONFIG_GPIOLIB=y, with no Makefile or Kconfig change. The PRIME_NUMBERS fix (4ea404fdbc39) is +2 lines in all_tests.config. A reviewer will read these as Kconfig-dependency or test-seed bugs. kfold would have flagged 'object not built', but nothing in the evidence shows that a Kbuild-condition check would have given the right repair.",
              "The all_tests.config header (re-read) says it targets UML and 'is manually maintained, though it uses KUNIT_ALL_TESTS=y to enable any tests whose dependencies are already satisfied'. Tests with unsatisfied dependencies are therefore left out by design, not by accident. kfold has only been run with an x86 entry. Evaluating all_tests.config needs either an arch/um entry (extra setup, risk) or kunit.py --arch=x86_64 with a merged fragment, which differs from what CI actually runs. Many KUnit tests are also compiled as '#include \"foo_test.c\"' inside a host object under #ifdef CONFIG_..._KUNIT_TEST. That is a C-preprocessor case invisible at object level. I did not measure the share of such tests, so any KUnit coverage figure must exclude or separately count them.",
              "For syzbot ci, kfold gives a sound NEGATIVE only. If the touched object's condition is false under the config, the patch is certainly not compiled. If it is true, the patch may still be compiled out by #ifdef, be header-only, or be unreachable. The existing check (base vs patched symbol hash) is exact for compiled-code differences. The dossier's claim that a precomputed table 'can replace' the two-build check holds only for the not-built case. The measurable quantity is the fraction of hash-equal 'skip' cases that kfold catches, and that needs ground truth that the available assets only partly provide (see experiment).",
              "Population mismatch. The 402 .c/.S commits of v7.2.7..v7.2.8 are stable backports, mostly bug fixes in drivers, fs and net. They are not new mainline patch series, which is what syzbot ci fuzzes. A percentage such as 'X% of commits are not built by the syzbot config' describes this sample only. Do not present it as a rate for syzbot ci traffic.",
              "The syzbot config is not an available asset: it must be downloaded (dashboard/config/linux/upstream-apparmor-kasan.config), is generated for another kernel version, and needs 'make olddefconfig' on 7.2.8 x86_64 (no build). Its =m-to-=y rewrite by syz-cluster was not re-opened by me. The LLM EnableConfigs overlay (the 2026-06 step) cannot be reproduced offline, so kfold cannot be compared with it head to head. The comparison is only against the static config (no LLM), the hash method, and klocalizer.",
              "Prior art is the main reviewer objection. klocalizer/krepair already repair a .config so that named objects or patch lines build, including #ifdef via SuperC, and reported 98.5% mean patch coverage on 507 patches (arXiv 2404.17966, from the dossier; not re-opened by me). Without a head-to-head against the saved Kmax run on the same objects, a PLDI reviewer will ask what kfold adds. The honest differentiators are measurable ones: per-object failing-conjunct explanation, speed (about 1 minute whole-tree), and the olddefconfig-verified minimal change. They must be measured, not asserted.",
              "The commit cfedfb24c9dd, named in the dossier's secondary experiment as a fourth known fix, is not in the dossier's evidence list. I did not verify it. Only three KUnit fix commits (4ea404fdbc39, cefd81e76a8c, 34b5c0132952) are verified here. A retrospective check on n=3 or 4 incidents is an anecdote and cannot be a headline.",
              "The 'demand' is operator artifacts only. No source shows a maintainer asking for a tool, and the syzbot ci LLM step is both the demand and the incumbent. The 2026-06 files are a moving target (latest change 2026-10-15 per the dossier), so cite a commit hash, not master."
            ],
            "corrections": [
              "PRIME_NUMBERS (4ea404fdbc39, re-read via GitHub API): the fix touches only tools/testing/kunit/configs/all_tests.config (+2 lines, CONFIG_PRIME_NUMBERS=y) and carries 'Fixes: 3f2925174f8b'. The select was removed in that earlier commit. The dossier's wording is right, but the fix is a seed-list edit, so describe it as 'dependency drift in a hand-kept list', not 'a missing Kbuild condition'. I could not re-read lib/math/Makefile (HTTP 429), so I do not know what Kbuild condition the test object has. Mark unverified.",
              "iommupt (cefd81e76a8c): the commit is dated 2026-01-10 in the commit API (committer Joerg Roedel; the dossier says 2026-01-06, probably the author date). It edits drivers/iommu/generic_pt/.kunitconfig (+CONFIG_COMPILE_TEST=y, -CONFIG_IOMMUFD_TEST=y) and Fixes 01569c216dde ('genpt: Make GENERIC_PT invisible') and 1dd4187f53c3. This is a Kconfig visibility problem. Do not present it as an object-selection case.",
              "GPIO (34b5c0132952): confirmed as one line, CONFIG_GPIOLIB=y, reviewed by David Gow, with the committed text 'the kunit test cases for GPIO core can be built with this config'. The commit does not name the dependency chain.",
              "all_tests.config: 40 CONFIG_ lines, all =y, confirmed on re-read. The header says 'under UML'. The dossier omits that this makes it an arch/um artifact.",
              "global-config.yaml (re-read): nine fuzz targets, each with kernel_config: upstream-apparmor-kasan.config; only the mm target sets skip_cover_check: true. The dossier's claim is confirmed. Two of the nine targets are labelled 'net' (a network and RDMA path set, and a network-filesystem path set), so 'nine' counts targets, not distinct subsystems.",
              "patch_triage.go (re-read): the validator checks only that each name is defined in the top-level Kconfig, and 'nothing else reads' the validated list within that file. Whether the list reaches the build (the dossier says it is 'injected into the kernel build configuration', from a commit message of 2026-06-09) was not re-opened by me. Say 'per the commit message' if cited.",
              "The 33 'dashboard/config enable' issues/PRs are a title-pattern count and must not be quoted as a measure of unbuilt code. The DVB leak anecdote is an #ifdef case. The '42% syzbot patch coverage' figure must not be cited (the dossier itself says it is unsupported)."
            ],
            "use_in_paper": "rq3_task",
            "summary": "VERDICT. Partly an object-selection problem. The Kbuild half is: given a concrete .config, decide which objects are built, name the failing conjuncts, and propose a minimal verified change. That half covers 'is the patched object in the fuzzed build' (syzbot ci) and 'does any maintained test config build this test object' (KUnit). The documented KUnit root causes are Kconfig-dependency drift: a removed select, an invisible option, a hand-kept seed list. Preprocessor, reachability and runtime issues (the DVB leak, #include-d tests, syscall reachability, UML-only options) are outside kfold. kfold gives a sound 'not built' but cannot prove 'reached'. The dossier's evidence and its overall confidence of 'medium' are fair. The demand evidence is real (the syzbot ci LLM step, 9 targets sharing one config, hand-maintained all_tests.config), but it is evidence of an operator problem, not of kfold's superiority.\\n\\nSINGLE STRONGEST EXPERIMENT (an extension of the existing config-for evaluation with a different base config). Question: for patches landing in the tree, how often is the touched object absent from the single config used by all nine syzbot ci production targets, and does kfold decide that, and repair it, correctly? Inputs: (1) the 402 .c/.S commits of v7.2.7..v7.2.8 already processed; (2) upstream-apparmor-kasan.config from syzkaller (pin the commit hash), with the same =m to =y rewrite as syz-cluster, then 'make olddefconfig' on Linux 7.2.8 x86_64 (no build) to get a concrete .config; (3) the four existing builds' object inventories (i386 tinyconfig, x86-64 defconfig, Debian, allmodconfig) as ground truth for kfold's verdicts; (4) the saved Kmax/klocalizer run on Linux as comparison; (5) the existing config-for runs with a base config, re-run with the syzbot config as base. Metrics: (a) per commit, all touched objects built / some / none under the syzbot config, with the failing-conjunct histogram grouped by MAINTAINERS entry; (b) soundness of kfold's verdicts: false-'unbuilt' and false-'built' rates against the four inventories over all touched objects (about 400 already checked, now 402 commits times 4 configs), reported per config; (c) for commits with an unbuilt object, the number of options config-for changes relative to the syzbot config (median, IQR) and the olddefconfig verification rate, against defconfig as base and against klocalizer on the same objects; (d) a spot check of the syzbot-config verdicts on about 20 objects with single-object 'make path/x.o' compiles, one process at a time (CLAUDE.md), because no build exists for that config. Baselines: B1 'assume built' (what path-regexp target selection plus skip_cover_check amounts to); B2 a naive heuristic (grep the nearest Makefile for the obj- line's CONFIG_ symbol and test it in .config, ignoring parent-directory gating); B3 Kmax/klocalizer verdict and repair size; B4 allmodconfig inventory as the 'just use a blanket config' coverage. The LLM EnableConfigs step cannot be reproduced and is excluded; say so. Headline form (no numbers yet, none are known): 'Under the one config used by all nine syzbot ci targets, X of 402 stable commits (a%) touch only unbuilt objects and Y (b%) touch some; kfold's verdicts agree with real builds on Z% of objects with no false-unbuilt case; config-for builds the missing objects by changing a median of k options (verified for v%), versus k' for klocalizer.' Secondary only if time allows (about 2 more person-days): for the 46 .kunitconfig files plus all_tests.config, list KUnit test objects (own .o, name *_kunit.o or *_test.o, option depends on KUNIT) that no config builds on x86_64 after olddefconfig, cross-checked against the allmodconfig inventory (tests built there but in no maintained test config), and report the three verified fix commits as retrospective cases (n=3, an anecdote, not a result).\\n\\nEFFORT. About 4 person-days for the primary part (1 day fetching the config and getting the verdicts across 4 builds; 1 day config-for with the syzbot base; 0.5 day for the 20-object compile check; 0.5 day for the Kmax head-to-head; 1 day for analysis and grouping). Kfold runs take minutes, there are no kernel builds beyond the 20 single-object compiles, and everything runs strictly one process at a time.\\n\\nGROUND TRUTH. The primary experiment does not need unavailable ground truth for kfold's accuracy: object existence in four real builds is a proxy for 'built', and the 400 earlier verdicts show the pipeline works. What is missing is (i) a build with the syzbot config (partly covered by the 20-object spot check) and (ii) the hash-equal 'skip' labels of syzbot ci, which need patched builds. Object existence therefore undercounts the skip cases where an object is built but the hunk is compiled out by #ifdef. Report this as a lower bound on what the hash check catches and do not claim equivalence. KUnit ground truth (real runs under UML) is unavailable, so the secondary part stays at the level of set differences and incident replays.\\n\\nFALSIFIERS. The claim 'kfold is a useful deterministic oracle for the object-selection step of test and fuzz configuration' is weakened or falsified if: (1) any systematic false-'unbuilt' appears (above about 2% of objects) in the four-build comparison or in the 20-object compile check, since a non-sound negative would make the oracle unusable; (2) fewer than about 5% of commits touch unbuilt objects under the syzbot config (the config is already broad enough that the LLM or two-build check is unnecessary); (3) config-for under the syzbot base verifies in clearly fewer cases than under defconfig (below about 80%), which would show that K's over-approximation hurts the very configs fuzzers use; (4) klocalizer's saved run agrees with kfold on verdicts and gives equal or smaller repairs, leaving only speed and explanation as differences. Report outcome (4) honestly if it occurs.\\n\\nTHREATS TO VALIDITY. Stable-backport population (not new series); one architecture (amd64) and one kernel version (7.2.8) against a syzbot config made for another version; the config is rewritten and re-solved with olddefconfig, so it is not byte-identical to what syzbot builds; header-only and #ifdef-only patches are out of scope (a sound negative only); #include-d KUnit tests and UML-specific options are not handled; the Kconfig model K is an over-approximation, so config-for results depend on it and are verified only by olddefconfig and object compilation; syzbot ci code is a moving target (cite a commit); comparison with an LLM step that was not reproduced; a possible coincidence with the authors' own tool choices (kfold's entry settings for x86); counts from GitHub code search are approximate.\\n\\nPLACEMENT. Use as one RQ3 developer task: 'derive a fuzzer configuration for the objects of a patch', implemented as the existing config-for evaluation with the syzbot config as base. It is cheap because it reuses the 402-commit run and gives a comparison with the saved Kmax run, and it speaks to a concrete operator (syzbot ci) that has already built a heuristic. Add one sentence of introduction motivation (hand-maintained configs and an LLM guess validated only for name existence), citing the re-verified syzkaller files and the KUnit fix commits, and no more. Put KUnit in related work and limitations: the incidents are Kconfig-dependency drift, tests can be #include-d under #ifdef, and kfold has no UML entry. Reviewer objections this placement answers: 'this is a CI engineering exercise, not a PL contribution' (it is one row of the evaluation, not a claim); 'klocalizer already does this' (head-to-head, with stated measurable differences only); 'did you show it prevents bugs' (no, and we do not claim it); 'is object granularity enough' (state the sound-negative-only scope and report how many verdicts #ifdef could change as a limitation). Do not use the DVB leak or the 42% figure. Do not lead with D11: it is the least independent of Kconfig correctness among the candidate problems and its strongest evidence is an anecdote.\"]",
            "sources_checked": [
              {
